\\n\\n- How many issues did testing catch?\\n- How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?\\n- What was the most helpful test you ran?\\n- What was the least helpful test you ran?\\n\\nEngine Feedback\\n\\n- What did you like most about the engine?\\n140→- Did you get stuck or guess wrong?\\n- What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?\\n- What was the hardest part about using the engine?\\n- What did you change in the engine, and why? (none if nothing)\\n- If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?\\n\\nPrompt Feedback\\n\\n- What did you like most about this prompt?\\n- What was confusing?\\n150→- What was the hardest thing to do?\\n- What would you change in this prompt?\\n\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223916__x488eb9/work/TASK.md\",\"offset\":null,\"raw_output\":\"SOURCE: Trauma Center: Second Opinion\\n\\nGenre: Pick the game genre(s) that best fit the source and would make the best game.\\n\\nPerspective: Pick the camera perspective(s) that best fit the genre and source and would make the best game.\\n\\nGame Title: A playful riff on SOURCE's name. Silently brainstorm 50 titles, then pick one with a real random number generator (e.g., run code).\\n\\nResearch: Whether and how much to research is your choice.\\n\\nPackage: For ease of deployment and sharing provide a single html file [GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223916].html. Dependencies that can be downloaded do not have to be included in the html file if that is easier.\\n\\nTech Stack: Build on https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/tree/main/dist/handoff. You may change anything in it, the engine included, and add whatever systems, art assets and animations the game needs. The rest of the tech stack is your choice.\\n\\nArt Direction: Use the engine’s native style as the base. Make new art assets for whatever makes the game feel like the source; the engine’s asset library is fine for generic props.\\n\\nAnimation: Use the engine’s native animation system as the base. Make new animations for whatever makes the game feel like the source.\\n\\nAudio: All music, sound effects and voices are written in code, with no audio files. Voices are Animalese/Simlish-style gibberish. Music is to be set to 10% volume by default.\\n\\nOnline: https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub\\n\\nWorking Environment and deliverable:\\nCreate a subfolder called [GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223916] to keep all your dev files in. You can develop separate modules, then bundle into a single file [GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223916].html in root when you’re done. You may use external CDN dependencies. Do not read files outside what you need: other agents work in the folders beside this one. They share this machine’s ports and processes too. Prefer file:// and the engine’s headless tools. If you need a server (the AI Hub harness does), bind 127.0.0.1 on a port the OS picks (`python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` prints it) and confirm the page is your game before trusting a result. Stop only processes you started, by their PID: never pkill, killall, fuser -k, or kill whatever holds a port.\\n\\nPerformance: Treat the performance budget as unlimited. Add as much detail, enemies, effects, lights, and shadows as the scene needs. Out of scope (handled later): FPS targets, optimization.\\n\\nTesting: Test as much as you like with headless tools: scripts that play the game and check its state, including a scripted playthrough of the whole game. Keep them behind one command that prints only failures and a one-line count, and rerun it only after changing the code it covers. Use screenshots only for what a script can’t check. Don’t use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\\n\\nEffort: Spend effort however you see fit.\\n\\n## This computer\\nChecked 2026-11-04 20:05 (America/Vancouver) by node check-environment.mjs.\\n\\n- **Computer:** Worki9: 13th Gen Intel(R) Core(TM) i9-13900K, 32 cores, 20 GB RAM, 8 GB swap. Ubuntu 26.04.1 LTS on WSL2 (Linux 6.18.33.1-microsoft-standard-WSL2).\\n- **Space:** 850 GB free in the home folder. /tmp: 9.8 GB, in memory (tmpfs), 8.2 GB free when checked.\\n- **Display:** none for runs (they run with DISPLAY unset), so a headed browser needs xvfb-run.\\n- **Browsers:** Playwright 1.63.0 for Python (from playwright.sync_api import sync_playwright), with Chromium 153.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered; WebGPU, software-rendered, when started with --enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader), Firefox 155.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered), WebKit 26.6 (WebGL 2) already downloaded. Phone and tablet device profiles are built in.\\n  WebGPU only works on a secure page: file:// or http://127.0.0.1. A WebGPU canvas can come out blank in a headless screenshot, so check the game's state instead.\\n  For Node, `npm install playwright@1.63.0` in your folder uses the browsers already downloaded; another version downloads its own.\\n  Playwright runs here with PLAYWRIGHT_HOST_PLATFORM_OVERRIDE=ubuntu24.04-x64 (already set for runs): this OS isn't one it supports by name.\\n- **Tools:** node 24.22.1, npm 11.19.1, python3 3.14.8, pip3 26.2.1, git 2.56.0, gh 2.46.0, curl 8.22.0, wget 1.25.0, jq 1.8.1, ffmpeg 9.0.2, ffprobe 9.0.2, sqlite3 3.53.4, zip 3.0, unzip 6.00, rustc 1.99.0, cargo 1.99.0, go 1.27.1, java 27, lighthouse 13.5.0, shot-scraper 1.12, xvfb-run, magick 7.1.2, esbuild 0.28.2, uv 0.12.23, tsc 7.0.2, wasm-bindgen 0.2.129.\\n- **Rust:** rustup's toolchains 1.96.0, 1.98.1, stable; the wasm32-unknown-unknown target on 1.98.1.\\n- **Not installed:** sox, bun, deno.\\n- **Network:** internet works (github.com, registry.npmjs.org).\\n- **Memory:** earlyoom is on: when memory runs out it ends the biggest browser, so \\\"Target closed\\\", \\\"page crashed\\\" or \\\"browser has been closed\\\" means memory ran out, not a bug in your game. Run fewer browsers at once rather than retrying.\\n\\n### What this computer doesn't have\\n\\nNo script can check these, so they're written by hand. Test with the stand-ins; anything that needs the real thing is checked as far as it can be here, never required.\\n\\n- **No physical controller.** Emulate one: replace navigator.getGamepads with a stub that returns a pad in the standard mapping (Playwright's add_init_script / addInitScript).\\n- **No touch device.** Use Playwright's phone and tablet profiles, which have touch, and tap with page.touchscreen.\\n- **Nobody to listen.** Check sound by what Web Audio produces: render it with an OfflineAudioContext, or read levels from an AnalyserNode.\\n- **No second player.** Play multiplayer with two browser pages (or contexts) on this computer.\\n- **No real iPhone or iPad.** WebKit with an iPhone profile is the closest; it isn't iOS Safari.\\n- **No TV.** Judge the 10-foot view from screenshots at 1920×1080.\\n\\n### Sharing this computer\\n\\n- Other agents run here at the same time. Keep one or two browsers open at once, and close them when you're done.\\n- Keep every file you make inside your own folder, not /tmp or a parent folder: /tmp is shared.\\n- The browsers are already downloaded: don't run `playwright install` or `playwright install-deps`.\\n- The system Python has Playwright and nothing else. For one (Pillow, numpy …), use uv: `uv run --with pillow python script.py`, adding `--with playwright==1.63.0` when the script drives a browser too (a newer Playwright can't find the browsers here).\\n\\n## Goal\\n\\nBuild an original game inspired by the source.\\n\\nScope\\n\\nCreate a feature-complete demo. Capture what makes the source distinctive (its world, characters, themes and tone) and turn it into the mechanics, pacing and UX of a great game. The demo must be long enough to show ALL features and gameplay mechanics so a player can clearly understand how the game plays and feels, and what they can and cannot do. Treat the demo as a complete, polished vertical slice. It should contain all the infrastructure, systems, and polish needed so the remainder of the game could later be built out with normal designer tools.\\n\\nInclude a dev mode, reachable from the pause menu, with the standard tools for testing the game quickly. Balance is key, so expose the most important gameplay variables as live sliders, with a reset to defaults.\\n\\nAttract Mode: After 10 seconds without input on the title/menu screen, start a looping AI-played demo or scripted replay (arcade/kiosk style); any input exits it. The AI (or replay) can be simple and may cheat freely (infinite health, ammo, abilities, no clip, etc). Its only job is to show as much of the demo as possible, the attract mode is not meant to balance the game.\\n\\nMultiplayer: Follow https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub for multiplayer integration into ai-hub as well as for the game on its own.\\n\\nNarrative tone: Use a writing style similar to the source’s.\\n\\n## UI & UX: 10-Foot Controller-First Philosophy\\nDesign the game around a PS5 DualSense (or any pad the browser reports in its standard mapping) played on a TV at 10 feet. With keyboard and mouse it's the same game on the same screens, not a separate desktop layout, but they should feel native, not like stand-ins for controller buttons. The keyboard uses the keys PC players expect for the genre. The mouse aims, looks, and clicks menu items and things in the world (hover moves the focus) wherever the genre uses it. On-screen prompts follow the input used last: PlayStation glyphs for the controller (with a Settings option to show Xbox glyphs, A B X Y, instead), keys and mouse buttons for keyboard and mouse.\\n\\nDo not use overly dense, web-style data screens.\\n\\nTouch-only devices: no touch controls; show \\\"Controller or keyboard required\\\" and the settings button. Let the attract mode run.\\n\\n## Done\\nNo TODOs, placeholders, or stubbed features. The demo is complete.\\n\\n## Source Information\\nCRITICAL: Put Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback in your chat reply and an in-game About/Credits screen. Write \\\"unknown\\\" for anything you can't confirm.\\n- Date: 2026-11-04 22:39:16\\n- Source: the work it's based on\\n- Plan: the file name of the plan you were given to build from, or none\\n- Genre and Perspective: genre and camera perspective used\\n- Tech: stack, libraries/engines, with versions if applicable\\n- Art Direction: style used\\n- Animation: system used\\n- Audio: music, sound effects and voice used\\n- Online: network features used, or none\\n- Testing: testing parameters allowed\\n- Research: research done, or none\\n- Environment: Grok CLI (headless); Linux\\n- Model: xAI, grok-4.7, reasoning effort xhigh\\n- Usage: write \\\"added on import\\\". You can't see your own cost, so don't estimate it: the runner records the real cost, tokens and time, and the agent that imports the game into AI Hub puts them on your About/Credits screen.\\n\\nExit interview: before you finish, also save these answers as exit-interview.json in this folder's root, beside the game's .html: one JSON object in exactly the shape below, every value filled in (\\\"unknown\\\" for anything you can't confirm). Tools read it (the game's run record and the AI Hub catalogue), so write plain text, and check that the file parses as JSON.\\n{\\n  \\\"sourceInformation\\\": {\\\"date\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"plan\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"genre\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"perspective\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"tech\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"artDirection\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"animation\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"audio\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"online\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"testing\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"research\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"environment\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"model\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"usage\\\": \\\"added on import\\\"},\\n  \\\"testingFeedback\\\": [\\\"your answer to each Testing Feedback question, in order\\\"],\\n  \\\"engineFeedback\\\": [\\\"your answer to each Engine Feedback question, in order\\\"],\\n  \\\"promptFeedback\\\": [\\\"your answer to each Prompt Feedback question, in order\\\"],\\n  \\\"aiHub\\\": {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"the game's title, as on its title screen\\\",\\n    \\\"tagline\\\": \\\"a few words to go under the title\\\",\\n    \\\"description\\\": \\\"one or two sentences for the catalogue\\\",\\n    \\\"type\\\": \\\"Game, Tech demo or Tool\\\",\\n    \\\"genre\\\": \\\"one of Action, Adventure, Fighting, Flight, Horror, Kids & Learning, Life Sim, Platformer, Puzzle, Racing, RPG, Shooter, Simulation, Sports, Stealth, Strategy\\\",\\n    \\\"source\\\": \\\"the work it's based on, e.g. Moby-Dick\\\",\\n    \\\"series\\\": \\\"its franchise, e.g. Star Fox, or empty\\\",\\n    \\\"players\\\": \\\"how many can play: 1, 1–2 or 1–4\\\",\\n    \\\"controls\\\": \\\"the controls, in one line\\\",\\n    \\\"tags\\\": [\\\"3 to 6 lowercase tags, e.g. 2d, 3d, arcade, story, co-op, versus, first-person, third-person, top-down, side-scrolling, pixel art, low-poly, retro, sci-fi, fantasy, comedy, vehicles, boss fights, exploration\\\"]\\n  }\\n}\\nThe catalogue's model, effort, CLI, date and usage come from the run's record after you finish, so they aren't asked here.\\n\\nTesting Feedback\\n\\n- How many issues did testing catch?\\n- How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?\\n- What was the most helpful test you ran?\\n- What was the least helpful test you ran?\\n\\nEngine Feedback\\n\\n- What did you like most about the engine?\\n- Did you get stuck or guess wrong?\\n- What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?\\n- What was the hardest part about using the engine?\\n- What did you change in the engine, and why? (none if nothing)\\n- If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?\\n\\nPrompt Feedback\\n\\n- What did you like most about this prompt?\\n- What was confusing?\\n- What was the hardest thing to do?\\n- What would you change in this prompt?\\n\",\"total_lines\":151}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa37-f7cf-7001-9198-38ad04ed0f5e","uuid":"d21b8e6c-d457-4caf-a63e-cc31b3b628c5"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_2","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The PLAN.md is very long. I need to read the Done checklist, testing remainder, and appendices summaries before writing the goal plan.\n\nSTAGE.md instructs building the game described in PLAN.md. TASK.md provides file names and hand-in requirements. PROGRESS.md tracks progress for resumption after context compaction. This is a massive implementation task. I am the Goal Plan Writer, not the implementer.\n\nThe goal plan must specify outcomes rather than architecture. It must not dictate module layouts, class names, or exact signatures. Acceptance criteria should be small and outcome-based. Visual objectives need static or structural fallbacks plus unit tests and browser script loading. Runnable deliverables require an entry-point launch check. Named games with established canon require research into defining mechanics first.\n\nThe game is Trauma Center: Second Opinion. PLAN.md already contains extensive research and locked design decisions for this named title. The implementer should build the full game as specified in PLAN.md and hand in all deliverables required by TASK.md.\n\nThe objective names STAGE.md, PLAN.md, and TASK.md rather than Trauma Center directly. STAGE.md instructs building the game PLAN.md describes. TASK.md lists Trauma Center: Second Opinion as the source material. The implementer must follow this document chain rather than relying on generic canon research.\n\nI will inspect the files named in the objective and context. PLAN.md is the implementer's authoritative specification, while TASK.md governs file names. A brief web search may confirm core defining mechanics, but the existing documents take precedence.","signature":"tco_69efe627-d526-94a4-a561-660d9e4764cc_call-f60a4706-d7c9-4b92-aa10-9f15980e5409-5_"},{"type":"text","text":"PLAN.md is long. I'll read the rest, especially the done checklist and what must be handed in."},{"type":"server_tool_use","id":"ws_69efe627-d526-94a4-a561-660d9e4764cc_call-f60a4706-d7c9-4b92-aa10-9f15980e5409-5","name":"web_search","input":{"query":"Trauma Center: Second Opinion defining gameplay mechanics operation tools Healing Touch vitals rank"}},{"type":"web_search_tool_result","tool_use_id":"ws_69efe627-d526-94a4-a561-660d9e4764cc_call-f60a4706-d7c9-4b92-aa10-9f15980e5409-5","content":[{"type":"web_search_result","url":"https://strategywiki.org/wiki/Trauma_Center:_Second_Opinion/Gameplay","title":"https://strategywiki.org/wiki/Trauma_Center:_Second_Opinion/Gameplay"},{"type":"web_search_result","url":"https://traumacentergame.fandom.com/wiki/Vitals","title":"https://traumacentergame.fandom.com/wiki/Vitals"},{"type":"web_search_result","url":"https://traumacentergame.fandom.com/wiki/Difficulty","title":"https://traumacentergame.fandom.com/wiki/Difficulty"},{"type":"web_search_result","url":"https://scathingaccuracy.com/guides/trauma-center-second-opinion-xs-guide/","title":"https://scathingaccuracy.com/guides/trauma-center-second-opinion-xs-guide/"},{"type":"web_search_result","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trauma_Center:_Second_Opinion","title":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trauma_Center:_Second_Opinion"}]},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-f60a4706-d7c9-4b92-aa10-9f15980e5409-4","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223916__x488eb9/work/PLAN.md","offset":1000,"limit":340}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":28527,"output_tokens":855,"cache_read_input_tokens":15232,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa37-f7cf-7001-9198-38ad04ed0f5e","uuid":"8addbb61-d0b6-4cd8-849d-220f9f8d0831"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-f60a4706-d7c9-4b92-aa10-9f15980e5409-4","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1000→- The exit interview and `src/about-data.json` parse as JSON, and every field is filled.\\n\\n**Browser (Playwright, one or two browsers at a time, from `file://` where possible):** launch Chromium with `--autoplay-policy=no-user-gesture-required` for audio checks. Without it, the console's AudioContext warning before the first gesture is not a bug, so don't count warnings as failures.\\n- The built HTML plays 30 s with no errors in Chromium, Firefox and WebKit.\\n- `node tools/check.mjs` on the page (add `#scene` to jump to a scene): errors, what's on screen, look notes. Act on every look note.\\n- Settings defaults: music .1; glyphs PlayStation; the Xbox option switches labels.\\n- Pad with the Appendix E stub: `R2` and `△` prompts, stick cursor, wheel, trigger; keyboard and mouse switch the prompts.\\n- Phone profiles (Chromium, and WebKit with an iPhone profile, the closest thing here to iOS): notice and Settings button show, no touch buttons, attract runs behind, and a tap on Settings (`page.touchscreen.tap`) opens it.\\n- Audio: render through an `OfflineAudioContext` or read an `AnalyserNode`: effects, each voice and the choir are not silent; at defaults music is well below effects.\\n- Sizes: 1280x720, 1920x1080, 2560x1440, 3440x1440: HUD inside the screen, cavity centered.\\n1010→- **Hub harness, all four runs (Appendix D's runner):** `players=2&auto=1`; `players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1`; the same with `&scenario=host-leave`; `players=2&scenario=server&auto=1`. Pass means `window.__hubCheck.pass === true`. Then open the page outside a session.\\n- This computer has no second player, so use the harness's frames, or two browser pages or contexts, as the stand-in. With 2 players in the harness, whose game frames are same-origin and reachable from the test: feed a client's cursor through a stroke, and check the stroke lands on the host's state.\\n\\n**Screenshots, for what scripts can't judge:** each site, each stage, the portraits, one frame of each strain, the wheel, Long Breath. Use `tools/filmstrip.mjs` for motion. Judge at 1920x1080 for the 10-foot view. WebGPU isn't used, so screenshots work.\\n\\n**This computer** (full detail in TASK.md): no display, so `xvfb-run` if you ever need headed; Playwright 1.63 is ready (`npm install playwright@1.63.0` in the dev folder for Node); never run `playwright install`; for a Python library such as Pillow, use `uv` as TASK.md says; `Target closed` means memory ran out, not a bug: run fewer browsers. Other agents share this machine, so keep one or two browsers open and only read what you need, never the folders beside yours. Keep all files in your folder, not `/tmp`. Prefer `file://` and the engine's headless tools. Other agents share this machine's ports and processes: use a server only for the harness, bound to `127.0.0.1` on a port the OS picks (`python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` prints it), and confirm the page is your game before trusting a result. Stop only processes you started, by their PID: never `pkill`, `killall`, `fuser -k`, or kill whatever holds a port.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 21. Build order\\n1020→\\nEach milestone ends with a playable, tested game. Multiplayer comes early because it constrains the sim.\\n\\n| # | Milestone | Done when |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| M0 | Title picked. Folders, vendor, manifest, bundler, dev page, test runner, Hub client in the page | Empty game builds, runs, one test passes, harness runner works on Appendix D |\\n| M1 | **Hands:** sim core, tissue painter, cursor, wheel, Forceps, Salve, Suture, Syringe, vitals, clock, grades, HUD, Results, Case 1 end to end, bot | A person would enjoy Case 1 on a stubbed pad and mouse. Tune this until it's good; everything rests on it |\\n| M2 | **Shell:** title, menu, settings, glyphs, pause, TV scale, About skeleton, Dev Mode, attract v1, touch notice, saves, story system, busts, voices, first music, Case 1's story | First launch to Case 1 to Results feels finished, all TASK.md's cross-cutting features exist |\\n| M3 | Scalpel, Suction, Cautery, Sonar, Lightbox, Code Blue, Long Breath, Cases 2 and 3, Chapter 1 complete | Chapter 1 plays through, bot and all |\\n| M4 | **Grand Rounds:** local, then Hub, own card | All four harness runs pass, co-op works with bots |\\n1030→| M5 | Staccato, Legato, Tremolo, ward, Cases 4 to 6, Chapter 2 | Chapter 2 plays through |\\n| M6 | Second Look, tilt, blackout, Da Capo, device gestures, Cases 7 to 9, Chapter 3 | Chapter 3 plays through |\\n| M7 | Consult, Fermata, Cantor, Cases 10 and 11, ending, credits | The whole story plays through |\\n| M8 | **Polish:** feel table complete, cinematics, stages, title art, full score and mix, attract final reel, Skills Lab, balance runs, three browsers, CONTENT.md | Section 22 all true |\\n| M9 | About text, exit interview, final build, final test run, chat reply | Done |\\n\\n**If you run out of runway**, cut content from the end of this list and keep everything else: Case 9, then Skills Lab, then Da Capo's round 2, then the ship's ward (reuse Ward 3), then a stage or two. **Never cut:** the cursor's quality, any of the eight tools, both gifts, the finale, Grand Rounds and the Hub tests, attract mode, dev mode, the 10-foot UI, About, the exit interview. Whatever ships is complete: no stubs, no TODOs.\\n\\n---\\n\\n1040→## 22. Done checklist (TASK.md, line by line)\\n\\nGo through this at the end. Everything must be true.\\n\\n**Source, genre, perspective, title.**\\n- [ ] Original game from *Trauma Center: Second Opinion* per the originality rule; genre and perspective per section 2.\\n- [ ] Title is a playful riff, chosen by TASK.md's method with a real RNG, stored in one constant.\\n\\n**Package and environment.**\\n- [ ] One HTML file at the root as TASK.md names it, self-contained, working from `file://`.\\n1050→- [ ] Dev files in the subfolder TASK.md names. Nothing read from other agents' folders. Only your own processes stopped, by PID.\\n\\n**Tech, art, animation.**\\n- [ ] Built on the engine's handoff package; engine patches noted.\\n- [ ] Native pixel look as the base, new art for everything surgical, asset store only for generic props.\\n- [ ] Native animation as the base, new animations per section 17.\\n\\n**Audio.**\\n- [ ] All music, effects and voices in code, no audio files. Gibberish voices.\\n- [ ] Music at 10% by default (test proves it).\\n1060→\\n**Online.**\\n- [ ] Hub session protocol followed; all four harness runs pass; runs outside a session; couch co-op on its own.\\n\\n**Performance, testing.**\\n- [ ] No optimization work done for its own sake.\\n- [ ] One test command, printing only failures and a one-line count, passes.\\n- [ ] Scripted full playthrough passes.\\n\\n**Scope.**\\n1070→- [ ] Every mechanic in sections 5 to 9 is shown in play: a player can see what they can and cannot do.\\n- [ ] Data-driven content, `CONTENT.md`, Dev Jump.\\n- [ ] Dev mode from the pause menu, live sliders, reset to defaults.\\n- [ ] Attract after 10 s idle on title and menus, looping AI demo, any input exits.\\n- [ ] Tone: section 10.\\n\\n**UI and UX.**\\n- [ ] Controller-first on the same screens as keyboard and mouse, which feel native; hover moves focus; mouse aims and clicks things in the world.\\n- [ ] Prompts follow the last device. PlayStation glyphs, Settings option for Xbox, keys and mouse buttons for keyboard and mouse.\\n- [ ] No dense, web-style data screens.\\n1080→- [ ] Touch-only: no touch controls, \\\"Controller or keyboard required\\\" and the Settings button, attract runs.\\n\\n**Done and reporting.**\\n- [ ] No TODOs, placeholders, stubs.\\n- [ ] Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback, Prompt Feedback in the in-game About/Credits **and** in your final chat reply, \\\"unknown\\\" where you can't confirm, Usage as `added on import`.\\n- [ ] The exit interview saved beside the HTML file, in TASK.md's exact shape, parsing as JSON, every value filled.\\n- [ ] Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback answer each TASK.md question in order, from `notes/feedback.md`, honestly.\\n\\n---\\n\\n1090→## Appendix A: tissue painter\\n\\nRan on this computer at 480x270, 3 to 5 ms a frame under software rendering. It also runs headless. One instance per site. Each frame: stamp pools, wounds and so on from `S`, then `shade(fx)`, then draw `F.cv`.\\n\\n- `stampDisc(x, y, r, height, mat)`, `stampLine(points, r, height, mat)`: per-frame height and material. Height alone (no `mat`) makes a ridge, for Legato.\\n- `shade({ beat, breath, slow, lens, light, dim, spot })`: see its comment. `dyn` and `over` clear after each shade.\\n- `slow: true` is Long Breath's palette. `lens` is Sonar's. `dim` and `spot` are the blackout. A moving `light` is the swaying lamp.\\n\\n```js\\n// Bake once per site, stamp from the sim's state each frame, then shade(). Cosmetic only: the sim never reads it. Needs E = My3D2dge in scope.\\n1100→const BAYER = [0,8,2,10,12,4,14,6,3,11,1,9,15,7,13,5].map(v => (v + .5) / 16 - .5);\\nconst hex3 = h => [parseInt(h.slice(1, 3), 16), parseInt(h.slice(3, 5), 16), parseInt(h.slice(5, 7), 16)];\\nfunction makeRamp(keys, n, slow) { // dark to light through hand-picked, hue-shifted keys. slow: the same ramp seen through Long Breath\\n  const c = keys.map(hex3), out = new Uint32Array(n);\\n  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) { const t = i / (n - 1) * (c.length - 1), j = Math.min(c.length - 2, t | 0), f = t - j; let r = c[j][0] + (c[j + 1][0] - c[j][0]) * f, g = c[j][1] + (c[j + 1][1] - c[j][1]) * f, b = c[j][2] + (c[j + 1][2] - c[j][2]) * f;\\n    if (slow) { const y = r * .3 + g * .59 + b * .11; r = y * .55 + 8; g = y * .95 + 14; b = y * 1.05 + 22; } out[i] = 0xff000000 | (Math.min(255, b) << 16) | (Math.min(255, g) << 8) | Math.min(255, r); }\\n  return out;\\n}\\nconst MAT_KEYS = { // name: [ramp keys, specular strength]\\n  wall: [['#1c060c', '#4a0f18', '#7d1f26', '#a93a38'], .35], bowel: [['#3a0e1c', '#8a2f3e', '#c75f62', '#eb9486', '#ffc9b0'], 1], liver: [['#1e0610', '#4e1022', '#7c1e30', '#a83a42', '#d0645c'], .55],\\n1110→  fat: [['#5a3a10', '#a8782a', '#dcb34c', '#f4d97e', '#fff2b8'], .7], vessel: [['#14103a', '#2c2a70', '#4a4aa0', '#7c78c8'], .8], artery: [['#3a0408', '#8c0f14', '#cf2a22', '#f4604a'], 1],\\n  skin: [['#6a3a22', '#a8683f', '#d39a68', '#eebf92'], .12], drape: [['#0c2c2a', '#14504a', '#1f7468', '#2f9a88'], 0], steel: [['#28303a', '#5a6a78', '#9aaab8', '#e8f0f8'], 1.6],\\n  blood: [['#180204', '#3c060a', '#680c10', '#8e1414'], 1.4], wound: [['#0c0104', '#2a0408', '#55090e', '#7a1216'], .5], char: [['#08070a', '#1c181a', '#332a28', '#4a3c34'], 0], gel: [['#0c4a38', '#1c8a64', '#54d0a0', '#b0ffe0'], 1.4] };\\nconst MAT_IDS = Object.keys(MAT_KEYS), MATS = MAT_IDS.map(k => ({ ramp: makeRamp(MAT_KEYS[k][0], 20), slow: makeRamp(MAT_KEYS[k][0], 20, true), spec: MAT_KEYS[k][1] }));\\nconst matId = n => MAT_IDS.indexOf(n);\\nfunction makeNoise(seed) { let a = seed >>> 0; const R = () => { a = (a + 0x6d2b79f5) >>> 0; let t = Math.imul(a ^ (a >>> 15), a | 1); t ^= t + Math.imul(t ^ (t >>> 7), t | 61); return ((t ^ (t >>> 14)) >>> 0) / 4294967296; };\\n  const L = new Float32Array(65536); for (let i = 0; i < L.length; i++) L[i] = R();\\n  const v = (x, y) => { const xi = Math.floor(x), yi = Math.floor(y), fx = x - xi, fy = y - yi, u = fx * fx * (3 - 2 * fx), w = fy * fy * (3 - 2 * fy), s = (p, q) => L[(p & 255) | ((q & 255) << 8)]; return (s(xi, yi) * (1 - u) + s(xi + 1, yi) * u) * (1 - w) + (s(xi, yi + 1) * (1 - u) + s(xi + 1, yi + 1) * u) * w; };\\n  return { R, fbm: (x, y, o = 4) => { let k = .5, f = 1, n = 0; for (let i = 0; i < o; i++) { n += k * v(x * f, y * f); k *= .5; f *= 2.03; } return n; } }; }\\nclass TissueField {\\n1120→  constructor(W, H, seed = 7) { this.W = W; this.H = H; const N = W * H; this.h = new Float32Array(N); this.m = new Uint8Array(N); this.pulse = new Float32Array(N); this.ao = new Float32Array(N).fill(1); this.dyn = new Float32Array(N); this.over = new Uint8Array(N); this.noise = makeNoise(seed);\\n    this.cv = E.mkCanvas(W, H); this.g = this.cv.getContext('2d'); this.img = this.g.createImageData(W, H); this.u = new Uint32Array(this.img.data.buffer); }\\n  blob(x, y, rx, ry, rot, hgt, mat, o = {}) { const { W, H, h, m, pulse, noise } = this, c = Math.cos(rot), s = Math.sin(rot), R = Math.max(rx, ry) + (o.warp || 0) + 2, id = matId(mat), warp = o.warp || 0, base = o.base || 0, add = !!o.add;\\n    for (let py = Math.max(0, y - R | 0); py < Math.min(H, y + R + 1); py++) for (let px = Math.max(0, x - R | 0); px < Math.min(W, x + R + 1); px++) {\\n      const wx = warp ? (noise.fbm(px * .03, py * .03, 2) - .5) * warp : 0, wy = warp ? (noise.fbm(px * .03 + 40, py * .03, 2) - .5) * warp : 0, dx = px - x + wx, dy = py - y + wy, u = (dx * c + dy * s) / rx, v = (-dx * s + dy * c) / ry, d = u * u + v * v; if (d >= 1) continue;\\n      const i = py * W + px, top = base + hgt * Math.sqrt(1 - d); if (add) { h[i] += hgt * (1 - d); m[i] = id; if (o.pulse) pulse[i] = Math.max(pulse[i], o.pulse * (1 - d)); } else if (top > h[i]) { h[i] = top; m[i] = id; if (o.pulse) pulse[i] = o.pulse * (1 - d); } } }\\n  tube(pts, r, hgt, mat, o = {}) { const step = Math.max(1, r * .35); for (let k = 0; k < pts.length - 1; k++) { const [x0, y0] = pts[k], [x1, y1] = pts[k + 1], n = Math.max(1, Math.hypot(x1 - x0, y1 - y0) / step | 0); for (let i = 0; i <= n; i++) this.blob(x0 + (x1 - x0) * i / n, y0 + (y1 - y0) * i / n, r, r, 0, hgt, mat, o); } }\\n  finish(detail = 1.1) { const { W, H, h, ao, noise } = this, N = W * H, blur = new Float32Array(N), tmp = new Float32Array(N), R = 7;\\n    for (let i = 0, y = 0; y < H; y++) for (let x = 0; x < W; x++, i++) h[i] += (noise.fbm(x * .11, y * .11, 3) - .5) * detail;\\n    for (let y = 0; y < H; y++) { let s = 0; for (let x = -R; x <= R; x++) s += h[y * W + Math.max(0, x)]; for (let x = 0; x < W; x++) { tmp[y * W + x] = s / (2 * R + 1); s += h[y * W + Math.min(W - 1, x + R + 1)] - h[y * W + Math.max(0, x - R)]; } }\\n1130→    for (let x = 0; x < W; x++) { let s = 0; for (let y = -R; y <= R; y++) s += tmp[Math.max(0, y) * W + x]; for (let y = 0; y < H; y++) { blur[y * W + x] = s / (2 * R + 1); s += tmp[Math.min(H - 1, y + R + 1) * W + x] - tmp[Math.max(0, y - R) * W + x]; } }\\n    for (let i = 0; i < N; i++) ao[i] = Math.max(.42, Math.min(1.08, 1 - (blur[i] - h[i]) * .055)); }\\n  // what the sim's objects stamp each frame, before shade(): dynamic height, and an override material\\n  stampDisc(x, y, r, hgt, mat) { const { W, H, dyn, over } = this, id = mat ? matId(mat) + 1 : 0; for (let py = Math.max(1, y - r | 0); py < Math.min(H - 1, y + r + 1); py++) for (let px = Math.max(1, x - r | 0); px < Math.min(W - 1, x + r + 1); px++) { const d = ((px - x) ** 2 + (py - y) ** 2) / (r * r); if (d < 1) { const i = py * W + px; dyn[i] = hgt < 0 ? Math.min(dyn[i], hgt * (1 - d)) : Math.max(dyn[i], hgt * (1 - d)); if (id) over[i] = id; } } }\\n  stampLine(pts, r, hgt, mat) { for (let k = 0; k < pts.length - 1; k++) { const [x0, y0] = pts[k], [x1, y1] = pts[k + 1], n = Math.max(1, Math.hypot(x1 - x0, y1 - y0) / Math.max(1, r * .5) | 0); for (let i = 0; i <= n; i++) this.stampDisc(x0 + (x1 - x0) * i / n, y0 + (y1 - y0) * i / n, r, hgt, mat); } }\\n  // fx: { beat 0..1, breath px, slow: Long Breath palette, lens: {x, y, r} shows the cold palette inside (Sonar), light: [lx, ly], dim: 0..1 darkness, spot: {x, y, r} stays lit while dim }\\n  shade(fx = {}) { const { W, H, h, m, ao, pulse, dyn, over, u } = this, beat = fx.beat || 0, off = Math.round(fx.breath || 0) * W, slow = !!fx.slow, lens = fx.lens, lr2 = lens ? lens.r * lens.r : 0, dim = fx.dim || 0, spot = fx.spot, sr2 = spot ? spot.r * spot.r : 0, lx = fx.light ? fx.light[0] : -.42, ly = fx.light ? fx.light[1] : -.52, lz = Math.sqrt(Math.max(.1, 1 - lx * lx - ly * ly)), hl = Math.hypot(lx, ly, lz + 1), hx = lx / hl, hy = ly / hl, hz = (lz + 1) / hl;\\n    for (let y = 1; y < H - 1; y++) for (let x = 1, i = y * W + 1; x < W - 1; x++, i++) {\\n      const j = Math.max(W + 1, Math.min(W * H - W - 2, i + off)), k = 1 + beat * pulse[j], dx = (h[j - 1] - h[j + 1]) * k * .5 + (dyn[i - 1] - dyn[i + 1]) * .5, dy = (h[j - W] - h[j + W]) * k * .5 + (dyn[i - W] - dyn[i + W]) * .5, nl = 1 / Math.sqrt(dx * dx + dy * dy + 1), nx = dx * nl, ny = dy * nl, nz = nl;\\n      const diff = Math.max(0, nx * lx + ny * ly + nz * lz), sh = Math.max(0, nx * hx + ny * hy + nz * hz), ov = over[i], M = MATS[ov ? ov - 1 : m[j]], cold = slow || (lens && (x - lens.x) ** 2 + (y - lens.y) ** 2 < lr2), r = cold ? M.slow : M.ramp, n = r.length, s2 = sh * sh, s4 = s2 * s2, s8 = s4 * s4, s16 = s8 * s8, s32 = s16 * s16;\\n1140→      const lit = dim && !(spot && (x - spot.x) ** 2 + (y - spot.y) ** 2 < sr2) ? 1 - dim : 1, lum = (diff * .92 + s8 * .16 * M.spec + beat * pulse[j] * .6) * (ov ? 1 : ao[j]) * lit, c = Math.max(0, Math.min(n - 1, (lum * (n - 1) + BAYER[(x & 3) | ((y & 3) << 2)] * 1.15 + .5) | 0));\\n      u[i] = s32 * s8 * M.spec * lit > .42 ? (cold ? 0xffffffe0 : 0xfff4f0ff) : r[c]; }\\n    this.g.putImageData(this.img, 0, 0); dyn.fill(0); over.fill(0); }\\n}\\n```\\n\\n## Appendix B: a site recipe\\n\\n`F.stampDisc` coordinates here use screen pixels. To place sim objects, add `OX, OY`.\\n\\n1150→```js\\n// A site recipe: the abdomen. Design space is 426 x 240 about the screen's centre; a bigger screen only shows more drape.\\nfunction bakeAbdomen(W, H, seed = 11) { const F = new TissueField(W, H, seed), cx = W / 2, cy = H / 2 - 4, n = F.noise, rx = 171, ry = 91;\\n  for (let y = 0; y < H; y++) for (let x = 0; x < W; x++) { const i = y * W + x, d = ((x - cx) / rx) ** 2 + ((y - cy) / ry) ** 2, fold = Math.sin(x * .09 + 1.6 * Math.sin(y * .05)) * 1.6 + Math.sin(y * .17 + x * .03) * .8;\\n    if (d < 1) { F.h[i] = -16 + 12 * d * d + Math.sin(x * .05 + y * .03) * 1.4; F.m[i] = matId('wall'); }            // the cavity\\n    else if (d < 1.28) { const e = (d - 1) / .28; F.h[i] = 4 + 7 * Math.sin(e * Math.PI); F.m[i] = matId('skin'); }  // lips of skin\\n    else { F.h[i] = 6 + fold; F.m[i] = matId('drape'); } }                                                            // drapes to any screen's edge\\n  F.blob(cx - rx * .55, cy - ry * .5, rx * .55, ry * .55, -.3, 17, 'liver', { base: -11, warp: 12 });\\n  for (let i = 0; i < 9; i++) { const a = n.R() * 6.28, r = .55 + n.R() * .32; F.blob(cx + Math.cos(a) * rx * r, cy + Math.sin(a) * ry * r, 9 + n.R() * 14, 7 + n.R() * 9, n.R() * 3, 8, 'fat', { base: -10, warp: 8 }); }\\n  const coil = (x0, y0, len, dir, r) => { const pts = [[x0, y0]]; let a = dir, x = x0, y = y0; for (let i = 0; i < len; i++) { a += (n.R() - .5) * 1.5 + Math.sin(i * .9) * .5; x += Math.cos(a) * r * 1.3; y += Math.sin(a) * r * 1.3; if (((x - cx) / (rx * .92)) ** 2 + ((y - cy) / (ry * .92)) ** 2 > 1) { a += 2.4; x += Math.cos(a) * r; y += Math.sin(a) * r; } pts.push([x, y]); } return pts; };\\n1160→  F.tube(coil(cx + rx * .05, cy - ry * .25, 34, .2, 9), 11, 14, 'bowel', { base: -8 }); F.tube(coil(cx + rx * .45, cy + ry * .25, 26, 2.6, 9), 10, 15, 'bowel', { base: -7 }); F.tube(coil(cx - rx * .35, cy + ry * .45, 22, -.3, 9), 10, 15, 'bowel', { base: -6 });\\n  const veins = (x, y, a, len, r, mat) => { const pts = [[x, y]]; for (let i = 0; i < len; i++) { a += (n.R() - .5) * .8; x += Math.cos(a) * 7; y += Math.sin(a) * 7; if (((x - cx) / rx) ** 2 + ((y - cy) / ry) ** 2 > .96) break; pts.push([x, y]); if (r > 1.2 && n.R() < .22) veins(x, y, a + (n.R() < .5 ? .9 : -.9), len * .5 | 0, r * .7, mat); } F.tube(pts, r, 1.6, mat, { add: true, pulse: mat === 'artery' ? .35 : 0 }); };\\n  veins(cx - rx * .7, cy - ry * .05, -.2, 16, 2.2, 'vessel'); veins(cx + rx * .7, cy - ry * .6, 2.4, 14, 2, 'artery'); veins(cx + rx * .1, cy + ry * .75, -1.5, 12, 1.8, 'vessel');\\n  for (const a of [-.45, .5, 2.7, 3.6]) { const ex = cx + Math.cos(a) * rx * 1.02, ey = cy + Math.sin(a) * ry * 1.02, ox = cx + Math.cos(a) * rx * 1.5, oy = cy + Math.sin(a) * ry * 1.5; F.tube([[ex, ey], [ox, oy]], 4, 5, 'steel', { base: 10 }); F.tube([[ex - Math.sin(a) * 9, ey + Math.cos(a) * 9], [ex + Math.sin(a) * 9, ey - Math.cos(a) * 9]], 3, 5, 'steel', { base: 10 }); } // retractors\\n  F.finish(1.1); return F; }\\n```\\n\\n## Appendix C: portrait painter\\n\\nBusts for the four leads. The other four follow the same shape: `frontHair`, `backHair` (polygons in face space, x from the center, y down), `glasses`, `moustache`, `scar`, `cap`, `mask` (`maskUp: true` covers the mouth, for theatre), `lines`, `blush`, `jaw`, `wide`. Cache each expression to a canvas with `E.mkCanvas(112, 128)`; call `E.px.reset` on the target after drawing on it with the canvas API.\\n1170→\\n```js\\n// A bust from data in hard-edged tones, 112 x 128 buffer pixels. Cache each expression in a canvas; the sim never reads it. Needs E = My3D2dge in scope.\\n// o: { expr: neutral | smile | angry | sad | shock | shout, blink: 0..1, talk: 0..1, look: -1..1 }\\nfunction drawBust(g, c, o = {}) { const px = E.px, P = (pts, col) => px.poly(g, pts, col), T = E.tones;\\n  const skin = T(c.skin), hair = T(c.hair), cloth = T(c.cloth), expr = o.expr || 'neutral', blink = o.blink || 0, talk = o.talk || 0, cx = 56, top = 20;\\n  const jaw = c.jaw || 1, wide = c.wide || 1, f = (x, y) => [cx + x * wide, top + y]; // face space: x from centre, y down\\n  // back hair\\n  if (c.backHair) for (const s of c.backHair) P(s.map(([x, y]) => f(x, y)), hair.sh);\\n  // shoulders and collar\\n1180→  P([[6, 128], [10, 104], [30, 92], [46, 88], [66, 88], [82, 92], [102, 104], [106, 128]], cloth.base);\\n  P([[6, 128], [10, 104], [30, 92], [34, 128]], cloth.sh); P([[78, 128], [82, 92], [102, 104], [106, 128]], cloth.lt);\\n  // neck\\n  P([[46, 66], [66, 66], [68, 92], [56, 104], [44, 92]], skin.sh);\\n  // V-neck and inner shirt\\n  P([[42, 88], [56, 110], [70, 88], [66, 86], [56, 100], [46, 86]], cloth.deep); P([[46, 86], [56, 100], [66, 86], [64, 92], [56, 104], [48, 92]], c.undershirt || skin.sh);\\n  if (c.coat) { P([[6, 128], [10, 104], [30, 92], [42, 88], [52, 128]], c.coat); P([[106, 128], [102, 104], [82, 92], [70, 88], [60, 128]], c.coat); P([[42, 88], [52, 128], [47, 128], [36, 92]], E.tones(c.coat).sh); P([[70, 88], [60, 128], [65, 128], [76, 92]], E.tones(c.coat).sh); }\\n  // ears\\n  P([f(-27, 30), f(-31, 34), f(-30, 42), f(-26, 45)], skin.sh); P([f(27, 30), f(31, 34), f(30, 42), f(26, 45)], skin.base);\\n  // head: wide cranium, cheek, chin\\n1190→  const J = jaw; P([f(-26, 12), f(-27, 34), f(-22, 48 * J), f(-11, 60 * J), f(0, 64 * J), f(11, 60 * J), f(22, 48 * J), f(27, 34), f(26, 12), f(14, 2), f(-14, 2)], skin.base);\\n  P([f(-27, 30), f(-22, 48 * J), f(-11, 60 * J), f(0, 64 * J), f(-6, 54 * J), f(-16, 44), f(-22, 30)], skin.sh); // cheek shadow, viewer's left\\n  if (c.blush) { px.ell(g, ...f(-15, 41), 4, 2, c.blush); px.ell(g, ...f(16, 41), 4, 2, c.blush); }\\n  if (c.lines) { px.line(g, ...f(-11, 45), ...f(-6, 50), skin.sh, 1); px.line(g, ...f(11, 45), ...f(6, 50), skin.sh, 1); px.line(g, ...f(-23, 26), ...f(-20, 28), skin.sh, 1); px.line(g, ...f(23, 26), ...f(20, 28), skin.sh, 1); }\\n  // eyes\\n  const eye = (sx) => { const x = sx * 13, y = 30 + (expr === 'shock' ? -1 : 0), w = 7, hh = expr === 'shock' ? 8 : expr === 'angry' ? 5 : 7;\\n    if (blink > .5 || expr === 'sad-closed') { px.line(g, ...f(x - w, y + 1), ...f(x + w, y + 1), c.lash || '#1a1016', 2); return; }\\n    P([f(x - w, y), f(x - w + 3, y - hh + 2), f(x + w - 3, y - hh + 2), f(x + w, y), f(x + w - 2, y + 4), f(x - w + 2, y + 4)], '#f8f4ee');\\n    const ix = x + (o.look || 0) * 2 + (expr === 'shock' ? 0 : 0); px.ell(g, ...f(ix, y - 1), 4, expr === 'shock' ? 4 : 5, c.iris || '#3a5a7a'); px.ell(g, ...f(ix, y), 2, 3, c.pupil || '#120c14'); px.rect(g, ...f(ix - 3, y - 4), 3, 2, '#ffffff'); px.dot(g, ...f(ix + 2, y + 2), '#ffffff');\\n    px.line(g, ...f(x - w - 1, y + (sx < 0 ? 1 : -1)), ...f(x - w + 4, y - hh + 2), c.lash || '#1a1016', 2); px.line(g, ...f(x - w + 4, y - hh + 2), ...f(x + w - 4, y - hh + 2), c.lash || '#1a1016', 2); px.line(g, ...f(x + w - 4, y - hh + 2), ...f(x + w + 1, y + (sx < 0 ? -1 : 1)), c.lash || '#1a1016', 2); };\\n1200→  eye(-1); eye(1);\\n  // brows\\n  const by = 17 + (c.browDrop || 0), br = expr === 'angry' ? 3 : expr === 'sad' ? -3 : expr === 'shock' ? -1 : 0, bc = c.brow || hair.deep, bw = c.browW || 2; px.line(g, ...f(-20, by + (expr === 'shock' ? -3 : 0) - br), ...f(-7, by + (expr === 'shock' ? -3 : 0) + br), bc, bw); px.line(g, ...f(7, by + (expr === 'shock' ? -3 : 0) + br), ...f(20, by + (expr === 'shock' ? -3 : 0) - br), bc, bw);\\n  // nose\\n  px.line(g, ...f(1, 40), ...f(-1, 45), skin.sh, 1); px.dot(g, ...f(0, 46), skin.deep);\\n  // mouth\\n  const my = 53, mo = talk > .5 || expr === 'shout' || expr === 'shock'; if (mo) { const a = expr === 'shout' || expr === 'shock' ? 7 : 4, w = expr === 'shout' ? 7 : 5; P([f(-w, my - 1), f(w, my - 1), f(w - 2, my + a), f(-w + 2, my + a)], '#5a1a24'); P([f(-w + 2, my + a - 2), f(w - 2, my + a - 2), f(w - 3, my + a), f(-w + 3, my + a)], '#c8505a'); if (a > 4) px.rect(g, ...f(-w + 1, my - 1), (w - 1) * 2, 2, '#f4f0e8'); }\\n  else if (expr === 'smile') { px.line(g, ...f(-7, my - 1), ...f(-3, my + 2), skin.deep, 1); px.line(g, ...f(-3, my + 2), ...f(3, my + 2), skin.deep, 1); px.line(g, ...f(3, my + 2), ...f(7, my - 1), skin.deep, 1); }\\n  else if (expr === 'sad' || expr === 'angry') { px.line(g, ...f(-5, my + 2), ...f(0, my), skin.deep, 1); px.line(g, ...f(0, my), ...f(5, my + 2), skin.deep, 1); }\\n  else px.line(g, ...f(-4, my), ...f(4, my), skin.deep, 1);\\n1210→  if (c.moustache) { P([f(-13, 50), f(-6, 46), f(0, 48), f(6, 46), f(13, 50), f(8, 52), f(0, 50), f(-8, 52)], c.moustache); px.line(g, ...f(-6, 47), ...f(6, 47), E.tones(c.moustache).lt, 1); }\\n  // front hair\\n  if (c.frontHair) for (const [s, tone] of c.frontHair) P(s.map(([x, y]) => f(x, y)), hair[tone || 'base']);\\n  if (c.hairGlint) for (const s of c.hairGlint) P(s.map(([x, y]) => f(x, y)), hair.hi);\\n  // cap\\n  if (c.cap) { const k = T(c.cap); P([f(-29, 16), f(-27, 2), f(-14, -9), f(14, -9), f(27, 2), f(29, 16), f(16, 11), f(-16, 11)], k.base); P([f(-29, 16), f(-27, 2), f(-14, -9), f(-6, -9), f(-14, 11), f(-16, 11)], k.sh); P([f(6, -8), f(14, -9), f(22, -3), f(14, -3)], k.lt); P([f(-29, 16), f(-16, 11), f(16, 11), f(29, 16), f(27, 18), f(16, 14), f(-16, 14), f(-27, 18)], k.deep); }\\n  // lowered mask, glasses\\n  if (c.mask && !o.maskUp) { const k = T(c.mask); P([f(-20, 62), f(20, 62), f(25, 76), f(0, 84), f(-25, 76)], k.base); P([f(-20, 62), f(-25, 76), f(0, 84), f(-6, 74)], k.sh); px.line(g, ...f(-20, 62), ...f(-28, 50), k.lt, 1); px.line(g, ...f(20, 62), ...f(28, 50), k.lt, 1); }\\n  if (c.glasses) { const m = c.glasses; for (const sx of [-1, 1]) { const x = sx * 13; px.line(g, ...f(x - 9, 25), ...f(x + 9, 25), m, 1); px.line(g, ...f(x - 9, 25), ...f(x - 8, 33), m, 1); px.line(g, ...f(x + 9, 25), ...f(x + 8, 33), m, 1); px.line(g, ...f(x - 8, 34), ...f(x + 8, 34), m, 1); } px.line(g, ...f(-4, 27), ...f(4, 27), m, 1); }\\n  if (o.maskUp && c.mask) { const k = T(c.mask); P([f(-27, 36), f(-8, 34), f(0, 36), f(8, 34), f(27, 36), f(25, 54), f(11, 66), f(0, 68), f(-11, 66), f(-25, 54)], k.base); P([f(-27, 36), f(-8, 34), f(-3, 50), f(-11, 66), f(-25, 54)], k.sh); for (const yy of [44, 52]) px.line(g, ...f(-18, yy), ...f(18, yy), k.lt, 1); px.line(g, ...f(-27, 38), ...f(-31, 32), k.lt, 1); px.line(g, ...f(27, 38), ...f(31, 32), k.lt, 1); }\\n1220→  if (c.scar) px.line(g, ...f(c.scar[0], c.scar[1]), ...f(c.scar[2], c.scar[3]), skin.deep, 1);\\n  if (c.tag) { px.rect(g, 72, 108, 16, 10, '#f0ece2'); px.rect(g, 74, 110, 12, 2, c.tag); px.rect(g, 74, 114, 8, 1, '#7a7468'); }\\n}\\nconst CAST = {\\n  vale: { name: 'DR. VALE', skin: '#f6d0b2', hair: '#c9562a', iris: '#3f8a58', cloth: '#2f9c8e', cap: '#2bb0a2', mask: '#bfe6dc', undershirt: '#e9fff6', blush: '#f0a48c', wide: .95,\\n    backHair: [[[-27, 14], [-30, 40], [-24, 52], [-16, 40], [-16, 14]], [[27, 14], [30, 40], [24, 52], [16, 40], [16, 14]]],\\n    frontHair: [[[[-27, 12], [-24, 24], [-16, 16], [-10, 22], [-3, 14], [3, 21], [10, 14], [18, 22], [27, 12], [14, 8], [-14, 8]], 'base'], [[[-27, 12], [-24, 24], [-16, 16], [-14, 10]], 'sh']],\\n    tag: '#c8452f' },\\n  quill: { name: 'NURSE QUILL', skin: '#dcae8e', hair: '#8d8a88', iris: '#4a4a55', cloth: '#3d62a6', cap: '#35558f', undershirt: '#dfe6f2', moustache: '#a7a3a0', lines: true, wide: 1.08, jaw: 1.04, browW: 3, brow: '#7c7875', browDrop: 2, mask: '#a9cfe0', frontHair: [] },\\n  oyelaran: { name: 'CHIEF OYELARAN', skin: '#7c4a2e', hair: '#c9c5bb', iris: '#2a1a12', cloth: '#2e2c36', coat: '#ece7da', undershirt: '#3a3644', lines: true, jaw: .98, glasses: '#d8c070',\\n1230→    backHair: [], frontHair: [[[[-27, 18], [-27, 2], [-14, -8], [14, -8], [27, 2], [27, 18], [20, 10], [8, 6], [-8, 6], [-20, 10]], 'base'], [[[-27, 18], [-27, 2], [-14, -8], [-8, 6], [-20, 10]], 'sh'], [[[6, -7], [16, -6], [22, 0], [12, 0]], 'lt']] },\\n  sokolova: { name: 'DR. SOKOLOVA', skin: '#e8c6aa', hair: '#dedad2', iris: '#6a8aa8', cloth: '#6b7a48', undershirt: '#f0ece0', lines: true, wide: 1.02, jaw: 1.02, scar: [10, 14, 17, 40], browW: 3, brow: '#b8b4ac',\\n    backHair: [[[-28, 6], [-34, 34], [-30, 58], [-20, 50], [-20, 10]], [[28, 6], [36, 26], [46, 52], [38, 64], [28, 44], [22, 10]]], frontHair: [[[[-27, 16], [-26, 2], [-12, -8], [14, -8], [27, 2], [27, 16], [16, 6], [-2, 12], [-16, 8]], 'base'], [[[-27, 16], [-26, 2], [-12, -8], [-6, 8], [-16, 8]], 'sh']] },\\n};\\n```\\n\\n## Appendix D: Hub skeleton, page head and harness runner\\n\\n**Page head.** Order matters.\\n\\n1240→```html\\n<!doctype html><html lang=\\\"en\\\"><head><meta charset=\\\"utf-8\\\">\\n<script>/* vendor/hub/session.js, pasted unmodified */</script>\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session\\\" content=\\\"1\\\"><meta name=\\\"hub:session-players\\\" content=\\\"1-4\\\"><meta name=\\\"hub:session-mode\\\" content=\\\"shared-world\\\"><meta name=\\\"hub:session-minutes\\\" content=\\\"5\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-goal\\\" content=\\\"Save the patient together; best hands top the board\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-howto\\\" content=\\\"Aim: right stick or mouse. Use tool: R2 or click. Tools: left stick or 1-8\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"viewport\\\" content=\\\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1, viewport-fit=cover\\\"><title>…</title>\\n<style>html,body{margin:0;height:100%;background:#000;overflow:hidden}#screen{width:100%;height:100%;display:block;image-rendering:pixelated;touch-action:none}html .esh-row small{display:none}html .esh-row:focus-within small{display:block}</style></head>\\n<body><!-- E.bootSplash's markup here --><canvas id=\\\"screen\\\"></canvas><script>/* engine */</script><script>/* game */</script></body></html>\\n```\\n1250→\\n**Skeleton.** A complete shared-world game on the engine. It passed all four harness runs and runs standalone. Replace the dots with the real sim, keeping its structure: connect, the event handlers, the host's step, the 12/s snapshot and the mirror. `HUB.send` for a real event batch is reliable by default.\\n\\n```js\\n// Hub skeleton: shared world on my-3D2dge. The host's copy runs the world; other copies mirror it and send their own hands.\\n(() => {\\nconst E = My3D2dge, HUB = window.AIHub, inSession = !!(HUB && HUB.active), { px } = E;\\nconst { attack, dash, skill, jump, ...base } = E.Input.DEFAULT;\\nconst game = window.G = new E.Game({ canvas: 'screen', res: [426, 240], expand: true, view: 'overhead', touch: 'never', live: inSession, autoPause: !inSession, corner: !inSession,\\n  input: { ...base, useTool: ['Pad7', 'Pad0', 'Mouse0', 'Space'], more: ['Pad3', 'KeyM'] }, menu: { quit: inSession ? false : 'title', settings: [{ key: 'music', value: .1 }, { key: 'glyphs', value: 'playstation', options: [['playstation', 'PlayStation'], ['xbox', 'Xbox']] }] } });\\n1260→const NAME = 'SKELETON', W0 = 426, H0 = 240, DOTS = 12;\\nlet info = null, phase = 'wait', card = null, over = false, sent = 0, heardSeq = 0, S = { v: 70, dots: [], p: {} }, mine = { x: 200, y: 120, t: 0 }, rng = null;\\nconst place = () => ({ x: 40 + rng() * 340, y: 40 + rng() * 160 });\\nfunction hostInit() { rng = HUB.rng('deal'); S.dots = Array.from({ length: DOTS }, (_, id) => ({ id, ...place(), alive: 1 })); for (const q of info.players) S.p[q.id] = { x: 200, y: 120, t: 0, score: 0, gone: false }; }\\nfunction finish(won) { if (over) return; over = true; if (HUB.server !== true && info.me != null && S.p[info.me]) Object.assign(S.p[info.me], mine, { score: S.p[info.me].score });\\n  const live = info.players.filter(q => !S.p[q.id].gone).sort((a, b) => S.p[b.id].score - S.p[a.id].score), standings = [];\\n  for (const q of live) { const last = standings[standings.length - 1]; if (last && S.p[last[0]].score === S.p[q.id].score) last.push(q.id); else standings.push([q.id]); }\\n  const scores = Object.fromEntries(info.players.map(q => [q.id, S.p[q.id].score]));\\n  HUB.send({ t: 'over', v: S.v }); HUB.finish({ standings, scores, won, awards: live.length ? [{ id: live[0].id, title: 'Steadiest Hand', detail: scores[live[0].id] + ' clean actions' }] : [] }); }\\nasync function connect() { info = await HUB.connect({ mode: 'shared-world', seats: [1, 4], card: true });\\n1270→  if (!info) { phase = 'solo'; return; }\\n  HUB.on('card', c => { card = c; });\\n  HUB.on('start', () => { phase = 'count'; });\\n  HUB.on('message', (d, from) => { if (info.isHost) { const p = S.p[from]; if (d.t === 'i' && p && d.n > (p.n || 0)) { p.n = d.n; p.x = d.x; p.y = d.y; p.t = d.t2; } } else if (from === info.host) { if (d.t === 's' && d.n > heardSeq) { heardSeq = d.n; S.v = d.v; S.dots = d.o.map(([id, x, y]) => ({ id, x, y, alive: 1 })); for (const [id, x, y, t, sc] of d.p) { const q = S.p[id] || (S.p[id] = {}); if (id !== info.me) { q.x = x; q.y = y; q.t = t; } q.score = sc; } } if (d.t === 'over') over = true; } });\\n  HUB.on('leave', ({ id }) => { if (S.p[id]) S.p[id].gone = true; });\\n  HUB.on('time-up', () => { if (info.isHost) finish(false); });\\n  HUB.on('end', () => { phase = 'over'; game.audio.music(null); game.audio.mute(true); });\\n  if (info.isHost) hostInit();\\n  HUB.ready(); }\\nfunction step(dt) { const I = game.input, m = I.move(), a = I.padAim, ptr = I.pointer(); let dx = (a ? a[0] : 0) + m[0], dy = (a ? a[1] : 0) + m[1]; mine.x = E.clamp(mine.x + dx * 300 * dt, 0, W0); mine.y = E.clamp(mine.y + dy * 300 * dt, 0, H0); if (ptr && I.lastDevice === 'mouse') { mine.x = ptr[0]; mine.y = ptr[1]; } mine.t = I.value('useTool'); }\\n1280→let snapN = 0, inN = 0, acc = 0;\\nfunction hostStep(dt) { S.v = Math.max(0, S.v - .05 * dt); if (info.me != null && S.p[info.me] && !info.server) Object.assign(S.p[info.me], mine, { score: S.p[info.me].score });\\n  for (const id in S.p) { const p = S.p[id]; if (p.gone || !p.t) continue; for (const d of S.dots) if (d.alive && Math.hypot(d.x - p.x, d.y - p.y) < 12) { d.alive = 0; p.score++; } } S.dots = S.dots.filter(d => d.alive); if (!S.dots.length) finish(true); }\\ngame.start({ scene: 'hub', scenes: { hub: { pausable: true, camera: { zoom: false, wheel: false },\\n  update(dt) { const I = game.input; game.focus(game.W / 2, game.H / 2, 0);\\n    if (!info && !inSession) return;\\n    if (phase === 'wait' && info) { if (I.pressed('confirm')) HUB.answer('ready'); if (I.pressed('more')) HUB.answer('more'); const c = I.clicked(); if (c) HUB.answer(c[1] > game.H * .7 ? 'more' : 'ready'); }\\n    if (phase === 'count' && HUB.time() >= 0) phase = 'play';\\n    if (phase === 'play' && !over) { step(dt); if (info.isHost) hostStep(dt); acc += dt; if (acc >= 1 / 12) { acc = 0; if (info.isHost) HUB.send({ t: 's', n: ++snapN, v: Math.round(S.v), o: S.dots.map(d => [d.id, d.x | 0, d.y | 0]), p: info.players.filter(q => !S.p[q.id].gone).map(q => { const p = S.p[q.id]; return [q.id, p.x | 0, p.y | 0, p.t > .25 ? 1 : 0, p.score | 0]; }) }, { reliable: false }); else HUB.send({ t: 'i', n: ++inN, x: mine.x | 0, y: mine.y | 0, t2: mine.t > .25 ? 1 : 0 }, { to: info.host, reliable: false }); } } },\\n  draw(r) { if (info && info.server) return; r.sky(['#143a36', '#14504a']); r.overlay(g => { E.font.text(g, NAME + ' ' + phase + (card && card.left != null ? ' ' + Math.ceil(card.left / 1000) : ''), 8, 8, '#fff', { outline: false }); for (const d of S.dots) px.disc(g, d.x, d.y, 4, '#5ff0e0'); if (info) for (const q of info.players) { const p = q.id === info.me ? mine : S.p[q.id]; if (p && !(S.p[q.id] || {}).gone) { px.disc(g, p.x, p.y, 5, q.color); E.font.text(g, q.name.slice(0, 12), p.x + 7, p.y - 4, q.color, { outline: false }); } } }); } } } });\\n1290→window.__hubProbe = () => ({ phase, over, dots: S.dots.length, v: S.v, t: HUB.active ? HUB.time() : 0 });\\nconnect();\\n})();\\n```\\n\\n**Harness runner.** The folder needs the game (as `game.html`) and copies of `harness.html`, `bridge.js`, `round.js`. It uses an OS-picked port, checks the frame's title is your game (replace the print with a test), and stops only its own server, by handle.\\n\\n```python\\nimport json, os, re, subprocess, sys, time\\nfrom playwright.sync_api import sync_playwright\\n1300→folder = os.path.abspath(sys.argv[1])\\nsrv = subprocess.Popen([sys.executable, '-u', '-m', 'http.server', '0', '--bind', '127.0.0.1'], cwd=folder, stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.STDOUT, text=True)\\ntry:\\n    line = srv.stdout.readline(); port = int(re.search(r'port (\\\\d+)', line).group(1))\\n    base = f'http://127.0.0.1:{port}/'\\n    RUNS = ['players=2&auto=1', 'players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1', 'players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&scenario=host-leave&auto=1', 'players=2&scenario=server&auto=1']\\n    with sync_playwright() as p:\\n        b = p.chromium.launch(args=['--autoplay-policy=no-user-gesture-required'])\\n        for q in RUNS:\\n            pg = b.new_page(viewport={'width': 1280, 'height': 720}); pg.goto(base + 'harness.html?game=game.html&' + q)\\n1310→            try: pg.wait_for_function(\\\"() => document.body.dataset.complete === 'true'\\\", timeout=90000)\\n            except Exception as e: print('TIMEOUT', q)\\n            r = pg.evaluate(\\\"() => { const h = window.__hubCheck; return h ? { pass: h.pass, failed: h.checks.filter(c => !c.pass).map(c => c.name + ': ' + c.detail), n: h.checks.length } : null }\\\")\\n            title = pg.evaluate(\\\"() => { const f = document.querySelector('iframe'); try { return f && f.contentDocument && f.contentDocument.title } catch (e) { return 'x' } }\\\")\\n            print(q, json.dumps(r)[:700], 'title:', title); pg.close()\\n        pg = b.new_page(viewport={'width': 1280, 'height': 720}); errs = []; pg.on('pageerror', lambda e: errs.append(str(e))); pg.goto(base + 'game.html'); pg.wait_for_timeout(1200); print('standalone', pg.evaluate(\\\"() => ({ active: AIHub.active, probe: window.__hubProbe() })\\\"), errs[:3]); pg.close()\\n        b.close()\\nfinally:\\n    srv.terminate(); srv.wait(timeout=5)\\n```\\n1320→\\n## Appendix E: gamepad stub and touch-notice patch\\n\\n**A DualSense in the standard mapping, for Playwright's `add_init_script`.** Set `__pad.axes[0..3]` (left x, left y, right x, right y), `__pad.buttons[i].value` and `.pressed`: 0 ✕, 1 ○, 2 □, 3 △, 4 L1, 5 R1, 6 L2, 7 R2, 8 Create, 9 Options, 12 to 15 D-pad, and bump `__pad.timestamp`.\\n\\n```js\\n(() => { const axes = [0, 0, 0, 0], buttons = Array.from({ length: 18 }, () => ({ pressed: false, touched: false, value: 0 }));\\n  const pad = { id: 'DualSense Wireless Controller (STANDARD GAMEPAD Vendor: 054c Product: 0ce6)', index: 0, connected: true, mapping: 'standard', timestamp: 0, axes, buttons,\\n    vibrationActuator: { playEffect() { return Promise.resolve('complete'); }, reset() { return Promise.resolve('complete'); } } };\\n  window.__pad = pad; navigator.getGamepads = () => [pad, null, null, null]; })();\\n1330→```\\n\\n**Touch notice.** In your copy of the engine, `Game.prototype._needSync` sets the notice's `style.cssText` to a full-screen dark layer. Change that one string, so the reel shows above and the notice sits at the bottom. *Verified* on an iPhone profile: the notice sits in the lower half, the canvas above it is clear, and a tap on Settings opens the settings:\\n\\n```js\\nd.style.cssText = 'position:absolute;left:0;right:0;bottom:0;z-index:20;display:flex;align-items:flex-end;justify-content:center;padding:48px 24px 24px;text-align:center;pointer-events:none;background:linear-gradient(transparent,rgba(5,3,12,.92) 45%);color:#f4f0ff;font:600 17px/1.45 system-ui,sans-serif';\\n// and on the button, add pointer-events:auto to its inline style\\n```\\n\\nNote it in your Engine Feedback.\",\"content_concise\":\"1000→- The exit interview and `src/about-data.json` parse as JSON, and every field is filled.\\n\\n**Browser (Playwright, one or two browsers at a time, from `file://` where possible):** launch Chromium with `--autoplay-policy=no-user-gesture-required` for audio checks. Without it, the console's AudioContext warning before the first gesture is not a bug, so don't count warnings as failures.\\n- The built HTML plays 30 s with no errors in Chromium, Firefox and WebKit.\\n- `node tools/check.mjs` on the page (add `#scene` to jump to a scene): errors, what's on screen, look notes. Act on every look note.\\n- Settings defaults: music .1; glyphs PlayStation; the Xbox option switches labels.\\n- Pad with the Appendix E stub: `R2` and `△` prompts, stick cursor, wheel, trigger; keyboard and mouse switch the prompts.\\n- Phone profiles (Chromium, and WebKit with an iPhone profile, the closest thing here to iOS): notice and Settings button show, no touch buttons, attract runs behind, and a tap on Settings (`page.touchscreen.tap`) opens it.\\n- Audio: render through an `OfflineAudioContext` or read an `AnalyserNode`: effects, each voice and the choir are not silent; at defaults music is well below effects.\\n- Sizes: 1280x720, 1920x1080, 2560x1440, 3440x1440: HUD inside the screen, cavity centered.\\n1010→- **Hub harness, all four runs (Appendix D's runner):** `players=2&auto=1`; `players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1`; the same with `&scenario=host-leave`; `players=2&scenario=server&auto=1`. Pass means `window.__hubCheck.pass === true`. Then open the page outside a session.\\n- This computer has no second player, so use the harness's frames, or two browser pages or contexts, as the stand-in. With 2 players in the harness, whose game frames are same-origin and reachable from the test: feed a client's cursor through a stroke, and check the stroke lands on the host's state.\\n\\n**Screenshots, for what scripts can't judge:** each site, each stage, the portraits, one frame of each strain, the wheel, Long Breath. Use `tools/filmstrip.mjs` for motion. Judge at 1920x1080 for the 10-foot view. WebGPU isn't used, so screenshots work.\\n\\n**This computer** (full detail in TASK.md): no display, so `xvfb-run` if you ever need headed; Playwright 1.63 is ready (`npm install playwright@1.63.0` in the dev folder for Node); never run `playwright install`; for a Python library such as Pillow, use `uv` as TASK.md says; `Target closed` means memory ran out, not a bug: run fewer browsers. Other agents share this machine, so keep one or two browsers open and only read what you need, never the folders beside yours. Keep all files in your folder, not `/tmp`. Prefer `file://` and the engine's headless tools. Other agents share this machine's ports and processes: use a server only for the harness, bound to `127.0.0.1` on a port the OS picks (`python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` prints it), and confirm the page is your game before trusting a result. Stop only processes you started, by their PID: never `pkill`, `killall`, `fuser -k`, or kill whatever holds a port.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 21. Build order\\n1020→\\nEach milestone ends with a playable, tested game. Multiplayer comes early because it constrains the sim.\\n\\n| # | Milestone | Done when |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| M0 | Title picked. Folders, vendor, manifest, bundler, dev page, test runner, Hub client in the page | Empty game builds, runs, one test passes, harness runner works on Appendix D |\\n| M1 | **Hands:** sim core, tissue painter, cursor, wheel, Forceps, Salve, Suture, Syringe, vitals, clock, grades, HUD, Results, Case 1 end to end, bot | A person would enjoy Case 1 on a stubbed pad and mouse. Tune this until it's good; everything rests on it |\\n| M2 | **Shell:** title, menu, settings, glyphs, pause, TV scale, About skeleton, Dev Mode, attract v1, touch notice, saves, story system, busts, voices, first music, Case 1's story | First launch to Case 1 to Results feels finished, all TASK.md's cross-cutting features exist |\\n| M3 | Scalpel, Suction, Cautery, Sonar, Lightbox, Code Blue, Long Breath, Cases 2 and 3, Chapter 1 complete | Chapter 1 plays through, bot and all |\\n| M4 | **Grand Rounds:** local, then Hub, own card | All four harness runs pass, co-op works with bots |\\n1030→| M5 | Staccato, Legato, Tremolo, ward, Cases 4 to 6, Chapter 2 | Chapter 2 plays through |\\n| M6 | Second Look, tilt, blackout, Da Capo, device gestures, Cases 7 to 9, Chapter 3 | Chapter 3 plays through |\\n| M7 | Consult, Fermata, Cantor, Cases 10 and 11, ending, credits | The whole story plays through |\\n| M8 | **Polish:** feel table complete, cinematics, stages, title art, full score and mix, attract final reel, Skills Lab, balance runs, three browsers, CONTENT.md | Section 22 all true |\\n| M9 | About text, exit interview, final build, final test run, chat reply | Done |\\n\\n**If you run out of runway**, cut content from the end of this list and keep everything else: Case 9, then Skills Lab, then Da Capo's round 2, then the ship's ward (reuse Ward 3), then a stage or two. **Never cut:** the cursor's quality, any of the eight tools, both gifts, the finale, Grand Rounds and the Hub tests, attract mode, dev mode, the 10-foot UI, About, the exit interview. Whatever ships is complete: no stubs, no TODOs.\\n\\n---\\n\\n1040→## 22. Done checklist (TASK.md, line by line)\\n\\nGo through this at the end. Everything must be true.\\n\\n**Source, genre, perspective, title.**\\n- [ ] Original game from *Trauma Center: Second Opinion* per the originality rule; genre and perspective per section 2.\\n- [ ] Title is a playful riff, chosen by TASK.md's method with a real RNG, stored in one constant.\\n\\n**Package and environment.**\\n- [ ] One HTML file at the root as TASK.md names it, self-contained, working from `file://`.\\n1050→- [ ] Dev files in the subfolder TASK.md names. Nothing read from other agents' folders. Only your own processes stopped, by PID.\\n\\n**Tech, art, animation.**\\n- [ ] Built on the engine's handoff package; engine patches noted.\\n- [ ] Native pixel look as the base, new art for everything surgical, asset store only for generic props.\\n- [ ] Native animation as the base, new animations per section 17.\\n\\n**Audio.**\\n- [ ] All music, effects and voices in code, no audio files. Gibberish voices.\\n- [ ] Music at 10% by default (test proves it).\\n1060→\\n**Online.**\\n- [ ] Hub session protocol followed; all four harness runs pass; runs outside a session; couch co-op on its own.\\n\\n**Performance, testing.**\\n- [ ] No optimization work done for its own sake.\\n- [ ] One test command, printing only failures and a one-line count, passes.\\n- [ ] Scripted full playthrough passes.\\n\\n**Scope.**\\n1070→- [ ] Every mechanic in sections 5 to 9 is shown in play: a player can see what they can and cannot do.\\n- [ ] Data-driven content, `CONTENT.md`, Dev Jump.\\n- [ ] Dev mode from the pause menu, live sliders, reset to defaults.\\n- [ ] Attract after 10 s idle on title and menus, looping AI demo, any input exits.\\n- [ ] Tone: section 10.\\n\\n**UI and UX.**\\n- [ ] Controller-first on the same screens as keyboard and mouse, which feel native; hover moves focus; mouse aims and clicks things in the world.\\n- [ ] Prompts follow the last device. PlayStation glyphs, Settings option for Xbox, keys and mouse buttons for keyboard and mouse.\\n- [ ] No dense, web-style data screens.\\n1080→- [ ] Touch-only: no touch controls, \\\"Controller or keyboard required\\\" and the Settings button, attract runs.\\n\\n**Done and reporting.**\\n- [ ] No TODOs, placeholders, stubs.\\n- [ ] Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback, Prompt Feedback in the in-game About/Credits **and** in your final chat reply, \\\"unknown\\\" where you can't confirm, Usage as `added on import`.\\n- [ ] The exit interview saved beside the HTML file, in TASK.md's exact shape, parsing as JSON, every value filled.\\n- [ ] Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback answer each TASK.md question in order, from `notes/feedback.md`, honestly.\\n\\n---\\n\\n1090→## Appendix A: tissue painter\\n\\nRan on this computer at 480x270, 3 to 5 ms a frame under software rendering. It also runs headless. One instance per site. Each frame: stamp pools, wounds and so on from `S`, then `shade(fx)`, then draw `F.cv`.\\n\\n- `stampDisc(x, y, r, height, mat)`, `stampLine(points, r, height, mat)`: per-frame height and material. Height alone (no `mat`) makes a ridge, for Legato.\\n- `shade({ beat, breath, slow, lens, light, dim, spot })`: see its comment. `dyn` and `over` clear after each shade.\\n- `slow: true` is Long Breath's palette. `lens` is Sonar's. `dim` and `spot` are the blackout. A moving `light` is the swaying lamp.\\n\\n```js\\n// Bake once per site, stamp from the sim's state each frame, then shade(). Cosmetic only: the sim never reads it. Needs E = My3D2dge in scope.\\n1100→const BAYER = [0,8,2,10,12,4,14,6,3,11,1,9,15,7,13,5].map(v => (v + .5) / 16 - .5);\\nconst hex3 = h => [parseInt(h.slice(1, 3), 16), parseInt(h.slice(3, 5), 16), parseInt(h.slice(5, 7), 16)];\\nfunction makeRamp(keys, n, slow) { // dark to light through hand-picked, hue-shifted keys. slow: the same ramp seen through Long Breath\\n  const c = keys.map(hex3), out = new Uint32Array(n);\\n  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) { const t = i / (n - 1) * (c.length - 1), j = Math.min(c.length - 2, t | 0), f = t - j; let r = c[j][0] + (c[j + 1][0] - c[j][0]) * f, g = c[j][1] + (c[j + 1][1] - c[j][1]) * f, b = c[j][2] + (c[j + 1][2] - c[j][2]) * f;\\n    if (slow) { const y = r * .3 + g * .59 + b * .11; r = y * .55 + 8; g = y * .95 + 14; b = y * 1.05 + 22; } out[i] = 0xff000000 | (Math.min(255, b) << 16) | (Math.min(255, g) << 8) | Math.min(255, r); }\\n  return out;\\n}\\nconst MAT_KEYS = { // name: [ramp keys, specular strength]\\n  wall: [['#1c060c', '#4a0f18', '#7d1f26', '#a93a38'], .35], bowel: [['#3a0e1c', '#8a2f3e', '#c75f62', '#eb9486', '#ffc9b0'], 1], liver: [['#1e0610', '#4e1022', '#7c1e30', '#a83a42', '#d0645c'], .55],\\n1110→  fat: [['#5a3a10', '#a8782a', '#dcb34c', '#f4d97e', '#fff2b8'], .7], vessel: [['#14103a', '#2c2a70', '#4a4aa0', '#7c78c8'], .8], artery: [['#3a0408', '#8c0f14', '#cf2a22', '#f4604a'], 1],\\n  skin: [['#6a3a22', '#a8683f', '#d39a68', '#eebf92'], .12], drape: [['#0c2c2a', '#14504a', '#1f7468', '#2f9a88'], 0], steel: [['#28303a', '#5a6a78', '#9aaab8', '#e8f0f8'], 1.6],\\n  blood: [['#180204', '#3c060a', '#680c10', '#8e1414'], 1.4], wound: [['#0c0104', '#2a0408', '#55090e', '#7a1216'], .5], char: [['#08070a', '#1c181a', '#332a28', '#4a3c34'], 0], gel: [['#0c4a38', '#1c8a64', '#54d0a0', '#b0ffe0'], 1.4] };\\nconst MAT_IDS = Object.keys(MAT_KEYS), MATS = MAT_IDS.map(k => ({ ramp: makeRamp(MAT_KEYS[k][0], 20), slow: makeRamp(MAT_KEYS[k][0], 20, true), spec: MAT_KEYS[k][1] }));\\nconst matId = n => MAT_IDS.indexOf(n);\\nfunction makeNoise(seed) { let a = seed >>> 0; const R = () => { a = (a + 0x6d2b79f5) >>> 0; let t = Math.imul(a ^ (a >>> 15), a | 1); t ^= t + Math.imul(t ^ (t >>> 7), t | 61); return ((t ^ (t >>> 14)) >>> 0) / 4294967296; };\\n  const L = new Float32Array(65536); for (let i = 0; i < L.length; i++) L[i] = R();\\n  const v = (x, y) => { const xi = Math.floor(x), yi = Math.floor(y), fx = x - xi, fy = y - yi, u = fx * fx * (3 - 2 * fx), w = fy * fy * (3 - 2 * fy), s = (p, q) => L[(p & 255) | ((q & 255) << 8)]; return (s(xi, yi) * (1 - u) + s(xi + 1, yi) * u) * (1 - w) + (s(xi, yi + 1) * (1 - u) + s(xi + 1, yi + 1) * u) * w; };\\n  return { R, fbm: (x, y, o = 4) => { let k = .5, f = 1, n = 0; for (let i = 0; i < o; i++) { n += k * v(x * f, y * f); k *= .5; f *= 2.03; } return n; } }; }\\nclass TissueField {\\n1120→  constructor(W, H, seed = 7) { this.W = W; this.H = H; const N = W * H; this.h = new Float32Array(N); this.m = new Uint8Array(N); this.pulse = new Float32Array(N); this.ao = new Float32Array(N).fill(1); this.dyn = new Float32Array(N); this.over = new Uint8Array(N); this.noise = makeNoise(seed);\\n    this.cv = E.mkCanvas(W, H); this.g = this.cv.getContext('2d'); this.img = this.g.createImageData(W, H); this.u = new Uint32Array(this.img.data.buffer); }\\n  blob(x, y, rx, ry, rot, hgt, mat, o = {}) { const { W, H, h, m, pulse, noise } = this, c = Math.cos(rot), s = Math.sin(rot), R = Math.max(rx, ry) + (o.warp || 0) + 2, id = matId(mat), warp = o.warp || 0, base = o.base || 0, add = !!o.add;\\n    for (let py = Math.max(0, y - R | 0); py < Math.min(H, y + R + 1); py++) for (let px = Math.max(0, x - R | 0); px < Math.min(W, x + R + 1); px++) {\\n      const wx = warp ? (noise.fbm(px * .03, py * .03, 2) - .5) * warp : 0, wy = warp ? (noise.fbm(px * .03 + 40, py * .03, 2) - .5) * warp : 0, dx = px - x + wx, dy = py - y + wy, u = (dx * c + dy * s) / rx, v = (-dx * s + dy * c) / ry, d = u * u + v * v; if (d >= 1) continue;\\n      const i = py * W + px, top = base + hgt * Math.sqrt(1 - d); if (add) { h[i] += hgt * (1 - d); m[i] = id; if (o.pulse) pulse[i] = Math.max(pulse[i], o.pulse * (1 - d)); } else if (top > h[i]) { h[i] = top; m[i] = id; if (o.pulse) pulse[i] = o.pulse * (1 - d); } } }\\n  tube(pts, r, hgt, mat, o = {}) { const step = Math.max(1, r * .35); for (let k = 0; k < pts.length - 1; k++) { const [x0, y0] = pts[k], [x1, y1] = pts[k + 1], n = Math.max(1, Math.hypot(x1 - x0, y1 - y0) / step | 0); for (let i = 0; i <= n; i++) this.blob(x0 + (x1 - x0) * i / n, y0 + (y1 - y0) * i / n, r, r, 0, hgt, mat, o); } }\\n  finish(detail = 1.1) { const { W, H, h, ao, noise } = this, N = W * H, blur = new Float32Array(N), tmp = new Float32Array(N), R = 7;\\n    for (let i = 0, y = 0; y < H; y++) for (let x = 0; x < W; x++, i++) h[i] += (noise.fbm(x * .11, y * .11, 3) - .5) * detail;\\n    for (let y = 0; y < H; y++) { let s = 0; for (let x = -R; x <= R; x++) s += h[y * W + Math.max(0, x)]; for (let x = 0; x < W; x++) { tmp[y * W + x] = s / (2 * R + 1); s += h[y * W + Math.min(W - 1, x + R + 1)] - h[y * W + Math.max(0, x - R)]; } }\\n1130→    for (let x = 0; x < W; x++) { let s = 0; for (let y = -R; y <= R; y++) s += tmp[Math.max(0, y) * W + x]; for (let y = 0; y < H; y++) { blur[y * W + x] = s / (2 * R + 1); s += tmp[Math.min(H - 1, y + R + 1) * W + x] - tmp[Math.max(0, y - R) * W + x]; } }\\n    for (let i = 0; i < N; i++) ao[i] = Math.max(.42, Math.min(1.08, 1 - (blur[i] - h[i]) * .055)); }\\n  // what the sim's objects stamp each frame, before shade(): dynamic height, and an override material\\n  stampDisc(x, y, r, hgt, mat) { const { W, H, dyn, over } = this, id = mat ? matId(mat) + 1 : 0; for (let py = Math.max(1, y - r | 0); py < Math.min(H - 1, y + r + 1); py++) for (let px = Math.max(1, x - r | 0); px < Math.min(W - 1, x + r + 1); px++) { const d = ((px - x) ** 2 + (py - y) ** 2) / (r * r); if (d < 1) { const i = py * W + px; dyn[i] = hgt < 0 ? Math.min(dyn[i], hgt * (1 - d)) : Math.max(dyn[i], hgt * (1 - d)); if (id) over[i] = id; } } }\\n  stampLine(pts, r, hgt, mat) { for (let k = 0; k < pts.length - 1; k++) { const [x0, y0] = pts[k], [x1, y1] = pts[k + 1], n = Math.max(1, Math.hypot(x1 - x0, y1 - y0) / Math.max(1, r * .5) | 0); for (let i = 0; i <= n; i++) this.stampDisc(x0 + (x1 - x0) * i / n, y0 + (y1 - y0) * i / n, r, hgt, mat); } }\\n  // fx: { beat 0..1, breath px, slow: Long Breath palette, lens: {x, y, r} shows the cold palette inside (Sonar), light: [lx, ly], dim: 0..1 darkness, spot: {x, y, r} stays lit while dim }\\n  shade(fx = {}) { const { W, H, h, m, ao, pulse, dyn, over, u } = this, beat = fx.beat || 0, off = Math.round(fx.breath || 0) * W, slow = !!fx.slow, lens = fx.lens, lr2 = lens ? lens.r * lens.r : 0, dim = fx.dim || 0, spot = fx.spot, sr2 = spot ? spot.r * spot.r : 0, lx = fx.light ? fx.light[0] : -.42, ly = fx.light ? fx.light[1] : -.52, lz = Math.sqrt(Math.max(.1, 1 - lx * lx - ly * ly)), hl = Math.hypot(lx, ly, lz + 1), hx = lx / hl, hy = ly / hl, hz = (lz + 1) / hl;\\n    for (let y = 1; y < H - 1; y++) for (let x = 1, i = y * W + 1; x < W - 1; x++, i++) {\\n      const j = Math.max(W + 1, Math.min(W * H - W - 2, i + off)), k = 1 + beat * pulse[j], dx = (h[j - 1] - h[j + 1]) * k * .5 + (dyn[i - 1] - dyn[i + 1]) * .5, dy = (h[j - W] - h[j + W]) * k * .5 + (dyn[i - W] - dyn[i + W]) * .5, nl = 1 / Math.sqrt(dx * dx + dy * dy + 1), nx = dx * nl, ny = dy * nl, nz = nl;\\n      const diff = Math.max(0, nx * lx + ny * ly + nz * lz), sh = Math.max(0, nx * hx + ny * hy + nz * hz), ov = over[i], M = MATS[ov ? ov - 1 : m[j]], cold = slow || (lens && (x - lens.x) ** 2 + (y - lens.y) ** 2 < lr2), r = cold ? M.slow : M.ramp, n = r.length, s2 = sh * sh, s4 = s2 * s2, s8 = s4 * s4, s16 = s8 * s8, s32 = s16 * s16;\\n1140→      const lit = dim && !(spot && (x - spot.x) ** 2 + (y - spot.y) ** 2 < sr2) ? 1 - dim : 1, lum = (diff * .92 + s8 * .16 * M.spec + beat * pulse[j] * .6) * (ov ? 1 : ao[j]) * lit, c = Math.max(0, Math.min(n - 1, (lum * (n - 1) + BAYER[(x & 3) | ((y & 3) << 2)] * 1.15 + .5) | 0));\\n      u[i] = s32 * s8 * M.spec * lit > .42 ? (cold ? 0xffffffe0 : 0xfff4f0ff) : r[c]; }\\n    this.g.putImageData(this.img, 0, 0); dyn.fill(0); over.fill(0); }\\n}\\n```\\n\\n## Appendix B: a site recipe\\n\\n`F.stampDisc` coordinates here use screen pixels. To place sim objects, add `OX, OY`.\\n\\n1150→```js\\n// A site recipe: the abdomen. Design space is 426 x 240 about the screen's centre; a bigger screen only shows more drape.\\nfunction bakeAbdomen(W, H, seed = 11) { const F = new TissueField(W, H, seed), cx = W / 2, cy = H / 2 - 4, n = F.noise, rx = 171, ry = 91;\\n  for (let y = 0; y < H; y++) for (let x = 0; x < W; x++) { const i = y * W + x, d = ((x - cx) / rx) ** 2 + ((y - cy) / ry) ** 2, fold = Math.sin(x * .09 + 1.6 * Math.sin(y * .05)) * 1.6 + Math.sin(y * .17 + x * .03) * .8;\\n    if (d < 1) { F.h[i] = -16 + 12 * d * d + Math.sin(x * .05 + y * .03) * 1.4; F.m[i] = matId('wall'); }            // the cavity\\n    else if (d < 1.28) { const e = (d - 1) / .28; F.h[i] = 4 + 7 * Math.sin(e * Math.PI); F.m[i] = matId('skin'); }  // lips of skin\\n    else { F.h[i] = 6 + fold; F.m[i] = matId('drape'); } }                                                            // drapes to any screen's edge\\n  F.blob(cx - rx * .55, cy - ry * .5, rx * .55, ry * .55, -.3, 17, 'liver', { base: -11, warp: 12 });\\n  for (let i = 0; i < 9; i++) { const a = n.R() * 6.28, r = .55 + n.R() * .32; F.blob(cx + Math.cos(a) * rx * r, cy + Math.sin(a) * ry * r, 9 + n.R() * 14, 7 + n.R() * 9, n.R() * 3, 8, 'fat', { base: -10, warp: 8 }); }\\n  const coil = (x0, y0, len, dir, r) => { const pts = [[x0, y0]]; let a = dir, x = x0, y = y0; for (let i = 0; i < len; i++) { a += (n.R() - .5) * 1.5 + Math.sin(i * .9) * .5; x += Math.cos(a) * r * 1.3; y += Math.sin(a) * r * 1.3; if (((x - cx) / (rx * .92)) ** 2 + ((y - cy) / (ry * .92)) ** 2 > 1) { a += 2.4; x += Math.cos(a) * r; y += Math.sin(a) * r; } pts.push([x, y]); } return pts; };\\n1160→  F.tube(coil(cx + rx * .05, cy - ry * .25, 34, .2, 9), 11, 14, 'bowel', { base: -8 }); F.tube(coil(cx + rx * .45, cy + ry * .25, 26, 2.6, 9), 10, 15, 'bowel', { base: -7 }); F.tube(coil(cx - rx * .35, cy + ry * .45, 22, -.3, 9), 10, 15, 'bowel', { base: -6 });\\n  const veins = (x, y, a, len, r, mat) => { const pts = [[x, y]]; for (let i = 0; i < len; i++) { a += (n.R() - .5) * .8; x += Math.cos(a) * 7; y += Math.sin(a) * 7; if (((x - cx) / rx) ** 2 + ((y - cy) / ry) ** 2 > .96) break; pts.push([x, y]); if (r > 1.2 && n.R() < .22) veins(x, y, a + (n.R() < .5 ? .9 : -.9), len * .5 | 0, r * .7, mat); } F.tube(pts, r, 1.6, mat, { add: true, pulse: mat === 'artery' ? .35 : 0 }); };\\n  veins(cx - rx * .7, cy - ry * .05, -.2, 16, 2.2, 'vessel'); veins(cx + rx * .7, cy - ry * .6, 2.4, 14, 2, 'artery'); veins(cx + rx * .1, cy + ry * .75, -1.5, 12, 1.8, 'vessel');\\n  for (const a of [-.45, .5, 2.7, 3.6]) { const ex = cx + Math.cos(a) * rx * 1.02, ey = cy + Math.sin(a) * ry * 1.02, ox = cx + Math.cos(a) * rx * 1.5, oy = cy + Math.sin(a) * ry * 1.5; F.tube([[ex, ey], [ox, oy]], 4, 5, 'steel', { base: 10 }); F.tube([[ex - Math.sin(a) * 9, ey + Math.cos(a) * 9], [ex + Math.sin(a) * 9, ey - Math.cos(a) * 9]], 3, 5, 'steel', { base: 10 }); } // retractors\\n  F.finish(1.1); return F; }\\n```\\n\\n## Appendix C: portrait painter\\n\\nBusts for the four leads. The other four follow the same shape: `frontHair`, `backHair` (polygons in face space, x from the center, y down), `glasses`, `moustache`, `scar`, `cap`, `mask` (`maskUp: true` covers the mouth, for theatre), `lines`, `blush`, `jaw`, `wide`. Cache each expression to a canvas with `E.mkCanvas(112, 128)`; call `E.px.reset` on the target after drawing on it with the canvas API.\\n1170→\\n```js\\n// A bust from data in hard-edged tones, 112 x 128 buffer pixels. Cache each expression in a canvas; the sim never reads it. Needs E = My3D2dge in scope.\\n// o: { expr: neutral | smile | angry | sad | shock | shout, blink: 0..1, talk: 0..1, look: -1..1 }\\nfunction drawBust(g, c, o = {}) { const px = E.px, P = (pts, col) => px.poly(g, pts, col), T = E.tones;\\n  const skin = T(c.skin), hair = T(c.hair), cloth = T(c.cloth), expr = o.expr || 'neutral', blink = o.blink || 0, talk = o.talk || 0, cx = 56, top = 20;\\n  const jaw = c.jaw || 1, wide = c.wide || 1, f = (x, y) => [cx + x * wide, top + y]; // face space: x from centre, y down\\n  // back hair\\n  if (c.backHair) for (const s of c.backHair) P(s.map(([x, y]) => f(x, y)), hair.sh);\\n  // shoulders and collar\\n1180→  P([[6, 128], [10, 104], [30, 92], [46, 88], [66, 88], [82, 92], [102, 104], [106, 128]], cloth.base);\\n  P([[6, 128], [10, 104], [30, 92], [34, 128]], cloth.sh); P([[78, 128], [82, 92], [102, 104], [106, 128]], cloth.lt);\\n  // neck\\n  P([[46, 66], [66, 66], [68, 92], [56, 104], [44, 92]], skin.sh);\\n  // V-neck and inner shirt\\n  P([[42, 88], [56, 110], [70, 88], [66, 86], [56, 100], [46, 86]], cloth.deep); P([[46, 86], [56, 100], [66, 86], [64, 92], [56, 104], [48, 92]], c.undershirt || skin.sh);\\n  if (c.coat) { P([[6, 128], [10, 104], [30, 92], [42, 88], [52, 128]], c.coat); P([[106, 128], [102, 104], [82, 92], [70, 88], [60, 128]], c.coat); P([[42, 88], [52, 128], [47, 128], [36, 92]], E.tones(c.coat).sh); P([[70, 88], [60, 128], [65, 128], [76, 92]], E.tones(c.coat).sh); }\\n  // ears\\n  P([f(-27, 30), f(-31, 34), f(-30, 42), f(-26, 45)], skin.sh); P([f(27, 30), f(31, 34), f(30, 42), f(26, 45)], skin.base);\\n  // head: wide cranium, cheek, chin\\n1190→  const J = jaw; P([f(-26, 12), f(-27, 34), f(-22, 48 * J), f(-11, 60 * J), f(0, 64 * J), f(11, 60 * J), f(22, 48 * J), f(27, 34), f(26, 12), f(14, 2), f(-14, 2)], skin.base);\\n  P([f(-27, 30), f(-22, 48 * J), f(-11, 60 * J), f(0, 64 * J), f(-6, 54 * J), f(-16, 44), f(-22, 30)], skin.sh); // cheek shadow, viewer's left\\n  if (c.blush) { px.ell(g, ...f(-15, 41), 4, 2, c.blush); px.ell(g, ...f(16, 41), 4, 2, c.blush); }\\n  if (c.lines) { px.line(g, ...f(-11, 45), ...f(-6, 50), skin.sh, 1); px.line(g, ...f(11, 45), ...f(6, 50), skin.sh, 1); px.line(g, ...f(-23, 26), ...f(-20, 28), skin.sh, 1); px.line(g, ...f(23, 26), ...f(20, 28), skin.sh, 1); }\\n  // eyes\\n  const eye = (sx) => { const x = sx * 13, y = 30 + (expr === 'shock' ? -1 : 0), w = 7, hh = expr === 'shock' ? 8 : expr === 'angry' ? 5 : 7;\\n    if (blink > .5 || expr === 'sad-closed') { px.line(g, ...f(x - w, y + 1), ...f(x + w, y + 1), c.lash || '#1a1016', 2); return; }\\n    P([f(x - w, y), f(x - w + 3, y - hh + 2), f(x + w - 3, y - hh + 2), f(x + w, y), f(x + w - 2, y + 4), f(x - w + 2, y + 4)], '#f8f4ee');\\n    const ix = x + (o.look || 0) * 2 + (expr === 'shock' ? 0 : 0); px.ell(g, ...f(ix, y - 1), 4, expr === 'shock' ? 4 : 5, c.iris || '#3a5a7a'); px.ell(g, ...f(ix, y), 2, 3, c.pupil || '#120c14'); px.rect(g, ...f(ix - 3, y - 4), 3, 2, '#ffffff'); px.dot(g, ...f(ix + 2, y + 2), '#ffffff');\\n    px.line(g, ...f(x - w - 1, y + (sx < 0 ? 1 : -1)), ...f(x - w + 4, y - hh + 2), c.lash || '#1a1016', 2); px.line(g, ...f(x - w + 4, y - hh + 2), ...f(x + w - 4, y - hh + 2), c.lash || '#1a1016', 2); px.line(g, ...f(x + w - 4, y - hh + 2), ...f(x + w + 1, y + (sx < 0 ? -1 : 1)), c.lash || '#1a1016', 2); };\\n1200→  eye(-1); eye(1);\\n  // brows\\n  const by = 17 + (c.browDrop || 0), br = expr === 'angry' ? 3 : expr === 'sad' ? -3 : expr === 'shock' ? -1 : 0, bc = c.brow || hair.deep, bw = c.browW || 2; px.line(g, ...f(-20, by + (expr === 'shock' ? -3 : 0) - br), ...f(-7, by + (expr === 'shock' ? -3 : 0) + br), bc, bw); px.line(g, ...f(7, by + (expr === 'shock' ? -3 : 0) + br), ...f(20, by + (expr === 'shock' ? -3 : 0) - br), bc, bw);\\n  // nose\\n  px.line(g, ...f(1, 40), ...f(-1, 45), skin.sh, 1); px.dot(g, ...f(0, 46), skin.deep);\\n  // mouth\\n  const my = 53, mo = talk > .5 || expr === 'shout' || expr === 'shock'; if (mo) { const a = expr === 'shout' || expr === 'shock' ? 7 : 4, w = expr === 'shout' ? 7 : 5; P([f(-w, my - 1), f(w, my - 1), f(w - 2, my + a), f(-w + 2, my + a)], '#5a1a24'); P([f(-w + 2, my + a - 2), f(w - 2, my + a - 2), f(w - 3, my + a), f(-w + 3, my + a)], '#c8505a'); if (a > 4) px.rect(g, ...f(-w + 1, my - 1), (w - 1) * 2, 2, '#f4f0e8'); }\\n  else if (expr === 'smile') { px.line(g, ...f(-7, my - 1), ...f(-3, my + 2), skin.deep, 1); px.line(g, ...f(-3, my + 2), ...f(3, my + 2), skin.deep, 1); px.line(g, ...f(3, my + 2), ...f(7, my - 1), skin.deep, 1); }\\n  else if (expr === 'sad' || expr === 'angry') { px.line(g, ...f(-5, my + 2), ...f(0, my), skin.deep, 1); px.line(g, ...f(0, my), ...f(5, my + 2), skin.deep, 1); }\\n  else px.line(g, ...f(-4, my), ...f(4, my), skin.deep, 1);\\n1210→  if (c.moustache) { P([f(-13, 50), f(-6, 46), f(0, 48), f(6, 46), f(13, 50), f(8, 52), f(0, 50), f(-8, 52)], c.moustache); px.line(g, ...f(-6, 47), ...f(6, 47), E.tones(c.moustache).lt, 1); }\\n  // front hair\\n  if (c.frontHair) for (const [s, tone] of c.frontHair) P(s.map(([x, y]) => f(x, y)), hair[tone || 'base']);\\n  if (c.hairGlint) for (const s of c.hairGlint) P(s.map(([x, y]) => f(x, y)), hair.hi);\\n  // cap\\n  if (c.cap) { const k = T(c.cap); P([f(-29, 16), f(-27, 2), f(-14, -9), f(14, -9), f(27, 2), f(29, 16), f(16, 11), f(-16, 11)], k.base); P([f(-29, 16), f(-27, 2), f(-14, -9), f(-6, -9), f(-14, 11), f(-16, 11)], k.sh); P([f(6, -8), f(14, -9), f(22, -3), f(14, -3)], k.lt); P([f(-29, 16), f(-16, 11), f(16, 11), f(29, 16), f(27, 18), f(16, 14), f(-16, 14), f(-27, 18)], k.deep); }\\n  // lowered mask, glasses\\n  if (c.mask && !o.maskUp) { const k = T(c.mask); P([f(-20, 62), f(20, 62), f(25, 76), f(0, 84), f(-25, 76)], k.base); P([f(-20, 62), f(-25, 76), f(0, 84), f(-6, 74)], k.sh); px.line(g, ...f(-20, 62), ...f(-28, 50), k.lt, 1); px.line(g, ...f(20, 62), ...f(28, 50), k.lt, 1); }\\n  if (c.glasses) { const m = c.glasses; for (const sx of [-1, 1]) { const x = sx * 13; px.line(g, ...f(x - 9, 25), ...f(x + 9, 25), m, 1); px.line(g, ...f(x - 9, 25), ...f(x - 8, 33), m, 1); px.line(g, ...f(x + 9, 25), ...f(x + 8, 33), m, 1); px.line(g, ...f(x - 8, 34), ...f(x + 8, 34), m, 1); } px.line(g, ...f(-4, 27), ...f(4, 27), m, 1); }\\n  if (o.maskUp && c.mask) { const k = T(c.mask); P([f(-27, 36), f(-8, 34), f(0, 36), f(8, 34), f(27, 36), f(25, 54), f(11, 66), f(0, 68), f(-11, 66), f(-25, 54)], k.base); P([f(-27, 36), f(-8, 34), f(-3, 50), f(-11, 66), f(-25, 54)], k.sh); for (const yy of [44, 52]) px.line(g, ...f(-18, yy), ...f(18, yy), k.lt, 1); px.line(g, ...f(-27, 38), ...f(-31, 32), k.lt, 1); px.line(g, ...f(27, 38), ...f(31, 32), k.lt, 1); }\\n1220→  if (c.scar) px.line(g, ...f(c.scar[0], c.scar[1]), ...f(c.scar[2], c.scar[3]), skin.deep, 1);\\n  if (c.tag) { px.rect(g, 72, 108, 16, 10, '#f0ece2'); px.rect(g, 74, 110, 12, 2, c.tag); px.rect(g, 74, 114, 8, 1, '#7a7468'); }\\n}\\nconst CAST = {\\n  vale: { name: 'DR. VALE', skin: '#f6d0b2', hair: '#c9562a', iris: '#3f8a58', cloth: '#2f9c8e', cap: '#2bb0a2', mask: '#bfe6dc', undershirt: '#e9fff6', blush: '#f0a48c', wide: .95,\\n    backHair: [[[-27, 14], [-30, 40], [-24, 52], [-16, 40], [-16, 14]], [[27, 14], [30, 40], [24, 52], [16, 40], [16, 14]]],\\n    frontHair: [[[[-27, 12], [-24, 24], [-16, 16], [-10, 22], [-3, 14], [3, 21], [10, 14], [18, 22], [27, 12], [14, 8], [-14, 8]], 'base'], [[[-27, 12], [-24, 24], [-16, 16], [-14, 10]], 'sh']],\\n    tag: '#c8452f' },\\n  quill: { name: 'NURSE QUILL', skin: '#dcae8e', hair: '#8d8a88', iris: '#4a4a55', cloth: '#3d62a6', cap: '#35558f', undershirt: '#dfe6f2', moustache: '#a7a3a0', lines: true, wide: 1.08, jaw: 1.04, browW: 3, brow: '#7c7875', browDrop: 2, mask: '#a9cfe0', frontHair: [] },\\n  oyelaran: { name: 'CHIEF OYELARAN', skin: '#7c4a2e', hair: '#c9c5bb', iris: '#2a1a12', cloth: '#2e2c36', coat: '#ece7da', undershirt: '#3a3644', lines: true, jaw: .98, glasses: '#d8c070',\\n1230→    backHair: [], frontHair: [[[[-27, 18], [-27, 2], [-14, -8], [14, -8], [27, 2], [27, 18], [20, 10], [8, 6], [-8, 6], [-20, 10]], 'base'], [[[-27, 18], [-27, 2], [-14, -8], [-8, 6], [-20, 10]], 'sh'], [[[6, -7], [16, -6], [22, 0], [12, 0]], 'lt']] },\\n  sokolova: { name: 'DR. SOKOLOVA', skin: '#e8c6aa', hair: '#dedad2', iris: '#6a8aa8', cloth: '#6b7a48', undershirt: '#f0ece0', lines: true, wide: 1.02, jaw: 1.02, scar: [10, 14, 17, 40], browW: 3, brow: '#b8b4ac',\\n    backHair: [[[-28, 6], [-34, 34], [-30, 58], [-20, 50], [-20, 10]], [[28, 6], [36, 26], [46, 52], [38, 64], [28, 44], [22, 10]]], frontHair: [[[[-27, 16], [-26, 2], [-12, -8], [14, -8], [27, 2], [27, 16], [16, 6], [-2, 12], [-16, 8]], 'base'], [[[-27, 16], [-26, 2], [-12, -8], [-6, 8], [-16, 8]], 'sh']] },\\n};\\n```\\n\\n## Appendix D: Hub skeleton, page head and harness runner\\n\\n**Page head.** Order matters.\\n\\n1240→```html\\n<!doctype html><html lang=\\\"en\\\"><head><meta charset=\\\"utf-8\\\">\\n<script>/* vendor/hub/session.js, pasted unmodified */</script>\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session\\\" content=\\\"1\\\"><meta name=\\\"hub:session-players\\\" content=\\\"1-4\\\"><meta name=\\\"hub:session-mode\\\" content=\\\"shared-world\\\"><meta name=\\\"hub:session-minutes\\\" content=\\\"5\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-goal\\\" content=\\\"Save the patient together; best hands top the board\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-howto\\\" content=\\\"Aim: right stick or mouse. Use tool: R2 or click. Tools: left stick or 1-8\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"viewport\\\" content=\\\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1, viewport-fit=cover\\\"><title>…</title>\\n<style>html,body{margin:0;height:100%;background:#000;overflow:hidden}#screen{width:100%;height:100%;display:block;image-rendering:pixelated;touch-action:none}html .esh-row small{display:none}html .esh-row:focus-within small{display:block}</style></head>\\n<body><!-- E.bootSplash's markup here --><canvas id=\\\"screen\\\"></canvas><script>/* engine */</script><script>/* game */</script></body></html>\\n```\\n1250→\\n**Skeleton.** A complete shared-world game on the engine. It passed all four harness runs and runs standalone. Replace the dots with the real sim, keeping its structure: connect, the event handlers, the host's step, the 12/s snapshot and the mirror. `HUB.send` for a real event batch is reliable by default.\\n\\n```js\\n// Hub skeleton: shared world on my-3D2dge. The host's copy runs the world; other copies mirror it and send their own hands.\\n(() => {\\nconst E = My3D2dge, HUB = window.AIHub, inSession = !!(HUB && HUB.active), { px } = E;\\nconst { attack, dash, skill, jump, ...base } = E.Input.DEFAULT;\\nconst game = window.G = new E.Game({ canvas: 'screen', res: [426, 240], expand: true, view: 'overhead', touch: 'never', live: inSession, autoPause: !inSession, corner: !inSession,\\n  input: { ...base, useTool: ['Pad7', 'Pad0', 'Mouse0', 'Space'], more: ['Pad3', 'KeyM'] }, menu: { quit: inSession ? false : 'title', settings: [{ key: 'music', value: .1 }, { key: 'glyphs', value: 'playstation', options: [['playstation', 'PlayStation'], ['xbox', 'Xbox']] }] } });\\n1260→const NAME = 'SKELETON', W0 = 426, H0 = 240, DOTS = 12;\\nlet info = null, phase = 'wait', card = null, over = false, sent = 0, heardSeq = 0, S = { v: 70, dots: [], p: {} }, mine = { x: 200, y: 120, t: 0 }, rng = null;\\nconst place = () => ({ x: 40 + rng() * 340, y: 40 + rng() * 160 });\\nfunction hostInit() { rng = HUB.rng('deal'); S.dots = Array.from({ length: DOTS }, (_, id) => ({ id, ...place(), alive: 1 })); for (const q of info.players) S.p[q.id] = { x: 200, y: 120, t: 0, score: 0, gone: false }; }\\nfunction finish(won) { if (over) return; over = true; if (HUB.server !== true && info.me != null && S.p[info.me]) Object.assign(S.p[info.me], mine, { score: S.p[info.me].score });\\n  const live = info.players.filter(q => !S.p[q.id].gone).sort((a, b) => S.p[b.id].score - S.p[a.id].score), standings = [];\\n  for (const q of live) { const last = standings[standings.length - 1]; if (last && S.p[last[0]].score === S.p[q.id].score) last.push(q.id); else standings.push([q.id]); }\\n  const scores = Object.fromEntries(info.players.map(q => [q.id, S.p[q.id].score]));\\n  HUB.send({ t: 'over', v: S.v }); HUB.finish({ standings, scores, won, awards: live.length ? [{ id: live[0].id, title: 'Steadiest Hand', detail: scores[live[0].id] + ' clean actions' }] : [] }); }\\nasync function connect() { info = await HUB.connect({ mode: 'shared-world', seats: [1, 4], card: true });\\n1270→  if (!info) { phase = 'solo'; return; }\\n  HUB.on('card', c => { card = c; });\\n  HUB.on('start', () => { phase = 'count'; });\\n  HUB.on('message', (d, from) => { if (info.isHost) { const p = S.p[from]; if (d.t === 'i' && p && d.n > (p.n || 0)) { p.n = d.n; p.x = d.x; p.y = d.y; p.t = d.t2; } } else if (from === info.host) { if (d.t === 's' && d.n > heardSeq) { heardSeq = d.n; S.v = d.v; S.dots = d.o.map(([id, x, y]) => ({ id, x, y, alive: 1 })); for (const [id, x, y, t, sc] of d.p) { const q = S.p[id] || (S.p[id] = {}); if (id !== info.me) { q.x = x; q.y = y; q.t = t; } q.score = sc; } } if (d.t === 'over') over = true; } });\\n  HUB.on('leave', ({ id }) => { if (S.p[id]) S.p[id].gone = true; });\\n  HUB.on('time-up', () => { if (info.isHost) finish(false); });\\n  HUB.on('end', () => { phase = 'over'; game.audio.music(null); game.audio.mute(true); });\\n  if (info.isHost) hostInit();\\n  HUB.ready(); }\\nfunction step(dt) { const I = game.input, m = I.move(), a = I.padAim, ptr = I.pointer(); let dx = (a ? a[0] : 0) + m[0], dy = (a ? a[1] : 0) + m[1]; mine.x = E.clamp(mine.x + dx * 300 * dt, 0, W0); mine.y = E.clamp(mine.y + dy * 300 * dt, 0, H0); if (ptr && I.lastDevice === 'mouse') { mine.x = ptr[0]; mine.y = ptr[1]; } mine.t = I.value('useTool'); }\\n1280→let snapN = 0, inN = 0, acc = 0;\\nfunction hostStep(dt) { S.v = Math.max(0, S.v - .05 * dt); if (info.me != null && S.p[info.me] && !info.server) Object.assign(S.p[info.me], mine, { score: S.p[info.me].score });\\n  for (const id in S.p) { const p = S.p[id]; if (p.gone || !p.t) continue; for (const d of S.dots) if (d.alive && Math.hypot(d.x - p.x, d.y - p.y) < 12) { d.alive = 0; p.score++; } } S.dots = S.dots.filter(d => d.alive); if (!S.dots.length) finish(true); }\\ngame.start({ scene: 'hub', scenes: { hub: { pausable: true, camera: { zoom: false, wheel: false },\\n  update(dt) { const I = game.input; game.focus(game.W / 2, game.H / 2, 0);\\n    if (!info && !inSession) return;\\n    if (phase === 'wait' && info) { if (I.pressed('confirm')) HUB.answer('ready'); if (I.pressed('more')) HUB.answer('more'); const c = I.clicked(); if (c) HUB.answer(c[1] > game.H * .7 ? 'more' : 'ready'); }\\n    if (phase === 'count' && HUB.time() >= 0) phase = 'play';\\n    if (phase === 'play' && !over) { step(dt); if (info.isHost) hostStep(dt); acc += dt; if (acc >= 1 / 12) { acc = 0; if (info.isHost) HUB.send({ t: 's', n: ++snapN, v: Math.round(S.v), o: S.dots.map(d => [d.id, d.x | 0, d.y | 0]), p: info.players.filter(q => !S.p[q.id].gone).map(q => { const p = S.p[q.id]; return [q.id, p.x | 0, p.y | 0, p.t > .25 ? 1 : 0, p.score | 0]; }) }, { reliable: false }); else HUB.send({ t: 'i', n: ++inN, x: mine.x | 0, y: mine.y | 0, t2: mine.t > .25 ? 1 : 0 }, { to: info.host, reliable: false }); } } },\\n  draw(r) { if (info && info.server) return; r.sky(['#143a36', '#14504a']); r.overlay(g => { E.font.text(g, NAME + ' ' + phase + (card && card.left != null ? ' ' + Math.ceil(card.left / 1000) : ''), 8, 8, '#fff', { outline: false }); for (const d of S.dots) px.disc(g, d.x, d.y, 4, '#5ff0e0'); if (info) for (const q of info.players) { const p = q.id === info.me ? mine : S.p[q.id]; if (p && !(S.p[q.id] || {}).gone) { px.disc(g, p.x, p.y, 5, q.color); E.font.text(g, q.name.slice(0, 12), p.x + 7, p.y - 4, q.color, { outline: false }); } } }); } } } });\\n1290→window.__hubProbe = () => ({ phase, over, dots: S.dots.length, v: S.v, t: HUB.active ? HUB.time() : 0 });\\nconnect();\\n})();\\n```\\n\\n**Harness runner.** The folder needs the game (as `game.html`) and copies of `harness.html`, `bridge.js`, `round.js`. It uses an OS-picked port, checks the frame's title is your game (replace the print with a test), and stops only its own server, by handle.\\n\\n```python\\nimport json, os, re, subprocess, sys, time\\nfrom playwright.sync_api import sync_playwright\\n1300→folder = os.path.abspath(sys.argv[1])\\nsrv = subprocess.Popen([sys.executable, '-u', '-m', 'http.server', '0', '--bind', '127.0.0.1'], cwd=folder, stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.STDOUT, text=True)\\ntry:\\n    line = srv.stdout.readline(); port = int(re.search(r'port (\\\\d+)', line).group(1))\\n    base = f'http://127.0.0.1:{port}/'\\n    RUNS = ['players=2&auto=1', 'players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1', 'players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&scenario=host-leave&auto=1', 'players=2&scenario=server&auto=1']\\n    with sync_playwright() as p:\\n        b = p.chromium.launch(args=['--autoplay-policy=no-user-gesture-required'])\\n        for q in RUNS:\\n            pg = b.new_page(viewport={'width': 1280, 'height': 720}); pg.goto(base + 'harness.html?game=game.html&' + q)\\n1310→            try: pg.wait_for_function(\\\"() => document.body.dataset.complete === 'true'\\\", timeout=90000)\\n            except Exception as e: print('TIMEOUT', q)\\n            r = pg.evaluate(\\\"() => { const h = window.__hubCheck; return h ? { pass: h.pass, failed: h.checks.filter(c => !c.pass).map(c => c.name + ': ' + c.detail), n: h.checks.length } : null }\\\")\\n            title = pg.evaluate(\\\"() => { const f = document.querySelector('iframe'); try { return f && f.contentDocument && f.contentDocument.title } catch (e) { return 'x' } }\\\")\\n            print(q, json.dumps(r)[:700], 'title:', title); pg.close()\\n        pg = b.new_page(viewport={'width': 1280, 'height': 720}); errs = []; pg.on('pageerror', lambda e: errs.append(str(e))); pg.goto(base + 'game.html'); pg.wait_for_timeout(1200); print('standalone', pg.evaluate(\\\"() => ({ active: AIHub.active, probe: window.__hubProbe() })\\\"), errs[:3]); pg.close()\\n        b.close()\\nfinally:\\n    srv.terminate(); srv.wait(timeout=5)\\n```\\n1320→\\n## Appendix E: gamepad stub and touch-notice patch\\n\\n**A DualSense in the standard mapping, for Playwright's `add_init_script`.** Set `__pad.axes[0..3]` (left x, left y, right x, right y), `__pad.buttons[i].value` and `.pressed`: 0 ✕, 1 ○, 2 □, 3 △, 4 L1, 5 R1, 6 L2, 7 R2, 8 Create, 9 Options, 12 to 15 D-pad, and bump `__pad.timestamp`.\\n\\n```js\\n(() => { const axes = [0, 0, 0, 0], buttons = Array.from({ length: 18 }, () => ({ pressed: false, touched: false, value: 0 }));\\n  const pad = { id: 'DualSense Wireless Controller (STANDARD GAMEPAD Vendor: 054c Product: 0ce6)', index: 0, connected: true, mapping: 'standard', timestamp: 0, axes, buttons,\\n    vibrationActuator: { playEffect() { return Promise.resolve('complete'); }, reset() { return Promise.resolve('complete'); } } };\\n  window.__pad = pad; navigator.getGamepads = () => [pad, null, null, null]; })();\\n1330→```\\n\\n**Touch notice.** In your copy of the engine, `Game.prototype._needSync` sets the notice's `style.cssText` to a full-screen dark layer. Change that one string, so the reel shows above and the notice sits at the bottom. *Verified* on an iPhone profile: the notice sits in the lower half, the canvas above it is clear, and a tap on Settings opens the settings:\\n\\n```js\\nd.style.cssText = 'position:absolute;left:0;right:0;bottom:0;z-index:20;display:flex;align-items:flex-end;justify-content:center;padding:48px 24px 24px;text-align:center;pointer-events:none;background:linear-gradient(transparent,rgba(5,3,12,.92) 45%);color:#f4f0ff;font:600 17px/1.45 system-ui,sans-serif';\\n// and on the button, add pointer-events:auto to its inline style\\n```\\n\\nNote it in your Engine Feedback.\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223916__x488eb9/work/PLAN.md\",\"offset\":1000,\"limit\":340,\"raw_output\":\"- The exit interview and `src/about-data.json` parse as JSON, and every field is filled.\\n\\n**Browser (Playwright, one or two browsers at a time, from `file://` where possible):** launch Chromium with `--autoplay-policy=no-user-gesture-required` for audio checks. Without it, the console's AudioContext warning before the first gesture is not a bug, so don't count warnings as failures.\\n- The built HTML plays 30 s with no errors in Chromium, Firefox and WebKit.\\n- `node tools/check.mjs` on the page (add `#scene` to jump to a scene): errors, what's on screen, look notes. Act on every look note.\\n- Settings defaults: music .1; glyphs PlayStation; the Xbox option switches labels.\\n- Pad with the Appendix E stub: `R2` and `△` prompts, stick cursor, wheel, trigger; keyboard and mouse switch the prompts.\\n- Phone profiles (Chromium, and WebKit with an iPhone profile, the closest thing here to iOS): notice and Settings button show, no touch buttons, attract runs behind, and a tap on Settings (`page.touchscreen.tap`) opens it.\\n- Audio: render through an `OfflineAudioContext` or read an `AnalyserNode`: effects, each voice and the choir are not silent; at defaults music is well below effects.\\n- Sizes: 1280x720, 1920x1080, 2560x1440, 3440x1440: HUD inside the screen, cavity centered.\\n- **Hub harness, all four runs (Appendix D's runner):** `players=2&auto=1`; `players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1`; the same with `&scenario=host-leave`; `players=2&scenario=server&auto=1`. Pass means `window.__hubCheck.pass === true`. Then open the page outside a session.\\n- This computer has no second player, so use the harness's frames, or two browser pages or contexts, as the stand-in. With 2 players in the harness, whose game frames are same-origin and reachable from the test: feed a client's cursor through a stroke, and check the stroke lands on the host's state.\\n\\n**Screenshots, for what scripts can't judge:** each site, each stage, the portraits, one frame of each strain, the wheel, Long Breath. Use `tools/filmstrip.mjs` for motion. Judge at 1920x1080 for the 10-foot view. WebGPU isn't used, so screenshots work.\\n\\n**This computer** (full detail in TASK.md): no display, so `xvfb-run` if you ever need headed; Playwright 1.63 is ready (`npm install playwright@1.63.0` in the dev folder for Node); never run `playwright install`; for a Python library such as Pillow, use `uv` as TASK.md says; `Target closed` means memory ran out, not a bug: run fewer browsers. Other agents share this machine, so keep one or two browsers open and only read what you need, never the folders beside yours. Keep all files in your folder, not `/tmp`. Prefer `file://` and the engine's headless tools. Other agents share this machine's ports and processes: use a server only for the harness, bound to `127.0.0.1` on a port the OS picks (`python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` prints it), and confirm the page is your game before trusting a result. Stop only processes you started, by their PID: never `pkill`, `killall`, `fuser -k`, or kill whatever holds a port.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 21. Build order\\n\\nEach milestone ends with a playable, tested game. Multiplayer comes early because it constrains the sim.\\n\\n| # | Milestone | Done when |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| M0 | Title picked. Folders, vendor, manifest, bundler, dev page, test runner, Hub client in the page | Empty game builds, runs, one test passes, harness runner works on Appendix D |\\n| M1 | **Hands:** sim core, tissue painter, cursor, wheel, Forceps, Salve, Suture, Syringe, vitals, clock, grades, HUD, Results, Case 1 end to end, bot | A person would enjoy Case 1 on a stubbed pad and mouse. Tune this until it's good; everything rests on it |\\n| M2 | **Shell:** title, menu, settings, glyphs, pause, TV scale, About skeleton, Dev Mode, attract v1, touch notice, saves, story system, busts, voices, first music, Case 1's story | First launch to Case 1 to Results feels finished, all TASK.md's cross-cutting features exist |\\n| M3 | Scalpel, Suction, Cautery, Sonar, Lightbox, Code Blue, Long Breath, Cases 2 and 3, Chapter 1 complete | Chapter 1 plays through, bot and all |\\n| M4 | **Grand Rounds:** local, then Hub, own card | All four harness runs pass, co-op works with bots |\\n| M5 | Staccato, Legato, Tremolo, ward, Cases 4 to 6, Chapter 2 | Chapter 2 plays through |\\n| M6 | Second Look, tilt, blackout, Da Capo, device gestures, Cases 7 to 9, Chapter 3 | Chapter 3 plays through |\\n| M7 | Consult, Fermata, Cantor, Cases 10 and 11, ending, credits | The whole story plays through |\\n| M8 | **Polish:** feel table complete, cinematics, stages, title art, full score and mix, attract final reel, Skills Lab, balance runs, three browsers, CONTENT.md | Section 22 all true |\\n| M9 | About text, exit interview, final build, final test run, chat reply | Done |\\n\\n**If you run out of runway**, cut content from the end of this list and keep everything else: Case 9, then Skills Lab, then Da Capo's round 2, then the ship's ward (reuse Ward 3), then a stage or two. **Never cut:** the cursor's quality, any of the eight tools, both gifts, the finale, Grand Rounds and the Hub tests, attract mode, dev mode, the 10-foot UI, About, the exit interview. Whatever ships is complete: no stubs, no TODOs.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 22. Done checklist (TASK.md, line by line)\\n\\nGo through this at the end. Everything must be true.\\n\\n**Source, genre, perspective, title.**\\n- [ ] Original game from *Trauma Center: Second Opinion* per the originality rule; genre and perspective per section 2.\\n- [ ] Title is a playful riff, chosen by TASK.md's method with a real RNG, stored in one constant.\\n\\n**Package and environment.**\\n- [ ] One HTML file at the root as TASK.md names it, self-contained, working from `file://`.\\n- [ ] Dev files in the subfolder TASK.md names. Nothing read from other agents' folders. Only your own processes stopped, by PID.\\n\\n**Tech, art, animation.**\\n- [ ] Built on the engine's handoff package; engine patches noted.\\n- [ ] Native pixel look as the base, new art for everything surgical, asset store only for generic props.\\n- [ ] Native animation as the base, new animations per section 17.\\n\\n**Audio.**\\n- [ ] All music, effects and voices in code, no audio files. Gibberish voices.\\n- [ ] Music at 10% by default (test proves it).\\n\\n**Online.**\\n- [ ] Hub session protocol followed; all four harness runs pass; runs outside a session; couch co-op on its own.\\n\\n**Performance, testing.**\\n- [ ] No optimization work done for its own sake.\\n- [ ] One test command, printing only failures and a one-line count, passes.\\n- [ ] Scripted full playthrough passes.\\n\\n**Scope.**\\n- [ ] Every mechanic in sections 5 to 9 is shown in play: a player can see what they can and cannot do.\\n- [ ] Data-driven content, `CONTENT.md`, Dev Jump.\\n- [ ] Dev mode from the pause menu, live sliders, reset to defaults.\\n- [ ] Attract after 10 s idle on title and menus, looping AI demo, any input exits.\\n- [ ] Tone: section 10.\\n\\n**UI and UX.**\\n- [ ] Controller-first on the same screens as keyboard and mouse, which feel native; hover moves focus; mouse aims and clicks things in the world.\\n- [ ] Prompts follow the last device. PlayStation glyphs, Settings option for Xbox, keys and mouse buttons for keyboard and mouse.\\n- [ ] No dense, web-style data screens.\\n- [ ] Touch-only: no touch controls, \\\"Controller or keyboard required\\\" and the Settings button, attract runs.\\n\\n**Done and reporting.**\\n- [ ] No TODOs, placeholders, stubs.\\n- [ ] Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback, Prompt Feedback in the in-game About/Credits **and** in your final chat reply, \\\"unknown\\\" where you can't confirm, Usage as `added on import`.\\n- [ ] The exit interview saved beside the HTML file, in TASK.md's exact shape, parsing as JSON, every value filled.\\n- [ ] Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback answer each TASK.md question in order, from `notes/feedback.md`, honestly.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Appendix A: tissue painter\\n\\nRan on this computer at 480x270, 3 to 5 ms a frame under software rendering. It also runs headless. One instance per site. Each frame: stamp pools, wounds and so on from `S`, then `shade(fx)`, then draw `F.cv`.\\n\\n- `stampDisc(x, y, r, height, mat)`, `stampLine(points, r, height, mat)`: per-frame height and material. Height alone (no `mat`) makes a ridge, for Legato.\\n- `shade({ beat, breath, slow, lens, light, dim, spot })`: see its comment. `dyn` and `over` clear after each shade.\\n- `slow: true` is Long Breath's palette. `lens` is Sonar's. `dim` and `spot` are the blackout. A moving `light` is the swaying lamp.\\n\\n```js\\n// Bake once per site, stamp from the sim's state each frame, then shade(). Cosmetic only: the sim never reads it. Needs E = My3D2dge in scope.\\nconst BAYER = [0,8,2,10,12,4,14,6,3,11,1,9,15,7,13,5].map(v => (v + .5) / 16 - .5);\\nconst hex3 = h => [parseInt(h.slice(1, 3), 16), parseInt(h.slice(3, 5), 16), parseInt(h.slice(5, 7), 16)];\\nfunction makeRamp(keys, n, slow) { // dark to light through hand-picked, hue-shifted keys. slow: the same ramp seen through Long Breath\\n  const c = keys.map(hex3), out = new Uint32Array(n);\\n  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) { const t = i / (n - 1) * (c.length - 1), j = Math.min(c.length - 2, t | 0), f = t - j; let r = c[j][0] + (c[j + 1][0] - c[j][0]) * f, g = c[j][1] + (c[j + 1][1] - c[j][1]) * f, b = c[j][2] + (c[j + 1][2] - c[j][2]) * f;\\n    if (slow) { const y = r * .3 + g * .59 + b * .11; r = y * .55 + 8; g = y * .95 + 14; b = y * 1.05 + 22; } out[i] = 0xff000000 | (Math.min(255, b) << 16) | (Math.min(255, g) << 8) | Math.min(255, r); }\\n  return out;\\n}\\nconst MAT_KEYS = { // name: [ramp keys, specular strength]\\n  wall: [['#1c060c', '#4a0f18', '#7d1f26', '#a93a38'], .35], bowel: [['#3a0e1c', '#8a2f3e', '#c75f62', '#eb9486', '#ffc9b0'], 1], liver: [['#1e0610', '#4e1022', '#7c1e30', '#a83a42', '#d0645c'], .55],\\n  fat: [['#5a3a10', '#a8782a', '#dcb34c', '#f4d97e', '#fff2b8'], .7], vessel: [['#14103a', '#2c2a70', '#4a4aa0', '#7c78c8'], .8], artery: [['#3a0408', '#8c0f14', '#cf2a22', '#f4604a'], 1],\\n  skin: [['#6a3a22', '#a8683f', '#d39a68', '#eebf92'], .12], drape: [['#0c2c2a', '#14504a', '#1f7468', '#2f9a88'], 0], steel: [['#28303a', '#5a6a78', '#9aaab8', '#e8f0f8'], 1.6],\\n  blood: [['#180204', '#3c060a', '#680c10', '#8e1414'], 1.4], wound: [['#0c0104', '#2a0408', '#55090e', '#7a1216'], .5], char: [['#08070a', '#1c181a', '#332a28', '#4a3c34'], 0], gel: [['#0c4a38', '#1c8a64', '#54d0a0', '#b0ffe0'], 1.4] };\\nconst MAT_IDS = Object.keys(MAT_KEYS), MATS = MAT_IDS.map(k => ({ ramp: makeRamp(MAT_KEYS[k][0], 20), slow: makeRamp(MAT_KEYS[k][0], 20, true), spec: MAT_KEYS[k][1] }));\\nconst matId = n => MAT_IDS.indexOf(n);\\nfunction makeNoise(seed) { let a = seed >>> 0; const R = () => { a = (a + 0x6d2b79f5) >>> 0; let t = Math.imul(a ^ (a >>> 15), a | 1); t ^= t + Math.imul(t ^ (t >>> 7), t | 61); return ((t ^ (t >>> 14)) >>> 0) / 4294967296; };\\n  const L = new Float32Array(65536); for (let i = 0; i < L.length; i++) L[i] = R();\\n  const v = (x, y) => { const xi = Math.floor(x), yi = Math.floor(y), fx = x - xi, fy = y - yi, u = fx * fx * (3 - 2 * fx), w = fy * fy * (3 - 2 * fy), s = (p, q) => L[(p & 255) | ((q & 255) << 8)]; return (s(xi, yi) * (1 - u) + s(xi + 1, yi) * u) * (1 - w) + (s(xi, yi + 1) * (1 - u) + s(xi + 1, yi + 1) * u) * w; };\\n  return { R, fbm: (x, y, o = 4) => { let k = .5, f = 1, n = 0; for (let i = 0; i < o; i++) { n += k * v(x * f, y * f); k *= .5; f *= 2.03; } return n; } }; }\\nclass TissueField {\\n  constructor(W, H, seed = 7) { this.W = W; this.H = H; const N = W * H; this.h = new Float32Array(N); this.m = new Uint8Array(N); this.pulse = new Float32Array(N); this.ao = new Float32Array(N).fill(1); this.dyn = new Float32Array(N); this.over = new Uint8Array(N); this.noise = makeNoise(seed);\\n    this.cv = E.mkCanvas(W, H); this.g = this.cv.getContext('2d'); this.img = this.g.createImageData(W, H); this.u = new Uint32Array(this.img.data.buffer); }\\n  blob(x, y, rx, ry, rot, hgt, mat, o = {}) { const { W, H, h, m, pulse, noise } = this, c = Math.cos(rot), s = Math.sin(rot), R = Math.max(rx, ry) + (o.warp || 0) + 2, id = matId(mat), warp = o.warp || 0, base = o.base || 0, add = !!o.add;\\n    for (let py = Math.max(0, y - R | 0); py < Math.min(H, y + R + 1); py++) for (let px = Math.max(0, x - R | 0); px < Math.min(W, x + R + 1); px++) {\\n      const wx = warp ? (noise.fbm(px * .03, py * .03, 2) - .5) * warp : 0, wy = warp ? (noise.fbm(px * .03 + 40, py * .03, 2) - .5) * warp : 0, dx = px - x + wx, dy = py - y + wy, u = (dx * c + dy * s) / rx, v = (-dx * s + dy * c) / ry, d = u * u + v * v; if (d >= 1) continue;\\n      const i = py * W + px, top = base + hgt * Math.sqrt(1 - d); if (add) { h[i] += hgt * (1 - d); m[i] = id; if (o.pulse) pulse[i] = Math.max(pulse[i], o.pulse * (1 - d)); } else if (top > h[i]) { h[i] = top; m[i] = id; if (o.pulse) pulse[i] = o.pulse * (1 - d); } } }\\n  tube(pts, r, hgt, mat, o = {}) { const step = Math.max(1, r * .35); for (let k = 0; k < pts.length - 1; k++) { const [x0, y0] = pts[k], [x1, y1] = pts[k + 1], n = Math.max(1, Math.hypot(x1 - x0, y1 - y0) / step | 0); for (let i = 0; i <= n; i++) this.blob(x0 + (x1 - x0) * i / n, y0 + (y1 - y0) * i / n, r, r, 0, hgt, mat, o); } }\\n  finish(detail = 1.1) { const { W, H, h, ao, noise } = this, N = W * H, blur = new Float32Array(N), tmp = new Float32Array(N), R = 7;\\n    for (let i = 0, y = 0; y < H; y++) for (let x = 0; x < W; x++, i++) h[i] += (noise.fbm(x * .11, y * .11, 3) - .5) * detail;\\n    for (let y = 0; y < H; y++) { let s = 0; for (let x = -R; x <= R; x++) s += h[y * W + Math.max(0, x)]; for (let x = 0; x < W; x++) { tmp[y * W + x] = s / (2 * R + 1); s += h[y * W + Math.min(W - 1, x + R + 1)] - h[y * W + Math.max(0, x - R)]; } }\\n    for (let x = 0; x < W; x++) { let s = 0; for (let y = -R; y <= R; y++) s += tmp[Math.max(0, y) * W + x]; for (let y = 0; y < H; y++) { blur[y * W + x] = s / (2 * R + 1); s += tmp[Math.min(H - 1, y + R + 1) * W + x] - tmp[Math.max(0, y - R) * W + x]; } }\\n    for (let i = 0; i < N; i++) ao[i] = Math.max(.42, Math.min(1.08, 1 - (blur[i] - h[i]) * .055)); }\\n  // what the sim's objects stamp each frame, before shade(): dynamic height, and an override material\\n  stampDisc(x, y, r, hgt, mat) { const { W, H, dyn, over } = this, id = mat ? matId(mat) + 1 : 0; for (let py = Math.max(1, y - r | 0); py < Math.min(H - 1, y + r + 1); py++) for (let px = Math.max(1, x - r | 0); px < Math.min(W - 1, x + r + 1); px++) { const d = ((px - x) ** 2 + (py - y) ** 2) / (r * r); if (d < 1) { const i = py * W + px; dyn[i] = hgt < 0 ? Math.min(dyn[i], hgt * (1 - d)) : Math.max(dyn[i], hgt * (1 - d)); if (id) over[i] = id; } } }\\n  stampLine(pts, r, hgt, mat) { for (let k = 0; k < pts.length - 1; k++) { const [x0, y0] = pts[k], [x1, y1] = pts[k + 1], n = Math.max(1, Math.hypot(x1 - x0, y1 - y0) / Math.max(1, r * .5) | 0); for (let i = 0; i <= n; i++) this.stampDisc(x0 + (x1 - x0) * i / n, y0 + (y1 - y0) * i / n, r, hgt, mat); } }\\n  // fx: { beat 0..1, breath px, slow: Long Breath palette, lens: {x, y, r} shows the cold palette inside (Sonar), light: [lx, ly], dim: 0..1 darkness, spot: {x, y, r} stays lit while dim }\\n  shade(fx = {}) { const { W, H, h, m, ao, pulse, dyn, over, u } = this, beat = fx.beat || 0, off = Math.round(fx.breath || 0) * W, slow = !!fx.slow, lens = fx.lens, lr2 = lens ? lens.r * lens.r : 0, dim = fx.dim || 0, spot = fx.spot, sr2 = spot ? spot.r * spot.r : 0, lx = fx.light ? fx.light[0] : -.42, ly = fx.light ? fx.light[1] : -.52, lz = Math.sqrt(Math.max(.1, 1 - lx * lx - ly * ly)), hl = Math.hypot(lx, ly, lz + 1), hx = lx / hl, hy = ly / hl, hz = (lz + 1) / hl;\\n    for (let y = 1; y < H - 1; y++) for (let x = 1, i = y * W + 1; x < W - 1; x++, i++) {\\n      const j = Math.max(W + 1, Math.min(W * H - W - 2, i + off)), k = 1 + beat * pulse[j], dx = (h[j - 1] - h[j + 1]) * k * .5 + (dyn[i - 1] - dyn[i + 1]) * .5, dy = (h[j - W] - h[j + W]) * k * .5 + (dyn[i - W] - dyn[i + W]) * .5, nl = 1 / Math.sqrt(dx * dx + dy * dy + 1), nx = dx * nl, ny = dy * nl, nz = nl;\\n      const diff = Math.max(0, nx * lx + ny * ly + nz * lz), sh = Math.max(0, nx * hx + ny * hy + nz * hz), ov = over[i], M = MATS[ov ? ov - 1 : m[j]], cold = slow || (lens && (x - lens.x) ** 2 + (y - lens.y) ** 2 < lr2), r = cold ? M.slow : M.ramp, n = r.length, s2 = sh * sh, s4 = s2 * s2, s8 = s4 * s4, s16 = s8 * s8, s32 = s16 * s16;\\n      const lit = dim && !(spot && (x - spot.x) ** 2 + (y - spot.y) ** 2 < sr2) ? 1 - dim : 1, lum = (diff * .92 + s8 * .16 * M.spec + beat * pulse[j] * .6) * (ov ? 1 : ao[j]) * lit, c = Math.max(0, Math.min(n - 1, (lum * (n - 1) + BAYER[(x & 3) | ((y & 3) << 2)] * 1.15 + .5) | 0));\\n      u[i] = s32 * s8 * M.spec * lit > .42 ? (cold ? 0xffffffe0 : 0xfff4f0ff) : r[c]; }\\n    this.g.putImageData(this.img, 0, 0); dyn.fill(0); over.fill(0); }\\n}\\n```\\n\\n## Appendix B: a site recipe\\n\\n`F.stampDisc` coordinates here use screen pixels. To place sim objects, add `OX, OY`.\\n\\n```js\\n// A site recipe: the abdomen. Design space is 426 x 240 about the screen's centre; a bigger screen only shows more drape.\\nfunction bakeAbdomen(W, H, seed = 11) { const F = new TissueField(W, H, seed), cx = W / 2, cy = H / 2 - 4, n = F.noise, rx = 171, ry = 91;\\n  for (let y = 0; y < H; y++) for (let x = 0; x < W; x++) { const i = y * W + x, d = ((x - cx) / rx) ** 2 + ((y - cy) / ry) ** 2, fold = Math.sin(x * .09 + 1.6 * Math.sin(y * .05)) * 1.6 + Math.sin(y * .17 + x * .03) * .8;\\n    if (d < 1) { F.h[i] = -16 + 12 * d * d + Math.sin(x * .05 + y * .03) * 1.4; F.m[i] = matId('wall'); }            // the cavity\\n    else if (d < 1.28) { const e = (d - 1) / .28; F.h[i] = 4 + 7 * Math.sin(e * Math.PI); F.m[i] = matId('skin'); }  // lips of skin\\n    else { F.h[i] = 6 + fold; F.m[i] = matId('drape'); } }                                                            // drapes to any screen's edge\\n  F.blob(cx - rx * .55, cy - ry * .5, rx * .55, ry * .55, -.3, 17, 'liver', { base: -11, warp: 12 });\\n  for (let i = 0; i < 9; i++) { const a = n.R() * 6.28, r = .55 + n.R() * .32; F.blob(cx + Math.cos(a) * rx * r, cy + Math.sin(a) * ry * r, 9 + n.R() * 14, 7 + n.R() * 9, n.R() * 3, 8, 'fat', { base: -10, warp: 8 }); }\\n  const coil = (x0, y0, len, dir, r) => { const pts = [[x0, y0]]; let a = dir, x = x0, y = y0; for (let i = 0; i < len; i++) { a += (n.R() - .5) * 1.5 + Math.sin(i * .9) * .5; x += Math.cos(a) * r * 1.3; y += Math.sin(a) * r * 1.3; if (((x - cx) / (rx * .92)) ** 2 + ((y - cy) / (ry * .92)) ** 2 > 1) { a += 2.4; x += Math.cos(a) * r; y += Math.sin(a) * r; } pts.push([x, y]); } return pts; };\\n  F.tube(coil(cx + rx * .05, cy - ry * .25, 34, .2, 9), 11, 14, 'bowel', { base: -8 }); F.tube(coil(cx + rx * .45, cy + ry * .25, 26, 2.6, 9), 10, 15, 'bowel', { base: -7 }); F.tube(coil(cx - rx * .35, cy + ry * .45, 22, -.3, 9), 10, 15, 'bowel', { base: -6 });\\n  const veins = (x, y, a, len, r, mat) => { const pts = [[x, y]]; for (let i = 0; i < len; i++) { a += (n.R() - .5) * .8; x += Math.cos(a) * 7; y += Math.sin(a) * 7; if (((x - cx) / rx) ** 2 + ((y - cy) / ry) ** 2 > .96) break; pts.push([x, y]); if (r > 1.2 && n.R() < .22) veins(x, y, a + (n.R() < .5 ? .9 : -.9), len * .5 | 0, r * .7, mat); } F.tube(pts, r, 1.6, mat, { add: true, pulse: mat === 'artery' ? .35 : 0 }); };\\n  veins(cx - rx * .7, cy - ry * .05, -.2, 16, 2.2, 'vessel'); veins(cx + rx * .7, cy - ry * .6, 2.4, 14, 2, 'artery'); veins(cx + rx * .1, cy + ry * .75, -1.5, 12, 1.8, 'vessel');\\n  for (const a of [-.45, .5, 2.7, 3.6]) { const ex = cx + Math.cos(a) * rx * 1.02, ey = cy + Math.sin(a) * ry * 1.02, ox = cx + Math.cos(a) * rx * 1.5, oy = cy + Math.sin(a) * ry * 1.5; F.tube([[ex, ey], [ox, oy]], 4, 5, 'steel', { base: 10 }); F.tube([[ex - Math.sin(a) * 9, ey + Math.cos(a) * 9], [ex + Math.sin(a) * 9, ey - Math.cos(a) * 9]], 3, 5, 'steel', { base: 10 }); } // retractors\\n  F.finish(1.1); return F; }\\n```\\n\\n## Appendix C: portrait painter\\n\\nBusts for the four leads. The other four follow the same shape: `frontHair`, `backHair` (polygons in face space, x from the center, y down), `glasses`, `moustache`, `scar`, `cap`, `mask` (`maskUp: true` covers the mouth, for theatre), `lines`, `blush`, `jaw`, `wide`. Cache each expression to a canvas with `E.mkCanvas(112, 128)`; call `E.px.reset` on the target after drawing on it with the canvas API.\\n\\n```js\\n// A bust from data in hard-edged tones, 112 x 128 buffer pixels. Cache each expression in a canvas; the sim never reads it. Needs E = My3D2dge in scope.\\n// o: { expr: neutral | smile | angry | sad | shock | shout, blink: 0..1, talk: 0..1, look: -1..1 }\\nfunction drawBust(g, c, o = {}) { const px = E.px, P = (pts, col) => px.poly(g, pts, col), T = E.tones;\\n  const skin = T(c.skin), hair = T(c.hair), cloth = T(c.cloth), expr = o.expr || 'neutral', blink = o.blink || 0, talk = o.talk || 0, cx = 56, top = 20;\\n  const jaw = c.jaw || 1, wide = c.wide || 1, f = (x, y) => [cx + x * wide, top + y]; // face space: x from centre, y down\\n  // back hair\\n  if (c.backHair) for (const s of c.backHair) P(s.map(([x, y]) => f(x, y)), hair.sh);\\n  // shoulders and collar\\n  P([[6, 128], [10, 104], [30, 92], [46, 88], [66, 88], [82, 92], [102, 104], [106, 128]], cloth.base);\\n  P([[6, 128], [10, 104], [30, 92], [34, 128]], cloth.sh); P([[78, 128], [82, 92], [102, 104], [106, 128]], cloth.lt);\\n  // neck\\n  P([[46, 66], [66, 66], [68, 92], [56, 104], [44, 92]], skin.sh);\\n  // V-neck and inner shirt\\n  P([[42, 88], [56, 110], [70, 88], [66, 86], [56, 100], [46, 86]], cloth.deep); P([[46, 86], [56, 100], [66, 86], [64, 92], [56, 104], [48, 92]], c.undershirt || skin.sh);\\n  if (c.coat) { P([[6, 128], [10, 104], [30, 92], [42, 88], [52, 128]], c.coat); P([[106, 128], [102, 104], [82, 92], [70, 88], [60, 128]], c.coat); P([[42, 88], [52, 128], [47, 128], [36, 92]], E.tones(c.coat).sh); P([[70, 88], [60, 128], [65, 128], [76, 92]], E.tones(c.coat).sh); }\\n  // ears\\n  P([f(-27, 30), f(-31, 34), f(-30, 42), f(-26, 45)], skin.sh); P([f(27, 30), f(31, 34), f(30, 42), f(26, 45)], skin.base);\\n  // head: wide cranium, cheek, chin\\n  const J = jaw; P([f(-26, 12), f(-27, 34), f(-22, 48 * J), f(-11, 60 * J), f(0, 64 * J), f(11, 60 * J), f(22, 48 * J), f(27, 34), f(26, 12), f(14, 2), f(-14, 2)], skin.base);\\n  P([f(-27, 30), f(-22, 48 * J), f(-11, 60 * J), f(0, 64 * J), f(-6, 54 * J), f(-16, 44), f(-22, 30)], skin.sh); // cheek shadow, viewer's left\\n  if (c.blush) { px.ell(g, ...f(-15, 41), 4, 2, c.blush); px.ell(g, ...f(16, 41), 4, 2, c.blush); }\\n  if (c.lines) { px.line(g, ...f(-11, 45), ...f(-6, 50), skin.sh, 1); px.line(g, ...f(11, 45), ...f(6, 50), skin.sh, 1); px.line(g, ...f(-23, 26), ...f(-20, 28), skin.sh, 1); px.line(g, ...f(23, 26), ...f(20, 28), skin.sh, 1); }\\n  // eyes\\n  const eye = (sx) => { const x = sx * 13, y = 30 + (expr === 'shock' ? -1 : 0), w = 7, hh = expr === 'shock' ? 8 : expr === 'angry' ? 5 : 7;\\n    if (blink > .5 || expr === 'sad-closed') { px.line(g, ...f(x - w, y + 1), ...f(x + w, y + 1), c.lash || '#1a1016', 2); return; }\\n    P([f(x - w, y), f(x - w + 3, y - hh + 2), f(x + w - 3, y - hh + 2), f(x + w, y), f(x + w - 2, y + 4), f(x - w + 2, y + 4)], '#f8f4ee');\\n    const ix = x + (o.look || 0) * 2 + (expr === 'shock' ? 0 : 0); px.ell(g, ...f(ix, y - 1), 4, expr === 'shock' ? 4 : 5, c.iris || '#3a5a7a'); px.ell(g, ...f(ix, y), 2, 3, c.pupil || '#120c14'); px.rect(g, ...f(ix - 3, y - 4), 3, 2, '#ffffff'); px.dot(g, ...f(ix + 2, y + 2), '#ffffff');\\n    px.line(g, ...f(x - w - 1, y + (sx < 0 ? 1 : -1)), ...f(x - w + 4, y - hh + 2), c.lash || '#1a1016', 2); px.line(g, ...f(x - w + 4, y - hh + 2), ...f(x + w - 4, y - hh + 2), c.lash || '#1a1016', 2); px.line(g, ...f(x + w - 4, y - hh + 2), ...f(x + w + 1, y + (sx < 0 ? -1 : 1)), c.lash || '#1a1016', 2); };\\n  eye(-1); eye(1);\\n  // brows\\n  const by = 17 + (c.browDrop || 0), br = expr === 'angry' ? 3 : expr === 'sad' ? -3 : expr === 'shock' ? -1 : 0, bc = c.brow || hair.deep, bw = c.browW || 2; px.line(g, ...f(-20, by + (expr === 'shock' ? -3 : 0) - br), ...f(-7, by + (expr === 'shock' ? -3 : 0) + br), bc, bw); px.line(g, ...f(7, by + (expr === 'shock' ? -3 : 0) + br), ...f(20, by + (expr === 'shock' ? -3 : 0) - br), bc, bw);\\n  // nose\\n  px.line(g, ...f(1, 40), ...f(-1, 45), skin.sh, 1); px.dot(g, ...f(0, 46), skin.deep);\\n  // mouth\\n  const my = 53, mo = talk > .5 || expr === 'shout' || expr === 'shock'; if (mo) { const a = expr === 'shout' || expr === 'shock' ? 7 : 4, w = expr === 'shout' ? 7 : 5; P([f(-w, my - 1), f(w, my - 1), f(w - 2, my + a), f(-w + 2, my + a)], '#5a1a24'); P([f(-w + 2, my + a - 2), f(w - 2, my + a - 2), f(w - 3, my + a), f(-w + 3, my + a)], '#c8505a'); if (a > 4) px.rect(g, ...f(-w + 1, my - 1), (w - 1) * 2, 2, '#f4f0e8'); }\\n  else if (expr === 'smile') { px.line(g, ...f(-7, my - 1), ...f(-3, my + 2), skin.deep, 1); px.line(g, ...f(-3, my + 2), ...f(3, my + 2), skin.deep, 1); px.line(g, ...f(3, my + 2), ...f(7, my - 1), skin.deep, 1); }\\n  else if (expr === 'sad' || expr === 'angry') { px.line(g, ...f(-5, my + 2), ...f(0, my), skin.deep, 1); px.line(g, ...f(0, my), ...f(5, my + 2), skin.deep, 1); }\\n  else px.line(g, ...f(-4, my), ...f(4, my), skin.deep, 1);\\n  if (c.moustache) { P([f(-13, 50), f(-6, 46), f(0, 48), f(6, 46), f(13, 50), f(8, 52), f(0, 50), f(-8, 52)], c.moustache); px.line(g, ...f(-6, 47), ...f(6, 47), E.tones(c.moustache).lt, 1); }\\n  // front hair\\n  if (c.frontHair) for (const [s, tone] of c.frontHair) P(s.map(([x, y]) => f(x, y)), hair[tone || 'base']);\\n  if (c.hairGlint) for (const s of c.hairGlint) P(s.map(([x, y]) => f(x, y)), hair.hi);\\n  // cap\\n  if (c.cap) { const k = T(c.cap); P([f(-29, 16), f(-27, 2), f(-14, -9), f(14, -9), f(27, 2), f(29, 16), f(16, 11), f(-16, 11)], k.base); P([f(-29, 16), f(-27, 2), f(-14, -9), f(-6, -9), f(-14, 11), f(-16, 11)], k.sh); P([f(6, -8), f(14, -9), f(22, -3), f(14, -3)], k.lt); P([f(-29, 16), f(-16, 11), f(16, 11), f(29, 16), f(27, 18), f(16, 14), f(-16, 14), f(-27, 18)], k.deep); }\\n  // lowered mask, glasses\\n  if (c.mask && !o.maskUp) { const k = T(c.mask); P([f(-20, 62), f(20, 62), f(25, 76), f(0, 84), f(-25, 76)], k.base); P([f(-20, 62), f(-25, 76), f(0, 84), f(-6, 74)], k.sh); px.line(g, ...f(-20, 62), ...f(-28, 50), k.lt, 1); px.line(g, ...f(20, 62), ...f(28, 50), k.lt, 1); }\\n  if (c.glasses) { const m = c.glasses; for (const sx of [-1, 1]) { const x = sx * 13; px.line(g, ...f(x - 9, 25), ...f(x + 9, 25), m, 1); px.line(g, ...f(x - 9, 25), ...f(x - 8, 33), m, 1); px.line(g, ...f(x + 9, 25), ...f(x + 8, 33), m, 1); px.line(g, ...f(x - 8, 34), ...f(x + 8, 34), m, 1); } px.line(g, ...f(-4, 27), ...f(4, 27), m, 1); }\\n  if (o.maskUp && c.mask) { const k = T(c.mask); P([f(-27, 36), f(-8, 34), f(0, 36), f(8, 34), f(27, 36), f(25, 54), f(11, 66), f(0, 68), f(-11, 66), f(-25, 54)], k.base); P([f(-27, 36), f(-8, 34), f(-3, 50), f(-11, 66), f(-25, 54)], k.sh); for (const yy of [44, 52]) px.line(g, ...f(-18, yy), ...f(18, yy), k.lt, 1); px.line(g, ...f(-27, 38), ...f(-31, 32), k.lt, 1); px.line(g, ...f(27, 38), ...f(31, 32), k.lt, 1); }\\n  if (c.scar) px.line(g, ...f(c.scar[0], c.scar[1]), ...f(c.scar[2], c.scar[3]), skin.deep, 1);\\n  if (c.tag) { px.rect(g, 72, 108, 16, 10, '#f0ece2'); px.rect(g, 74, 110, 12, 2, c.tag); px.rect(g, 74, 114, 8, 1, '#7a7468'); }\\n}\\nconst CAST = {\\n  vale: { name: 'DR. VALE', skin: '#f6d0b2', hair: '#c9562a', iris: '#3f8a58', cloth: '#2f9c8e', cap: '#2bb0a2', mask: '#bfe6dc', undershirt: '#e9fff6', blush: '#f0a48c', wide: .95,\\n    backHair: [[[-27, 14], [-30, 40], [-24, 52], [-16, 40], [-16, 14]], [[27, 14], [30, 40], [24, 52], [16, 40], [16, 14]]],\\n    frontHair: [[[[-27, 12], [-24, 24], [-16, 16], [-10, 22], [-3, 14], [3, 21], [10, 14], [18, 22], [27, 12], [14, 8], [-14, 8]], 'base'], [[[-27, 12], [-24, 24], [-16, 16], [-14, 10]], 'sh']],\\n    tag: '#c8452f' },\\n  quill: { name: 'NURSE QUILL', skin: '#dcae8e', hair: '#8d8a88', iris: '#4a4a55', cloth: '#3d62a6', cap: '#35558f', undershirt: '#dfe6f2', moustache: '#a7a3a0', lines: true, wide: 1.08, jaw: 1.04, browW: 3, brow: '#7c7875', browDrop: 2, mask: '#a9cfe0', frontHair: [] },\\n  oyelaran: { name: 'CHIEF OYELARAN', skin: '#7c4a2e', hair: '#c9c5bb', iris: '#2a1a12', cloth: '#2e2c36', coat: '#ece7da', undershirt: '#3a3644', lines: true, jaw: .98, glasses: '#d8c070',\\n    backHair: [], frontHair: [[[[-27, 18], [-27, 2], [-14, -8], [14, -8], [27, 2], [27, 18], [20, 10], [8, 6], [-8, 6], [-20, 10]], 'base'], [[[-27, 18], [-27, 2], [-14, -8], [-8, 6], [-20, 10]], 'sh'], [[[6, -7], [16, -6], [22, 0], [12, 0]], 'lt']] },\\n  sokolova: { name: 'DR. SOKOLOVA', skin: '#e8c6aa', hair: '#dedad2', iris: '#6a8aa8', cloth: '#6b7a48', undershirt: '#f0ece0', lines: true, wide: 1.02, jaw: 1.02, scar: [10, 14, 17, 40], browW: 3, brow: '#b8b4ac',\\n    backHair: [[[-28, 6], [-34, 34], [-30, 58], [-20, 50], [-20, 10]], [[28, 6], [36, 26], [46, 52], [38, 64], [28, 44], [22, 10]]], frontHair: [[[[-27, 16], [-26, 2], [-12, -8], [14, -8], [27, 2], [27, 16], [16, 6], [-2, 12], [-16, 8]], 'base'], [[[-27, 16], [-26, 2], [-12, -8], [-6, 8], [-16, 8]], 'sh']] },\\n};\\n```\\n\\n## Appendix D: Hub skeleton, page head and harness runner\\n\\n**Page head.** Order matters.\\n\\n```html\\n<!doctype html><html lang=\\\"en\\\"><head><meta charset=\\\"utf-8\\\">\\n<script>/* vendor/hub/session.js, pasted unmodified */</script>\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session\\\" content=\\\"1\\\"><meta name=\\\"hub:session-players\\\" content=\\\"1-4\\\"><meta name=\\\"hub:session-mode\\\" content=\\\"shared-world\\\"><meta name=\\\"hub:session-minutes\\\" content=\\\"5\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-goal\\\" content=\\\"Save the patient together; best hands top the board\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-howto\\\" content=\\\"Aim: right stick or mouse. Use tool: R2 or click. Tools: left stick or 1-8\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"viewport\\\" content=\\\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1, viewport-fit=cover\\\"><title>…</title>\\n<style>html,body{margin:0;height:100%;background:#000;overflow:hidden}#screen{width:100%;height:100%;display:block;image-rendering:pixelated;touch-action:none}html .esh-row small{display:none}html .esh-row:focus-within small{display:block}</style></head>\\n<body><!-- E.bootSplash's markup here --><canvas id=\\\"screen\\\"></canvas><script>/* engine */</script><script>/* game */</script></body></html>\\n```\\n\\n**Skeleton.** A complete shared-world game on the engine. It passed all four harness runs and runs standalone. Replace the dots with the real sim, keeping its structure: connect, the event handlers, the host's step, the 12/s snapshot and the mirror. `HUB.send` for a real event batch is reliable by default.\\n\\n```js\\n// Hub skeleton: shared world on my-3D2dge. The host's copy runs the world; other copies mirror it and send their own hands.\\n(() => {\\nconst E = My3D2dge, HUB = window.AIHub, inSession = !!(HUB && HUB.active), { px } = E;\\nconst { attack, dash, skill, jump, ...base } = E.Input.DEFAULT;\\nconst game = window.G = new E.Game({ canvas: 'screen', res: [426, 240], expand: true, view: 'overhead', touch: 'never', live: inSession, autoPause: !inSession, corner: !inSession,\\n  input: { ...base, useTool: ['Pad7', 'Pad0', 'Mouse0', 'Space'], more: ['Pad3', 'KeyM'] }, menu: { quit: inSession ? false : 'title', settings: [{ key: 'music', value: .1 }, { key: 'glyphs', value: 'playstation', options: [['playstation', 'PlayStation'], ['xbox', 'Xbox']] }] } });\\nconst NAME = 'SKELETON', W0 = 426, H0 = 240, DOTS = 12;\\nlet info = null, phase = 'wait', card = null, over = false, sent = 0, heardSeq = 0, S = { v: 70, dots: [], p: {} }, mine = { x: 200, y: 120, t: 0 }, rng = null;\\nconst place = () => ({ x: 40 + rng() * 340, y: 40 + rng() * 160 });\\nfunction hostInit() { rng = HUB.rng('deal'); S.dots = Array.from({ length: DOTS }, (_, id) => ({ id, ...place(), alive: 1 })); for (const q of info.players) S.p[q.id] = { x: 200, y: 120, t: 0, score: 0, gone: false }; }\\nfunction finish(won) { if (over) return; over = true; if (HUB.server !== true && info.me != null && S.p[info.me]) Object.assign(S.p[info.me], mine, { score: S.p[info.me].score });\\n  const live = info.players.filter(q => !S.p[q.id].gone).sort((a, b) => S.p[b.id].score - S.p[a.id].score), standings = [];\\n  for (const q of live) { const last = standings[standings.length - 1]; if (last && S.p[last[0]].score === S.p[q.id].score) last.push(q.id); else standings.push([q.id]); }\\n  const scores = Object.fromEntries(info.players.map(q => [q.id, S.p[q.id].score]));\\n  HUB.send({ t: 'over', v: S.v }); HUB.finish({ standings, scores, won, awards: live.length ? [{ id: live[0].id, title: 'Steadiest Hand', detail: scores[live[0].id] + ' clean actions' }] : [] }); }\\nasync function connect() { info = await HUB.connect({ mode: 'shared-world', seats: [1, 4], card: true });\\n  if (!info) { phase = 'solo'; return; }\\n  HUB.on('card', c => { card = c; });\\n  HUB.on('start', () => { phase = 'count'; });\\n  HUB.on('message', (d, from) => { if (info.isHost) { const p = S.p[from]; if (d.t === 'i' && p && d.n > (p.n || 0)) { p.n = d.n; p.x = d.x; p.y = d.y; p.t = d.t2; } } else if (from === info.host) { if (d.t === 's' && d.n > heardSeq) { heardSeq = d.n; S.v = d.v; S.dots = d.o.map(([id, x, y]) => ({ id, x, y, alive: 1 })); for (const [id, x, y, t, sc] of d.p) { const q = S.p[id] || (S.p[id] = {}); if (id !== info.me) { q.x = x; q.y = y; q.t = t; } q.score = sc; } } if (d.t === 'over') over = true; } });\\n  HUB.on('leave', ({ id }) => { if (S.p[id]) S.p[id].gone = true; });\\n  HUB.on('time-up', () => { if (info.isHost) finish(false); });\\n  HUB.on('end', () => { phase = 'over'; game.audio.music(null); game.audio.mute(true); });\\n  if (info.isHost) hostInit();\\n  HUB.ready(); }\\nfunction step(dt) { const I = game.input, m = I.move(), a = I.padAim, ptr = I.pointer(); let dx = (a ? a[0] : 0) + m[0], dy = (a ? a[1] : 0) + m[1]; mine.x = E.clamp(mine.x + dx * 300 * dt, 0, W0); mine.y = E.clamp(mine.y + dy * 300 * dt, 0, H0); if (ptr && I.lastDevice === 'mouse') { mine.x = ptr[0]; mine.y = ptr[1]; } mine.t = I.value('useTool'); }\\nlet snapN = 0, inN = 0, acc = 0;\\nfunction hostStep(dt) { S.v = Math.max(0, S.v - .05 * dt); if (info.me != null && S.p[info.me] && !info.server) Object.assign(S.p[info.me], mine, { score: S.p[info.me].score });\\n  for (const id in S.p) { const p = S.p[id]; if (p.gone || !p.t) continue; for (const d of S.dots) if (d.alive && Math.hypot(d.x - p.x, d.y - p.y) < 12) { d.alive = 0; p.score++; } } S.dots = S.dots.filter(d => d.alive); if (!S.dots.length) finish(true); }\\ngame.start({ scene: 'hub', scenes: { hub: { pausable: true, camera: { zoom: false, wheel: false },\\n  update(dt) { const I = game.input; game.focus(game.W / 2, game.H / 2, 0);\\n    if (!info && !inSession) return;\\n    if (phase === 'wait' && info) { if (I.pressed('confirm')) HUB.answer('ready'); if (I.pressed('more')) HUB.answer('more'); const c = I.clicked(); if (c) HUB.answer(c[1] > game.H * .7 ? 'more' : 'ready'); }\\n    if (phase === 'count' && HUB.time() >= 0) phase = 'play';\\n    if (phase === 'play' && !over) { step(dt); if (info.isHost) hostStep(dt); acc += dt; if (acc >= 1 / 12) { acc = 0; if (info.isHost) HUB.send({ t: 's', n: ++snapN, v: Math.round(S.v), o: S.dots.map(d => [d.id, d.x | 0, d.y | 0]), p: info.players.filter(q => !S.p[q.id].gone).map(q => { const p = S.p[q.id]; return [q.id, p.x | 0, p.y | 0, p.t > .25 ? 1 : 0, p.score | 0]; }) }, { reliable: false }); else HUB.send({ t: 'i', n: ++inN, x: mine.x | 0, y: mine.y | 0, t2: mine.t > .25 ? 1 : 0 }, { to: info.host, reliable: false }); } } },\\n  draw(r) { if (info && info.server) return; r.sky(['#143a36', '#14504a']); r.overlay(g => { E.font.text(g, NAME + ' ' + phase + (card && card.left != null ? ' ' + Math.ceil(card.left / 1000) : ''), 8, 8, '#fff', { outline: false }); for (const d of S.dots) px.disc(g, d.x, d.y, 4, '#5ff0e0'); if (info) for (const q of info.players) { const p = q.id === info.me ? mine : S.p[q.id]; if (p && !(S.p[q.id] || {}).gone) { px.disc(g, p.x, p.y, 5, q.color); E.font.text(g, q.name.slice(0, 12), p.x + 7, p.y - 4, q.color, { outline: false }); } } }); } } } });\\nwindow.__hubProbe = () => ({ phase, over, dots: S.dots.length, v: S.v, t: HUB.active ? HUB.time() : 0 });\\nconnect();\\n})();\\n```\\n\\n**Harness runner.** The folder needs the game (as `game.html`) and copies of `harness.html`, `bridge.js`, `round.js`. It uses an OS-picked port, checks the frame's title is your game (replace the print with a test), and stops only its own server, by handle.\\n\\n```python\\nimport json, os, re, subprocess, sys, time\\nfrom playwright.sync_api import sync_playwright\\nfolder = os.path.abspath(sys.argv[1])\\nsrv = subprocess.Popen([sys.executable, '-u', '-m', 'http.server', '0', '--bind', '127.0.0.1'], cwd=folder, stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.STDOUT, text=True)\\ntry:\\n    line = srv.stdout.readline(); port = int(re.search(r'port (\\\\d+)', line).group(1))\\n    base = f'http://127.0.0.1:{port}/'\\n    RUNS = ['players=2&auto=1', 'players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1', 'players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&scenario=host-leave&auto=1', 'players=2&scenario=server&auto=1']\\n    with sync_playwright() as p:\\n        b = p.chromium.launch(args=['--autoplay-policy=no-user-gesture-required'])\\n        for q in RUNS:\\n            pg = b.new_page(viewport={'width': 1280, 'height': 720}); pg.goto(base + 'harness.html?game=game.html&' + q)\\n            try: pg.wait_for_function(\\\"() => document.body.dataset.complete === 'true'\\\", timeout=90000)\\n            except Exception as e: print('TIMEOUT', q)\\n            r = pg.evaluate(\\\"() => { const h = window.__hubCheck; return h ? { pass: h.pass, failed: h.checks.filter(c => !c.pass).map(c => c.name + ': ' + c.detail), n: h.checks.length } : null }\\\")\\n            title = pg.evaluate(\\\"() => { const f = document.querySelector('iframe'); try { return f && f.contentDocument && f.contentDocument.title } catch (e) { return 'x' } }\\\")\\n            print(q, json.dumps(r)[:700], 'title:', title); pg.close()\\n        pg = b.new_page(viewport={'width': 1280, 'height': 720}); errs = []; pg.on('pageerror', lambda e: errs.append(str(e))); pg.goto(base + 'game.html'); pg.wait_for_timeout(1200); print('standalone', pg.evaluate(\\\"() => ({ active: AIHub.active, probe: window.__hubProbe() })\\\"), errs[:3]); pg.close()\\n        b.close()\\nfinally:\\n    srv.terminate(); srv.wait(timeout=5)\\n```\\n\\n## Appendix E: gamepad stub and touch-notice patch\\n\\n**A DualSense in the standard mapping, for Playwright's `add_init_script`.** Set `__pad.axes[0..3]` (left x, left y, right x, right y), `__pad.buttons[i].value` and `.pressed`: 0 ✕, 1 ○, 2 □, 3 △, 4 L1, 5 R1, 6 L2, 7 R2, 8 Create, 9 Options, 12 to 15 D-pad, and bump `__pad.timestamp`.\\n\\n```js\\n(() => { const axes = [0, 0, 0, 0], buttons = Array.from({ length: 18 }, () => ({ pressed: false, touched: false, value: 0 }));\\n  const pad = { id: 'DualSense Wireless Controller (STANDARD GAMEPAD Vendor: 054c Product: 0ce6)', index: 0, connected: true, mapping: 'standard', timestamp: 0, axes, buttons,\\n    vibrationActuator: { playEffect() { return Promise.resolve('complete'); }, reset() { return Promise.resolve('complete'); } } };\\n  window.__pad = pad; navigator.getGamepads = () => [pad, null, null, null]; })();\\n```\\n\\n**Touch notice.** In your copy of the engine, `Game.prototype._needSync` sets the notice's `style.cssText` to a full-screen dark layer. Change that one string, so the reel shows above and the notice sits at the bottom. *Verified* on an iPhone profile: the notice sits in the lower half, the canvas above it is clear, and a tap on Settings opens the settings:\\n\\n```js\\nd.style.cssText = 'position:absolute;left:0;right:0;bottom:0;z-index:20;display:flex;align-items:flex-end;justify-content:center;padding:48px 24px 24px;text-align:center;pointer-events:none;background:linear-gradient(transparent,rgba(5,3,12,.92) 45%);color:#f4f0ff;font:600 17px/1.45 system-ui,sans-serif';\\n// and on the button, add pointer-events:auto to its inline style\\n```\\n\\nNote it in your Engine Feedback.\\n\",\"total_lines\":1339}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa37-f7cf-7001-9198-38ad04ed0f5e","uuid":"01349071-1cb4-4da1-87c8-a114cc009f72"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_3","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"This is a massive game-building objective.\n\nThe plan must stay outcome-based. It cannot dictate file layout, class names, or exact signatures. Acceptance criteria should be small and gating. Visual checks will anchor on static structure, unit tests for pure logic, and browser script loading rather than end-to-end playthroughs. A launch check for the entry point is required. Mechanics will be grouped into a tight criteria set.\n\nSTAGE.md directs building the game described in PLAN.md. TASK.md specifies file names and hand-in requirements. Progress must be tracked in PROGRESS.md. The plan is a narrow contract for observable outcomes, not a restatement of the full architecture.\n\nThe plan must not reproduce PLAN.md section 19's detailed architecture. TASK.md and PLAN.md section 22 provide the concrete done checklist. Acceptance criteria will anchor on observable deliverables, beginning with the hand-in files.\n\nThe HTML filename is not yet fixed because the title is chosen by random selection from fifty brainstormed options. The acceptance criterion will instead require the filename to match TASK.md's pattern, which embeds the game title and version string.\n\nTASK.md specifies a single HTML file and a matching subfolder. SOURCE is fixed as \"Trauma Center: Second Opinion\". The plan will list the game mechanics from PLAN.md as the authority.\n\nThe game mechanics are now defined. It is a first-person surgery simulation with time limits and vitals that fail the operation if they reach zero or time expires. Players select tools from a radial menu using a nunchuk stick. Available tools include drains, lasers, forceps, sutures, stabilizer injections, antibiotic gel, and scalpels. A healing touch mechanic requires drawing a star. Two doctors have distinct abilities. GUILT parasite bosses appear during operations. The story remains melodramatic between cases. Difficulty levels are Easy, Normal, and Hard.\n\nThe deliverable must be an original game based on PLAN.md, not a faithful Trauma Center clone. The hard originality rule forbids using Caduceus names, Healing Touch, or GUILT. The named thing is the game PLAN.md describes, inspired by Trauma Center. The classic-name test applies only when the objective names a classic.\n\nThe criteria will be grouped into a tight set of three to five items. The target is not a hard ceiling but a practical cap; every core mechanic must be explicitly listed so nothing is silently omitted.\n\nThe first acceptance criterion is a single playable surgical vertical slice. It must include all eight tools with distinct gestures, vitals and clock mechanics, grades and ranks, both doctor gifts, Code Blue, the eleven specified cases including HUSH strains and the finale, story between cases, and a walkable ward.\n\nThe second criterion covers cross-cutting product requirements from TASK.md. These include a controller-first ten-foot UI with glyphs, an attract mode, a dev mode with live sliders, a touch notice, synthesized audio at ten percent music, gibberish voices, and the single HTML file served via file://.\n\nThe third criterion requires multiplayer support. This includes couch co-op Grand Rounds one through four and an AI Hub shared-world protocol across the four harness scenarios.\n\nThe fourth criterion specifies the hand-in artifacts. The deliverable must contain the HTML file, the matching dev folder, an exit-interview.json, an About screen with source information, PROGRESS.md, no TODOs or stubs, and a test command.\n\nThe plan must avoid a single holistic end-to-end gate. It will be decomposed into separate checks.\n\nThe surgical simulation will be validated by unit tests on the real simulator from its start state, not by full playthroughs alone.\n\nContent completeness will be checked through structural and scripted playthroughs covering all cases, story, ward, and originality constraints.\n\nShell UX will be verified separately for attract mode, dev mode, glyphs, touch notice, audio, and the ten-foot controller-first interface.\n\nThe verification plan will use real in-repo tests driving shipped functions. Playwright is available, so the launch check will be a live browser test: serve the file, load the page, confirm zero errors, verify the render surface is substantially filled, and ensure input produces visible change. Captured output will be stored under {SCRATCH}. An honest fallback is reserved if the launcher cannot run.\n\nThe deliverable must run from disk without CDNs or ES modules that break file://. The single HTML file is the sole entry point. PLAN section 20 specifies a Node test runner at `tests/run.mjs` that prints only failures and a one-line count. The verification plan will require this command as a gating step.\n\nThe verification plan will require the repository's single test command to run from disk. It must print only failures and a one-line count of passed checks, with output captured under {SCRATCH}. The tests must exercise the shipped operation simulator directly, covering tools, cursor behavior, vitals, gifts, strains, determinism, and a full playthrough.\n\nThe browser launch check will be a single gating step: load the HTML from disk in Playwright Chromium, and confirm it runs for thirty seconds across all three engines with zero page errors and a filled render surface. The multiplayer Hub harness must pass four runs. Verification steps may exceed the five criteria.\n\nThe scope is enormous. PLAN section 21 reserves the right to cut content from the end if runway runs out, but it explicitly protects the cursor, all eight tools, both gifts, the finale, Grand Rounds, Hub tests, attract mode, dev mode, the ten-foot UI, About screen, and exit interview. The objective requires completing everything in PLAN.md that can be done on this computer, so the plan must not authorize cutting any of these core elements.\n\nThe implementation approach will treat PLAN.md as the authoritative design specification. It will suggest a test-friendly structure and data-driven case architecture without prescribing module or file names. This keeps the plan from dictating layout while still guiding implementation.\n\nThe implementation approach will keep the operation simulator as pure data so tests can step it without a browser. It will also suggest separating simulation logic from rendering without naming files, and bundling everything into one HTML file.\n\nThe plan will be compressed into an eight-step checklist ordered by PLAN milestones. The harness will mine the first unchecked box as the next task. The first step is title selection per TASK.md. PROGRESS.md must be present because STAGE.md requires it. Hand-in artifacts belong in the work folder root, a dev subfolder, and the game itself.\n\nThe HTML filename must follow the pattern `[GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-YYYYMMDD-HHMMSS].html`, where SOURCE is \"Trauma Center: Second Opinion\". The exact slug for the title and date should be stated clearly so verifiers can check it.\n\nThe exact filename template is ambiguous. It is unclear whether spaces and colons from the title and source string should be preserved or replaced.\n\nThe filename template will join the chosen title, source name, and literal suffix with illegal characters replaced so the file can exist on disk. The same string will be used for the dev subfolder and the HTML deliverable in the work folder root. That root is the folder containing STAGE.md.\n\nThe About screen must contain the same fields as the exit interview. A criterion will ban forbidden names and allow verification by grep. The first acceptance criterion is atomic: a single self-contained HTML file and a dev subfolder must exist in the work folder root, named from the TASK.md template with a playful title chosen by brainstorming fifty titles and selecting one via a real random number generator whose seed is recorded for reproducibility.\n\nThe deliverable must run from disk without network access. The exit-interview.json must parse as JSON in TASK.md's exact shape with every value filled, including usage set to \"added on import\". PROGRESS.md must exist. The in-game About screen must display Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback, and Prompt Feedback in TASK.md's field order and match the JSON. No TODOs, FIXMEs, placeholders, or stubs may remain in the game source.\n\nThe surgical action loop must be validated by direct use of the shipped simulator. It must support all eight tools with their gestures and failure modes, including forceps tears, scalpel slips, suture, salve, syringe IV and targeted injections, cautery heat and lock, suction, and sonar reveal. Vitals and countdown must lose the patient at zero. Code Blue must trigger once per case. Each action must yield a grade and contribute to the final case rank. Long Breath must slow world time while hands stay full speed. Second Look must restore exact state plus ghosts.\n\nThe surgical action loop must use the original cast and disease names, not the source’s proper names, gesture-drawn Healing Touch, or GUILT.\n\nA scripted playthrough from New Game through the ending and credits must run the story, the walkable ward, and every case in PLAN.md sections 9–11 on Resident with cheats off. The bot drives real input. Each case introduces its new mechanic.\n\nLightbox second-opinion, Skills Lab, and the three difficulty tiers must be reachable. Intern and Resident trigger Code Blue; Attending does not. Case content is data-driven, so a short note in the dev folder explains how to add a case without editing the simulator.\n\nThe 10-foot shell, audio, attract mode, and dev mode must work for pad, keyboard, and mouse. Prompts follow the last device via the engine's label API: PlayStation glyphs by default, Xbox from Settings, key or mouse labels when those were used last.\n\nMusic volume defaults to 0.1. All audio is synthesized; no external files are used, and voices are gibberish.\n\nAfter ten seconds of idle time on the title screen, menus, case files, or lobby, a looping AI attract reel of real scenes begins. Any input during this reel returns to the title and is consumed.\n\nDev mode opens from the pause menu with live tuning sliders and a reset-to-defaults button. It cannot be accessed inside a Hub session.\n\nTouch-only mode displays \"Controller or keyboard required\" alongside Settings, with no touch controls present. The attract reel still runs in this mode.\n\nA fifth criterion covers multiplayer. Grand Rounds must run for one to four players on a single couch using local pads or AI colleagues, and in an AI Hub shared-world session where the host runs the simulator and clients mirror it. Each player reports standings after finishing. The four harness scenarios in PLAN.md section 20 and Appendix D must pass with `window.__hubCheck.pass === true`, and the page must also run outside any session.\n\nThe five criteria now group the defining mechanics: package and hand-in, surgical action loop, vertical slice content, ten-foot shell with audio and dev mode, and multiplayer.\n\nSkills Lab and Lightbox are not treated as defining mechanics because PLAN.md explicitly allows cutting them if runway runs out.\n\nLightbox is a named mechanic of \"Second Opinion\" and will remain in criterion 3. Cuttable items are listed under Risks as an implementer contingency, but they do not relax the acceptance criteria unless the run is actually out of time. Verifiers will fail the goal if Skills Lab is missing.\n\nA single test command will load the shipped simulator and assert surgical action loop and vertical slice behaviors. Output must be captured to a scratch log. Only failures and a one-line count are printed, and the count must read zero.\n\nThe test suite must exercise every tool gesture, cursor mode, wheel direction, vitals and countdown states, grades, each strain’s end and failure branch, Long Breath timing, Second Look deep-equal restore, determinism with and without draw, content IDs, the full New Game through credits playthrough, attract idle and exit behavior, dev sliders and reset, and Grand Rounds with one to four bots including retractor solo and duo. The simulator must not be mocked and no passes must be hardcoded.\n\nThe gating step requires launching the real HTML from file:// with Playwright at least twice.\n\nThe Playwright launch must assert zero page errors. The canvas backing size must match the engine’s expanded buffer for that window. The painted area must be substantially filled. A driven input such as start or confirm must visibly change state. Screenshot output goes to scratch. The test repeats for consistency. Chromium, Firefox, and WebKit each run for thirty seconds with zero page errors. Present but empty content is insufficient.\n\nThe primary observable for the Playwright launch is the title screen displaying the game title and a start prompt, followed by a visible transition to the menu or difficulty selection after input. The render surface must draw at its intended target dimensions and appear substantially filled.\n\nThe Hub harness test requires capturing {SCRATCH}/hub.log with pass true for each of the four scenarios. The standalone page must confirm AIHub.active is falsy while the game still runs, and the served page title must match this game.\n\nAudio must be validated via OfflineAudioContext or AnalyserNode to confirm effects, voices, and choir are audible while music stays well below effects at the default 0.1 level. This check can be folded into the test command rather than run as a separate flaky browser step.\n\nScreenshots of sites, portraits, strains, the wheel, and Long Breath at 1920x1080 must be captured as evidence.\n\nA final gating step parses exit-interview.json and compares it to About data. The game source is grepped for forbidden names and TODO or stub markers. Filename patterns, PROGRESS.md, and the absence of audio files in the package are also verified.\n\nForbidden names will be listed explicitly in the criterion to make the check unambiguous. The multiplayer criterion should briefly note that session.js must remain unmodified as the first script under the Hub protocol. Browser testing for touch and pad modes will use a gamepad stub with a phone profile. I am also checking whether the scripted full-game playthrough in criterion 3 violates the instruction against writing a single holistic end-to-end gate.\n\nThe full playthrough will be decomposed into separate atomic checks rather than a single holistic gate. PLAN requires this test, and the instruction demands driving real shipped functions.\n\nSim mechanics will be checked from a fresh operation state rather than only through the full game. Content reachability will be verified per case: each case must be startable from its own definition and finishable by the bot, not just as part of one long chain. The full story playthrough may remain as a verification step, but it must stay fast by avoiding drawn sequences. A story-graph validator will confirm every node is reachable.\n\nThe five criteria are now numbered and titled. Criterion 1 covers the hand-in package, exit interview, About data, progress file, stub removal, and the file:// page. Criterion 2 validates operation outcomes from a fresh state. Criterion 3 requires every case and strain to finish under the bot from its own start, plus story graph and ward existence and originality. Criterion 4 covers the ten-foot shell: input prompts, attract reel, dev mode, touch notice, and audio synthesis. Criterion 5 is still being drafted.\n\nCriterion 3 is now phrased as independently checkable per-case clears from each case’s own data. The full playthrough remains a verification step, not the sole criterion, because PLAN section 22 still requires it.\n\nOperation state will be JSON-safe and stepped by a pure function so tests can run without a browser. Presentation code reads events but never mutates simulation state. The full playthrough is gated inside the single test command. The bundle must be one HTML file that loads from file://. Design numbers follow PLAN.md; if the engine API conflicts with PLAN section 3, the cloned engine docs win.\n\nThe task checklist is now a six-item sequence. The first item covers title selection, folder setup, engine and hub cloning, and PROGRESS.md. The second item covers the simulator core, cursor, wheel, eight tools, vitals, grades, HUD, Case 1, and bot tests. The third item covers the shell: title, menus, settings, glyphs, pause, TV shell, attract, dev mode, touch notice, story, voices, and music. The fourth item covers the remaining tools, Code Blue, Long Breath, Lightbox, and Cases 2–3. The fifth item covers Grand Rounds couch and Hub protocol with four harness runs. The sixth item covers strains, ward, Cases 4–11, Second Look, Consult, and ending.\n\nThe checklist now adds a seventh item for polish: audio feel, cinematics, art, Skills Lab, attract reel, and balance. The eighth item covers About data, exit-interview.json, the single-file bundle, the full test command, and three-browser launch evidence in scratch.\n\nNon-goals are explicitly listed: FPS optimization, medical accuracy, reproducing Trauma Center names or assets, WebGPU or 3D, real speech or audio files, touch gameplay controls, Hub chat or emotes, and a second campaign beyond the eleven-case slice.\n\nPhysical controllers and real iOS devices are out of scope; stubs and WebKit will be used instead. Extra power-ups or score systems beyond PLAN’s rank and flow are excluded. The work folder is STAGE.md, PLAN.md, and TASK.md. A new dev subfolder will hold vendor clones of my-3d2dge and ai-hub. Playwright 1.63, Node, and Python are already present. Sibling agent folders must not be read and other processes must not be killed. The cloned engine and Hub versions may have moved since PLAN section 3 was verified against my-3d2dge v0.89.0 and Hub client 1.4.0.\n\nThe cloned engine’s API.md overrides PLAN.md when they conflict, and the adaptation must be recorded. TASK.md requirements cannot be dropped to match older notes.\n\nThe full playthrough stays headless and avoids drawing except for one case to prevent memory exhaustion from earlyoom. If a headless screenshot is blank while the game state confirms the scene rendered, investigate before falling back. If Playwright fails to start, capture that failure in {SCRATCH} and accept unit tests plus structural checks.\n\nThe title is random, so the filename cannot be known in advance. The implementer must verify that the title constant, page title, title screen, About data, and Hub card name all match.\n\nPLAN §21’s “if you run out of runway” note is an authoring guideline, not permission to ship stubs. Shipping a cut is a failure of the checklist criteria, not a pass.\n\nPROGRESS.md is a process file, not a game feature, so it will be listed as a hand-in requirement rather than a criterion. The implementer must also include the same source text in their final chat reply and the in-game About screen; the plan itself only needs to match exit-interview.json.\n\nThe implementer reply must contain the four source sections, but verification gates on the JSON and About screen files. Web research confirmed mechanics against Wikipedia and StrategyWiki; PLAN.md already adapts them to the game’s own names and the Steady gesture.\n\nThe aiHub JSON genre is \"Simulation\" per PLAN.md, not \"Action\", because TASK.md lets PLAN.md decide the open field. The exit interview will use TASK's schema but with PLAN's values where PLAN filled them, replacing only what is actually true of the build.\n\nExit-interview.json must match TASK.md’s schema exactly, including array lengths of four, six, and four. The usage field is the literal string `added on import`. Source, plan, genre, perspective, and online fields must describe this build truthfully. A slight tagline variation is acceptable as long as the shape remains exact. Array lengths are confirmed as testing four, engine six, and prompt four.\n\nThe exact file path for the goal plan is now fixed. The write tool will create any missing parent directories automatically. The primary launch observable will be that the page title and on-screen logo text match the chosen game title.\n\nA visible \"press start\" prompt must appear on launch. The canvas buffer must match PLAN's 480×270 viewport expansion rules at 1920×1080; the base resolution is fixed and cannot be altered.\n\nThe default 1920×1080 launch viewport must show a substantially painted 480×270 buffer with title art, not a blank or letterboxed empty field. The driven confirm input must reliably advance to the next screen across two launches.\n\nCriterion 5 will require session.js as the first script and meta tags for the Hub.\n\nCriterion 4 will require that changing a dev mode tuning slider alters live gameplay variables such as drain rate, and that resetting restores the original defaults.\n\nThe attract reel must last ten seconds and cycle through title, menu, case files, lobby, but not About or Settings. Keyboard bindings are native WASD and number keys for tools; verification will spot-check label() for the use tool, gift, and a tool key rather than listing every key as a separate criterion.\n\nThe plan will stay short for small models. Verification checks will use concrete pass observations rather than appendices. The implementer must not hardcode expected values or mock the unit under test in their own tests, though PLAN.md may still specify exact vitals like a -4 tear penalty.\n\nTests must call the shipped simulator and assert PLAN’s specified outcomes, such as grades and drain rates, rather than hardcoding the function’s return value. The full playthrough must remain headless and gating despite possible slowness. The goal kind is code-change. The headline will be a single-sentence paraphrase of the objective.\n\nTASK.md specifies a single HTML file and a subfolder named with bracketed template placeholders for GameTitle and SOURCE. The implementer must substitute the chosen game title and the source line Trauma Center: Second Opinion into both names.\n\nThe literal source string \"Trauma Center: Second Opinion\" will be used in the filename template, including its colon and spaces. Linux tolerates these characters, so the resulting name may be ugly but will match the required bracketed format exactly.\n\nThe filename must contain the chosen game title, the source text, and the run identifier. A filesystem-safe slug may replace the colon and spaces in the source name, provided all three required segments remain present.\n\nThe deliverable HTML must sit in the work-folder root, not inside the dev subfolder. The dev folder and the root folder share the same name, built from the chosen title, the source text, and the literal run identifier.\n\nexit-interview.json must sit beside the HTML file in the work folder root. PROGRESS.md is required by STAGE.md, so it will be listed as a hand-in artifact and included in the checklist even though it is not gated.\n\nPROGRESS.md will be criterion 1, a light requirement recording progress in the work folder. PLAN.md requires notes/feedback.md inside the dev folder for honest feedback; the exit interview must contain those answers, not the file itself. The implementer must follow PLAN.md’s architecture, but the objective only gates on outcomes, so exact source-tree replication is unnecessary.\n\nThe plan will not mandate module layout or the exact file tree from PLAN.md. Acceptance criteria will instead derive from the outcomes of PLAN sections 4 through 18 and section 22. Criterion 3 will require a one-page content-authoring note in the dev folder, without prescribing the path `CONTENT.md`; PLAN section 22 names that file as part of its data-driven content requirement.\n\nThe dev folder must contain a file named `CONTENT.md` explaining how to add cases, strains, sites, characters, and story nodes without changing the simulator. TASK.md also requires a single test command. The plan will be written tightly with numbered verification steps, beginning with gating tests, a double `file://` launch, and three-browser viewport checks.\n\nThe browser viewport checks will be merged into a single gating step. The hand-in check will also verify no audio files, no TODO markers, and no forbidden filenames remain. Screenshots will serve as evidence. Audio verification will use Playwright Chromium with an autoplay policy flag. The plan will specify exact scratch paths for tests.log, launch.log, the two launch screenshots, and hub.log.\n\nIf Playwright fails to start, the error must be captured to {SCRATCH}/launch-error.log and the static/unit bar stands. A successful readback that returns a blank or partial buffer is the app's output; the implementer must fix the cause rather than fall back. The surgical game is stylized and E10+, so child_sex and bio fields are acceptable. The verifier must not reject divergence from the plan.\n\nThe plan will be prefixed as guidance, with PLAN.md treated as the implementer's design document and the acceptance criteria as the sole verifier contract. Specific design numbers from PLAN.md—music volume 0.1, ten-second attract reel, eight tools, eleven cases, one to four players, four harness runs, vitals and time failure, two gifts—will be folded directly into the criteria.\n\nFeel numbers such as 330 units per second and 80 millisecond wheel delay will be grouped into the simulator criterion as \"matching the gesture rules in PLAN.md §5\". This avoids forty separate checkboxes while still giving small-model verifiers a concrete target to open and inspect.\n\nCriterion 3 will require that each of the eight tools—Forceps, Scalpel, Suture, Salve, Syringe, Cautery, Suction, and Sonar—finishes its matching target when used with the gesture in PLAN.md §5.5, and produces the specified mistake—tear, slip, loose stitch, air bubble, char/lockout, or no effect—when used incorrectly.\n\nVitals and the clock start from each case’s initial values. Hazards apply the drain rates in PLAN.md §7. Reaching zero vitals triggers Code Blue once, not on Attending and not when the clock hits zero, then loses the patient if the shock window is missed. Finished actions score Sloppy, Clean, or Textbook and a case rank against par. Long Breath runs world time at quarter speed while hand speed stays full. Second Look restores an earlier simulation snapshot exactly, including vitals, clock, objects, and RNG state, then fills ghost data. The same seed and inputs hash identically whether drawing is on or off.\n\nCriterion 3 will require that every case in PLAN.md §9 and every HUSH strain in §8 can be started from its own data, cleared by the shipped bot on Intern, and finished with time and vitals remaining for the perfect bot. Story scenes in §10 must play between cases, the walkable ward must show saved patients, and a chained New Game through credits must complete.\n\nCriterion 4 requires a ten-second idle timeout on the title, menu, case files, and lobby. During that window the screen loops an AI-played reel of real scenes. Any input returns to the title and does not activate the control under the cursor.\n\nDev mode opened from the pause menu outside a Hub session must allow live tuning of drain, cursor, strain, and gift values. It must also reset those values to documented defaults and support cheats and case or phase jumps.\n\nPrompts must use the engine’s last-device labels: PlayStation glyphs by default, Xbox when that Settings option is on, and keyboard or mouse labels when those were used last.\n\nMouse hover must move menu and ward focus. Mouse aim must work during surgery.\n\nA touch-only profile must show the text “Controller or keyboard required” and a Settings control. It must not display on-screen touch buttons. The attract reel must still run.\n\nAll music, effects, and voices are generated in code. No audio files are allowed. Voices are gibberish, not real words. Music volume starts at 0.1 and is quieter than effects. Pause, settings, and controls must be readable at TV scale.\n\nGrand Rounds is a shared patient for one to four surgeons. It supports couch play with local pads and AI colleagues, plus a shared-world AI Hub session where the unmodified client is the first script, the host simulates, clients mirror, each hand moves locally, the session card can ready up, and finish reports standings.\n\nThe four harness URLs in PLAN.md §20 must all pass their harness check. Opening any of those pages without an active session still runs the single-player game.\n\nSolo play can hold the retractor with the nurse's help. Two players can hold and cut.\n\nThe work folder must contain PROGRESS.md and exit-interview.json. It must also contain exactly one self-contained game HTML whose filename and sibling dev-folder name follow the TASK.md template with the chosen title, source Trauma Center: Second Opinion, and run ID grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223916. The title was selected by silently listing fifty playful riffs and then a real RNG. That same string must appear as the page title, the title-screen logo, the About name, and aiHub.name. The HTML must run from file:// without any CDN or network fetch.\n\nexit-interview.json must parse as a single JSON object with testingFeedback of length four, engineFeedback of length six, promptFeedback of length four, and usage exactly set to \"added on import\", with no empty strings. The in-game About and Credits pages must display the same Source Information, Testing, Engine, and Prompt answers. The game source must contain no TODO, FIXME, placeholder, or stubbed feature.\n\nThe verifier will run the project’s single test command. PLAN.md §20 specifies `node tests/run.mjs` from the dev folder, but the implementer may rename it. The runner must still print only failures and a one-line count.\n\nThe verifier will run `node tests/run.mjs` from the dev folder and capture output to `{SCRATCH}/tests.log`. The implementer must record this command in PROGRESS.md so verifiers can locate it.\n\nThe verifier will launch the game via Playwright using a file:// URL at 1920x1080, running it twice. The Hub harness requires a local server and will be gated by PLAN’s runner.\n\nThe implementer must produce the test logs and scratch evidence for verifiers to audit. The HTML game scripts must execute with a window global and must not depend on Node module or require, since they run from file:// in a browser. A Node unit test is an optional extra.","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"The source is a named surgical game, and PLAN.md already locks the original version of those mechanics. I’ll turn that into a short outcome contract the implementer and verifiers can check."},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-f425e778-80b0-48c6-b898-a1ac7475eb19-6","name":"write","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/.grok/sessions/%2Fhome%2Fmicha%2Fdev%2Fcc-matrix%2Fruns%2Fgrok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223916__x488eb9%2Fwork/01a1aa37-f7cf-7001-9198-38ad04ed0f5e/goal/plan-01a1aa37-fb1e-7421-bdd9-5a5eb6d5d3fa.md","content":"# Plan: Build the original surgical-action game in PLAN.md and hand in every TASK.md artifact in this folder.\n\n## Goal kind\ncode-change\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n1. The work folder (the folder that contains STAGE.md) holds PROGRESS.md, `exit-interview.json` beside the game, and one self-contained HTML file whose name and sibling dev-folder name follow TASK.md’s `[GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223916]` template: the chosen title, the source name Trauma Center: Second Opinion, and the literal run id `grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223916` (spaces may be hyphens). The title is a playful riff picked by listing 50 options and then a real RNG, and that same string is the page `<title>`, the title-screen logo, and `aiHub.name`. The HTML runs from `file://` with no CDN and no network fetch. `exit-interview.json` parses as one object in TASK.md’s exact shape (testingFeedback length 4, engineFeedback length 6, promptFeedback length 4, `usage` exactly `added on import`, no empty strings). The in-game About/Credits pages show the same Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback, and Prompt Feedback, in TASK.md’s order. Shipped game source contains no TODO, FIXME, placeholder, or stubbed feature.\n2. From a fresh operation created by the shipped sim, each of the eight tools (Forceps, Scalpel, Suture, Salve, Syringe, Cautery, Suction, Sonar) completes the matching target with the gesture in PLAN.md §5.5 and applies that section’s mistake when used wrong (tear, slip, loose stitch, air bubble, char/lockout, or no effect). Vitals and the clock drain as in §5.2 and §7; vitals at 0 offer Code Blue once (not on Attending, and not when the clock hits 0) and then lose the patient if the shock window is missed. Finished actions score Sloppy, Clean, or Textbook, and the case rank is scored against par. Long Breath runs world time at quarter speed while the hands stay at full speed. Second Look restores an earlier snapshot exactly (vitals, clock, objects, RNG) and fills ghost data. The same seed and inputs produce the same state hash with drawing on and with drawing off.\n3. Each of the eleven cases in PLAN.md §9 and each HUSH strain in §8 (Staccato, Legato, Tremolo, Da Capo, Fermata, Cantor, including Legato snapping, Da Capo regrowth, and the Cantor’s forced rewind) starts from that case’s own data and is cleared by the shipped bot; the perfect bot finishes every case with time and vitals still left. Story scenes in the §10 voice play between operations, a walkable ward shows patients already saved, and one chained New Game run reaches the ending and credits. Lightbox second opinions, Skills Lab, and Intern / Resident / Attending differences are reachable. A content note in the dev folder explains how to add a case, strain, site, character, and story node without editing the sim. Forbidden source names and close variants from PLAN.md §1 do not appear in play or text, except the source title on the About source line and in the title riff.\n4. After 10 seconds with no input on the title, the menu, case files, and the lobby, a looping AI reel of the real scenes starts; any input returns to the title and does not also fire the control under the pointer. It does not start on About, Settings, or Controls. Dev mode opens from the pause menu outside a Hub session, live-changes the main tuning groups (patient, hands, strains, gift, fluids), and can reset them to the documented defaults; cheats and jumps to any case or phase work. Prompts follow the last device: PlayStation glyphs by default, Xbox glyphs from Settings, keyboard and mouse labels when those were used last. The mouse aims in surgery and hover-moves focus on menus and in the ward. A touch-only profile shows the text \"Controller or keyboard required\" and a Settings control, with no touch buttons, while attract still runs. Music, effects, and voices are synthesized in code (no audio files); voices are gibberish; a fresh start sets music volume to 0.1 so music is quieter than effects.\n5. Grand Rounds is one shared patient for 1 to 4 surgeons, on one couch (local pads and AI colleagues) and in an AI Hub shared-world session. The unmodified Hub session client is the first script, the host simulates, clients mirror, each cursor moves locally, the session card can ready and request more, and finish reports standings, scores, won, and awards. Solo play can still hold the retractor; two players can hold and cut. All four harness runs in PLAN.md §20 pass, and the same page still plays the single-player game when no session is present.\n\n## Verification plan\n1. gating: From the dev folder, run the one test command PLAN.md §20 names (`node tests/run.mjs`). It must load the shipped sim and scenes (not a reimplementation or a mock of them) and assert criteria 2, 3, and 4: every tool’s success and mistake, cursor full-stick / fine / magnet / guide-follow, all eight wheel directions, vitals and clock math, Code Blue window, phase-checkpoint restore, grades and rank, each strain’s success and listed failure branch, Long Breath’s 0.25 world-time factor with hand speed unchanged, Second Look deep-equality plus ghosts, determinism with and without drawing, content-id resolution, per-case bot clears, the chained New Game → credits playthrough, attract idle and consumed exit, dev-slider live change and reset, and Grand Rounds with 1 to 4 bots including the retractor alone and with two. Capture stdout to `{SCRATCH}/tests.log`. Pass only if the log contains a one-line count, zero failures, and no other chatter. Re-run after any change to code those tests cover.\n2. gating: Launch the real root HTML from `file://` with Playwright 1.63 (Chromium, `--autoplay-policy=no-user-gesture-required`) at 1920×1080, twice in a row. Each run must have zero page errors, a drawing buffer of 480×270 (PLAN.md §3 screen-size rule, not a stale default), and a substantially filled frame (title art and the chosen title, not a blank or a few pixels). A confirm/start input must change what is on screen (menu follows the title). Save `{SCRATCH}/launch-1.png`, `{SCRATCH}/launch-2.png`, and `{SCRATCH}/launch.log`. Both runs must show the same success. Then load the same file for 30 seconds in Chromium, Firefox, and WebKit with zero page errors; with the Appendix E pad stub, confirm a PlayStation R2 prompt, an Xbox prompt after the Settings glyph option, and a keyboard/mouse prompt after those devices; on an iPhone-profile touch page, confirm the required notice and Settings control, no touch buttons, and that a Settings tap opens settings. Capture `{SCRATCH}/browsers.log`. If Playwright itself fails to start, save that output to `{SCRATCH}/launch-error.log` and treat criteria 2–3’s unit tests plus the file’s presence as the bar. A launch that starts and reads back a blank or partial frame is a game failure, not an environment failure.\n3. gating: Run the four Hub harness URLs in PLAN.md §20 (2 players; 4 players with latency, jitter, and loss; host-leave; server), plus a no-session open of the same page. Serve only this game on `127.0.0.1` and an OS-picked port, confirm the framed document title is this game, and stop only that server by its PID. Pass means each run has `window.__hubCheck.pass === true`, and the standalone open is not in a session and still reaches the title. With two same-origin harness frames, a client stroke must show up on the host’s state. Capture `{SCRATCH}/hub.log`.\n4. gating: Parse `exit-interview.json` and confirm criterion 1’s shape, non-empty answers, and `usage`. Confirm the HTML inlines the game (no external script or audio URLs), contains no `TODO` / `FIXME` / `placeholder` / `stub`, and does not use the §1 forbidden names outside the allowed source-title spots. Confirm an `OfflineAudioContext` or `AnalyserNode` render of an effect, one voice, and the choir is not silent, and that at default settings the music bus is well below effects. Append these observations to `{SCRATCH}/tests.log` or `{SCRATCH}/handoff.log`.\n5. evidence: At 1920×1080, save stills of each operative site, the main portraits, one frame of each strain, the tool wheel, and Long Breath’s cold field to `{SCRATCH}/shots/`. Absence of stills alone does not fail the goal once steps 1–4 pass.\n\n## Non-goals\n- A port of Trauma Center levels, operations, music, UI, or proper names (those are forbidden).\n- FPS targets, optimization passes, WebGPU, a 3D stack, or CDN libraries.\n- Real speech, recorded audio files, touch-play controls, chat, emotes, or custom Hub names.\n- Cases beyond the eleven-case slice, Grand Rounds, and Skills Lab. The post-credits \"Incoming\" chart is a door, not more gameplay.\n- Motion-capture clips, split-screen, and a second human on another machine (use the harness or a second local page).\n\n## Assumed scope\n- This work folder only: STAGE.md, PLAN.md, TASK.md, plus new PROGRESS.md, `exit-interview.json`, the named HTML, and the named dev subfolder. Do not read or write sibling agent folders, and do not stop processes you did not start.\n- Fresh clones of `my-3d2dge` `dist/handoff` and `ai-hub` `lib/hub` under the dev folder. Engine and Hub APIs are whatever those clones document; PLAN.md §3 was checked against my-3D2dge v0.89.0 and Hub client 1.4.0.\n- Playwright 1.63 (already installed; do not run `playwright install`), Node, and system Python. No audio-file or network dependency in the shipped page.\n- Defining source mechanics (first-person timed surgery, vitals, radial tools, graded results, a once-per-operation supernatural aid, two surgeons, parasite bosses, melodrama) were checked against Wikipedia and StrategyWiki. Where PLAN.md deliberately changes them (button Steady instead of a drawn star, original cast, HUSH instead of the source’s parasite names), PLAN.md wins.\n\n## Implementation approach\nPLAN.md is the design document (flow, numbers, story, Hub message shape). This goal plan does not freeze file or function names; verifiers judge the criteria above, not a matching directory tree. Keep the operation as plain JSON-safe state stepped by a pure function with a seeded RNG, and keep drawing, audio, and DOM out of that state so tests, rewind, checkpoints, and net snapshots share one path. Drive the bot through the same cursor and trigger path as a pad. Content (cases, strains, sites, lines) should be data plus a short authoring note. Bundle last into the single root HTML, with the Hub session script unmodified and first, and escape any `</script` inside inlined source. Record engine patches and honest test/engine/prompt answers as you go so the About pages and `exit-interview.json` match.\n\n## Task checklist\n- [ ] Pick the title (50 riffs, then a real RNG), create the named dev folder and PROGRESS.md, clone the engine handoff and Hub client, and get an empty page to boot from `file://`.\n- [ ] Build the pure operation sim, cursor, wheel, eight tools, vitals, clock, grades, and the Case 1 loop, with the bot on the real input path and tests for those hands.\n- [ ] Add the title, menus, TV-scale pause and settings, glyph prompts, attract, dev mode, touch notice, story scenes, portraits, gibberish voices, and music at 10%.\n- [ ] Add the remaining tools, Code Blue, Long Breath, the Lightbox, and Cases 2 and 3 so Chapter 1 clears under the bot.\n- [ ] Add couch Grand Rounds and the Hub shared-world session, and make all four harness runs pass.\n- [ ] Add both gifts’ full use, Consult, the ward, strains, and Cases 4 through 11 through the ending and credits.\n- [ ] Fill feel, cinematics, synthesized score, the attract reel, and Skills Lab; run the single test command and the browser and Hub launches, saving logs and shots under `{SCRATCH}`.\n- [ ] Write About data and `exit-interview.json` from real results, bundle the final HTML into the work-folder root, and confirm the test command still prints zero failures.\n\n## Risks / Contradictions\n- PLAN.md §21 says to cut late content if time runs out. That note does not lower this contract: a missing never-cut feature (cursor, any of the eight tools, either gift, the finale, Grand Rounds, Hub passes, attract, dev mode, 10-foot UI, About, exit interview) or a stubbed case fails the criteria.\n- Cloned engine or Hub docs may disagree with PLAN.md §3. Trust the clone, adapt, and record the patch. Do not drop a TASK.md requirement to match an older note.\n- The chained playthrough is long. Run it headless without drawing except where a draw-error check is required. `Target closed` means the machine ran out of memory; run one or two browsers at a time.\n- There is no physical DualSense and no real iPhone. The pad stub and a WebKit iPhone profile are the accepted stand-ins. WebKit is not iOS Safari.\n- The title is chosen at build time by RNG, so the filename cannot be fixed here. Match the template and the on-screen title; do not invent a second title in the verifier.\n"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":19295,"output_tokens":13670,"cache_read_input_tokens":43648,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa37-f7cf-7001-9198-38ad04ed0f5e","uuid":"6d221877-b23d-4783-9e6f-7caea82d8de6"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-f425e778-80b0-48c6-b898-a1ac7475eb19-6","content":"{\"type\":\"SearchReplace\",\"EditsApplied\":{\"old_string\":\"\",\"new_string\":\"# Plan: Build the original surgical-action game in PLAN.md and hand in every TASK.md artifact in this folder.\\n\\n## Goal kind\\ncode-change\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n1. The work folder (the folder that contains STAGE.md) holds PROGRESS.md, `exit-interview.json` beside the game, and one self-contained HTML file whose name and sibling dev-folder name follow TASK.md’s `[GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223916]` template: the chosen title, the source name Trauma Center: Second Opinion, and the literal run id `grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223916` (spaces may be hyphens). The title is a playful riff picked by listing 50 options and then a real RNG, and that same string is the page `<title>`, the title-screen logo, and `aiHub.name`. The HTML runs from `file://` with no CDN and no network fetch. `exit-interview.json` parses as one object in TASK.md’s exact shape (testingFeedback length 4, engineFeedback length 6, promptFeedback length 4, `usage` exactly `added on import`, no empty strings). The in-game About/Credits pages show the same Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback, and Prompt Feedback, in TASK.md’s order. Shipped game source contains no TODO, FIXME, placeholder, or stubbed feature.\\n2. From a fresh operation created by the shipped sim, each of the eight tools (Forceps, Scalpel, Suture, Salve, Syringe, Cautery, Suction, Sonar) completes the matching target with the gesture in PLAN.md §5.5 and applies that section’s mistake when used wrong (tear, slip, loose stitch, air bubble, char/lockout, or no effect). Vitals and the clock drain as in §5.2 and §7; vitals at 0 offer Code Blue once (not on Attending, and not when the clock hits 0) and then lose the patient if the shock window is missed. Finished actions score Sloppy, Clean, or Textbook, and the case rank is scored against par. Long Breath runs world time at quarter speed while the hands stay at full speed. Second Look restores an earlier snapshot exactly (vitals, clock, objects, RNG) and fills ghost data. The same seed and inputs produce the same state hash with drawing on and with drawing off.\\n3. Each of the eleven cases in PLAN.md §9 and each HUSH strain in §8 (Staccato, Legato, Tremolo, Da Capo, Fermata, Cantor, including Legato snapping, Da Capo regrowth, and the Cantor’s forced rewind) starts from that case’s own data and is cleared by the shipped bot; the perfect bot finishes every case with time and vitals still left. Story scenes in the §10 voice play between operations, a walkable ward shows patients already saved, and one chained New Game run reaches the ending and credits. Lightbox second opinions, Skills Lab, and Intern / Resident / Attending differences are reachable. A content note in the dev folder explains how to add a case, strain, site, character, and story node without editing the sim. Forbidden source names and close variants from PLAN.md §1 do not appear in play or text, except the source title on the About source line and in the title riff.\\n4. After 10 seconds with no input on the title, the menu, case files, and the lobby, a looping AI reel of the real scenes starts; any input returns to the title and does not also fire the control under the pointer. It does not start on About, Settings, or Controls. Dev mode opens from the pause menu outside a Hub session, live-changes the main tuning groups (patient, hands, strains, gift, fluids), and can reset them to the documented defaults; cheats and jumps to any case or phase work. Prompts follow the last device: PlayStation glyphs by default, Xbox glyphs from Settings, keyboard and mouse labels when those were used last. The mouse aims in surgery and hover-moves focus on menus and in the ward. A touch-only profile shows the text \\\"Controller or keyboard required\\\" and a Settings control, with no touch buttons, while attract still runs. Music, effects, and voices are synthesized in code (no audio files); voices are gibberish; a fresh start sets music volume to 0.1 so music is quieter than effects.\\n5. Grand Rounds is one shared patient for 1 to 4 surgeons, on one couch (local pads and AI colleagues) and in an AI Hub shared-world session. The unmodified Hub session client is the first script, the host simulates, clients mirror, each cursor moves locally, the session card can ready and request more, and finish reports standings, scores, won, and awards. Solo play can still hold the retractor; two players can hold and cut. All four harness runs in PLAN.md §20 pass, and the same page still plays the single-player game when no session is present.\\n\\n## Verification plan\\n1. gating: From the dev folder, run the one test command PLAN.md §20 names (`node tests/run.mjs`). It must load the shipped sim and scenes (not a reimplementation or a mock of them) and assert criteria 2, 3, and 4: every tool’s success and mistake, cursor full-stick / fine / magnet / guide-follow, all eight wheel directions, vitals and clock math, Code Blue window, phase-checkpoint restore, grades and rank, each strain’s success and listed failure branch, Long Breath’s 0.25 world-time factor with hand speed unchanged, Second Look deep-equality plus ghosts, determinism with and without drawing, content-id resolution, per-case bot clears, the chained New Game → credits playthrough, attract idle and consumed exit, dev-slider live change and reset, and Grand Rounds with 1 to 4 bots including the retractor alone and with two. Capture stdout to `{SCRATCH}/tests.log`. Pass only if the log contains a one-line count, zero failures, and no other chatter. Re-run after any change to code those tests cover.\\n2. gating: Launch the real root HTML from `file://` with Playwright 1.63 (Chromium, `--autoplay-policy=no-user-gesture-required`) at 1920×1080, twice in a row. Each run must have zero page errors, a drawing buffer of 480×270 (PLAN.md §3 screen-size rule, not a stale default), and a substantially filled frame (title art and the chosen title, not a blank or a few pixels). A confirm/start input must change what is on screen (menu follows the title). Save `{SCRATCH}/launch-1.png`, `{SCRATCH}/launch-2.png`, and `{SCRATCH}/launch.log`. Both runs must show the same success. Then load the same file for 30 seconds in Chromium, Firefox, and WebKit with zero page errors; with the Appendix E pad stub, confirm a PlayStation R2 prompt, an Xbox prompt after the Settings glyph option, and a keyboard/mouse prompt after those devices; on an iPhone-profile touch page, confirm the required notice and Settings control, no touch buttons, and that a Settings tap opens settings. Capture `{SCRATCH}/browsers.log`. If Playwright itself fails to start, save that output to `{SCRATCH}/launch-error.log` and treat criteria 2–3’s unit tests plus the file’s presence as the bar. A launch that starts and reads back a blank or partial frame is a game failure, not an environment failure.\\n3. gating: Run the four Hub harness URLs in PLAN.md §20 (2 players; 4 players with latency, jitter, and loss; host-leave; server), plus a no-session open of the same page. Serve only this game on `127.0.0.1` and an OS-picked port, confirm the framed document title is this game, and stop only that server by its PID. Pass means each run has `window.__hubCheck.pass === true`, and the standalone open is not in a session and still reaches the title. With two same-origin harness frames, a client stroke must show up on the host’s state. Capture `{SCRATCH}/hub.log`.\\n4. gating: Parse `exit-interview.json` and confirm criterion 1’s shape, non-empty answers, and `usage`. Confirm the HTML inlines the game (no external script or audio URLs), contains no `TODO` / `FIXME` / `placeholder` / `stub`, and does not use the §1 forbidden names outside the allowed source-title spots. Confirm an `OfflineAudioContext` or `AnalyserNode` render of an effect, one voice, and the choir is not silent, and that at default settings the music bus is well below effects. Append these observations to `{SCRATCH}/tests.log` or `{SCRATCH}/handoff.log`.\\n5. evidence: At 1920×1080, save stills of each operative site, the main portraits, one frame of each strain, the tool wheel, and Long Breath’s cold field to `{SCRATCH}/shots/`. Absence of stills alone does not fail the goal once steps 1–4 pass.\\n\\n## Non-goals\\n- A port of Trauma Center levels, operations, music, UI, or proper names (those are forbidden).\\n- FPS targets, optimization passes, WebGPU, a 3D stack, or CDN libraries.\\n- Real speech, recorded audio files, touch-play controls, chat, emotes, or custom Hub names.\\n- Cases beyond the eleven-case slice, Grand Rounds, and Skills Lab. The post-credits \\\"Incoming\\\" chart is a door, not more gameplay.\\n- Motion-capture clips, split-screen, and a second human on another machine (use the harness or a second local page).\\n\\n## Assumed scope\\n- This work folder only: STAGE.md, PLAN.md, TASK.md, plus new PROGRESS.md, `exit-interview.json`, the named HTML, and the named dev subfolder. Do not read or write sibling agent folders, and do not stop processes you did not start.\\n- Fresh clones of `my-3d2dge` `dist/handoff` and `ai-hub` `lib/hub` under the dev folder. Engine and Hub APIs are whatever those clones document; PLAN.md §3 was checked against my-3D2dge v0.89.0 and Hub client 1.4.0.\\n- Playwright 1.63 (already installed; do not run `playwright install`), Node, and system Python. No audio-file or network dependency in the shipped page.\\n- Defining source mechanics (first-person timed surgery, vitals, radial tools, graded results, a once-per-operation supernatural aid, two surgeons, parasite bosses, melodrama) were checked against Wikipedia and StrategyWiki. Where PLAN.md deliberately changes them (button Steady instead of a drawn star, original cast, HUSH instead of the source’s parasite names), PLAN.md wins.\\n\\n## Implementation approach\\nPLAN.md is the design document (flow, numbers, story, Hub message shape). This goal plan does not freeze file or function names; verifiers judge the criteria above, not a matching directory tree. Keep the operation as plain JSON-safe state stepped by a pure function with a seeded RNG, and keep drawing, audio, and DOM out of that state so tests, rewind, checkpoints, and net snapshots share one path. Drive the bot through the same cursor and trigger path as a pad. Content (cases, strains, sites, lines) should be data plus a short authoring note. Bundle last into the single root HTML, with the Hub session script unmodified and first, and escape any `</script` inside inlined source. Record engine patches and honest test/engine/prompt answers as you go so the About pages and `exit-interview.json` match.\\n\\n## Task checklist\\n- [ ] Pick the title (50 riffs, then a real RNG), create the named dev folder and PROGRESS.md, clone the engine handoff and Hub client, and get an empty page to boot from `file://`.\\n- [ ] Build the pure operation sim, cursor, wheel, eight tools, vitals, clock, grades, and the Case 1 loop, with the bot on the real input path and tests for those hands.\\n- [ ] Add the title, menus, TV-scale pause and settings, glyph prompts, attract, dev mode, touch notice, story scenes, portraits, gibberish voices, and music at 10%.\\n- [ ] Add the remaining tools, Code Blue, Long Breath, the Lightbox, and Cases 2 and 3 so Chapter 1 clears under the bot.\\n- [ ] Add couch Grand Rounds and the Hub shared-world session, and make all four harness runs pass.\\n- [ ] Add both gifts’ full use, Consult, the ward, strains, and Cases 4 through 11 through the ending and credits.\\n- [ ] Fill feel, cinematics, synthesized score, the attract reel, and Skills Lab; run the single test command and the browser and Hub launches, saving logs and shots under `{SCRATCH}`.\\n- [ ] Write About data and `exit-interview.json` from real results, bundle the final HTML into the work-folder root, and confirm the test command still prints zero failures.\\n\\n## Risks / Contradictions\\n- PLAN.md §21 says to cut late content if time runs out. That note does not lower this contract: a missing never-cut feature (cursor, any of the eight tools, either gift, the finale, Grand Rounds, Hub passes, attract, dev mode, 10-foot UI, About, exit interview) or a stubbed case fails the criteria.\\n- Cloned engine or Hub docs may disagree with PLAN.md §3. Trust the clone, adapt, and record the patch. Do not drop a TASK.md requirement to match an older note.\\n- The chained playthrough is long. Run it headless without drawing except where a draw-error check is required. `Target closed` means the machine ran out of memory; run one or two browsers at a time.\\n- There is no physical DualSense and no real iPhone. The pad stub and a WebKit iPhone profile are the accepted stand-ins. WebKit is not iOS Safari.\\n- The title is chosen at build time by RNG, so the filename cannot be fixed here. Match the template and the on-screen title; do not invent a second title in the verifier.\\n\",\"tool_output_for_prompt\":\"The file /home/micha/.grok/sessions/%2Fhome%2Fmicha%2Fdev%2Fcc-matrix%2Fruns%2Fgrok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223916__x488eb9%2Fwork/01a1aa37-f7cf-7001-9198-38ad04ed0f5e/goal/plan-01a1aa37-fb1e-7421-bdd9-5a5eb6d5d3fa.md has been created.\",\"tool_output_for_prompt_concise\":\"The file /home/micha/.grok/sessions/%2Fhome%2Fmicha%2Fdev%2Fcc-matrix%2Fruns%2Fgrok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223916__x488eb9%2Fwork/01a1aa37-f7cf-7001-9198-38ad04ed0f5e/goal/plan-01a1aa37-fb1e-7421-bdd9-5a5eb6d5d3fa.md has been created.\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/.grok/sessions/%2Fhome%2Fmicha%2Fdev%2Fcc-matrix%2Fruns%2Fgrok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223916__x488eb9%2Fwork/01a1aa37-f7cf-7001-9198-38ad04ed0f5e/goal/plan-01a1aa37-fb1e-7421-bdd9-5a5eb6d5d3fa.md\",\"edits\":{\"details\":[{\"old_string\":\"\",\"old_line\":1,\"new_string\":\"# Plan: Build the original surgical-action game in PLAN.md and hand in every TASK.md artifact in this folder.\\n\\n## Goal kind\\ncode-change\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n1. The work folder (the folder that contains STAGE.md) holds PROGRESS.md, `exit-interview.json` beside the game, and one self-contained HTML file whose name and sibling dev-folder name follow TASK.md’s `[GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223916]` template: the chosen title, the source name Trauma Center: Second Opinion, and the literal run id `grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223916` (spaces may be hyphens). The title is a playful riff picked by listing 50 options and then a real RNG, and that same string is the page `<title>`, the title-screen logo, and `aiHub.name`. The HTML runs from `file://` with no CDN and no network fetch. `exit-interview.json` parses as one object in TASK.md’s exact shape (testingFeedback length 4, engineFeedback length 6, promptFeedback length 4, `usage` exactly `added on import`, no empty strings). The in-game About/Credits pages show the same Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback, and Prompt Feedback, in TASK.md’s order. Shipped game source contains no TODO, FIXME, placeholder, or stubbed feature.\\n2. From a fresh operation created by the shipped sim, each of the eight tools (Forceps, Scalpel, Suture, Salve, Syringe, Cautery, Suction, Sonar) completes the matching target with the gesture in PLAN.md §5.5 and applies that section’s mistake when used wrong (tear, slip, loose stitch, air bubble, char/lockout, or no effect). Vitals and the clock drain as in §5.2 and §7; vitals at 0 offer Code Blue once (not on Attending, and not when the clock hits 0) and then lose the patient if the shock window is missed. Finished actions score Sloppy, Clean, or Textbook, and the case rank is scored against par. Long Breath runs world time at quarter speed while the hands stay at full speed. Second Look restores an earlier snapshot exactly (vitals, clock, objects, RNG) and fills ghost data. The same seed and inputs produce the same state hash with drawing on and with drawing off.\\n3. Each of the eleven cases in PLAN.md §9 and each HUSH strain in §8 (Staccato, Legato, Tremolo, Da Capo, Fermata, Cantor, including Legato snapping, Da Capo regrowth, and the Cantor’s forced rewind) starts from that case’s own data and is cleared by the shipped bot; the perfect bot finishes every case with time and vitals still left. Story scenes in the §10 voice play between operations, a walkable ward shows patients already saved, and one chained New Game run reaches the ending and credits. Lightbox second opinions, Skills Lab, and Intern / Resident / Attending differences are reachable. A content note in the dev folder explains how to add a case, strain, site, character, and story node without editing the sim. Forbidden source names and close variants from PLAN.md §1 do not appear in play or text, except the source title on the About source line and in the title riff.\\n4. After 10 seconds with no input on the title, the menu, case files, and the lobby, a looping AI reel of the real scenes starts; any input returns to the title and does not also fire the control under the pointer. It does not start on About, Settings, or Controls. Dev mode opens from the pause menu outside a Hub session, live-changes the main tuning groups (patient, hands, strains, gift, fluids), and can reset them to the documented defaults; cheats and jumps to any case or phase work. Prompts follow the last device: PlayStation glyphs by default, Xbox glyphs from Settings, keyboard and mouse labels when those were used last. The mouse aims in surgery and hover-moves focus on menus and in the ward. A touch-only profile shows the text \\\"Controller or keyboard required\\\" and a Settings control, with no touch buttons, while attract still runs. Music, effects, and voices are synthesized in code (no audio files); voices are gibberish; a fresh start sets music volume to 0.1 so music is quieter than effects.\\n5. Grand Rounds is one shared patient for 1 to 4 surgeons, on one couch (local pads and AI colleagues) and in an AI Hub shared-world session. The unmodified Hub session client is the first script, the host simulates, clients mirror, each cursor moves locally, the session card can ready and request more, and finish reports standings, scores, won, and awards. Solo play can still hold the retractor; two players can hold and cut. All four harness runs in PLAN.md §20 pass, and the same page still plays the single-player game when no session is present.\\n\\n## Verification plan\\n1. gating: From the dev folder, run the one test command PLAN.md §20 names (`node tests/run.mjs`). It must load the shipped sim and scenes (not a reimplementation or a mock of them) and assert criteria 2, 3, and 4: every tool’s success and mistake, cursor full-stick / fine / magnet / guide-follow, all eight wheel directions, vitals and clock math, Code Blue window, phase-checkpoint restore, grades and rank, each strain’s success and listed failure branch, Long Breath’s 0.25 world-time factor with hand speed unchanged, Second Look deep-equality plus ghosts, determinism with and without drawing, content-id resolution, per-case bot clears, the chained New Game → credits playthrough, attract idle and consumed exit, dev-slider live change and reset, and Grand Rounds with 1 to 4 bots including the retractor alone and with two. Capture stdout to `{SCRATCH}/tests.log`. Pass only if the log contains a one-line count, zero failures, and no other chatter. Re-run after any change to code those tests cover.\\n2. gating: Launch the real root HTML from `file://` with Playwright 1.63 (Chromium, `--autoplay-policy=no-user-gesture-required`) at 1920×1080, twice in a row. Each run must have zero page errors, a drawing buffer of 480×270 (PLAN.md §3 screen-size rule, not a stale default), and a substantially filled frame (title art and the chosen title, not a blank or a few pixels). A confirm/start input must change what is on screen (menu follows the title). Save `{SCRATCH}/launch-1.png`, `{SCRATCH}/launch-2.png`, and `{SCRATCH}/launch.log`. Both runs must show the same success. Then load the same file for 30 seconds in Chromium, Firefox, and WebKit with zero page errors; with the Appendix E pad stub, confirm a PlayStation R2 prompt, an Xbox prompt after the Settings glyph option, and a keyboard/mouse prompt after those devices; on an iPhone-profile touch page, confirm the required notice and Settings control, no touch buttons, and that a Settings tap opens settings. Capture `{SCRATCH}/browsers.log`. If Playwright itself fails to start, save that output to `{SCRATCH}/launch-error.log` and treat criteria 2–3’s unit tests plus the file’s presence as the bar. A launch that starts and reads back a blank or partial frame is a game failure, not an environment failure.\\n3. gating: Run the four Hub harness URLs in PLAN.md §20 (2 players; 4 players with latency, jitter, and loss; host-leave; server), plus a no-session open of the same page. Serve only this game on `127.0.0.1` and an OS-picked port, confirm the framed document title is this game, and stop only that server by its PID. Pass means each run has `window.__hubCheck.pass === true`, and the standalone open is not in a session and still reaches the title. With two same-origin harness frames, a client stroke must show up on the host’s state. Capture `{SCRATCH}/hub.log`.\\n4. gating: Parse `exit-interview.json` and confirm criterion 1’s shape, non-empty answers, and `usage`. Confirm the HTML inlines the game (no external script or audio URLs), contains no `TODO` / `FIXME` / `placeholder` / `stub`, and does not use the §1 forbidden names outside the allowed source-title spots. Confirm an `OfflineAudioContext` or `AnalyserNode` render of an effect, one voice, and the choir is not silent, and that at default settings the music bus is well below effects. Append these observations to `{SCRATCH}/tests.log` or `{SCRATCH}/handoff.log`.\\n5. evidence: At 1920×1080, save stills of each operative site, the main portraits, one frame of each strain, the tool wheel, and Long Breath’s cold field to `{SCRATCH}/shots/`. Absence of stills alone does not fail the goal once steps 1–4 pass.\\n\\n## Non-goals\\n- A port of Trauma Center levels, operations, music, UI, or proper names (those are forbidden).\\n- FPS targets, optimization passes, WebGPU, a 3D stack, or CDN libraries.\\n- Real speech, recorded audio files, touch-play controls, chat, emotes, or custom Hub names.\\n- Cases beyond the eleven-case slice, Grand Rounds, and Skills Lab. The post-credits \\\"Incoming\\\" chart is a door, not more gameplay.\\n- Motion-capture clips, split-screen, and a second human on another machine (use the harness or a second local page).\\n\\n## Assumed scope\\n- This work folder only: STAGE.md, PLAN.md, TASK.md, plus new PROGRESS.md, `exit-interview.json`, the named HTML, and the named dev subfolder. Do not read or write sibling agent folders, and do not stop processes you did not start.\\n- Fresh clones of `my-3d2dge` `dist/handoff` and `ai-hub` `lib/hub` under the dev folder. Engine and Hub APIs are whatever those clones document; PLAN.md §3 was checked against my-3D2dge v0.89.0 and Hub client 1.4.0.\\n- Playwright 1.63 (already installed; do not run `playwright install`), Node, and system Python. No audio-file or network dependency in the shipped page.\\n- Defining source mechanics (first-person timed surgery, vitals, radial tools, graded results, a once-per-operation supernatural aid, two surgeons, parasite bosses, melodrama) were checked against Wikipedia and StrategyWiki. Where PLAN.md deliberately changes them (button Steady instead of a drawn star, original cast, HUSH instead of the source’s parasite names), PLAN.md wins.\\n\\n## Implementation approach\\nPLAN.md is the design document (flow, numbers, story, Hub message shape). This goal plan does not freeze file or function names; verifiers judge the criteria above, not a matching directory tree. Keep the operation as plain JSON-safe state stepped by a pure function with a seeded RNG, and keep drawing, audio, and DOM out of that state so tests, rewind, checkpoints, and net snapshots share one path. Drive the bot through the same cursor and trigger path as a pad. Content (cases, strains, sites, lines) should be data plus a short authoring note. Bundle last into the single root HTML, with the Hub session script unmodified and first, and escape any `</script` inside inlined source. Record engine patches and honest test/engine/prompt answers as you go so the About pages and `exit-interview.json` match.\\n\\n## Task checklist\\n- [ ] Pick the title (50 riffs, then a real RNG), create the named dev folder and PROGRESS.md, clone the engine handoff and Hub client, and get an empty page to boot from `file://`.\\n- [ ] Build the pure operation sim, cursor, wheel, eight tools, vitals, clock, grades, and the Case 1 loop, with the bot on the real input path and tests for those hands.\\n- [ ] Add the title, menus, TV-scale pause and settings, glyph prompts, attract, dev mode, touch notice, story scenes, portraits, gibberish voices, and music at 10%.\\n- [ ] Add the remaining tools, Code Blue, Long Breath, the Lightbox, and Cases 2 and 3 so Chapter 1 clears under the bot.\\n- [ ] Add couch Grand Rounds and the Hub shared-world session, and make all four harness runs pass.\\n- [ ] Add both gifts’ full use, Consult, the ward, strains, and Cases 4 through 11 through the ending and credits.\\n- [ ] Fill feel, cinematics, synthesized score, the attract reel, and Skills Lab; run the single test command and the browser and Hub launches, saving logs and shots under `{SCRATCH}`.\\n- [ ] Write About data and `exit-interview.json` from real results, bundle the final HTML into the work-folder root, and confirm the test command still prints zero failures.\\n\\n## Risks / Contradictions\\n- PLAN