{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_24","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I have a good story system and will use E.\n\nNow designing the bot logic. The generic bot will prioritize four actions: waiting for a code blue peak, pressing gifts when steady enough, performing tutorial freezes, and finding the current objective object.\n\nBot plans for each object type are now defined. Shards and fragments use forceps with depth pulls. Lacerations and patches use salve grids. Wounds need sutures between two points. Bleeders and polyps require cautery holds. Pools need suction until empty. Artery tears need three sutures. Masses need scalpel rings followed by forceps lifts. Hooks need scalpel cuts then forceps pulls. Clots need targeted syringes then forceps.\n\nNest objects need sonar first if hidden, then a scalpel slash and forceps. Retractors require holding forceps on them. Screws need three forceps circles around them. Wires need scalpel cuts in order. Strain-specific tasks will be handled by a strain planner when active.\n\nPhase completion requires all required objects to be done and the strain controller to report clear. Scripted events will run at specific times during steps.\n\nStrains will be implemented as a registry inside sim.js with a `strain.botGoal(S)` query that returns the tool, position, and action.\n\nA coordinate helper will detect objects inside the cavity centered at (213, 116). Objects will be placed in a grid around that center. The difficulty clock scales the base Resident time by 1.3 for Interns and 0.8 for Attending, with Drains scaled similarly. The simulation step uses engine dt = 1/120 * timeScale; standalone tests use 1/60 or 1/30.\n\nBot runs will use 1/30 second timesteps to keep simulation cheap. Code blue events will use 1/120 second timesteps so the ±0.14 second window is not missed. The ±0.06 second code blue window is too narrow for 1/30 steps.\n\nThe bot will run at a fixed 1/60 second timestep for all phases, including code blue. This simplifies timing logic and keeps the simulation under five seconds. The step budget is roughly 200,000 steps across eleven cases. I will avoid allocations in the hot path.\n\nEvent arrays will be cleared and reused rather than cloned every step. A ring buffer will handle structured cloning at 10 Hz.\n\nStaccato strain goals are now explicit for the bot. Hidden nests require sonar first. Beads can be cauterized anywhere to kill them in 0.12 seconds. The bot will slash the nest guide and use forceps.\n\nLegato strain phases are now defined. The bot must sonar-reveal the head, cut the short guide near the ridge, pull slowly with forceps, release and cautery at the barb, split the mass at half length, and repeat until the length is zero. Pull speed snaps above 70 units per second; the bot pulls at 50 and tests at 200.\n\nTremolo requires targeting the heart phase. The bot syringes during the 0.3-second heart flare, cautery-then-forceps-pulls the mass after the amplitude drops, and sedates the heart between steps.\n\nDa Capo timing is now fixed so the bot can compute its window. The first round opens lobe A at t=6 and B at t=7.5. The bot cauterizes A by t=7, which starts the countdown at t=8. It then slashes B before t=9.5. Round two repeats faster.\n\nDa Capo round two will use doubled lobe timers while the countdown stays at ten seconds. Fermata requires waiting outside domes, then cauterizing an eye that opens for 0.7 seconds after a pause.\n\nFermata will place the eye at a known spot when its dome collapses. Stage two hides the eye and adds two short legato guides with four segments and no barbs. Stage three requires a forty-unit slow forceps pull. Cantor will use four explicit, bot-scripted movements.\n\nCantor's first movement will have three tendrils appear sequentially for the bot to slash, followed by three uvulas that need cautery for 1.5 seconds each. The second movement hides the voice box behind three beat windows; the bot must sonar-reveal it and syringe on beats. Jitter in Tremolo shifts the cursor by ±5 units, which risks landing outside the 8–10 unit tool tolerances if applied after the cursor update.\n\nFine mode reduces Tremolo jitter to 1.75 units, which fits inside every tool tolerance. The perfect bot will use fine mode during both Tremolo and Cantor development to counteract cursor drift. Recapitulation will trigger a 3-second rewind every 20 seconds.\n\nCantor recapitulation will slash the repeat sign during each 3-second windup to prevent rewind. Coda requires advancing only between beats, then forceps and six stitches. The clock refills +120 seconds at movements two, three, and four.\n\nCase 3 triggers a code blue that drains vitals to zero. The bot must then cautery six ruptures in a ring within twelve seconds. Each rupture takes about 0.35 seconds, and travel between them is minimal, so the total should finish in roughly four seconds.\n\nThe twelve-second seal window is tight without long breath but easy with it. The bot will activate long breath if steady at thirty-five or above to satisfy the prompt and attract reel. Six cauteries plus travel take about three seconds.\n\nCase 3 will use 1.5-second cautery per rupture to push total work to about fifteen seconds, which fits inside the forty-eight-second real-time window when long breath is active. Steady must be charged during phase one and two so the gift can trigger. Case nine wires require cutting in the exact sonar-revealed order; an incorrect sequence deducts thirty vitals and thirty clock.\n\nScrew circling will be detected by accumulating absolute angle around each center; three full rotations complete the task. Low-tension forceps will cap pull speed at forty units per second, and the bot will pull at thirty. The grand rounds retractor window opens only when a player holds it within twelve units or when Quill alone is present; without a holder, cut progress stalls.\n\nSolo retractor progress completes when one player cuts. Two-player progress requires player zero to hold the retractor while player one cuts; without a holder, the cut stalls. Par will be computed after a perfect bot run and stored for rank tests.\n\nA calibration mode will print scores to a file during development, then hardcode the final par. An `Op.rank` helper will compute rank from score and par. Mistakes tests will use direct `Op.step` calls with manual positions rather than the bot, since those are more reliable; the bot is reserved for case clears. A guide-follow test will place the cursor on a horizontal guide and push the stick at forty-five degrees; without guide follow the path stays diagonal, but with it the perpendicular component is scaled to zero.\n\nGuide follow only activates during a Scalpel stroke or stitch, so the test must press on the line before pushing the stick. A magnet test will place a target twenty-two units away and verify the cursor lands within two units after one second. Full-stick aim must clear a thirty-unit threshold, but the engine deadzone of 0.18 means an axis value of one still yields roughly one unit of motion; Cursor.update receives the stick after this deadzone is applied.\n\nDirect Cursor.update with stick [1,0] will test the full-stick aim criterion without deadzone. Fine mode must still keep distance within 120 units.\n\nWheel tests will cover all eight directions and keys 1 through 8. A mouse-hold flick test will use wheelHold plus a flick, with a new Wheel.pickFromAngle helper.\n\nA dev-slider test will clone the default tuning, raise patient drain to five, and run both simulations for two seconds to confirm the tuned version drains vitals faster. The dev module’s set and reset functions will be exercised directly, then the game state will be checked against DEFAULT_TUNING after reset. An idle timer is also needed in the game to support attract reel testing.\n\nThe playthrough test will drive the game directly by calling the bot each frame and writing its output to the pad axes. This avoids any debug flag and exercises the real input path.\n\nThe test writes the bot’s move, aim, trigger, fine, and gift signals directly to the pad axes and buttons before each `game.advance(dt)` call. Because `advance` internally steps the physics at 1/120 s, a single pad update lasts the full frame duration, so held stick inputs remain constant across the step.\n\nThe bot must be queried every frame because its aim updates as the cursor moves. The test will call `Bot.device` each frame, apply the resulting pad signals, and advance the game by 1/120 s. The simulation state is available through `G.op`, so the test can feed it directly. Only the OP scene will be driven; other scenes are skipped.\n\nThe playthrough test will hold the skip action during story scenes to force rapid page advancement, tap confirm during menus, and steer the stick toward the ward door. It is still unclear whether holding skip automatically triggers page turns or if the story must turn pages separately.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-7207f674-1dd6-4a3c-85d2-3a064c70c74e-34","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"this\\.skip|skip","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223916__x488eb9/work/Open-Table-Second-Opinion-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223916/vendor/my-3d2dge/dist/handoff/my-3d2dge.js","glob":"*.js","head_limit":20}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2048,"output_tokens":4467,"cache_read_input_tokens":160768,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa37-f7cf-7001-9198-38ad04ed0f5e","uuid":"6afe503f-074e-463b-99a9-c3313d4ce5bf"}
