=\\\"button\\\" style=\\\"font:inherit;padding:10px 22px;border-radius:10px;border:2px solid #b8a8ff;background:#2a2050;color:#fff;min-width:44px;min-height:44px\\\">Settings</button></div>';\",\"old_line\":4023,\"new_string\":\"    d.style.cssText = 'position:absolute;left:0;right:0;bottom:0;z-index:20;display:flex;align-items:flex-end;justify-content:center;padding:48px 24px 24px;text-align:center;pointer-events:none;background:linear-gradient(transparent,rgba(5,3,12,.92) 45%);color:#f4f0ff;font:600 17px/1.45 system-ui,sans-serif';\\n    d.innerHTML = '<div><div style=\\\"font-size:21px;margin-bottom:8px\\\">Controller or keyboard required</div><div style=\\\"opacity:.8;margin-bottom:18px\\\">This game plays with a controller or a keyboard. Connect one, then press any button.</div><button type=\\\"button\\\" style=\\\"pointer-events:auto;font:inherit;padding:10px 22px;border-radius:10px;border:2px solid #b8a8ff;background:#2a2050;color:#fff;min-width:44px;min-height:44px\\\">Settings</button></div>';\",\"new_line\":4023,\"context_before\":\"  if (!need) { if (el) el.style.display = 'none'; return; }\\n  if (!el) {\\n    const d = this._needEl = document.createElement('div'); d.setAttribute('data-sys', 'need'); d.setAttribute('role', 'alert');\\n\",\"context_after\":\"    d.querySelector('button').onclick = () => this.menu('settings');\\n    (this.screen.canvas.parentElement || document.body).appendChild(d);\\n  }\\n\",\"line_prefix\":\"\"}]}}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa37-f7cf-7001-9198-38ad04ed0f5e","uuid":"aeb7d227-dd30-45cf-9fd1-f58c95abac38"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_32","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"A note indicates that the display is set to flex when the notice appears.\n\nThe notice display toggles correctly between flex and none based on shell state. I'm now designing the simulation core, starting with tool constants and wheel direction mappings.\n\nThe wheel direction constants are now defined as eight directional vectors. The `create` function is being drafted to initialize the simulation state with difficulty-based clock multipliers and player counts.\n\nThe `create` function now initializes the full simulation state. It sets the clock, vitals, drain, phase, objects, players, gift type, and game flags such as code blue, lost, won, and score.\n\nThe `create` function now initializes rank cap, flow, streak, strain, flags, tutorial state, phase timing, dressing, par, site, tilt, dim, script queue, best flow, technique stats, and dev mode. It then creates the specified number of players and loads the initial phase. A question arises about whether to store the case definition reference for phase transitions during simulation steps.\n\nThe simulation state will store only a case ID, with phase definitions kept in a registry so the state remains JSON-safe. The `loadPhase` function will clear objects, spawn phase-specific items, set tutorial flags, run entry callbacks, reset timing, and snapshot checkpoints. Spawner helpers will return plain object arrays. The bot is being designed as a per-player state machine stored outside the simulation state to survive rewinds.\n\nBot memory will be stored on each player object and cleared on rewind. The bot device function reads the simulation state and returns movement, aiming, triggers, fine adjustments, gift, consult, and cancel actions. Cursor update uses device aim, fine, keys, and mouse.\n\nCursor.update will set position directly from dev.mouse in field units. The game converts screen pointers to field units before passing them to Cursor.\n\nWheel.update handles tool selection via dev.toolKey and left stick magnitude. A stable sector accumulates for 0.08 seconds before the tool changes. Tool swapping takes 0.12 seconds, but the test requirement is that the player picks its tool within 100 milliseconds.\n\nWheel.update will also handle number keys through dev.digit. The simulation code will be written as one file split into logical sections. A minimal createRaw helper is being added for deterministic unit tests, while the full create function remains the shipped API.\n\nGrade names will be stored as lowercase strings and displayed capitalized. Suture grades are computed from the mean of per-stitch times using thresholds of 0.6 for textbook and 1.2 for clean. Scalpel performance is judged by averaging the distance samples taken while pressing along the guide.\n\nForceps par time is set to default depth divided by ninety plus 0.15 seconds. A tear-free catch under that time earns textbook grade.\n\nRelease-to-dish mechanics are defined: an object becomes free after reaching its depth, then follows the cursor until released at least twenty-four units from its origin. The player must pull past depth before carrying it away.\n\nDepth is set to 16 so pulling 24 units along the axis frees the object. The bot will grip and pull to depth plus eight, then release. A tear occurs while gripped if speed exceeds 120 or perpendicular distance exceeds 8; this deducts four vitals and 30 percent of progress.\n\nOff-axis distance is measured as perpendicular distance from the axis line. A focused test harness will be written first to iterate on simulation and bot logic before building the game shell. Playthrough time is estimated at roughly twenty seconds for the first case and two to three minutes for the last.\n\nThe dressing phase is a timing ring that closes the case when pressed inside a 0.2-second window. A missed press still closes the case with a smaller bonus, preventing softlocks. The lost state records the reason for failure. Retry logic is unnecessary because the perfect bot will test checkpoint restoration directly.\n\nThe state hash will exclude the ring and checkpoint to avoid recursion and drawing artifacts. It will only include rewindable data, player positions, and phase.\n\nThe ring will store samples every 0.1 world seconds, each capturing the rewindable state, player cursors, and strain ghost positions. Restoring from a ring index rebuilds ghosts by slicing the history from that point and mapping each sample to a ghost object with timestamp, cursor positions, and strains.\n\ntrySecondLook requires forty-five steady and a look or both gift, excluding grand rounds. It costs forty-five, restores the oldest sample within the last six seconds, keeps player positions, clears gripped and stroke states, fills ghosts at six seconds back, and does nothing if no sample exists.\n\nLong breath requires thirty-five steady and a doctor or both gift. It scales world time by the gift's breath factor of zero point two five and drains steady at sixteen per real second.\n\nLong breath lasts six seconds from a full steady gauge. Pressing again stops it early. Hands use real delta time during the effect, so cursor speed stays unchanged while the clock slows by a quarter.\n\nConsult toggles the active doctor between Vale and Sokolova. Cases ten and eleven set a consult flag.\n\nCode blue triggers when vitals reach zero while the case is not lost. It checks whether the clock is empty or the difficulty is attending or code blue was already used before declaring a loss.\n\nCode blue initializes a waiting state with zero vitals and no hits. The clock is checked before vitals so a simultaneous zero does not trigger code blue. During code blue the world freezes and the clock stops draining. A peak timing pattern at 0.45 seconds plus 0.9-second intervals is being considered.\n\nCode blue timing is now defined as four peaks at 0.9, 1.8, 2.7, and 3.6 seconds with a ±0.14 window. The minigame tracks an attempt counter that advances on a missed press edge. A successful hit requires the press to occur within the window of the current peak.\n\nTextbook grade for code blue requires a press within 0.06 seconds of the peak. After a successful code blue, vitals reset to thirty. Story nodes will be stored in a separate file at src/data/story.js. Concrete case object lists are being sketched so the bot's generic logic can target specific phases and items. The generic bot algorithm is being drafted with a goal function that returns a codeblue action when the code blue state is active.\n\nThe bot goal function now returns a dressing action when the case is in the dressing phase. It also returns idle if the case is already won or lost. Gift requests are queued for long breath and second look based on steady gauge thresholds. The bot scans active objects to find hidden items that remain unmarked, then issues a sonar command at their coordinates. Pool selection logic is being added for phases that require them.\n\nThe bot now selects a suction target from any pool with more than half volume when objects are blocked or the phase requires suction. It checks for a strain goal next, then picks the first unfinished non-pool, non-nerve object that is not hidden and unmarked. If only pools remain, it suction-sucks them; otherwise it plans to interact with the chosen object.\n\nPhase advance occurs when no required incomplete objects remain and the strain is clear. Optional objects do not block progress. Pools block only while they retain volume, and nerves never block. Objects placed under pools cannot be targeted until the pool is suctioned first. Each object carries an `order` number so the bot processes them in sequence, with pools that hide items receiving lower priority.\n\nStrain mechanics are being modeled as virtual goals. Each step sets a strain goal with coordinates, tool, action, and deadline, spawning objects the bot already recognizes. Strain completion allows phase advancement. Staccato will spawn hidden nest objects requiring a slash-then-forceps sequence.\n\nBeads are moving cautery objects that spawn during staccato. They vanish after three seconds once every nest is complete, and landing on them creates lacerations.\n\nThe bot handles nests by slashing them first, then pulling with forceps.\n\nLegato will use a dedicated `legatoBot` state machine: it starts in a hidden-head find stage, then switches to a cut stage where slur guides appear as scalpel objects when the ridge nears.\n\nLegato's cut stage will use three short guides that only count when the head is within forty units. The pull stage applies forceps tension to extract segments, with every fourth segment acting as a barb that must be released in a hold zone before further pulling tears it. When the worm reaches half length, it splits into a shorter duplicate for the bot to repeat.\n\nA snap condition will trigger a flee stage when head speed exceeds seventy units, causing the head to move away and regrow; the bot must stay under fifty.\n\nTremolo uses five reeds that accept syringe injections only during a flare window, spawning lacerations if injected outside. A mass object appears in stage two for cautery, then forceps pulls it in stage three. The bot waits for the flare before syringing each reed and keeps fine mode on to reduce jitter.\n\nDa Capo involves three lobe objects that are only targetable while open.\n\nDa Capo lobe objects countdown while open and regrow when the timer hits zero. Fermata domes block tools inside them; the bot waits outside at the eye position, then cautery works immediately once the dome collapses.\n\nThe eye position is known in stage one because the dome sits on it. In stage two it hides and the bot must sonar it.\n\nCantor will spawn objects per movement. The first movement spawns three slash guides as tendrils, then three cautery targets.\n\nCantor movement two spawns a hidden voice box that accepts syringe only during beat windows, with three doses. Movement three introduces a slash target repeat sign that appears for three seconds every twenty seconds for six times; missing it rewinds the field while preserving steady. Movement four uses a long scalpel guide that advances progress only between beats, followed by a forceps object and six stitches. This reduces Cantor to timed object spawning handled by the simulator's special rules. A forced rewind function is being drafted to restore core state from six seconds ago while preserving steady and breath.\n\nA test confirms that an action undone by rewind preserves the steady gauge. The design is finalized and implementation begins with config.js and the simulator, written in parallel batches.\n\nCases will be generated programmatically inside content.js to keep coordinates consistent. Par will be computed from a perfect bot run once the simulator is ready. A calibration script may be added later. Writing sim.js now with extra care to avoid bugs.\n\nThe step function now separates hand time from world time. Hand time is the raw delta, while world time is scaled by the breath factor and frozen or code-blue states. Player inputs are applied per player inside this loop.\n\nThe step function now maps input axes, tool selection, and trigger states to each player. It detects edge transitions for triggers, gifts, consultations, and cancellations. If a code blue is active, the function exits early to run the code blue logic before continuing.\n\nThe step function now processes gift and consultation edges. During breath, steady gauge drains by a scaled amount per hand delta, and the world time scale follows the breath factor. Difficulty settings are being applied to clock speed and hazard generation.\n\nThe step function now accumulates hazard from active objects and applies bleed damage to the vitals gauge. It advances the clock and steady gauge, then runs tool logic and object updates.\n\nThe step function now runs strain and script updates. It checks for time loss, vitals failure, and phase completion. A ring accumulator pushes objects every tenth of a second. Ghost timers decay each hand frame. The tutorial freezes clock and vitals until the first successful grade.\n\nThe tool application order is being finalized. Vitals drain before tools run, so a syringe used in the same step can heal the patient and prevent death. Code blue triggers only after tools are processed, so a successful heal also blocks it.\n\nThe forceps tool is being implemented with a 0.3 trigger threshold. It grabs the nearest valid object within twelve units on trigger press and tracks pull time. To compute movement deltas correctly, previous position will be stored at the end of each step.\n\nForceps pull mechanics now compute along-axis and perpendicular displacement from the held object's origin. Instantaneous speed is capped at 120 units per second, and lateral drift beyond 8 units tears the object. Progress advances along the axis until depth is reached, then the object becomes free to follow the cursor. Pull time accumulates each frame, and release detection begins.\n\nForceps release logic now distinguishes three outcomes: dropping the object when it is free and outside the cavity, handing it off to a hold zone, or tearing it if the player leaves too early. The previous position will be stored at the start of each tool application and updated at the end of the step.\n\nDirect tests will advance the cursor in small steps to avoid false tears from instantaneous speed spikes. The scalpel tool is being drafted: it grabs the nearest guide start within eight units on trigger press, then tracks stroke progress by arc length and distance from the polyline. A slip condition triggers if the distance exceeds nine units for 0.12 seconds. On release, the stroke grades if progress is complete.\n\nScalpel release now allows resuming the stroke within six units of the last point. A healthy-tissue slip triggers if the player holds the trigger without a guide and moves six units inside the cavity. Suture requires pressing near the next target point and releasing near the far point within time. Salve marks cells within ten units while held. Syringe fills over half a second, caps at one, and releases as a dose if the player pulls away more than ten units; otherwise it infuses vitals scaled by difficulty. Cautery is being drafted next.\n\nCautery heat rises by 0.5 per second while firing and cools by 0.6 when idle. It locks for 1.2 seconds once it hits one. On a cautery target progress advances each frame. On bare tissue it burns for 0.25 seconds, then slips after 0.6 seconds and drains vitals at -0.8 per second.\n\nSuction drains 60 volume units per second within fourteen units of a pool.\n\nSonar uses a lens radius of 38, scaling up to 47.5 with the vale passive. Hidden objects inside the lens are marked for four seconds after the player leaves. They remain touchable during that window.\n\nHidden objects will stay marked for four seconds after the player leaves the lens, then expire so the bot must re-ping to rediscover them. A target is valid only if it is not done and not under a pool with volume greater than one.\n\nA nerve polyline will trigger -12 vitals and a slip if a scalpel or cautery touches it within six units. Tool targeting now also requires an open window and forbids fermata domes. The simulator, content generator, bot, and unit tests will be written first, followed by the game shell. Eleven cases plus a grand finale and skills lab will be generated programmatically. Story nodes will be embedded as data in content.js.\n\nEach case will have a before node and an after node. The title \"Open Table Second Opinion\" must fit the acceptance criteria as the page title, logo, and aiHub.name. The logo can be drawn as two lines that together read that exact string.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-51f79872-8457-4b29-a850-b5da63b6d56c-44","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"title\\(g|toUpperCase","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223916__x488eb9/work/Open-Table-Second-Opinion-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223916/vendor/my-3d2dge/dist/handoff/my-3d2dge.js","head_limit":15}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2451,"output_tokens":8074,"cache_read_input_tokens":173184,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa37-f7cf-7001-9198-38ad04ed0f5e","uuid":"f453f497-6bff-41e3-8b88-c42e82d8fef3"}
