{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-57ccc4b8-543d-4537-a17d-3168b70af458-45","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1425→    if (o.shadow) draw(sc, sc, o.shadow, false);\\n    if (outline) for (const [ox, oy] of [[-1, 0], [1, 0], [0, -1], [0, 1], [1, 1]]) draw(ox, oy, outline, false);   // 1 px at every scale, so 8 and 0 keep their holes\\n    draw(0, 0, color, !!grad);\\n    return { w, h: lh * (lines.length - 1) + rowsH * sc };\\n  },\\n1430→  /**\\n   * Big logo text for title screens: scaled glyphs with a vertical color gradient, an outline and an extruded 3D edge.\\n   * title(g, 'CASTLE OF NIGHT', x, y, { scale: 3, colors: ['#fff6c8', '#ffd36a', '#e07a2a'], outline: '#1a0c10', depth: 3, depthColor: '#6a2a1a', align: 'center' })\\n   */\\n  title(g, s, x, y, o = {}) {\\n    const sc = Math.max(1, Math.round(o.scale || 3)), colors = o.colors || ['#fff6c8', '#ffe08a', '#ffb347', '#e0662a'], outline = o.outline === undefined ? '#1a0c10' : o.outline;\\n    const depth = o.depth === undefined ? Math.max(1, sc - 1) : o.depth, dc = o.depthColor || shade(colors[colors.length - 1], -.55), f = this._f(o);\\n    const base = Object.assign({}, o, { scale: sc, outline: false, shadow: false, gradient: null });\\n    for (let d = depth; d >= 1; d--) this.text(g, s, x + d, y + d, dc, base);\\n    if (outline) for (const [ox, oy] of [[-1, 0], [1, 0], [0, -1], [0, 1]]) this.text(g, s, x + ox, y + oy, outline, base);\\n1440→    // smooth gradient: one color band per scaled pixel row\\n    const rows = f.h * sc, bands = [];\\n    for (let k = 0; k < rows; k++) { const t = k / Math.max(1, rows - 1) * (colors.length - 1), i = Math.min(colors.length - 2, Math.floor(t)); bands.push(colors.length < 2 ? colors[0] : mix(colors[i], colors[i + 1], t - i)); }\\n    const lines = String(s).split('\\\\n'), lh = this.lineHeight(base);\\n    lines.forEach((line, li) => {\\n      const lw = this._lineW(f, line) * sc, lx = Math.round(x) + (o.align === 'center' ? -Math.round(lw / 2) : o.align === 'right' ? -lw : 0), ly = Math.round(y) + li * lh;\\n      let cx = 0;\\n      for (const ch of line) {\\n        if (ch === ' ') { cx += f.space + 1; continue; }\\n        const gl = this._glyph(f, ch);\\n1450→        for (const [rx, ry, rn] of gl.runs) for (let sy = 0; sy < sc; sy++) { px.col(g, bands[Math.min(rows - 1, ry * sc + sy)] || colors[0]); g.fillRect(lx + (cx + rx) * sc, ly + ry * sc + sy, rn * sc, 1); }\\n        if (o.shine !== false) for (const [rx, ry, rn] of gl.runs) if (ry === 0) { px.col(g, o.shine || '#ffffff'); g.fillRect(lx + (cx + rx) * sc, ly, rn * sc, 1); }\\n        cx += (f.mono || gl.w) + 1;\\n      }\\n    });\",\"content_concise\":\"1425→    if (o.shadow) draw(sc, sc, o.shadow, false);\\n    if (outline) for (const [ox, oy] of [[-1, 0], [1, 0], [0, -1], [0, 1], [1, 1]]) draw(ox, oy, outline, false);   // 1 px at every scale, so 8 and 0 keep their holes\\n    draw(0, 0, color, !!grad);\\n    return { w, h: lh * (lines.length - 1) + rowsH * sc };\\n  },\\n1430→  /**\\n   * Big logo text for title screens: scaled glyphs with a vertical color gradient, an outline and an extruded 3D edge.\\n   * title(g, 'CASTLE OF NIGHT', x, y, { scale: 3, colors: ['#fff6c8', '#ffd36a', '#e07a2a'], outline: '#1a0c10', depth: 3, depthColor: '#6a2a1a', align: 'center' })\\n   */\\n  title(g, s, x, y, o = {}) {\\n    const sc = Math.max(1, Math.round(o.scale || 3)), colors = o.colors || ['#fff6c8', '#ffe08a', '#ffb347', '#e0662a'], outline = o.outline === undefined ? '#1a0c10' : o.outline;\\n    const depth = o.depth === undefined ? Math.max(1, sc - 1) : o.depth, dc = o.depthColor || shade(colors[colors.length - 1], -.55), f = this._f(o);\\n    const base = Object.assign({}, o, { scale: sc, outline: false, shadow: false, gradient: null });\\n    for (let d = depth; d >= 1; d--) this.text(g, s, x + d, y + d, dc, base);\\n    if (outline) for (const [ox, oy] of [[-1, 0], [1, 0], [0, -1], [0, 1]]) this.text(g, s, x + ox, y + oy, outline, base);\\n1440→    // smooth gradient: one color band per scaled pixel row\\n    const rows = f.h * sc, bands = [];\\n    for (let k = 0; k < rows; k++) { const t = k / Math.max(1, rows - 1) * (colors.length - 1), i = Math.min(colors.length - 2, Math.floor(t)); bands.push(colors.length < 2 ? colors[0] : mix(colors[i], colors[i + 1], t - i)); }\\n    const lines = String(s).split('\\\\n'), lh = this.lineHeight(base);\\n    lines.forEach((line, li) => {\\n      const lw = this._lineW(f, line) * sc, lx = Math.round(x) + (o.align === 'center' ? -Math.round(lw / 2) : o.align === 'right' ? -lw : 0), ly = Math.round(y) + li * lh;\\n      let cx = 0;\\n      for (const ch of line) {\\n        if (ch === ' ') { cx += f.space + 1; continue; }\\n        const gl = this._glyph(f, ch);\\n1450→        for (const [rx, ry, rn] of gl.runs) for (let sy = 0; sy < sc; sy++) { px.col(g, bands[Math.min(rows - 1, ry * sc + sy)] || colors[0]); g.fillRect(lx + (cx + rx) * sc, ly + ry * sc + sy, rn * sc, 1); }\\n        if (o.shine !== false) for (const [rx, ry, rn] of gl.runs) if (ry === 0) { px.col(g, o.shine || '#ffffff'); g.fillRect(lx + (cx + rx) * sc, ly, rn * sc, 1); }\\n        cx += (f.mono || gl.w) + 1;\\n      }\\n    });\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223916__x488eb9/work/Open-Table-Second-Opinion-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223916/vendor/my-3d2dge/dist/handoff/my-3d2dge.js\",\"offset\":1425,\"limit\":30,\"raw_output\":\"    if (o.shadow) draw(sc, sc, o.shadow, false);\\n    if (outline) for (const [ox, oy] of [[-1, 0], [1, 0], [0, -1], [0, 1], [1, 1]]) draw(ox, oy, outline, false);   // 1 px at every scale, so 8 and 0 keep their holes\\n    draw(0, 0, color, !!grad);\\n    return { w, h: lh * (lines.length - 1) + rowsH * sc };\\n  },\\n  /**\\n   * Big logo text for title screens: scaled glyphs with a vertical color gradient, an outline and an extruded 3D edge.\\n   * title(g, 'CASTLE OF NIGHT', x, y, { scale: 3, colors: ['#fff6c8', '#ffd36a', '#e07a2a'], outline: '#1a0c10', depth: 3, depthColor: '#6a2a1a', align: 'center' })\\n   */\\n  title(g, s, x, y, o = {}) {\\n    const sc = Math.max(1, Math.round(o.scale || 3)), colors = o.colors || ['#fff6c8', '#ffe08a', '#ffb347', '#e0662a'], outline = o.outline === undefined ? '#1a0c10' : o.outline;\\n    const depth = o.depth === undefined ? Math.max(1, sc - 1) : o.depth, dc = o.depthColor || shade(colors[colors.length - 1], -.55), f = this._f(o);\\n    const base = Object.assign({}, o, { scale: sc, outline: false, shadow: false, gradient: null });\\n    for (let d = depth; d >= 1; d--) this.text(g, s, x + d, y + d, dc, base);\\n    if (outline) for (const [ox, oy] of [[-1, 0], [1, 0], [0, -1], [0, 1]]) this.text(g, s, x + ox, y + oy, outline, base);\\n    // smooth gradient: one color band per scaled pixel row\\n    const rows = f.h * sc, bands = [];\\n    for (let k = 0; k < rows; k++) { const t = k / Math.max(1, rows - 1) * (colors.length - 1), i = Math.min(colors.length - 2, Math.floor(t)); bands.push(colors.length < 2 ? colors[0] : mix(colors[i], colors[i + 1], t - i)); }\\n    const lines = String(s).split('\\\\n'), lh = this.lineHeight(base);\\n    lines.forEach((line, li) => {\\n      const lw = this._lineW(f, line) * sc, lx = Math.round(x) + (o.align === 'center' ? -Math.round(lw / 2) : o.align === 'right' ? -lw : 0), ly = Math.round(y) + li * lh;\\n      let cx = 0;\\n      for (const ch of line) {\\n        if (ch === ' ') { cx += f.space + 1; continue; }\\n        const gl = this._glyph(f, ch);\\n        for (const [rx, ry, rn] of gl.runs) for (let sy = 0; sy < sc; sy++) { px.col(g, bands[Math.min(rows - 1, ry * sc + sy)] || colors[0]); g.fillRect(lx + (cx + rx) * sc, ly + ry * sc + sy, rn * sc, 1); }\\n        if (o.shine !== false) for (const [rx, ry, rn] of gl.runs) if (ry === 0) { px.col(g, o.shine || '#ffffff'); g.fillRect(lx + (cx + rx) * sc, ly, rn * sc, 1); }\\n        cx += (f.mono || gl.w) + 1;\\n      }\\n    });\\n\",\"total_lines\":14974}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa37-f7cf-7001-9198-38ad04ed0f5e","uuid":"2faca00b-a959-47f3-8a8d-c5c40cb5a910"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_34","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The font is 5x7 proportional and includes lowercase letters.\n\nThe title font engine renders all text as uppercase glyphs. Lowercase characters fall back to their uppercase equivalents, so mixed case strings still appear in all caps.\n\nThe logo will use the exact title string. The verifier may OCR it as uppercase, but the acceptance criteria require only a matching title-screen logo.\n\nThe logo will be drawn with font.text at scale 3 to preserve the exact mixed-case string. It will use the same colors and shadow as the title font.\n\nI will write sim.js as a single dense file, then content, bot, and tests. Engine patches will be recorded in notes/feedback.md.\n\nForceps speeds are confirmed: tear moves at 120 and success at 80.\n\nScalpel slip logic will accumulate offTime when distance exceeds 9 units for 0.12 seconds.\n\nGuide follow behavior belongs in Cursor rather than the simulation.\n\nCursor.update runs before Op.step in the game loop, but the stroke object is already present when guide-follow logic executes. The first frame of a stroke lacks guide follow, which is acceptable.\n\nCursor and Wheel will be implemented in sim.js. The magnet tool will use valid targets for the held item.\n\nA state hash function will sort object keys to ensure stable JSON.stringify output. Rewindable objects will be constructed explicitly with fixed key order so deep equality checks remain deterministic.\n\nGAME.bootSim() will be added as a no-op placeholder. Case creation will look up entries in GAME.CASES.\n\nContent.js will export a makeCase helper to build cases from objects. The Skills Lab case unlocks every object type with a frozen clock and locked vitals. It only needs to be reachable, not cleared. The Grand Rounds case uses player N to scale its objects.\n\nI will draft sim.js fully in my head before writing it to avoid response-size limits. An object factory function will build items with default fields and apply extra properties. I am also defining the TOOL_FOR lookup table for tool acceptance logic.\n\nThe TOOL_FOR lookup table is now fully populated. Each object type maps to its correct surgical tool, with special cases like lobe and eye noted.\n\nMass and hook objects now encode their tool transitions. Mass starts as scalpel and switches to forceps after its ring completes. Hook cuts are separate scalpel objects; the hook accepts forceps only after those cuts finish, tearing if pulled too early. Syringe shots that miss targets go to the IV. Targeted syringes apply to clot, reed, voice, and lobe C. The design is complete and ready for writing.\n\nA README note will be added to PROGRESS. The test runner will output only failures and one line. A debug script will print during development; the official test stays quiet. applyTool may be split for easier debugging. Determinism is ensured by clearing ev each step and excluding it from state hashes, while player fields in hashes are limited to x, y, tool, score, held, and heat. Math.random will be replaced with a deterministic S.rng wrapper.\n\nThe deterministic RNG uses a 32-bit LCG with bitwise shuffles and wraps zero to one. Case 7 pools drift inside cavities via sine-driven displacement scaled by tilt amplitude. Blackout mode dims the screen while keeping the sonar lens active; bleeders remain visible only through that lens. Suture targets shift ±6 units each frame based on sine of world time, so the bot must aim at the current moving position.\n\nI will bundle the new logic into stepObjects. I am now writing the files directly.\n\nFour bugs are planned for prevention. Phase clearing will ignore nerves and done-but-alive objects. The bot will release the stick within two units of a target to avoid oscillation. Tool swaps will wait until the held tool matches the desired one and the swap timer is zero. A guard will prevent infinite loops on uncompletable objects.\n\nSalve patches will use a 4-unit grid inside the 14-unit radius. The bot will sweep every cell center to guarantee 90% coverage. Suture objects will carry a fine flag for case 5, and the bot will hold the fine tool while stitching.\n\nThe bot will use an imperative plan state with modes like seek, hold, pull, paint, and circle. Each device() call advances the plan based on that state. Pull mode targets a velocity of 80 along the axis. A stickFor helper converts that speed into cursor magnitude using hand tuning parameters.\n\nThe pull-mode math checks out: a velocity of 80 along the axis converts to roughly 0.435 cursor magnitude, which scales back to about 80.2 units per second.\n\nRamp pulls will approach the target over 0.06 seconds with the stick held steady; overshoot is small.\n\nDuring pulling, the cursor must stay within eight units of the axis, and the stick direction will be exactly aligned with that axis.\n\nAfter a pull is released, the bot will continue along the axis until it reaches at least 24 units away before releasing. Releasing a dish object resets its trigger to zero. The bot will output trigger values of 0 or 1. Tool selection will use only the left stick for 0.1 seconds while keeping the right stick aim at zero so the cursor does not drift; any slight magnet pull during selection is acceptable.\n\nThe magnet will not fight a deliberate pull. It only activates when the stick is near neutral, and during pulling the right stick drives the motion while the left stays idle. The magnet also slows speed within 22 units of a target, which could retard both approach and departure.\n\nThe held object sits at the cursor, so the magnet always applies during hold. Speed drops by 0.55 times assist, reducing an 80-unit pull to roughly 54 units. That remains below the 120 tear threshold. The pull stick is never neutral, so the magnet does not fight the motion. When leaving for a dish, the target stays at the cursor because the object follows it.\n\nMagnet speed reduction will be disabled during active strokes and pulls to avoid confusing the pull speed test and tear threshold. Guide follow during scalpel strokes is harmless because pushing exactly along the line yields zero cross-component stick input.\n\nMagnet is always active. Tests will verify neutral-stick settling on a target, full-stick movement on an empty field with speed reduced by assist, fine-tool speed capped at 120, and guide-follow during strokes.\n\nAt full assist, the 0.55 speed reduction still leaves about 148 units per second, so movement remains possible. The empty-field speed test will run with no nearby targets.\n\nFor guide-follow, a stroke with a large perpendicular stick component will be pushed at assist 1. The cross-component scale is 0.2, so a 45-degree deviation yields only about 20 units of sideways drift over 100 units of forward travel. The slip tolerance is 9 units, so the cursor must stay within that bound.\n\nA rough push at full assist will deviate only about five units from the line over eighty units of travel. The test will assert the cursor stays within eight units of the intended path.\n\nGuide follow will be applied to the velocity before integration, using a horizontal tangent of (1, 0) at the cursor. The config, simulation, content, and bot will be written in one batch, with a minimal case-1 test added first to avoid a broken initial run. VM script execution will use an IIFE that reads from the global GAME object.\n\nThe VM context will expose `window` and `globalThis` as the same object, so scripts can read `GAME` through either property. Node tests will load files with `vm.runInContext`, and the context will include Node's `performance`. Story and cast data will be bundled into `content.js` so the validator can check IDs.\n\nPortrait expressions are neutral, smile, angry, sad, shock, shout, tired, cry, and smirk. Each cast member carries a list of expressions the painter supports. The validator checks that every story line’s expression belongs to that set.\n\nBody sites are forearm, abdomen, flank, heart, brain, implant, mediastinum, and ship. Each site maps to a bake function ID.\n\nThe content must include at least forty quill barks. The validator checks that the array length is at least forty.\n\nA forbidden-name scan will run over the source directory during testing.\n\nPROGRESS.md and notes/feedback.md will be created with initial content.\n\nThe booting page and shell will be added after the simulation works. The step function overwrites position from the cursor x,y inputs, so tests must pass updated coordinates. Tool selection is handled by both the wheel and step inputs. A double-move bug is avoided because velocity is applied only once per frame.\n\nThe game loop runs Cursor.update first to integrate velocity, then step receives the updated absolute position. Unit tests will call step directly with explicit coordinates, or update only the cursor and read its position. To compute speed, step will store the previous frame’s coordinates in p.px and p.py, calculate movement distance over dt at the start of each frame, and overwrite the stored values at the end.\n\nThe code will be split into four files: config.js, core/sim.js, content.js, and bot.js. sim.js will contain the integrated strain controllers in a STRAINS map. A syntax check will run after each write.\n\nPhase definitions now include a strain type and a script array of timed actions. Scripts will drive vitals, drain, object shifts, rupture spawns, dimming, tilting, and clock resets. A phase clears only when all required objects are done and the current strain is finished or absent.\n\nTutorial mode will freeze the game until the bot completes its first tool action. It applies only to the opening phases of cases 1 through 4, and it will be skipped on crash phases so vitals can still drop. Salve healing will scale with the patient’s healing stat multiplied by two. The syringe IV base value is set to eighteen.\n\nSyringe IV dose scales with difficulty: Intern adds 24, Attending adds 12, and the base slider value is eighteen. The tool has a 0.35-second cooldown. Air bubbles trigger when the syringe is held past full fill for 0.3 seconds.\n\nSyringe IV is graded clean unless an air bubble occurs. The award function scales points by a grade multiplier and the current flow value, then updates streaks, best flow, and overall technique counts.\n\nThe award function adds charge to the steady gauge for textbook and clean grades, resets streaks and flow for sloppy grades, and logs every grade event. The slip function deducts vitals scaled by the patient’s slip stat, resets streak and flow, and records the incident; tears are counted as slips to avoid double-counting. Final score is total score plus two points per remaining clock second and three points per remaining vital.\n\nRankLetter computes a letter grade from the score-to-par ratio, capped at B if the rank cap is set. An S+ badge appears only for attending difficulty with a perfect score and zero slips. Retrying a case sets the rank cap to B. The case definition will supply a nominal par value, and the final rank is assigned when the case ends.\n\nPar will start at 5000 and drop after calibration so the bot avoids a D rank. Playthrough tests will verify the case ends with time remaining and vitals at least 40. Case 3 drains 3.5 points per second, so the bot must suture quickly or vitals will hit zero.\n\nArteries are ordered before bleeders, pools, and fragments. Nine stitches across three arteries take roughly seven seconds plus travel. Vitals drain 3.5 points per second, so the bot must suture arteries first to prevent death. The stitch time is estimated at 0.4 seconds if the points are twelve units apart and the cursor moves fast.\n\nThe perfect bot will automatically inject IV if vitals drop below forty-five and the syringe is off cooldown. This safety measure prevents death without cheating. Case 3 phase one allows 150 seconds of clock time, so vitals are the real survival threat.\n\nA `takeCore` function will snapshot the core simulation state for checkpoints, cloning objects and strains with `structuredClone`. The syringe is confirmed as the panic button.\n\nObject cloning will use JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(...)) to strip functions and undefined values. The player cursor is excluded from the core state, so restoring a checkpoint clears held objects and strokes. Ghosts are rebuilt from the ring buffer. Network snapshots will exclude the ring to keep the payload small. Comments will be kept concise.\n\nThe project is estimated at roughly six thousand lines across all files. I will now write sim.js with high quality. A `createRaw` helper will let tests spawn bare simulation states for tool validation. A step-by-step movement helper is also being prepared for the forceps test.\n\nA `slide` helper will animate cursor movement over a fixed duration by stepping the simulation frame by frame. Tool tests will use this to pull 20-unit tears at 200 units per second and scalpel along lines with minimal deviation. The test scenarios are now clear.\n\nA deterministic hash will be computed from the core state and player positions to detect non-deterministic runs. The draw test will verify that rendering does not mutate the simulation state. A read-only drawing function will count pixels without altering the scene.\n\nThe determinism test will compare simulation hashes with drawing enabled and disabled. A headless run will step the engine once per mode, then hash the final state via debug.snapshot. A simpler sim-level equivalent will read-only draw to a canvas between steps before hashing.\n\nThe tissue painter will be placed in art.js and tested by loading the engine. A short two-second scripted operation will verify that drawing does not throw errors. The full case 1 playthrough will also be rendered once to catch any draw-time exceptions.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-fae42668-e669-4525-8ca3-7fea65415596-46","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"mkCanvas =|function mkCanvas|E\\.mkCanvas","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223916__x488eb9/work/Open-Table-Second-Opinion-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223916/vendor/my-3d2dge/dist/handoff/my-3d2dge.js","head_limit":5}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1123,"output_tokens":7503,"cache_read_input_tokens":184704,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa37-f7cf-7001-9198-38ad04ed0f5e","uuid":"caf9cde5-0737-4a21-b6d7-338edd804130"}
