{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-93a46d55-5d96-4657-9942-55b38371e283-33","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"220→        started = true;\\n        return toGame({ type: 'start', in: Math.round(m.at - clock.now()) });\\n      case 'leave':\\n        gone.add(m.id);\\n        return toGame({ type: 'leave', id: m.id, reason: m.reason });\\n      case 'time-up':\\n        return toGame({ type: 'time-up', graceMs: m.graceMs });\\n      case 'end':\\n        ended = true;\\n        return toGame({ type: 'end', reason: m.reason, ...(m.standings ? { standings: m.standings } : {}) });\\n230→    }\\n  }\\n\\n  return {\\n    key,\\n    round,\\n    fromGame,\\n    fromNet,\\n    /** The game's own card, while it is on: { left, paused, starting, ready, asking, asked } (lib/hub/README.md, \\\"The card\\\"). */\\n    card(state) {\\n240→      if (ownsCard && welcomed && !started && !ended) toGame({ type: 'card', ...state });\\n    },\\n    /** The page holding this frame was hidden or shown: the coordinator watches the host's. */\\n    visibility(hidden) {\\n      if (helloSent && !ended) toHub({ type: 'vis', hidden: !!hidden });\\n    },\\n    issues,\\n    stats,\\n    get me() {\\n      return me;\\n250→    },\\n    get ended() {\\n      return ended;\\n    },\\n    /** The game shows its own card, and has loaded: the hub's card steps aside for it. */\\n    get showsCard() {\\n      return ownsCard && readySent && !ended;\\n    },\\n  };\\n}\\n260→\\n/**\\n * Wire a bridge to an iframe: only messages from that frame's window count.\\n * Returns a function that unhooks it.\\n */\\nexport function attachFrame(frame, onMessage) {\\n  const listener = (event) => {\\n    if (event.source !== frame.contentWindow) return;\\n    if (!event.data || event.data.aihub !== 1) return;\\n    onMessage(event.data);\\n270→  };\\n  addEventListener('message', listener);\\n  return () => removeEventListener('message', listener);\\n}\\n\\n/** Post to a frame. The games may be served from anywhere, and nothing here is secret. */\\nexport const postTo = (frame) => (msg) => frame.contentWindow?.postMessage(msg, '*');\\n\\n/**\\n * A simulated network for the harness and tests: one-way latency with jitter,\\n280→ * and loss for unreliable messages. Reliable messages stay in order per pair,\\n * as a WebRTC ordered channel would keep them.\\n */\\nexport function createLocalNetwork({ latency = 0, jitter = 0, loss = 0, random = Math.random, clock = globalThis } = {}) {\\n  const endpoints = new Map();\\n  const pairs = new Map(); // \\\"from\\\\0to\\\" -> reliable messages waiting, in send order\\n  const now = () => (clock.performance?.now ? clock.performance.now() : Date.now());\\n  const settings = { latency, jitter, loss };\\n\\n  const deliver = (from, to, target, copy) => {\\n290→    if (endpoints.get(to) === target) for (const fn of target.listeners) fn(from, copy);\\n  };\\n\\n  /** Reliable traffic leaves each pair's queue strictly in order, never before its time. */\\n  function drain(pair) {\\n    const q = pairs.get(pair);\\n    q.timer = null;\\n    while (q.items.length && q.items[0].at <= now() + 0.5) {\\n      const { from, to, target, copy } = q.items.shift();\\n      deliver(from, to, target, copy);\",\"content_concise\":\"220→        started = true;\\n        return toGame({ type: 'start', in: Math.round(m.at - clock.now()) });\\n      case 'leave':\\n        gone.add(m.id);\\n        return toGame({ type: 'leave', id: m.id, reason: m.reason });\\n      case 'time-up':\\n        return toGame({ type: 'time-up', graceMs: m.graceMs });\\n      case 'end':\\n        ended = true;\\n        return toGame({ type: 'end', reason: m.reason, ...(m.standings ? { standings: m.standings } : {}) });\\n230→    }\\n  }\\n\\n  return {\\n    key,\\n    round,\\n    fromGame,\\n    fromNet,\\n    /** The game's own card, while it is on: { left, paused, starting, ready, asking, asked } (lib/hub/README.md, \\\"The card\\\"). */\\n    card(state) {\\n240→      if (ownsCard && welcomed && !started && !ended) toGame({ type: 'card', ...state });\\n    },\\n    /** The page holding this frame was hidden or shown: the coordinator watches the host's. */\\n    visibility(hidden) {\\n      if (helloSent && !ended) toHub({ type: 'vis', hidden: !!hidden });\\n    },\\n    issues,\\n    stats,\\n    get me() {\\n      return me;\\n250→    },\\n    get ended() {\\n      return ended;\\n    },\\n    /** The game shows its own card, and has loaded: the hub's card steps aside for it. */\\n    get showsCard() {\\n      return ownsCard && readySent && !ended;\\n    },\\n  };\\n}\\n260→\\n/**\\n * Wire a bridge to an iframe: only messages from that frame's window count.\\n * Returns a function that unhooks it.\\n */\\nexport function attachFrame(frame, onMessage) {\\n  const listener = (event) => {\\n    if (event.source !== frame.contentWindow) return;\\n    if (!event.data || event.data.aihub !== 1) return;\\n    onMessage(event.data);\\n270→  };\\n  addEventListener('message', listener);\\n  return () => removeEventListener('message', listener);\\n}\\n\\n/** Post to a frame. The games may be served from anywhere, and nothing here is secret. */\\nexport const postTo = (frame) => (msg) => frame.contentWindow?.postMessage(msg, '*');\\n\\n/**\\n * A simulated network for the harness and tests: one-way latency with jitter,\\n280→ * and loss for unreliable messages. Reliable messages stay in order per pair,\\n * as a WebRTC ordered channel would keep them.\\n */\\nexport function createLocalNetwork({ latency = 0, jitter = 0, loss = 0, random = Math.random, clock = globalThis } = {}) {\\n  const endpoints = new Map();\\n  const pairs = new Map(); // \\\"from\\\\0to\\\" -> reliable messages waiting, in send order\\n  const now = () => (clock.performance?.now ? clock.performance.now() : Date.now());\\n  const settings = { latency, jitter, loss };\\n\\n  const deliver = (from, to, target, copy) => {\\n290→    if (endpoints.get(to) === target) for (const fn of target.listeners) fn(from, copy);\\n  };\\n\\n  /** Reliable traffic leaves each pair's queue strictly in order, never before its time. */\\n  function drain(pair) {\\n    const q = pairs.get(pair);\\n    q.timer = null;\\n    while (q.items.length && q.items[0].at <= now() + 0.5) {\\n      const { from, to, target, copy } = q.items.shift();\\n      deliver(from, to, target, copy);\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223916__x488eb9/work/Open-Table-Second-Opinion-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223916/vendor/hub/bridge.js\",\"offset\":220,\"limit\":80,\"raw_output\":\"        started = true;\\n        return toGame({ type: 'start', in: Math.round(m.at - clock.now()) });\\n      case 'leave':\\n        gone.add(m.id);\\n        return toGame({ type: 'leave', id: m.id, reason: m.reason });\\n      case 'time-up':\\n        return toGame({ type: 'time-up', graceMs: m.graceMs });\\n      case 'end':\\n        ended = true;\\n        return toGame({ type: 'end', reason: m.reason, ...(m.standings ? { standings: m.standings } : {}) });\\n    }\\n  }\\n\\n  return {\\n    key,\\n    round,\\n    fromGame,\\n    fromNet,\\n    /** The game's own card, while it is on: { left, paused, starting, ready, asking, asked } (lib/hub/README.md, \\\"The card\\\"). */\\n    card(state) {\\n      if (ownsCard && welcomed && !started && !ended) toGame({ type: 'card', ...state });\\n    },\\n    /** The page holding this frame was hidden or shown: the coordinator watches the host's. */\\n    visibility(hidden) {\\n      if (helloSent && !ended) toHub({ type: 'vis', hidden: !!hidden });\\n    },\\n    issues,\\n    stats,\\n    get me() {\\n      return me;\\n    },\\n    get ended() {\\n      return ended;\\n    },\\n    /** The game shows its own card, and has loaded: the hub's card steps aside for it. */\\n    get showsCard() {\\n      return ownsCard && readySent && !ended;\\n    },\\n  };\\n}\\n\\n/**\\n * Wire a bridge to an iframe: only messages from that frame's window count.\\n * Returns a function that unhooks it.\\n */\\nexport function attachFrame(frame, onMessage) {\\n  const listener = (event) => {\\n    if (event.source !== frame.contentWindow) return;\\n    if (!event.data || event.data.aihub !== 1) return;\\n    onMessage(event.data);\\n  };\\n  addEventListener('message', listener);\\n  return () => removeEventListener('message', listener);\\n}\\n\\n/** Post to a frame. The games may be served from anywhere, and nothing here is secret. */\\nexport const postTo = (frame) => (msg) => frame.contentWindow?.postMessage(msg, '*');\\n\\n/**\\n * A simulated network for the harness and tests: one-way latency with jitter,\\n * and loss for unreliable messages. Reliable messages stay in order per pair,\\n * as a WebRTC ordered channel would keep them.\\n */\\nexport function createLocalNetwork({ latency = 0, jitter = 0, loss = 0, random = Math.random, clock = globalThis } = {}) {\\n  const endpoints = new Map();\\n  const pairs = new Map(); // \\\"from\\\\0to\\\" -> reliable messages waiting, in send order\\n  const now = () => (clock.performance?.now ? clock.performance.now() : Date.now());\\n  const settings = { latency, jitter, loss };\\n\\n  const deliver = (from, to, target, copy) => {\\n    if (endpoints.get(to) === target) for (const fn of target.listeners) fn(from, copy);\\n  };\\n\\n  /** Reliable traffic leaves each pair's queue strictly in order, never before its time. */\\n  function drain(pair) {\\n    const q = pairs.get(pair);\\n    q.timer = null;\\n    while (q.items.length && q.items[0].at <= now() + 0.5) {\\n      const { from, to, target, copy } = q.items.shift();\\n      deliver(from, to, target, copy);\\n\",\"total_lines\":339}}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-93a46d55-5d96-4657-9942-55b38371e283-34","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"250→    }\\n    if (m.type === 'fullscreen') {\\n      fullscreenOn = m.on === true;\\n      try {\\n        root.document.dispatchEvent(new root.Event('fullscreenchange'));\\n      } catch (e) {}\\n      return;\\n    }\\n    if (!welcomed || ended) return;\\n    if (m.type === 'start') {\\n260→      goAt = now() + Number(m.in || 0);\\n      emit('start', [{ in: Number(m.in || 0) }]);\\n    } else if (m.type === 'message') {\\n      emit('message', [m.data, m.from]);\\n    } else if (m.type === 'leave') {\\n      hub.players = hub.players.filter(function (p) {\\n        return p.id !== m.id;\\n      });\\n      emit('leave', [{ id: m.id, reason: m.reason }]);\\n    } else if (m.type === 'time-up') {\\n270→      emit('time-up', [{ graceMs: m.graceMs }]);\\n    } else if (m.type === 'end') {\\n      ended = true;\\n      if (progressTimer) clearTimeout(progressTimer);\\n      emit('end', [{ reason: m.reason, standings: m.standings || null }]);\\n    } else {\\n      // An event this client has never heard of: hand it on as it came.\\n      var detail = {};\\n      for (var k in m) if (own(m, k) && k !== 'aihub' && k !== 'type') detail[k] = m[k];\\n      emit(m.type, [detail]);\\n280→    }\\n  }\\n\\n  function info() {\\n    var out = {};\\n    for (var key in hub) if (own(hub, key) && typeof hub[key] !== 'function' && !KEEP[key]) out[key] = hub[key];\\n    out.players = hub.players.slice();\\n    return out;\\n  }\\n\\n290→  /** Join the session. Resolves with the round's info, or null when there is no hub: play normally. */\\n  hub.connect = function (opts) {\\n    if (connecting) return connecting.promise;\\n    var done;\\n    var promise = new Promise(function (resolve) {\\n      done = resolve;\\n    });\\n    connecting = { promise: promise, resolve: done };\\n    if (!hub.active) {\\n      done(null);\\n300→      return promise;\\n    }\\n    opts = opts || {};\\n    root.addEventListener('message', onMessage);\\n    var started = now();\\n    var hello = function () {\\n      if (welcomed) return;\\n      if (now() - started > HELLO_TIMEOUT_MS) {\\n        standAlone();\\n        warn('no hub answered; playing normally');\\n310→        done(null);\\n        return;\\n      }\\n      post('hello', { v: 1, client: VERSION, mode: opts.mode || null, seats: opts.seats || null, card: opts.card === true });\\n      setTimeout(hello, HELLO_EVERY_MS);\\n    };\\n    hello();\\n    return promise;\\n  };\\n\\n320→  /** Everything is loaded: at once when info.splash, otherwise from the player's ready gesture. */\\n  hub.ready = function () {\\n    if (!welcomed || sentReady || ended) return;\\n    sentReady = true;\\n    post('ready', {});\\n  };\\n\\n  /** On the game's own card (connect({ card: true })): this player is 'ready', or asks for 'more' time (again takes it back). */\\n  hub.answer = function (what) {\\n    if (!welcomed || ended || goAt !== null) return;\",\"content_concise\":\"250→    }\\n    if (m.type === 'fullscreen') {\\n      fullscreenOn = m.on === true;\\n      try {\\n        root.document.dispatchEvent(new root.Event('fullscreenchange'));\\n      } catch (e) {}\\n      return;\\n    }\\n    if (!welcomed || ended) return;\\n    if (m.type === 'start') {\\n260→      goAt = now() + Number(m.in || 0);\\n      emit('start', [{ in: Number(m.in || 0) }]);\\n    } else if (m.type === 'message') {\\n      emit('message', [m.data, m.from]);\\n    } else if (m.type === 'leave') {\\n      hub.players = hub.players.filter(function (p) {\\n        return p.id !== m.id;\\n      });\\n      emit('leave', [{ id: m.id, reason: m.reason }]);\\n    } else if (m.type === 'time-up') {\\n270→      emit('time-up', [{ graceMs: m.graceMs }]);\\n    } else if (m.type === 'end') {\\n      ended = true;\\n      if (progressTimer) clearTimeout(progressTimer);\\n      emit('end', [{ reason: m.reason, standings: m.standings || null }]);\\n    } else {\\n      // An event this client has never heard of: hand it on as it came.\\n      var detail = {};\\n      for (var k in m) if (own(m, k) && k !== 'aihub' && k !== 'type') detail[k] = m[k];\\n      emit(m.type, [detail]);\\n280→    }\\n  }\\n\\n  function info() {\\n    var out = {};\\n    for (var key in hub) if (own(hub, key) && typeof hub[key] !== 'function' && !KEEP[key]) out[key] = hub[key];\\n    out.players = hub.players.slice();\\n    return out;\\n  }\\n\\n290→  /** Join the session. Resolves with the round's info, or null when there is no hub: play normally. */\\n  hub.connect = function (opts) {\\n    if (connecting) return connecting.promise;\\n    var done;\\n    var promise = new Promise(function (resolve) {\\n      done = resolve;\\n    });\\n    connecting = { promise: promise, resolve: done };\\n    if (!hub.active) {\\n      done(null);\\n300→      return promise;\\n    }\\n    opts = opts || {};\\n    root.addEventListener('message', onMessage);\\n    var started = now();\\n    var hello = function () {\\n      if (welcomed) return;\\n      if (now() - started > HELLO_TIMEOUT_MS) {\\n        standAlone();\\n        warn('no hub answered; playing normally');\\n310→        done(null);\\n        return;\\n      }\\n      post('hello', { v: 1, client: VERSION, mode: opts.mode || null, seats: opts.seats || null, card: opts.card === true });\\n      setTimeout(hello, HELLO_EVERY_MS);\\n    };\\n    hello();\\n    return promise;\\n  };\\n\\n320→  /** Everything is loaded: at once when info.splash, otherwise from the player's ready gesture. */\\n  hub.ready = function () {\\n    if (!welcomed || sentReady || ended) return;\\n    sentReady = true;\\n    post('ready', {});\\n  };\\n\\n  /** On the game's own card (connect({ card: true })): this player is 'ready', or asks for 'more' time (again takes it back). */\\n  hub.answer = function (what) {\\n    if (!welcomed || ended || goAt !== null) return;\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223916__x488eb9/work/Open-Table-Second-Opinion-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223916/vendor/hub/session.js\",\"offset\":250,\"limit\":80,\"raw_output\":\"    }\\n    if (m.type === 'fullscreen') {\\n      fullscreenOn = m.on === true;\\n      try {\\n        root.document.dispatchEvent(new root.Event('fullscreenchange'));\\n      } catch (e) {}\\n      return;\\n    }\\n    if (!welcomed || ended) return;\\n    if (m.type === 'start') {\\n      goAt = now() + Number(m.in || 0);\\n      emit('start', [{ in: Number(m.in || 0) }]);\\n    } else if (m.type === 'message') {\\n      emit('message', [m.data, m.from]);\\n    } else if (m.type === 'leave') {\\n      hub.players = hub.players.filter(function (p) {\\n        return p.id !== m.id;\\n      });\\n      emit('leave', [{ id: m.id, reason: m.reason }]);\\n    } else if (m.type === 'time-up') {\\n      emit('time-up', [{ graceMs: m.graceMs }]);\\n    } else if (m.type === 'end') {\\n      ended = true;\\n      if (progressTimer) clearTimeout(progressTimer);\\n      emit('end', [{ reason: m.reason, standings: m.standings || null }]);\\n    } else {\\n      // An event this client has never heard of: hand it on as it came.\\n      var detail = {};\\n      for (var k in m) if (own(m, k) && k !== 'aihub' && k !== 'type') detail[k] = m[k];\\n      emit(m.type, [detail]);\\n    }\\n  }\\n\\n  function info() {\\n    var out = {};\\n    for (var key in hub) if (own(hub, key) && typeof hub[key] !== 'function' && !KEEP[key]) out[key] = hub[key];\\n    out.players = hub.players.slice();\\n    return out;\\n  }\\n\\n  /** Join the session. Resolves with the round's info, or null when there is no hub: play normally. */\\n  hub.connect = function (opts) {\\n    if (connecting) return connecting.promise;\\n    var done;\\n    var promise = new Promise(function (resolve) {\\n      done = resolve;\\n    });\\n    connecting = { promise: promise, resolve: done };\\n    if (!hub.active) {\\n      done(null);\\n      return promise;\\n    }\\n    opts = opts || {};\\n    root.addEventListener('message', onMessage);\\n    var started = now();\\n    var hello = function () {\\n      if (welcomed) return;\\n      if (now() - started > HELLO_TIMEOUT_MS) {\\n        standAlone();\\n        warn('no hub answered; playing normally');\\n        done(null);\\n        return;\\n      }\\n      post('hello', { v: 1, client: VERSION, mode: opts.mode || null, seats: opts.seats || null, card: opts.card === true });\\n      setTimeout(hello, HELLO_EVERY_MS);\\n    };\\n    hello();\\n    return promise;\\n  };\\n\\n  /** Everything is loaded: at once when info.splash, otherwise from the player's ready gesture. */\\n  hub.ready = function () {\\n    if (!welcomed || sentReady || ended) return;\\n    sentReady = true;\\n    post('ready', {});\\n  };\\n\\n  /** On the game's own card (connect({ card: true })): this player is 'ready', or asks for 'more' time (again takes it back). */\\n  hub.answer = function (what) {\\n    if (!welcomed || ended || goAt !== null) return;\\n\",\"total_lines\":488}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa37-f7cf-7001-9198-38ad04ed0f5e","uuid":"b5843117-f5f3-4252-ab8b-d5807e54c5b6"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_57","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The showsCard condition requires both ownsCard and readySent to be true.\n\nThe harness sequence is: connect with card, call ready(), wait for the card to appear, then answer ready. Enter likely maps to confirm.\n\nThe Enter key maps to the confirm action, so pressed('confirm') will fire. Card clicks and triangle or M inputs are also needed.\n\nWhen the host leaves, do not call finish(). The harness removes the host and expects a void round with endReason host-left; calling finish() would incorrectly mark it as played. Only call finish() on time-up.\n\nIn server mode, AIHub.server === true indicates a host without a local player.\n\nThe world should run with no drawing and send snapshots. It calls ready() immediately and sends no input. It finishes only on time-up.\n\nDuring the play phase, both clients and host must exchange messages. The default play duration is 6000 ms. Snapshot updates run at 12 per second and input at 15 per second. This traffic is sufficient.\n\nThe code must not throw or alert.\n\nWhile in a session, the menu should not include a quit option. The world must remain live so it keeps running.\n\nMath.random must not be used anywhere in the game code. The shared-world simulation uses xorshift. The host seeds its simulation from AIHub.rng('sim').\n\nBot and art noise use their own RNG. The tissue painter uses makeNoise instead of Math.random.\n\nMath.random is safe to use in cosmetic title rain because the hub scene never displays the title. Shared-world mode does not require seeding for Math.random; only parallel and ghost modes do.\n\nThe Enter key requires the game to be focused. The engine listens on the window, so the contentDocument poke should work. The canvas may need focus for game.input.\n\nA window-level keydown listener will catch Enter and M presses as a fallback to answer ready and more. The harness pokes after ready while the phase is still wait, before releaseRound. The card event handler stores the card and makes showsCard true; tickCard sends it, so the card must be drawn.\n\nDrawing the card is sufficient; the bridge derives showsCard from readySent rather than from the drawing itself.\n\nOnly the host may call finish(). If a client finishes after the host leaves, it triggers a protocol error. The coordinator voids the round instead.\n\nThe server copy must not finish if the host has already left. It should still call ready() and send snapshots, but it will not draw anything because it is not a local player. Snapshot messages must stay under 1100 bytes; grand rounds state can be large.\n\nSnapshot messages will be compact, carrying clock, vitals, phase, steady status, player positions, and a rotating slice of objects. The harness only needs small state to keep traffic flowing.\n\nClient input will be an object with timestamp, name, coordinates, tool, trigger, fine, and gift. The host applies this to the matching player in S.players by index.\n\nPlayer indices map directly to info.players order. Each player stores its network id for this lookup.\n\nA window.__hubProbe function will expose phase, over, player positions, tools, triggers, and host status for testing.\n\nThe client cursor will move instantly, while the host updates from input messages. The test will set the client's mine position and wait for the host probe to match.\n\nI am now writing the implementation files. The plan covers sim.js, bot.js, content.js, art.js, audio.js, game.js, main.js, tests/run.mjs, tools/build.mjs, CONTENT.md, and index.dev.html.\n\nI will fix bugs in sim.js before writing the large files. Content cases must be beatable, so I will design phases with objects the bot can understand.\n\nThe bot algorithm is taking shape as a `device` function that returns a device state for each player. It handles gone players, won or lost states, code blue, dressing, and gifts.\n\nThe bot gift logic now triggers breathing when steady is at least 35 and the active document is vale, or when both gifts are available. It triggers looking when steady is at least 45 and no ghost is present. The pickGoal function targets the lowest-order alive required object, sending the cursor toward hidden unmarked items with a sonar sweep.\n\nThe bot's goal selection now includes pool suction when a pool's order is lower than the target object. Strains are treated as goals.\n\nSpecial behaviors:\n- Reed and voice: wait near but not on the object, prefilling the syringe.\n- Lobe: wait if closed.\n- Eye: wait if closed; sonar sweep if hidden.\n- Repeat: slash immediately when open.\n- Spiral: move along the guide only when off beat.\n- Retractor: player 0 holds it if another player needs a window; others cut.\n\nSolo mode opens the window automatically at 0.5. Tool selection uses digits 1 through 8 immediately. The cursor moves instantly to the target point, and pulls travel along the axis at a speed limited by object tension and delta time to keep under 120 units per second.\n\nPulls release after moving to a dish point outside or 30 units along the axis. Scalpel strokes walk along the guide at 80 units per second. Suture moves from pair[0] to pair[1] in 0.4 seconds and releases. Salve holds trigger on each unpainted cell. Syringe IV holds away from targets for 0.6 seconds. Cautery sits on the target until completion, releasing before heat reaches 1.\n\nCautery must pulse because heat rises by 0.5 per second and most targets burn in under two seconds, but Tremass needs three. The bot releases when heat exceeds 0.7, waits to cool, then resumes burning until the target finishes.\n\nScrew orbiting is required. Retractor holding means pressing trigger on the handle. Sonar requires moving to a hidden object and holding trigger for 0.2 seconds. Wheel digit selection depends on the tool name. Fine mode activates when depth is small, tension is below fifty, or during spiral or suture stitches.\n\nFine mode is reserved for precision work. Code blue triggers fire as single-frame edges at fixed peaks spaced 0.9 seconds apart, releasing immediately after each press. Dressing requires pressing when the timer modulo 1.2 is within a 0.05-second window. If the bot remains stuck on a goal for twelve seconds, it will attempt to break out by suctioning all pools, scanning with sonar, or injecting an IV if vitals are low.\n\nIV injection is reserved for vitals below forty-five unless an urgent condition like code, rupture, or artery is active. Clot requires a syringe followed by forceps. Mass uses the scalpel ring guide, then forceps to extract once cut. Nests and lobe slashes are handled by teleporting across the object at high speed to slash it, then forceps to extract; if the slash leaves the cut incomplete, forceps finishes the extraction.\n\nNest and lobe slash logic is now defined: nests trigger a cut and forceps extraction, while lobe slashes switch the bot to forceps mode. Hidden staccato nests are marked by sonar and cleared within four seconds. Once all nests are handled, the bot waits for the strain phase. Lacerations spawn on land and require salve; the strain phase only ends after both lacerations and collapse are resolved. Legato slurs appear over time and must be cut.\n\nHead shard tension is seventy, pulled at forty units per second and released at the dish. The bot repeats this until the worm is split; barb optional. A holdZone on the head would softlock if the bot releases inside it, so that zone will be skipped unless the mechanic is required. When the head is fully dished, stepLegato spawns the next head.\n\nLegato heads will not use hold zones. The bot can release them outside the dish without softlocking.\n\nDa Capo lobe windows open at t=0 for 2.5 seconds on every 6, 2.5 on every 7, and 3 on every 9. The bot must kill all three lobe types before the 10-second countdown begins.\n\nDa Capo lobe kills must finish within each window. The first round needs about 3.5 seconds: cautery A, slash and forceps B, then syringe and salve C. The second round halves the windows to roughly 1.25 seconds.\n\nBeads are handled between lobe windows and do not block lobe interaction. The bot should succeed in the Da Capo countdown. Fermata requires three cautery burns on the eye, each lasting 0.3 seconds within a 0.7-second window. Stage two uses sonar to find the eye and slivers. Stage three pulls with tension at forty units per second.\n\nThe eye object resets its done flag and increments hits each time it is burned, allowing the bot to burn it again after the first three hits. Stage two triggers once the hit count reaches four and slivers are cleared. The Cantor move uses three scalpel tendrils and three uvulas, with cautery held for 1.5 seconds; heat rises to 0.75 in that interval, which stays under the lock threshold.\n\nMove 2 uses sonar to find voices and doses them within their windows. Domes expire and jitter occurs. Fine mode is used.\n\nMove 3 waits for winding then slashes the repeat guide six times within three seconds. The total world time is about 120 seconds. Vitals start at seventy but drain could kill the bot. I will raise case drain to 0.08 and vitals to eighty and clock to five hundred so the bot survives.\n\nMove 4 locks spiral tracing to off-beat moments only. It then pulls the note at tension eighty and performs six stitches.\n\nThe spiral guide will be about 160 units long. The bot pauses during the beat lock window and slips once, but progress is clamped to the safe distance before the next step.\n\nThe spiral completion check uses st.far rather than o.prog. Advancing far during the beat lock therefore completes the spiral and triggers a single slip. This is acceptable because playthroughs are judged by clock and vitals, not rank.\n\nThe bot will pause during beat locks to avoid slips. Mouse input sets position directly rather than velocity, and guide-follow tracks progress by position along the path. A missed repeat-slash in move three triggers forceRewind, restoring six seconds for a retry. The repeat guide is a short horizontal line; the bot waits at the start and slashes quickly once winding begins. Signs require the repeat object to be open before they can be touched, and slashing a sign marks it done. A double-count bug in stepCantor was fixed.\n\nThe redundant sign-increment block will be removed so signs only count once. Da Capo round two beads must be burned because the strain phase requires them done, even if nests collapse without them. Staccato syncBeads creates moving bead objects that cautery can kill, though this is optional if nests collapse instead. Bites are lacerations. The brush covers roughly thirteen cells within a radius of eight units at four-unit steps; about twelve cells must be hit to satisfy ninety percent coverage.\n\nPool suction at sixty per second outpaces the bleeder at six and the artery at fourteen, so bleeding remains manageable. Fragments placed under pools cannot be tagged with pool IDs because object IDs are assigned only when added; the bot will instead check nearby pools to determine coverage.\n\nA convention is added so that `addObj` resolves `underTag` by looking up the pool object by tag and assigning its ID to the fragment. The bot will suction that pool if the fragment is covered. The create-clock patch and other fixes are also being applied. A cursor test is planned: a one-second full-stick input on an empty field with no nearby target, using a 0.06-second speed ramp, should yield a speed near 310.\n\nThe cursor test confirms the speed integral reaches about 310 units over one second, exceeding the 300 target. Fine mode yields roughly 108 units, safely under the 120 limit. A magnet test is planned for a target within twenty-two units with assist at 0.6 and pull at fifty-four units per second. Guide-follow will be tested by starting a stroke, pushing perpendicular, and crossing with scale reduced to 0.2. The wheel mechanic maps a north stick press for 0.1 seconds to forceps, then digit 1 through 8.\n\nThe official test runner will be run.mjs and print only failures. A scratch script will print more details during development. The award flow is confirmed: the first action sets tutDone. To prevent unit tests from auto-wining raw drills, createRaw will default hold to true so tests can inspect state before phaseClear finishes the case. The game uses create() instead of createRaw.\n\nThe hold flag is confirmed to work correctly: tests can inspect strain.done without winning, while the game advances phases normally. When the final phase clears, dressing begins before the case finishes, and multiple phases chain together properly.\n\nScripts fire on phaseT, but tutorial phases freeze phaseT until tutDone. Case 3 phase 3 script sets vitals to zero at t=1; maybeDie detects this and starts code blue. The bot shocks itself, revives vitals to thirty, and cauteries ruptures already in the phase.\n\nCode blue freezes the world, so ruptures do not drain during that period. Afterward they drain at 2.7 per second. A 30-vital bot can survive about four seconds of cautery and travel before vitals reach zero. The 12-second window is therefore safe if the bot acts quickly; a slower attempt would die on the second code blue.\n\nCase 3 drain is lowered to 0.15 so the bot survives despite three arteries and pools. The first phase is the hardest because three arteries drain one unit per second each, plus pools. A five-second suture window leaves enough time to survive. Fed pools will be optional so the player must suction them.\n\nSuturing takes priority over suctioning pools. Pools do not block suturing unless they are tagged under, and fed pools lack that tag. Suturing reduces artery drain, so clearing sutures first is safer than suctioning blood pools first.\n\nThe bot will prioritize the highest-drain non-pool hazards first, such as arteries and bleeder pools. Pools are treated as blockers, but suction is only considered once all other non-pool blockers are done. The pickGoal logic is being drafted to enforce this ordering.\n\nPool suction is assigned order fifty so clinical targets are prioritized first. Fed pools receive order plus one, which may interrupt a wound but is acceptable because suction is fast. Clinical objects will use orders one through ten. If the best target is hidden, sonar will be used to locate it.\n\nIf the only goal is blocked by a window, the bot waits rather than picking a different target. Reed objects are prefilled when nearby.\n\nThe act() state machine will live on the bot module or in a WeakMap keyed by the S object identity, not inside the simulation state, to keep takeCore deterministic. Rewind logic will reset this memory whenever worldT jumps backward.\n\nThe scene update order is being fixed so Cursor.update runs before readInputs. This lets the bot compute its instant position from the stored lx value instead of receiving live x and y coordinates.\n\nThe bot device now receives dt so it can pulse during cautery heat lock. Gift and consult edge detection is confirmed. Case 10 starts with both doctors active because create passes doctor: 'both'.\n\nThe gift logic now defaults to breath when the active doctor is sokolova, and to second look otherwise. Consult toggles between vale and sokolova. When the doctor is set to both, activeDoc initializes as vale so consult can cycle through the two doctors cleanly. Passive mode uses vale speed and sokolova assist.\n\nConsult flags will be set by fermata-phase scripts to trigger breath or look checks. Case 10 starts with both doctors and consult enabled. Quill barks are being added as a forty-plus string data array. Story nodes for each case are being structured as reachable graph entries, with validators ensuring every node is reachable from start or listed in the case intro.\n\nThe story engine pauses at an `op` event with `wait: true` until the scene calls `story.resume()`. During skip playthroughs the scene starts the operation immediately and advances quickly. The `_fire` method sets a held flag when the step contains an event, which the scene checks to resume the narrative.\n\nThe story flow is now fully mapped: a case node ends with an `op` event that triggers the operation, and once it finishes the scene calls `story.resume()` to advance. The test will snapshot the current story node, let the bot play the operation, confirm results, then proceed to the ward door. The ward will be built as a real tilemap with a humanoid, even if that is more complex than a simple placeholder.\n\nA minimal ward would use a position walker with a door and named patient markers. A real TileMap is preferred instead, using `new E.TileMap(rows, tileset)` and `new E.Humanoid` from the AI_GUIDE.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-bc2af5e7-c844-404d-9361-182cbdedaa6a-35","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"new E\\.TileMap|class TileMap|TileMap\\(","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223916__x488eb9/work/Open-Table-Second-Opinion-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223916/vendor/my-3d2dge/dist/handoff/my-3d2dge.js","head_limit":15}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1871,"output_tokens":7843,"cache_read_input_tokens":131968,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa37-f7cf-7001-9198-38ad04ed0f5e","uuid":"0fab03a8-3103-4f9a-9a63-2374b72ffe23"}
