t\":1,\"file_matches\":[{\"path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223916__x488eb9/work/Open-Table-Second-Opinion-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223916/vendor/my-3d2dge/dist/handoff/API-INDEX.md\",\"matches\":[{\"line_number\":286,\"content\":\"- `input.consumeAll()`: forget every pending press (after closing a menu or dialog, so the same press does not also attack)\"}]}]}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa37-f7cf-7001-9198-38ad04ed0f5e","uuid":"d2fda1ad-39c4-4c50-b63d-4d1c67331809"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_62","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Beginning implementation. I'll write the simulation patches first, then tackle the larger files.\n\nFixing the `stepTremolo` signature mismatch. Removing double speed from `stepDacapo`. Correcting lobe C cell initialization when syringes dose a salve target.\n\nLobe salve cells are placed in a 6-unit radius around the target. The brush radius of 10 covers them completely from the center, so one frame should paint all cells and finish the salve.\n\nThe second round's 1.5-second window is too short for sequential actions. Doing C first still leaves A without enough time before the window closes. Parallel execution is impossible with one bot and one cursor. I'm weighing alternative strategies.\n\nThe 10-second countdown starts at the first kill. B and C windows repeat every 3.5 and 4.5 seconds, so they remain available for roughly 1.25 and 1.5 seconds each. This gives enough time to kill all three lobes sequentially within the window limit.\n\nKilling all three lobes while the 10-second countdown is still running prevents round two from starting. The code only advances to round two once the count reaches zero. This means the bot must finish the first round before the timer expires.\n\nKilling all three lobes before the 10-second countdown expires triggers a clean round-two revive with no vitals penalty. The code sets the count to negative one and immediately starts the next round. Missing one lobe would revive all and penalize the bot instead. Round two ends once all beads are dead.\n\nRound two uses a 10-second countdown per cycle. If the bot fails to kill all three lobes before the timer expires, it revives them and increments repeated. The round loops until every lobe is killed within that window.\n\nRound two ends immediately when all beads are dead, even if the countdown is still running. The bot will kill the three lobes in round one, then kill the four beads and remaining lobes in round two. It tracks the current round using `S.strain.round` and `S.strain.kind`.\n\nThe bot will idle while waiting for the countdown to expire after a lobe dies. Beads spawn at round two and can be burned immediately. Suture mean grade is 0.4 seconds, matching the textbook target. Scalpel mean distance is near zero when the cursor rests on the line.\n\nForceps pull times are being checked against parTime. At depth 16 the target is 0.55 seconds, and pulling 16 units at 80 units per second plus dish travel takes about 0.3 seconds, so it fits easily. At depth 40 the target is 0.85 seconds; pulling 40 units at 58 speed plus exit might exceed that, but a clean grade is acceptable for survival.\n\nNo tears and a pull that exceeds parTime still earns clean, not sloppy. The bot will inject IV when vitals fall below fifty and the cool timer is zero, restoring eighteen points. Mouse steps must stay small to avoid snapping. The bot memory object will track state, goal, phase, time, and fill time.\n\nThe bot will use a phase per tool. A `play(S, seconds)` helper will run unit tests at sixty frames per second until the strain ends. If the bot stays stuck on the same goal for eight seconds with no progress, it will log the issue and attempt an IV injection or skip. In grand rounds with multiple players, player zero holds the retractor while the others cut; with one player the window is halved. The test case mixes staccato and dacapo strains, so I may scale down the numbers to avoid requiring four players.\n\nA simple round-robin assignment will let each bot claim a unique goal. Players sort the available goals by index and pick the ones whose index modulo player count matches their own. If no goals fit, they fall back to a shared default. This prevents multiple bots from targeting the same object at once.\n\nTwo players pulling the same shard could conflict because each player holds one grip. Partitioning goals is safer.\n\nIn grand rounds, player zero holds the retractor while the other bots cut. A solo window target can be cut directly. Player zero only holds the retractor and does not cut.\n\nPlayer zero will hold the retractor by triggering it at its position. The retractor must be excluded from phaseClear checks, or it would block the phase forever while alive and unfinished. Nerve, eloquent, light, spot, and screw objects will also be ignored in phaseClear.\n\nThe simulation patch list is now complete. It covers player creation, input reading, tear cooldowns, slash targeting, syringe salve following, clock double removal, active document switching, phaseClear retractor exclusion, and default hold behavior in createRaw. Tremolo will use a one-second period with a 0.7 flare threshold and clear reeds upon completion. Dacapo will lose its double time multiplier.\n\nCantor signs once, dome ticks, and voices play outside. Step cantor dome decay is added. UnderTag is included in addObj. Cheat flags are handled in step. The fed pool suction behavior is left unchanged. Mass cuts already set done correctly. Fast slash on a nest will be fixed. Solo cut logic is fine. ForceRewind on an empty ring leaves the sign unwound until the next cycle. A bug was found where stepCantor mutates the old state after forceRewind.\n\nThe bot will pulse during heat. It will not release the syringe reed while it is on a window. Tutorial phases freeze the clock until the first successful action, then unlock it for the rest of that phase. This applies to case one and phase four.\n\nOnly tutorial phase one will freeze the clock. Phase four dip will run at t=0.5 instead, so the IV injection can award the tutorial unlock immediately.\n\nLightbox is not part of the simulation.\n\nRank par calibration will be added later by running the bot and setting par to its score. The test for known inputs will compute rankLetter directly from that score.\n\nPar will be set to eighty percent of the measured perfect bot score so the rank stays stable at S. The test will verify that a finished case yields a letter grade and that rankLetter works correctly.\n\nA hash stability test will verify that deterministic runs with draw enabled produce the same hash as runs without it. The engine will not mutate state during drawing, and jitter uses world time so identical inputs yield identical hashes. Player positions are excluded from the hash.\n\nThe hash test will compare Op.hash(S1) against Op.hash(S2) and also verify that player positions match between runs. The core state hash excludes player coordinates, so cursor differences will not affect the result. Drawing does not alter the random number generator.\n\nGift breath world scale is verified: tool progress uses dt while the clock advances by worldDt. The second look test requires a filled ring and steady at least forty-five to confirm vitals survive damage and ghosts appear. A code blue test is also being drafted.\n\nA code-blue test will drain vitals to one, step until the code-blue state triggers, and verify that pressing at the peaks restores vitals to thirty. A lost state with zero vitals or a zero clock still loses without code blue. All tests will live in run.mjs, using a check helper to collect failures. The script will skip browser checks when given a --fast flag and exit with a non-zero status if any tests fail.\n\nThe test runner will print only FAIL lines and a one-line count, with no extra output. Debug logging in the game code must be avoided or gated so it does not interfere with verification.\n\nI will suppress engine warnings during tests to keep stdout clean. The test runner will set game.fadeTime to zero. Eleven playthrough cases should finish in roughly ten to thirty seconds.\n\nEleven test cases at full engine speed may take thirty to sixty seconds. The bot play function can step with a larger dt while the scene still advances at one-twelfth-hundred per update, keeping the simulation in sync with engine time.\n\nThe test game will be constructed with a 1/60 second step to halve runtime, while the human page keeps the default 1/120. The determinism test will use the same step value in both runs. Cursor updates are independent of the game step.\n\nI will follow the test.mjs pattern for input.tap during advance. I am starting to write files now, beginning with the simulation patches via multiple search_replace edits. Then I will create bot.js, content.js, art.js, audio.js, game.js, the tests, and the build script. game.js will contain both scenes and hub code to avoid load-order issues. The main boot call will be placed at the bottom of game.js so tests can load it without triggering an immediate startup.\n\nThe test runner will split into two VM contexts. Sim and bot tests will run in a headless environment without the engine. Playthrough tests will load the full engine. Art.js will guard its top-level My3D2dge reference so it can be imported safely in both contexts.\n\nDev setters will live in config.js so sim-only tests can mutate GAME.TUNE without loading the engine. Tests will verify that tuning changes vitals and that reset restores them.\n\nGAME.Dev.set splits a dot-separated path to mutate GAME.TUNE and marks it dirty. reset clones the base tuning. Tests omit an explicit tune argument so Op.step reads the live config directly. The clock bug in createRaw is also being patched.\n\nContent.js will be generated compactly. Story nodes will use a `line(who, say)` helper. Quill barks will be an array of forty-five strings. Every character gets the full set of nine portrait expressions. Cast IDs are vale, quill, oyelaran, sokolova, percy, odalys, hale, and clementine. Patients are agnes, slide, tam, ned, ilse, yusuf, perpetua, hollis, and chief is oyelaran, percy, and clementine.\n\nLightbox films for cases two, four, and ten will use a mark position inside the scene chart anomaly. The snapshot lightbox stores case ID, mark coordinates, tries, and done state. Mark positions are defined in content.js under GAME.LIGHT. PROGRESS.md will be updated at the end. The goal plan checklist will only flip after verification passes.\n\nTears will spawn a laceration to match the plan. The test will check vitals and slip kind, and confirm the laceration exists. The laceration will be required so the player must salve it.\n\nA small laceration with radius six and drain 0.2 will spawn at the tear location, tagged as 'tear', to match the plan. A 0.35-second cooldown prevents repeated spawns. Scalpel slips will use an optional cut so tests are not blocked. readInputs also needs fixing because it sets p.lx at the end.\n\nThe cursor position is initialized to the player spawn point so the first frame computes a zero delta. During test input, x and y coordinates are copied into the player state before the instant velocity is calculated from the previous position.\n\nWheel and cursor tests do not step the simulation. The cheat system will be placed inside the drain section so infSteady forces steady to one hundred, lockVitals preserves vitals, freezeClock pauses the clock, and oneHit multiplies tool duration by thirty.\n\nA `cheat` helper will be added to config.js so both the dev panel and tests can toggle hidden cheat flags on a live state object. Tests will verify that `lockVitals` preserves vitals during drain, while the UI test calls the same function directly.\n\nA `GAME.Dev.KEYS` list will enumerate every dev slider with its min, max, and step values. The test will iterate each key, set it to maximum, step a relevant simulation to verify the effect, then reset to default.\n\nThe full dev slider list now includes scalpel threshold, bead speed, cautery duration, bleeder drain, staccato timing, gift charge and breath drain, ghost time, and fluid pool rates. The test will verify a representative subset—drain, clock, cursor, suction, breathFactor, and syringe—confirming each change takes effect live and reset restores the default tuning.\n\nReset will use cloneTune so the default tuning is copied rather than referenced, and Op.step will read live GAME.TUNE each call to avoid stale cache. The wound-on-mass object will be added explicitly in content.js as a separate done-gated phase; case 2 phase four covers both mass and cork.\n\nCork will be placed in its own phase rather than hidden under the mass. The bot will play through all six phases.\n\nCase 4 gaff is split into three phases. Phase one holds two short guides and a long shard. Phase two holds sonar nests. Phase three holds repair bites and the gaff wound. Bites spawn during the strain phase and must be salved before that phase clears. The bot will salve them in phase two before the nests collapse. Phase clear requires both nests to collapse and all bites to be salved.","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"The sim’s rules are in place. I’m fixing the clock, hand-speed, and strain bugs that would stop a perfect run, then I’ll drive those paths with the shipped sim."},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-e460719f-544c-4184-b164-b57079a5ff3f-41","name":"search_replace","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223916__x488eb9/work/Open-Table-Second-Opinion-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223916/src/core/sim.js","old_string":"      gone: false,\n    };\n  }","new_string":"      gone: false, lx: 213, ly: 150, charT: 0, charSlip: false, bare: 0, bareT: 0,\n    };\n  }"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-e460719f-544c-4184-b164-b57079a5ff3f-42","name":"search_replace","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223916__x488eb9/work/Open-Table-Second-Opinion-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223916/src/core/sim.js","old_string":"    if (o.need == null) {","new_string":"    if (o.type === 'retractorHandle') o.type = 'retractor';\n    if (spec.underTag) {\n      const pool = S.objects.find(q => q.tag === spec.underTag);\n      if (pool) o.under = pool.id;\n    }\n    if (o.need == null) {"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-e460719f-544c-4184-b164-b57079a5ff3f-43","name":"search_replace","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223916__x488eb9/work/Open-Table-Second-Opinion-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223916/src/core/sim.js","old_string":"      if (o.type === 'nerve' || o.type === 'eloquent' || o.type === 'hold') continue;","new_string":"      if (o.type === 'nerve' || o.type === 'eloquent' || o.type === 'hold' || o.type === 'retractor') continue;"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-e460719f-544c-4184-b164-b57079a5ff3f-44","name":"search_replace","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223916__x488eb9/work/Open-Table-Second-Opinion-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223916/src/core/sim.js","old_string":"  function advancePhase(S) {\n    const def = (GAME.CASES || {})[S.id];\n    if (!def) { finishCase(S); return; }","new_string":"  function advancePhase(S) {\n    if (S.hold) return;\n    const def = (GAME.CASES || {})[S.id];\n    if (!def) { finishCase(S); return; }"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-e460719f-544c-4184-b164-b57079a5ff3f-45","name":"search_replace","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223916__x488eb9/work/Open-Table-Second-Opinion-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223916/src/core/sim.js","old_string":"    const diff = opts.difficulty || opts.diff || 'resident';\n    const clockMul = diff === 'intern' ? 1.3 : diff === 'attending' ? 0.8 : 1;\n    const S = blank({\n      id: def.id, seed: opts.seed || def.seed || 1, players: opts.players || 1,\n      difficulty: diff, clock: def.clock * clockMul, vitals: def.vitals, drain: def.drain,\n      gift: def.doctor === 'sokolova' ? 'look' : def.doctor === 'both' ? 'both' : 'breath',\n      doctor: def.doctor || 'vale', site: def.site, par: def.par || 1000,\n      mode: def.mode || 'case', 