mass') { o.done = false; o.alive = true; o.cut = true; o.type = 'mass'; }\\n          if (o.type === 'nest' || (o.type === 'lobe' && o.slash)) {\\n            o.done = false; o.alive = true; o.cut = true;\\n            if (o.type === 'lobe') o.mode = 'forceps';\\n          }\",\"new_line\":545,\"context_before\":\"        if (st.far >= (hit.total || 1) - 0.5) {\\n          const mean = st.n ? st.sum / st.n : 99;\\n          const g = st.slipped ? 'sloppy' : gradeMean(mean, 1.8 * tol, 4 * tol);\\n\",\"context_after\":\"          if (o.type === 'wire') { /* caller checks order */ wireCut(S, p, o, tune); }\\n          else award(S, p, g, 110, tune);\\n          p.stroke = null;\\n\",\"line_prefix\":\"\"}]}}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa37-f7cf-7001-9198-38ad04ed0f5e","uuid":"98e9e357-84de-46c5-8317-cd39c95055f9"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_67","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The grip predicate includes a check for lobe objects.\n\nSalve on lobe mode salve is confirmed. The bot implementation is the next critical piece. I will add dev keys to config.js, write content.js, and build a scratch test runner for iteration.\n\nThe bot code is being drafted with a WeakMap memory cache keyed by simulation state. A `mem` helper resets stale state after 0.05 seconds of inactivity. A `blockers` function filters out dead, finished, or optional objects.\n\nThe `blockers` function is being refined to explicitly exclude nerves, eloquent, hold, retractor, and standard beads. Dacapo beads are preserved because the strain phase ignores them but the bot still needs them. A new `ignored` helper is being drafted to centralize this logic.\n\nThe `ignored` helper is now complete. It excludes dead, finished, optional, nerve, eloquent, hold, retractor, and non-dacapo beads. Staccato beads are also ignored because the collapse phase kills them.\n\nA `goalsFor` function is being drafted to sort goals by pool coverage, ascending order, and player partition.\n\nThe `goalsFor` function is being implemented. It filters out ignored objects and replaces covered items with their parent pool. Pools that are fed, have volume, and have all clinical tasks done are included as goals.\n\nThe `goalsFor` function now sorts goals by order, drain rate, and ID. It returns the full list when only one player is active. For multiple players, it filters goals by player index modulo the total player count. If a player's share is empty, player 0 takes leftover goals. A better approach would let other players help when their share is done.\n\nThe bot will use a simple round-robin strategy. Every player works the same goal list, picking `(i + pi) % length` as their first choice to spread tasks evenly. Player 0 takes retractor duty when a retractor is unfinished and a window needs opening.\n\nThe bot will avoid duplicate goals by skipping a target already assigned to player 0 when multiple players are active. A `toolFor` helper is being drafted to map objects to tools, using sonar for hidden unmarked items and suction for pools. The bot will also wait and prefill with syringe if it cannot touch an object because the reed window, lobe, or eye is open.\n\nThe `toolFor` helper now maps lacerations, patches, and bites to salve. Lobe modes are mapped to their specific tools: salve, syringe, forceps, scalpel, or cautery. Wounds and artery tears map to suture.\n\nThe `toolFor` helper is being expanded to map bleeder, polyp, rupture, bead, uvula, eye, tremass, and barb to cautery. Masses and nests are mapped to scalpel when uncut and forceps when cut. Slur, tendril, spiral, wire, repeat, and cut are mapped to scalpel.\n\nThe `toolFor` helper is now complete. It maps reeds, voices, and clots to syringe or forceps depending on dosing state. Screws, shards, fragments, corks, hooks, gaffs, notes, and retractors map to forceps. Objects with guides use scalpel, paired objects use suture, and cells use salve. Undetermined objects default to forceps. Barbed hooks are noted as a potential complication but will be excluded from content.\n\nA `dev` helper is being drafted to construct device actions with tool digits, mouse coordinates, and trigger flags. The scene applies the digit via wheel and the position via cursor. A `pullMouse` function is being written to calculate pull limits and distances along an object's axis.\n\nThe `pullMouse` function now implements the pull logic. When an object is not free and the player has pulled less than 0.4 units past its depth, it advances along the axis by the pull limit scaled by delta time. Otherwise it moves toward a point 28 units beyond the object's depth, stepping only if the distance to that point is under 24 units.\n\nThe `pullMouse` function now releases the object by returning a forceps action with trigger 0. A grip helper is being drafted to ensure the player holds the target object before pulling; it moves toward the object with trigger 0 until within 3 units, then triggers 1 to create an edge. The next step is to implement the screw action.\n\nA `mem.ang` cache now drives the screw orbit, incrementing by 0.45 each frame to trace a circle around the object. The bot must hold the target before starting this orbit. A scalpel trace function is also being drafted: it moves toward the guide start point with trigger off, then triggers on once within three units, and advances along the stroke distance at speed 100.\n\nThe scalpel trace now holds still when a beat lock is active and the beat flag is set. It advances along the guide by one unit to keep the along distance increasing. A local pointAlong helper will be written because Sim does not export it. A slash action for nests is being drafted to swipe across from left to right.\n\nThe nest slash was refined to a 12-unit left-to-right swipe. A 28-unit jump would miss the nest because the cursor would land 14 units away, exceeding the 10-unit proximity threshold. The 12-unit span keeps the cursor within range at all frame rates.\n\nSuture logic is drafted. It moves to the first stitch point with trigger off, then triggers on within two units. It advances toward the second stitch point at 80 units per second, releasing once within 1.5 units. Salve logic is next.\n\nSalve logic now targets the first empty cell in an object's cells array, or the object center if none remain. Syringe IV logic waits at a fixed 40-unit offset until the player's fill level reaches 95 percent. For reeds, voices, and openable lobes, the bot stays near the target with trigger off while the window is closed to avoid spitting.\n\nSyringe IV logic now pre-fills off to the side at a fixed 20-unit offset. It triggers only during the 0.15–0.45 strain beat window and dumps if fill exceeds 92 percent while the over flag is active. For reeds and voices with open windows, it fills directly on the object when fill drops below 90 percent.\n\nSyringe logic for reeds and voices now pre-fills off to the side at a fixed 18-unit offset. It triggers only during the 0.18–0.7 strain beat window when fill is below 93 percent and cooldown is zero. If the player misses the window, it releases away before the bubble appears.\n\nSyringe logic now fills directly on the object when the window is open, releasing once fill reaches 88 percent. A release-away path for missed IV windows risks desync and will be tuned if tests fail. Cautery logic begins by releasing when heat exceeds 0.72 or a lock is active.\n\nCautery now waits off the tissue with trigger 0 while an eye or lobe is closed, then holds on the target once open. Sonar and suction actions are drafted as simple device calls. The bot will also check for code-blue and dressing gifts at the start of each device.\n\nGift and consult flags are now calculated. The bot triggers a breath gift when steady is at least 35 and the active document or gift matches the need. It triggers a look gift when steady is at least 45 and the active document or gift matches the need. Consultation is set for Sokolova if breathing is needed, or for Vale if looking is needed.\n\nGifts are now gated so they do not conflict with active work on the same frame. The bot returns a gift with trigger 0 when it matters. IV is reserved for urgent cases such as code-blue, dressing, rupture, artery, or strain boss, and only fires when vitals fall below 42 while cooldown is zero. Code-blue presses are timed to the exact 0.9, 1.8, 2.7, and 3.6 second peaks.\n\nThe code-blue edge logic is confirmed to fire exactly once per peak, even if the trigger stays on for two frames. A dressing action is now being drafted using the dressing timer modulo 1.2 seconds.\n\nRepeat signs that are open and not yet done are promoted to the front of the target list. Spiral and note objects are also prioritized when present.\n\nHidden sonar is handled before other work on that object.\n\nTool swaps take effect immediately because wheel.update sets the tool digit before step, while the swap timer only decrements without blocking actions. The scene must call wheel.update before step.\n\nThe `play` loop is being drafted. It iterates over players, calling `device` for each one to generate inputs. The scene must use the bot's field-space mouse coordinates when a driver is active, and fall back to the screen pointer for human input.\n\nThe `play` loop now updates the wheel and cursor, then passes tool, trigger, fine mode, gift, consult, cancel, and mouse state to `Op.step`. A stuck detector inside `device` will advance to the next goal if the current one remains unchanged for eight seconds, unless it is a window-waiting task. If vitals are about to hit zero, IV is triggered immediately.\n\nCase clocks and drains are being tuned to generous values for bot-friendly play. Tutorial easy is 180 seconds with 80 drain and 0.05 speed; the remaining cases range from 200 to 480 seconds, with arteries set as the drain at 0.12. Par is a placeholder of 1000 for later calibration. A quiet calibration mode will be added to tests so official runs stay silent.\n\nA scratch runner will measure scores and set par to 70% of the result so the bot can aim for an S rank. The case creation logic now requires GAME.CASES to be populated before create() runs, which content.js handles. A ferry grand rounds generator is being drafted: it places bleeders and artery tears in a grid pattern and sets the clock to 240 plus forty seconds per round.\n\nThe ferry grand rounds case is now fully defined. It sets vitals to 80, drain to 0.05, and mode to 'rounds'. The phase sequence is Triage with bleeders, a Swarm staccato phase with nests and beads, a Window phase containing a retractor and a mass needing a window, a Choir dacapo phase, and a Close phase placing wounds.\n\nStaccato swarm beads are being spaced out to reduce bite damage. The spawn function now adds 0.15 seconds per bead to the landIn delay, and rounds will use an 8-second delay so nests collapse before beads land. Even with this spacing, four nests may still be too slow for a four-player bot.\n\nStaccato landIn is set to 6 seconds for rounds so nests collapse before beads land. A skills lab case is being drafted with a 999-second clock, 80 vitals, and zero drain. It will use a cheat lock to freeze vitals and clock during creation.\n\nThe skills lab now sets `S.hold = true` so the phase never clears. When an object is marked `lab`, it respawns automatically in `stepWorld` after one second by resetting its done flag, alive state, progress, and cut status.\n\nRespawn delay is being raised to two seconds so the player sees success. The story will be written as a large content.js file with quill lines and narrative nodes. Each case follows an in-out pattern: an opening node triggers the operation, then a closing node advances to the next case. Story reachability is guaranteed by linking every node from the start through goto chains.\n\nStory nodes now use a wait-and-resume pattern. The opening node triggers the operation and pauses until it finishes, then advances to the closing node. That node displays the ward scene and pauses again until the player opens the door, then jumps to the next case. Auto-advance is enabled for tests so skip mode can advance through eight-line scenes at 0.08 seconds per line. A choice option will be included in one scene so the test can confirm the first selection.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-0addadf3-7f22-401b-b8b4-9938a7eedeb4-63","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"this\\.choices|this\\.open","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223916__x488eb9/work/Open-Table-Second-Opinion-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223916/vendor/my-3d2dge/dist/handoff/my-3d2dge.js","head_limit":15}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1035,"output_tokens":7501,"cache_read_input_tokens":161408,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa37-f7cf-7001-9198-38ad04ed0f5e","uuid":"482d761d-f82b-4350-9870-97527c7b3f5f"}
