 p28 freeundefined dnfalse snapundefined xy250,90 vit 67 clk 233\\nt 27.7 why pull tool forceps trig 1 at 250 79 held 7 st 3 hits 4 eye hidfalse mktrue opfalse dnfalse altrue sl Dhf,Dhf note p28 freeundefined dnfalse snapundefined xy250,90 vit 67 clk 232\\nt 28.0 why pull tool forceps trig 1 at 250 72 held 7 st 3 hits 4 eye hidfalse mktrue opfalse dnfalse altrue sl Dhf,Dhf note p28 freeundefined dnfalse snapundefined xy250,90 vit 67 clk 232\\nt 28.3 why pull tool forceps trig 1 at 250 66 held 7 st 3 hits 4 eye hidfalse mktrue opfalse dnfalse altrue sl Dhf,Dhf note p28 freeundefined dnfalse snapundefined xy250,90 vit 67 clk 232\\nt 28.5 why pull tool forceps trig 1 at 250 89 held 7 st 3 hits 4 eye hidfalse mktrue opfalse dnfalse altrue sl Dhf,Dhf note p28 freeundefined dnfalse snapundefined xy250,90 vit 67 clk 231\\nt 28.8 why pull tool forceps trig 1 at 250 83 held 7 st 3 hits 4 eye hidfalse mktrue opfalse dnfalse altrue sl Dhf,Dhf note p28 freeundefined dnfalse snapundefined xy250,90 vit 66 clk 231\\nt 29.1 why pull tool forceps trig 1 at 250 76 held 7 st 3 hits 4 eye hidfalse mktrue opfalse dnfalse altrue sl Dhf,Dhf note p28 freeundefined dnfalse snapundefined xy250,90 vit 66 clk 231\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"command\":\"node /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223916__x488eb9/tmp/grok-goal-08d1b960e391/implementer/ferm.js 2>/dev/null | head -n 40\",\"truncated\":false,\"signal\":null,\"timed_out\":false,\"description\":\"Show the first stage-3 fermata actions\",\"current_dir\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223916__x488eb9/work\",\"output_file\":\"/home/micha/.grok/sessions/%2Fhome%2Fmicha%2Fdev%2Fcc-matrix%2Fruns%2Fgrok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223916__x488eb9%2Fwork/01a1aa37-f7cf-7001-9198-38ad04ed0f5e/terminal/call-79fdcb08-458e-4516-92d4-c2bdd8948699-54.log\",\"total_bytes\":10719}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa37-f7cf-7001-9198-38ad04ed0f5e","uuid":"7ff29a7e-c33b-4189-9c4d-5b3cd2edde41"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_116","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The note reaches program 28 at depth 28, so it should become free.\n\nThe bot's pull code is causing the oscillation. It pulls upward but then snaps back toward the origin without releasing the note.\n\nThe pull condition `along < depth - 0.3` never becomes false, so the bot stays in pull mode instead of switching to carry. The carry target is calculated as origin plus axis times 58.\n\nThe oscillation pattern is now clear: the cursor drops roughly 6 units per 0.25-second sample, then snaps back up by about 23 units. The pull limit is only 24, so a SNAP event from tension should not be causing this jump.\n\nThe jump is the cursor itself moving back toward the origin, not the object. The bot is commanding that position. The suspicion is that something is resetting p.bx while the note remains held.\n\nThe carry target is y=32. The bot computes the step size as `Math.min(d, limit * dt)`. This cap may be preventing the cursor from reaching the target.\n\nThe bot releases the note when it reaches distance 28 from the origin. The simulation only completes the task if the object is already free; otherwise it drops the grip without finishing. This premature release explains why the note never reaches the dish.\n\nThe bot releases the note when it is 27.7 units from the origin. The simulation only marks the object as free once `along` reaches 28. Because the bot checks `!o.free`, it dishes before the object becomes free. The release happens too early.\n\nThe log shows the bot never actually releases the note. The held counter stays at 7, and the why field remains pull even when the cursor jumps back toward the origin. This contradicts the earlier theory that premature dish caused the oscillation.\n\nThe jump from y=65 to y=88 while the bot still reports pull mode means `along` was computed as very small. The formula `along = oy - p.by` should give 25 when the cursor is at y=65, which would place the next target near 64.5 rather than 88. The bot may be reading a different cursor position than what the log shows.\n\nThe why field is captured at the start of each frame, while the logged position reflects the cursor after the step. This means a why=pull entry paired with a y=88 reading confirms the bot genuinely intended to pull toward the origin. The earlier suspicion that the bot was reading a different cursor position was incorrect.\n\nThe jump from y=65 to y=88 likely occurred between the 0.25-second log samples. The bot probably released the note, re-gripped at the origin, and resumed pulling toward 88 before the next sample was recorded.\n\nThe re-grip frame at t=24.8 confirms the jump was a release and re-grip. The carry threshold of 26 units is too small for this depth, so the bot dishes before the object becomes free.\n\nThe carry threshold of 26 units works for shallow objects but fails for deep ones. At depth 16 the note is free at distance 16, which is below the 26-unit limit, so the bot carries to 26 and dishes correctly. At depth 28 the note is free at distance 28, which exceeds the 26-unit limit, so the bot dishes immediately when it exits the pull branch before the simulation can mark it free.\n\nThe step size is 24/45, so the cursor reaches exactly 28.03 on the frame after the object becomes free. The bot then dishes at distance 28 and the simulation should complete the task. This means the oscillation is not caused by the carry threshold being too small.\n\nThe simulation code sets `held.free = true` once `along` reaches the depth. The log shows the note never becomes free, so the condition is never met. A snap event is not the cause because it does not return early.\n\nThe jump from y=65 to y=88 in one frame equals an instantaneous speed of 1035, which would trigger a snap. Since snaps do not move the cursor, the bot itself is commanding that jump. The origin may be shifting while the bot calculates along, or an inst > limit condition is moving the object.\n\nThe bot computes `along` from `p.bx`, so the cursor position it reads may be getting modified by the simulation during forceps while the note is not yet free. The next step is to re-examine `stepForceps` to see if the simulation alters `p.bx` while the object remains held.\n\nThe simulation does not overwrite held.x while the note is held, so the cursor position stays where the bot places it. The oscillation period is roughly 0.8 seconds, which matches the expected travel distance. A snap event triggered by excessive instantaneous tension is now suspected as the mechanism that resets the note near depth.\n\nThe pull limit is 24, so the cursor should reach depth on the frame after the note becomes free. The bot dishes immediately when it sees the note is free, which means the simulation never actually marks the object as free.\n\nThe simulation should complete the dish when the note is free and distance is at least 24, yet it does not. Either the free flag is not persisting, or the cursor position is being altered before release. The next step is to check whether snapping every frame prevents the free state from sticking, and whether the note type requires a different completion path.\n\nRe-examining the exact `stepForceps` code to see if a hidden condition unsets the free flag or blocks completion for notes. The pull branch also ends at 27.7, so the cursor should cross 28 on the very next frame and trigger the free state.\n\nThe bot and simulation both use a 1/45-second timestep, so the frame interval is not the cause. A 0.53-unit jump from 27.6 to 28.13 yields an instantaneous tension of only 24, which is below the snap threshold, ruling out a snap event. The next step is to isolate the note in a tiny script that logs the free flag, along value, and release decision frame by frame to find why the note never becomes free.\n\nThe jump from y=65 to y=88 means the bot computed a tiny along value of roughly 2. The cursor was already near y=88 when device() ran, not at y=65. This rules out the cursor being moved back between frames; the bot itself is commanding that jump.\n\nThe jump from y=65 to y=88 cannot be explained by a normal pull toward depth 28. The bot may be reading a different origin or axis direction than expected.\n\nThe simulation only completes a dish when the note is marked free and the cursor is at least 24 units from the origin. Because the note never becomes free, the release drops it without finishing. This confirms the oscillation is caused by the simulation failing to set the free flag.\n\nThe bot exits the pull branch at 27.7, one frame before the simulation marks the note free at 28. Because the dish threshold is only 26, it releases immediately in that gap and never reaches the free state.\n\nThe fix is to extend the pull threshold by 0.05 units so the cursor reaches depth before dishing. This lets the simulation mark the note free and allows the bot to complete the release.\n\nThe fix is to pull the cursor slightly past the free threshold—about 1.5 units beyond depth—so the simulation marks the note free before the bot can dish. Once free, the bot carries out until distance reaches at least 26, or releases immediately if already there.\n\nThe fix is now written. The bot pulls the cursor slightly past the free threshold, then carries it outward until distance reaches 26 before releasing.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-195338cf-522c-4653-b4b9-5fd1901b62f1-55","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223916__x488eb9/work/Open-Table-Second-Opinion-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223916/src/bot/bot.js","offset":184,"limit":30}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2710,"output_tokens":5290,"cache_read_input_tokens":134144,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa37-f7cf-7001-9198-38ad04ed0f5e","uuid":"d45229de-c2b8-43a9-9b78-5aa1df4ff97b"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-195338cf-522c-4653-b4b9-5fd1901b62f1-55","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"184→      return go('forceps', o.x + Math.cos(m.ang) * 12, o.y + Math.sin(m.ang) * 12, 1, { why: 'turn' });\\n    }\\n\\n    if (tool === 'forceps') {\\n      if (p.held && p.held !== o.id) return go('forceps', p.bx, p.by, 0, { why: 'drop' });\\n      if (p.held !== o.id) {\\n190→        const on = near(o.x, o.y, 2.5);\\n        return go('forceps', o.x, o.y, on && p.prevTrig <= 0.3, { fine, why: 'grip' });\\n      }\\n      const limit = Math.min(78, (o.tension || 120) * 0.6);\\n      const ox = o.origin[0], oy = o.origin[1];\\n      const along = (p.bx - ox) * o.axis[0] + (p.by - oy) * o.axis[1];\\n      if (!o.free && along < o.depth - 0.3) {\\n        const n = along + limit * dt;\\n        return go('forceps', ox + o.axis[0] * n, oy + o.axis[1] * n, 1, { fine: limit < 70, why: 'pull' });\\n      }\\n200→      const tx = ox + o.axis[0] * (Math.max(o.depth, 0) + 30);\\n      const ty = oy + o.axis[1] * (Math.max(o.depth, 0) + 30);\\n      const dx = tx - p.bx, dy = ty - p.by, d = hyp(dx, dy) || 1;\\n      if (hyp(p.bx - ox, p.by - oy) < 26) {\\n        const step = Math.min(d, limit * dt);\\n        return go('forceps', p.bx + dx / d * step, p.by + dy / d * step, 1, { fine: true, why: 'carry' });\\n      }\\n      return go('forceps', p.bx, p.by, 0, { why: 'dish' });\\n    }\\n    return go(tool, o.x, o.y, 1, { why: 'generic' });\\n210→  }\\n\\n  function device(S, i, dt) {\\n    const p = S.players[i] || S.players[0];\",\"content_concise\":\"184→      return go('forceps', o.x + Math.cos(m.ang) * 12, o.y + Math.sin(m.ang) * 12, 1, { why: 'turn' });\\n    }\\n\\n    if (tool === 'forceps') {\\n      if (p.held && p.held !== o.id) return go('forceps', p.bx, p.by, 0, { why: 'drop' });\\n      if (p.held !== o.id) {\\n190→        const on = near(o.x, o.y, 2.5);\\n        