{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_127","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The engine calls navigator.getGamepads.\n\nNode tests need a navigator mock. The engine catches missing getGamepads. I'll set parameter c10 and write the large files. The full story playthrough with skip should take about 12 seconds of story plus fast operations.\n\nFull playthrough wall time should be under a few seconds. Story skip still waits on choices. The flow object will track scene intent. When an operation starts, the story scene update checks for a pending operation and switches to the operation scene. After the operation ends, the game resumes the story.\n\nThe flow object tracks resident status, chapter, saved data, case ID, active operation, and flags for attract mode and developer tools. The scene list includes title, menu, difficulty selection, story, chart, operation, results, ward, credits, about, lobby, and attract. A custom canvas menu is preferred over the engine's pause menu for easier testing. The title scene advances to the menu on confirmation.\n\nThe menu uses start and confirm inputs. The ward scene will be a tile map with patients and a door. Hover moves focus and click or interact triggers walking or talking. The test door will sit close to spawn so the bot can auto-walk there. The playthrough test will skip story text, tap through choices, let the debug driver run the operation when the snapshot switches to the op scene, then tap confirm on results.\n\nThe ward scene will use confirm to exit when the player is near the door. The test can repeat this until it reaches credits and title without knowing coordinates.\n\nFor the attract mode test, the game should advance ten seconds with no input and set the attract flag. Tapping start afterward must return to the title scene without also firing the menu control under the pointer.\n\nThe input hook resets the idle timer on any input. If attract mode is active, it returns to the title scene and consumes all input so the start button does not also open the menu. The idle timer advances only while attract screens are displayed. After ten seconds of idle time on those screens, attract mode begins.\n\nA dev mode test will pause during an operation, open the panel, adjust a slider, verify the TUNE value changes, and confirm reset restores it. The UI can be toggled from the pause menu, but headless HTML testing may fail. The test can also call GAME.Dev.set directly to bypass the UI.\n\nThe engine menu configures a Dev Mode item that calls openDev. Headless HTML menu testing is unreliable, so the dev panel will be exposed as a scene opened from the pause menu and also through a direct debug function on GAME. The test can invoke that function directly or verify the menu item. This satisfies the criterion that Dev mode opens from the pause menu outside a Hub session and live-changes the main tuning groups.\n\nThe menu item will call `GAME.openDev`. The test will invoke that function directly, verify the dev scene loads, adjust a slider through the same internal function the panel uses, reset, and cheat. It will also assert that the engine stores pause menu items so the Dev Mode label can be read.\n\nFor the Grand Rounds test, the bot will play roundsCase(1) through (4) and win all of them. A Retractor case will use two players with one holding.\n\nA unit test will step a few frames with player 0 holding the retractor and player 1 cutting. The hub integration tests live in `tests/hub.py` and are run by `node tests/run.mjs`. The `--fast` flag skips browser tests, but the full command must still pass. The verification requires running the complete suite.\n\nThe gating verification requires `node tests/run.mjs` to capture `tests.log` and assert criteria 2, 3, and 4. The hub harness is a separate verification step. To keep the log clean, `run.mjs` will run unit and scene tests quietly.\n\nrun.mjs must suppress hub.py output to keep tests.log free of chatter. The official log must contain only FAIL lines and the pass count. Hub.py prints would violate this rule, so run.mjs should not emit hub details.\n\nHub tests will run inside `run.mjs` only when `--fast` is absent, with stdout suppressed unless hub fails. Unit tests and game booting come first; hub is step two. The c10 parameter change is also needed. A score of 2613 against a 2000 par correctly yields an S rank.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-aef0997e-7914-47e1-a4ac-e28b3938fbf9-77","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"music\\(name|music\\(track|prototype\\.music","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223916__x488eb9/work/Open-Table-Second-Opinion-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223916/vendor/my-3d2dge/dist/handoff/API-INDEX.md","head_limit":20}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-aef0997e-7914-47e1-a4ac-e28b3938fbf9-78","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223916__x488eb9/work/Open-Table-Second-Opinion-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223916/vendor/my-3d2dge/dist/handoff/API-INDEX.md","offset":1040,"limit":80}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2114,"output_tokens":2201,"cache_read_input_tokens":185856,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa37-f7cf-7001-9198-38ad04ed0f5e","uuid":"bb9e46ab-6701-4676-aecb-7cc23fca59e7"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-aef0997e-7914-47e1-a4ac-e28b3938fbf9-77","content":"{\"type\":\"GrepSearch\",\"stdout\":[60,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,114,101,115,117,108,116,32,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,112,97,116,104,61,34,47,104,111,109,101,47,109,105,99,104,97,47,100,101,118,47,99,99,45,109,97,116,114,105,120,47,114,117,110,115,47,103,114,111,107,45,120,104,105,103,104,46,98,117,105,108,100,45,118,50,95,95,50,48,50,54,49,49,48,52,45,50,50,51,57,49,54,95,95,120,52,56,56,101,98,57,47,119,111,114,107,34,62,10,78,111,32,109,97,116,99,104,101,115,32,102,111,117,110,100,10,60,47,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,114,101,115,117,108,116,62],\"stderr\":[],\"exit_code\":1,\"match_count\":0,\"file_matches\":[]}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-aef0997e-7914-47e1-a4ac-e28b3938fbf9-78","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1040→- `shots.fire(b)`: fire one bullet. Fields: x, y, z, vx, vy, vz, r, dmg, life, team, color, core, sprite, pierce, grav, data\\n- `shots.hit(targets, fn, team)`: test bullets of `team` against targets (objects with x, y, z and r, or w/h in side view). fn(bullet, target). Returns hits\\n- `shots.update(dt, map)`: move them one step; with a map they stop at walls (a TileMap, or a PlatformMap in side views)\\n- `shots.list = []`: the live bullets\\n- `shots.max`: at most this many at once\\n- `shots.plane`: 'ground' (they fly over x and y) or 'side' (x and z)\\n- `shots.sparks`: a puff of sparks where one hits a wall\\n- `E.pattern`: velocity helpers for bullet patterns. Angles in radians (0 = east / right). Each returns [vx, v2] or a list of them\\n- `E.pattern.aim(a, b, speed)`: aim from a to b on the ground plane (x, y); aimSide uses x, z\\n- `E.pattern.aimSide(a, b, speed)`: a velocity from a toward b in a side view (x, z)\\n1050→- `E.pattern.dir(angle, speed)`: one velocity at an angle\\n- `E.pattern.ring(n, speed, offset = 0)`: n bullets in a full circle, rotated by offset\\n- `E.pattern.spread(angle, n, arc, speed)`: n bullets fanned across `arc` radians around `angle`\\n\\n## 19. Spatial hash\\n\\n- `new E.SpatialHash(cell = 32) → grid`: new E.SpatialHash(cell = 32): buckets things by position, to find what is near cheaply; map: the buckets\\n- `grid.add(o, x = o.x, y = o.y)`: put o in the bucket at (x, y)\\n- `grid.build(list, xk = 'x', yk = 'y')`: clear, then add every live thing in list (skips o.dead); do it once a step\\n- `grid.clear()`: empty it\\n1060→- `grid.near(x, y, r, fn)`: call fn(o) for everything in the cells touching the circle (check the exact distance yourself)\\n- `grid.query(x, y, r)`: what lies within about r of (x, y), as a list (near() calls a function instead)\\n- `grid.cell`: new E.SpatialHash(cell = 32): buckets things by position, to find what is near cheaply. cell: the bucket size in world units; map: the buckets\\n\\n## 20. Audio\\n\\n- `E.note(n)`: note name -> frequency: E.note('A4') = 440, E.note('C#5'), E.note('Bb2'). Numbers pass through\\n- `E.songs: title, adventure, dungeon, boss, victory`: ready-made chiptune loops (original compositions). game.audio.music('adventure')\\n- `game.audio.applyVolume()`: apply volume, musicVolume, sfxVolume and mute after setting them directly (setVolume and mute call it)\\n- `game.audio.at(step, fn)` †: call fn(step) on the update step the music reaches step (counted from its start, on as it loops); the song's change cancels it. Returns { cancel() }\\n1070→- `game.audio.bus(name = 'sfx')`: a bus for a sound of the game's own (Web Audio nodes it makes on audio.ctx: a babble voice, a loop, an instrument) to connect to, so it follows the volume, the mute and the player's settings: 'sfx', 'music', 'voice' (its level is voiceVolume) or 'master'. Returns a GainNode, or null where there is no sound (headless, no Web Audio)\\n- `game.audio.calibrate()` †: the audio offset's calibration: a click on each beat (100 bpm, the first of a bar higher) as the music; tap(time) on each one heard (an event's timeStamp, or nothing: this update step). After 8 taps, result is the offset to set (ms: settings.audioOffset) and done is true; taps go on refining it. stop() puts the music back\\n- `game.audio.define(name, voice)`: add or replace a named sound: audio.define('zap', { wave: 'saw', freq: 1200, to: 200, dur: .2 })\\n- `game.audio.duck(on = true)`: quiet the music (to 35%) while on: under a game's own pause, map or dialog screen. The engine also ducks it while game.paused\\n- `game.audio.instrument(name, fn)` †: an instrument of the game's own: fn(ctx, bus, time, freq, dur, vol) makes a note's sound (Web Audio, into bus, from time); its name is a wave a music track (wave: 'pluck') or a voice takes\\n- `game.audio.layer(track, vol = 1, fade = .5)` †: a track's volume as the song plays, 0..1, faded over fade seconds: layers of one song (the chase's drums in when the guards see you, out when they lose you). track: its index in song.tracks, or its name\\n- `game.audio.music(song, o = {})`: Loop a song (or play once with { loop: false }). song = { bpm: 120, steps: 4, tracks: [ { wave: 'square', vol: .2, notes: 'C5 - E5 - G5 - . . | ...' }, one token per step { wave: 'triangle', notes: 'C3 . C3 . G2 . G2 .' }, { wave: 'drums', notes: 'k . h . s . h . k k h . s . h .' } ] } tokens: C4 F#3 Bb2 = note, '-' holds the previous note, '.' = rest, '|' is ignored (bar lines). steps = steps per beat (4 = sixteenth notes). Each track loops on its own length within the song's (len: steps, else its longest track's); a track may have a name, and on: false starts it silent (a layer that audio.layer brings in). THE MUSIC CLOCK: a song starts at the game's time and where it is is game time (audio.pos, headless too: the same steps hear the same beats); the sound follows that clock. o: { loop, sync: true (it pauses with the game, on game.time; else it plays on through a pause, on game.real), fade: seconds (a crossfade from the song playing), restart: true (again from its start, though it is the one playing) }. E.songs has ready-made loops: title, adventure, dungeon, boss, victory.\\n- `game.audio.mute(on = !this.muted)`: toggle (or set) mute; returns the new state\\n- `game.audio.onBeat(fn)` †: call fn(beat, pos) on the update step of each beat of whatever music plays (a dancer's step, a light's pulse). Returns { cancel() }\\n- `game.audio.pos` †: where the music is, on the game's clock (headless too): { t: seconds since it started, step (from 0, counting on as it loops), beat, bar (four beats), at (the step in its loop), loop (times round), bpm, layers (each track's volume), playing }\\n1080→- `game.audio.ready`: true once the browser lets the game make sound\\n- `game.audio.setVolume(v)`: set the master volume, 0..1\\n- `game.audio.sfx(what, o = {})`: Play a sound effect: a preset name ('jump', 'coin', 'hit', 'explode', ...), a voice object, or a list of voices. opts: { vol: 0..1, pitch: multiplier, vary: random pitch spread (default 0.03), step: a step of the music playing (it sounds on that step of the music clock, scheduled with the music: a rhythm game's calls and claps), pan: -1 (left) .. 1 (right), loop: true (it sounds on until stopped: an engine, rain; through the effects' bus, so it follows the volume and the mute) }. Returns its handle: { stop(fade .03), set({ pan, vol }), playing, pan }: a handle that does nothing when nothing plays (headless, before the first key, an unknown name)\\n- `game.audio.sting(song, o = {})` †: play a short song once over the music (a goal's jingle): o.at 'now', 'beat' or 'bar' (on the music's next beat or bar), o.duck (the music's volume under it, .4)\\n- `game.audio.stopMusic(fade)`: stop the music (it fades out over fade seconds, .06 by default)\\n- `game.audio.tempo(bpm)` †: change the music's tempo as it plays (hurry up!): where it is stays, the steps after come at the new bpm\\n- `game.audio.anchors = 0` †: how many times the sound was moved to meet the clock\\n- `game.audio.ctx = null`: the Web Audio context, made at the first sound (null before, or where there is none)\\n- `game.audio.drift = 0` †: what is heard against the game's clock at the last frame (seconds: the sound follows the clock, so it stays near 0)\\n- `game.audio.ducked = false`: true while duck(true) holds the music down\\n1090→- `game.audio.failed = null`: why there is no sound (a message), or null\\n- `game.audio.musicVolume = .5`, `game.audio.sfxVolume = 1`, `game.audio.voiceVolume = 1`: 0..1 for effects, music and the voice bus (bus('voice'))\\n- `game.audio.muted = false`: true while mute() is on\\n- `game.audio.offset = 0` †: seconds the music sounds early, for speakers and headphones that lag (settings.audioOffset)\\n- `game.audio.presets`: the named sound effects, sfx(name) plays one and define(name, voice) adds one\\n- `game.audio.song = null`: the music playing (null for none)\\n- `game.audio.volume = .7`: master volume 0..1\\n\\n## 21b. Backdrops\\n\\n1100→- `new E.Backdrop(o = {}) → backdrop`: new E.Backdrop('castle-night') (a preset: E.Backdrop.presets) or new E.Backdrop({ sky: [colors top to bottom], sun, moon, stars, layers: [{ kind: 'mountains' | 'hills' | 'forest' | 'city' | 'castle' | 'clouds' | 'sea' | 'fog' | 'stalactites', color, y, height, parallax, seed }] }): a parallax sky and scenery drawn behind a level. sun, moon: { x, y (0..1 of the screen), r, color }\\n- `E.Backdrop.presets` †: the preset names, all ten: day, dusk, night, castle-night, city-night, desert, forest, ocean, cave, space\\n- `backdrop.draw(r)` †: draw the backdrop first in draw(r), before the level; it scrolls with the camera by each layer's parallax\\n- `backdrop.layers` †: its scenery layers, back to front, with their defaults filled in\\n- `backdrop.sky` †: the sky's gradient colors, top to bottom\\n\\n## 21c. Props\\n\\n- `E.PROPS: torch, candle, candelabra, chandelier, brazier, window, banner, pillar, statue, crate, barrel, chest, pot, sign, fence, lamp, door, tree, pine, bush, flowers, grass, rock, crystal, bones, skull, vines, cobweb, lantern, streetlamp, awning, hydrant, trashcan, firedrum, sandbags, cactus, palm, mushroom` †: the names of the built-in props for r.prop(name, x, y, z, opts) and E.prop(name, opts)\\n- `E.prop(name, o = {})`: build (and cache) a prop: { frames: [sprite], glow: [sprite] | null, w, h }\\n1110→\\n## 21e. Shapes\\n\\n- `E.SHAPES: barrel, crate, chest, table, chair, stool, bench, bed, bookcase, shelf, pot, jar, sack, cart, ... (25)`: the shapes by id: { about, tags, mats: { slot: material }, parts | make(params), params, foot, lights, flames } (the core library's, the sets' as 'SET:id' from E.addAssets, and your own: E.SHAPES.stool = {...})\\n- `E.addAssets(set)`: register a store set (src/assets/sets/*.js: { set, format: 1, credit, sources, materials, shapes, sprites }): its entries are named 'SET:id' (so one set never changes another's, or the core's) and find their own set's materials and shapes first. An id that is already there warns and keeps the first. Returns { shapes: [ids], materials: [ids], sprites: { id: sprite } } (sprites are E.sprite data; a color may be 'material:tone', tone deep | sh | base | lt | hi)\\n- `E.materialTile(id, o = {})`: a material's pattern as a tile of pixels, for a 3D renderer's texture: E.materialTile(id, { size: 32 (world units square: it repeats every size; a face pattern's whole face), px: 2 (pixels a unit), lid: false (true: as on a face looking up), curved (as on a turned face), band: 2 (the light: 0 dark .. 4 bright) }) -> { w, h, rgba (Uint8ClampedArray, top row first, v up the tile) }; null for an unknown material\\n- `E.shapeBox(id, x, y, z = 0, o = {})`: where a placed shape stands, as both engines collide with it: E.shapeBox(id | entry, x, y, z, { facing, scale, flip, params }) -> { x, y (its footprint's middle), z, top, hx, hy (half its footprint, along its own x and y), c, s (the turn's cosine and sine) }; null for an unknown shape. map.solid makes one solid; world.shape gives it a collider\\n- `E.shapeInfo(id, o = {})`: what a shape draws, for tools and tests: E.shapeInfo(id, { view: 'iso', facing, scale, mats, params }) -> { w, h, ox, oy, tris, prims (its primitives), parts (each primitive's pixels on screen: 0 is never seen there), colors (distinct), pixels, bounds [x0, y0, z0, x1, y1, z1], rgba }; null for an unknown shape\\n- `E.shapeMesh(id, o = {})`: a shape's triangles, for a 3D renderer (the 3D engine's world.shape): E.shapeMesh(id | entry, { params, mats, seg }) -> { groups: [{ mat, key, lid, curved, face, pos, nrm, uv }], bounds [x0, y0, z0, x1, y1, z1] } in the entry's own units and frame (z up, its front +y, its own scale applied: place it with the placement's facing, scale and flip). The same parts as r.shape draws; null for an unknown shape\\n- `E.shapeMesh({ seg })`: seg(r): the segments round a radius r (default 8 to 24)\",\"content_concise\":\"1040→- `shots.fire(b)`: fire one bullet. Fields: x, y, z, vx, vy, vz, r, dmg, life, team, color, core, sprite, pierce, grav, data\\n- `shots.hit(targets, fn, team)`: test bullets of `team` against targets (objects with x, y, z and r, or w/h in side view). fn(bullet, target). Returns hits\\n- `shots.update(dt, map)`: move them one step; with a map they stop at walls (a TileMap, or a PlatformMap in side views)\\n- `shots.list = []`: the live bullets\\n- `shots.max`: at most this many at once\\n- `shots.plane`: 'ground' (they fly over x and y) or 'side' (x and z)\\n- `shots.sparks`: a puff of sparks where one hits a wall\\n- `E.pattern`: velocity helpers for bullet patterns. Angles in radians (0 = east / right). Each returns [vx, v2] or a list of them\\n- `E.pattern.aim(a, b, speed)`: aim from a to b on the ground plane (x, y); aimSide uses x, z\\n- `E.pattern.aimSide(a, b, speed)`: a velocity from a toward b in a side view (x, z)\\n1050→- `E.pattern.dir(angle, speed)`: one velocity at an angle\\n- `E.pattern.ring(n, speed, offset = 0)`: n bullets in a full circle, rotated by offset\\n- `E.pattern.spread(angle, n, arc, speed)`: n bullets fanned across `arc` radians around `angle`\\n\\n## 19. Spatial hash\\n\\n- `new E.SpatialHash(cell = 32) → grid`: new E.SpatialHash(cell = 32): buckets things by position, to find what is near cheaply; map: the buckets\\n- `grid.add(o, x = o.x, y = o.y)`: put o in the bucket at (x, y)\\n- `grid.build(list, xk = 'x', yk = 'y')`: clear, then add every live thing in list (skips o.dead); do it once a step\\n- `grid.clear()`: empty it\\n1060→- `grid.near(x, y, r, fn)`: call fn(o) for everything in the cells touching the circle (check the exact distance yourself)\\n- `grid.query(x, y, r)`: what lies within about r of (x, y), as a list (near() calls a function instead)\\n- `grid.cell`: new E.SpatialHash(cell = 32): buckets things by position, to find what is near cheaply. cell: the bucket size in world units; map: the buckets\\n\\n## 20. Audio\\n\\n- `E.note(n)`: note name -> frequency: E.note('A4') = 440, E.note('C#5'), E.note('Bb2'). Numbers pass through\\n- `E.songs: title, adventure, dungeon, boss, victory`: ready-made chiptune loops (original compositions). game.audio.music('adventure')\\n- `game.audio.applyVolume()`: apply volume, musicVolume, sfxVolume and mute after setting them directly (setVolume and mute call it)\\n- `game.audio.at(step, fn)` †: call fn(step) on the update step the music reaches step (counted from its start, on as it loops); the song's change cancels it. Returns { cancel() }\\n1070→- `game.audio.bus(name = 'sfx')`: a bus for a sound of the game's own (Web Audio nodes it makes on audio.ctx: a babble voice, a loop, an instrument) to connect to, so it follows the volume, the mute and the player's settings: 'sfx', 'music', 'voice' (its level is voiceVolume) or 'master'. Returns a GainNode, or null where there is no sound (headless, no Web Audio)\\n- `game.audio.calibrate()` †: the audio offset's calibration: a click on each beat (100 bpm, the first of a bar higher) as the music; tap(time) on each one heard (an event's timeStamp, or nothing: this update step). After 8 taps, result is the offset to set (ms: settings.audioOffset) and done is true; taps go on refining it. stop() puts the music back\\n- `game.audio.define(name, voice)`: add or replace a named sound: audio.define('zap', { wave: 'saw', freq: 1200, to: 200, dur: .2 })\\n- `game.audio.duck(on = true)`: quiet the music (to 35%) while on: under a game's own pause, map or dialog screen. The engine also ducks it while game.paused\\n- `game.audio.instrument(name, fn)` †: an instrument of the game's own: fn(ctx, bus, time, freq, dur, vol) makes a note's sound (Web Audio, into bus, from time); its name is a wave a music track (wave: 'pluck') or a voice takes\\n- `game.audio.layer(track, vol = 1, fade = .5)` †: a track's volume as the song plays, 0..1, faded over fade seconds: layers of one song (the chase's drums in when the guards see you, out when they lose you). track: its index in song.tracks, or its name\\n- `game.audio.music(song, o = {})`: Loop a song (or play once with { loop: false }). song = { bpm: 120, steps: 4, tracks: [ { wave: 'square', vol: .2, notes: 'C5 - E5 - G5 - . . | ...' }, one token per step { wave: 'triangle', notes: 'C3 . C3 . G2 . G2 .' }, { wave: 'drums', notes: 'k . h . s . h . k k h . s . h .' } ] } tokens: C4 F#3 Bb2 = note, '-' holds the previous note, '.' = rest, '|' is ignored (bar lines). steps = steps per beat (4 = sixteenth notes). Each track loops on its own length within the song's (len: steps, else its longest track's); a track may have a name, and on: false starts it silent (a layer that audio.layer brings in). THE MUSIC CLOCK: a song starts at the game's time and where it is is game time (audio.pos, headless too: the same steps hear the same beats); the sound follows that clock. o: { loop, sync: true (it pauses with the game, on game.time; else it plays on through a pause, on game.real), fade: seconds (a crossfade from the song playing), restart: true (again from its start, though it is the one playing) }. E.songs has ready-made loops: title, adventure, dungeon, boss, victory.\\n- `game.audio.mute(on = !this.muted)`: toggle (or set) mute; returns the new state\\n- `game.audio.onBeat(fn)` †: call fn(beat, pos) on the update step of each beat of whatever music plays (a dancer's step, a light's pulse). Returns { cancel() }\\n- `game.audio.pos` †: where the music is, on the game's clock (headless too): { t: seconds since it started, step (from 0, counting on as it loops), beat, bar (four beats), at (the step in its loop), loop (times round), bpm, layers (each track's volume), playing }\\n1080→- `game.audio.ready`: true once the browser lets the game make sound\\n- `game.audio.setVolume(v)`: set the master volume, 0..1\\n- `game.audio.sfx(what, o = {})`: Play a sound effect: a preset name ('jump', 'coin', 'hit', 'explode', ...), a voice object, or a list of voices. opts: { vol: 0..1, pitch: multiplier, vary: random pitch spread (default 0.03), step: a step of the music playing (it sounds on that step of the music clock, scheduled with the music: a rhythm game's calls and claps), pan: -1 (left) .. 1 (right), loop: true (it sounds on until stopped: an engine, rain; through the effects' bus, so it follows the volume and the mute) }. Returns its handle: { stop(fade .03), set({ pan, vol }), playing, pan }: a handle that does nothing when nothing plays (headless, before the first key, an unknown name)\\n- `game.audio.sting(song, o = {})` †: play a short song once over the music (a goal's jingle): o.at 'now', 'beat' or 'bar' (on the music's next beat or bar), o.duck (the music's volume under it, .4)\\n- `game.audio.stopMusic(fade)`: stop the music (it fades out over fade seconds, .06 by default)\\n- `game.audio.tempo(bpm)` †: change the music's tempo as it plays (hurry up!): where it is stays, the steps after come at the new bpm\\n- `game.audio.anchors = 0` †: how many times the sound was moved to meet the clock\\n- `game.audio.ctx = null`: the Web Audio context, made at the first sound (null before, or where there is none)\\n- `game.audio.drift = 0` †: what is heard against the game's clock at the last frame (seconds: the sound follows the clock, so it stays near 0)\\n- `game.audio.ducked = false`: true while duck(true) holds the music down\\n1090→- `game.audio.failed = null`: why there is no sound (a message), or null\\n- `game.audio.musicVolume = .5`, `game.audio.sfxVolume = 1`, `game.audio.voiceVolume = 1`: 0..1 for effects, music and the voice bus (bus('voice'))\\n- `game.audio.muted = false`: true while mute() is on\\n- `game.audio.offset = 0` †: seconds the music sounds early, for speakers and headphones that lag (settings.audioOffset)\\n- `game.audio.presets`: the named sound effects, sfx(name) plays one and define(name, voice) adds one\\n- `game.audio.song = null`: the music playing (null for none)\\n- `game.audio.volume = .7`: master volume 0..1\\n\\n## 21b. Backdrops\\n\\n1100→- `new E.Backdrop(o = {}) → backdrop`: new E.Backdrop('castle-night') (a preset: E.Backdrop.presets) or new E.Backdrop({ sky: [colors top to bottom], sun, moon, stars, layers: [{ kind: 'mountains' | 'hills' | 'forest' | 'city' | 'castle' | 'clouds' | 'sea' | 'fog' | 'stalactites', color, y, height, parallax, seed }] }): a parallax sky and scenery drawn behind a level. sun, moon: { x, y (0..1 of the screen), r, color }\\n- `E.Backdrop.presets` †: the preset names, all ten: day, dusk, night, castle-night, city-night, desert, forest, ocean, cave, space\\n- `backdrop.draw(r)` †: draw the backdrop first in draw(r), before the level; it scrolls with the camera by each layer's parallax\\n- `backdrop.layers` †: its scenery layers, back to front, with their defaults filled in\\n- `backdrop.sky` †: the sky's gradient colors, top to bottom\\n\\n## 21c. Props\\n\\n- `E.PROPS: torch, candle, candelabra, chandelier, brazier, window, banner, pillar, statue, crate, barrel, chest, pot, sign, fence, lamp, door, tree, pine, bush, flowers, grass, rock, crystal, bones, skull, vines, cobweb, lantern, streetlamp, awning, hydrant, trashcan, firedrum, sandbags, cactus, palm, mushroom` †: the names of the built-in props for r.prop(name, x, y, z, opts) and E.prop(name, opts)\\n- `E.prop(name, o = {})`: build (and cache) a prop: { frames: [sprite], glow: [sprite] | null, w, h }\\n1110→\\n## 21e. Shapes\\n\\n- `E.SHAPES: barrel, crate, chest, table, chair, stool, bench, bed, bookcase, shelf, pot, jar, sack, cart, ... (25)`: the shapes by id: { about, tags, mats: { slot: material }, parts | make(params), params, foot, lights, flames } (the core library's, the sets' as 'SET:id' from E.addAssets, and your own: E.SHAPES.stool = {...})\\n- `E.addAssets(set)`: register a store set (src/assets/sets/*.js: { set, format: 1, credit, sources, materials, shapes, sprites }): its entries are named 'SET:id' (so one set never changes another's, or the core's) and find their own set's materials and shapes first. An id that is already there warns and keeps the first. Returns { shapes: [ids], materials: [ids], sprites: { id: sprite } } (sprites are E.sprite data; a color may be 'material:tone', tone deep | sh | base | lt | hi)\\n- `E.materialTile(id, o = {})`: a material's pattern as a tile of pixels, for a 3D renderer's texture: E.materialTile(id, { size: 32 (world units square: it repeats every size; a face pattern's whole face), px: 2 (pixels a unit), lid: false (true: as on a face looking up), curved (as on a turned face), band: 2 (the light: 0 dark .. 4 bright) }) -> { w, h, rgba (Uint8ClampedArray, top row first, v up the tile) }; null for an unknown material\\n- `E.shapeBox(id, x, y, z = 0, o = {})`: where a placed shape stands, as both engines collide with it: E.shapeBox(id | entry, x, y, z, { facing, scale, flip, params }) -> { x, y (its footprint's middle), z, top, hx, hy (half its footprint, along its own x and y), c, s (the turn's cosine and sine) }; null for an unknown shape. map.solid makes one solid; world.shape gives it a collider\\n- `E.shapeInfo(id, o = {})`: what a shape draws, for tools and tests: E.shapeInfo(id, { view: 'iso', facing, scale, mats, params }) -> { w, h, ox, oy, tris, prims (its primitives), parts (each primitive's pixels on screen: 0 is never seen there), colors (distinct), pixels, bounds [x0, y0, z0, x1, y1, z1], rgba }; null for an unknown shape\\n- `E.shapeMesh(id, o = {})`: a shape's triangles, for a 3D renderer (the 3D engine's world.shape): E.shapeMesh(id | entry, { params, mats, seg }) -> { groups: [{ mat, key, lid, curved, face, pos, nrm, uv }], bounds [x0, y0, z0, x1, y1, z1] } in the entry's own units and frame (z up, its front +y, its own scale applied: place it with the placement's facing, scale and flip). The same parts as r.shape draws; null for an unknown shape\\n- `E.shapeMesh({ seg })`: seg(r): the segments round a radius r (default 8 to 24)\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223916__x488eb9/work/Open-Table-Second-Opinion-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223916/vendor/my-3d2dge/dist/handoff/API-INDEX.md\",\"offset\":1040,\"limit\":80,\"raw_output\":\"- `shots.fire(b)`: fire one bullet. Fields: x, y, z, vx, vy, vz, r, dmg, life, team, color, core, sprite, pierce, grav, data\\n- `shots.hit(targets, fn, team)`: test bullets of `team` against targets (objects with x, y, z and r, or w/h in side view). fn(bullet, target). Returns hits\\n- `shots.update(dt, map)`: move them one step; with a map they stop at walls (a TileMap, or a PlatformMap in side views)\\n- `shots.list = []`: the live bullets\\n- `shots.max`: at most this many at once\\n- `shots.plane`: 'ground' (they fly over x and y) or 'side' (x and z)\\n- `shots.sparks`: a puff of sparks where one hits a wall\\n- `E.pattern`: velocity helpers for bullet patterns. Angles in radians (0 = east / right). Each returns [vx, v2] or a list of them\\n- `E.pattern.aim(a, b, speed)`: aim from a to b on the ground plane (x, y); aimSide uses x, z\\n- `E.pattern.aimSide(a, b, speed)`: a velocity from a toward b in a side view (x, z)\\n- `E.pattern.dir(angle, speed)`: one velocity at an angle\\n- `E.pattern.ring(n, speed, offset = 0)`: n bullets in a full circle, rotated by offset\\n- `E.pattern.spread(angle, n, arc, speed)`: n bullets fanned across `arc` radians around `angle`\\n\\n## 19. Spatial hash\\n\\n- `new E.SpatialHash(cell = 32) → grid`: new E.SpatialHash(cell = 32): buckets things by position, to find what is near cheaply; map: the buckets\\n- `grid.add(o, x = o.x, y = o.y)`: put o in the bucket at (x, y)\\n- `grid.build(list, xk = 'x', yk = 'y')`: clear, then add every live thing in list (skips o.dead); do it once a step\\n- `grid.clear()`: empty it\\n- `grid.near(x, y, r, fn)`: call fn(o) for everything in the cells touching the circle (check the exact distance yourself)\\n- `grid.query(x, y, r)`: what lies within about r of (x, y), as a list (near() calls a function instead)\\n- `grid.cell`: new E.SpatialHash(cell = 32): buckets things by position, to find what is near cheaply. cell: the bucket size in world units; map: the buckets\\n\\n## 20. Audio\\n\\n- `E.note(n)`: note name -> frequency: E.note('A4') = 440, E.note('C#5'), E.note('Bb2'). Numbers pass through\\n- `E.songs: title, adventure, dungeon, boss, victory`: ready-made chiptune loops (original compositions). game.audio.music('adventure')\\n- `game.audio.applyVolume()`: apply volume, musicVolume, sfxVolume and mute after setting them directly (setVolume and mute call it)\\n- `game.audio.at(step, fn)` †: call fn(step) on the update step the music reaches step (counted from its start, on as it loops); the song's change cancels it. Returns { cancel() }\\n- `game.audio.bus(name = 'sfx')`: a bus for a sound of the game's own (Web Audio nodes it makes on audio.ctx: a babble voice, a loop, an instrument) to connect to, so it follows the volume, the mute and the player's settings: 'sfx', 'music', 'voice' (its level is voiceVolume) or 'master'. Returns a GainNode, or null where there is no sound (headless, no Web Audio)\\n- `game.audio.calibrate()` †: the audio offset's calibration: a click on each beat (100 bpm, the first of a bar higher) as the music; tap(time) on each one heard (an event's timeStamp, or nothing: this update step). After 8 taps, result is the offset to set (ms: settings.audioOffset) and done is true; taps go on refining it. stop() puts the music back\\n- `game.audio.define(name, voice)`: add or replace a named sound: audio.define('zap', { wave: 'saw', freq: 1200, to: 200, dur: .2 })\\n- `game.audio.duck(on = true)`: quiet the music (to 35%) while on: under a game's own pause, map or dialog screen. The engine also ducks it while game.paused\\n- `game.audio.instrument(name, fn)` †: an instrument of the game's own: fn(ctx, bus, time, freq, dur, vol) makes a note's sound (Web Audio, into bus, from time); its name is a wave a music track (wave: 'pluck') or a voice takes\\n- `game.audio.layer(track, vol = 1, fade = .5)` †: a track's volume as the song plays, 0..1, faded over fade seconds: layers of one song (the chase's drums in when the guards see you, out when they lose you). track: its index in song.tracks, or its name\\n- `game.audio.music(song, o = {})`: Loop a song (or play once with { loop: false }). song = { bpm: 120, steps: 4, tracks: [ { wave: 'square', vol: .2, notes: 'C5 - E5 - G5 - . . | ...' }, one token per step { wave: 'triangle', notes: 'C3 . C3 . G2 . G2 .' }, { wave: 'drums', notes: 'k . h . s . h . k k h . s . h .' } ] } tokens: C4 F#3 Bb2 = note, '-' holds the previous note, '.' = rest, '|' is ignored (bar lines). steps = steps per beat (4 = sixteenth notes). Each track loops on its own length within the song's (len: steps, else its longest track's); a track may have a name, and on: false starts it silent (a layer that audio.layer brings in). THE MUSIC CLOCK: a song starts at the game's time and where it is is game time (audio.pos, headless too: the same steps hear the same beats); the sound follows that clock. o: { loop, sync: true (it pauses with the game, on game.time; else it plays on through a pause, on game.real), fade: seconds (a crossfade from the song playing), restart: true (again from its start, though it is the one playing) }. E.songs has ready-made loops: title, adventure, dungeon, boss, victory.\\n- `game.audio.mute(on = !this.muted)`: toggle (or set) mute; returns the new state\\n- `game.audio.onBeat(fn)` †: call fn(beat, pos) on the update step of each beat of whatever music plays (a dancer's step, a light's pulse). Returns { cancel() }\\n- `game.audio.pos` †: where the music is, on the game's clock (headless too): { t: seconds since it started, step (from 0, counting on as it loops), beat, bar (four beats), at (the step in its loop), loop (times round), bpm, layers (each track's volume), playing }\\n- `game.audio.ready`: true once the browser lets the game make sound\\n- `game.audio.setVolume(v)`: set the master volume, 0..1\\n- `game.audio.sfx(what, o = {})`: Play a sound effect: a preset name ('jump', 'coin', 'hit', 'explode', ...), a voice object, or a list of voices. opts: { vol: 0..1, pitch: multiplier, vary: random pitch spread (default 0.03), step: a step of the music playing (it sounds on that step of the music clock, scheduled with the music: a rhythm game's calls and claps), pan: -1 (left) .. 1 (right), loop: true (it sounds on until stopped: an engine, rain; through the effects' bus, so it follows the volume and the mute) }. Returns its handle: { stop(fade .03), set({ pan, vol }), playing, pan }: a handle that does nothing when nothing plays (headless, before the first key, an unknown name)\\n- `game.audio.sting(song, o = {})` †: play a short song once over the music (a goal's jingle): o.at 'now', 'beat' or 'bar' (on the music's next beat or bar), o.duck (the music's volume under it, .4)\\n- `game.audio.stopMusic(fade)`: stop the music (it fades out over fade seconds, .06 by default)\\n- `game.audio.tempo(bpm)` †: change the music's tempo as it plays (hurry up!): where it is stays, the steps after come at the new bpm\\n- `game.audio.anchors = 0` †: how many times the sound was moved to meet the clock\\n- `game.audio.ctx = null`: the Web Audio context, made at the first sound (null before, or where there is none)\\n- `game.audio.drift = 0` †: what is heard against the game's clock at the last frame (seconds: the sound follows the clock, so it stays near 0)\\n- `game.audio.ducked = false`: true while duck(true) holds the music down\\n- `game.audio.failed = null`: why there is no sound (a message), or null\\n- `game.audio.musicVolume = .5`, `game.audio.sfxVolume = 1`, `game.audio.voiceVolume = 1`: 0..1 for effects, music and the voice bus (bus('voice'))\\n- `game.audio.muted = false`: true while mute() is on\\n- `game.audio.offset = 0` †: seconds the music sounds early, for speakers and headphones that lag (settings.audioOffset)\\n- `game.audio.presets`: the named sound effects, sfx(name) plays one and define(name, voice) adds one\\n- `game.audio.song = null`: the music playing (null for none)\\n- `game.audio.volume = .7`: master volume 0..1\\n\\n## 21b. Backdrops\\n\\n- `new E.Backdrop(o = {}) → backdrop`: new E.Backdrop('castle-night') (a preset: E.Backdrop.presets) or new E.Backdrop({ sky: [colors top to bottom], sun, moon, stars, layers: [{ kind: 'mountains' | 'hills' | 'forest' | 'city' | 'castle' | 'clouds' | 'sea' | 'fog' | 'stalactites', color, y, height, parallax, seed }] }): a parallax sky and scenery drawn behind a level. sun, moon: { x, y (0..1 of the screen), r, color }\\n- `E.Backdrop.presets` †: the preset names, all ten: day, dusk, night, castle-night, city-night, desert, forest, ocean, cave, space\\n- `backdrop.draw(r)` †: draw the backdrop first in draw(r), before the level; it scrolls with the camera by each layer's parallax\\n- `backdrop.layers` †: its scenery layers, back to front, with their defaults filled in\\n- `backdrop.sky` †: the sky's gradient colors, top to bottom\\n\\n## 21c. Props\\n\\n- `E.PROPS: torch, candle, candelabra, chandelier, brazier, window, banner, pillar, statue, crate, barrel, chest, pot, sign, fence, lamp, door, tree, pine, bush, flowers, grass, rock, crystal, bones, skull, vines, cobweb, lantern, streetlamp, awning, hydrant, trashcan, firedrum, sandbags, cactus, palm, mushroom` †: the names of the built-in props for r.prop(name, x, y, z, opts) and E.prop(name, opts)\\n- `E.prop(name, o = {})`: build (and cache) a prop: { frames: [sprite], glow: [sprite] | null, w, h }\\n\\n## 21e. Shapes\\n\\n- `E.SHAPES: barrel, crate, chest, table, chair, stool, bench, bed, bookcase, shelf, pot, jar, sack, cart, ... (25)`: the shapes by id: { about, tags, mats: { slot: material }, parts | make(params), params, foot, lights, flames } (the core library's, the sets' as 'SET:id' from E.addAssets, and your own: E.SHAPES.stool = {...})\\n- `E.addAssets(set)`: register a store set (src/assets/sets/*.js: { set, format: 1, credit, sources, materials, shapes, sprites }): its entries are named 'SET:id' (so one set never changes another's, or the core's) and find their own set's materials and shapes first. An id that is already there warns and keeps the first. Returns { shapes: [ids], materials: [ids], sprites: { id: sprite } } (sprites are E.sprite data; a color may be 'material:tone', tone deep | sh | base | lt | hi)\\n- `E.materialTile(id, o = {})`: a material's pattern as a tile of pixels, for a 3D renderer's texture: E.materialTile(id, { size: 32 (world units square: it repeats every size; a face pattern's whole face), px: 2 (pixels a unit), lid: false (true: as on a face looking up), curved (as on a turned face), band: 2 (the light: 0 dark .. 4 bright) }) -> { w, h, rgba (Uint8ClampedArray, top row first, v up the tile) }; null for an unknown material\\n- `E.shapeBox(id, x, y, z = 0, o = {})`: where a placed shape stands, as both engines collide with it: E.shapeBox(id | entry, x, y, z, { facing, scale, flip, params }) -> { x, y (its footprint's middle), z, top, hx, hy (half its footprint, along its own x and y), c, s (the turn's cosine and sine) }; null for an unknown shape. map.solid makes one solid; world.shape gives it a collider\\n- `E.shapeInfo(id, o = {})`: what a shape draws, for tools and tests: E.shapeInfo(id, { view: 'iso', facing, scale, mats, params }) -> { w, h, ox, oy, tris, prims (its primitives), parts (each primitive's pixels on screen: 0 is never seen there), colors (distinct), pixels, bounds [x0, y0, z0, x1, y1, z1], rgba }; null for an unknown shape\\n- `E.shapeMesh(id, o = {})`: a shape's triangles, for a 3D renderer (the 3D engine's world.shape): E.shapeMesh(id | entry, { params, mats, seg }) -> { groups: [{ mat, key, lid, curved, face, pos, nrm, uv }], bounds [x0, y0, z0, x1, y1, z1] } in the entry's own units and frame (z up, its front +y, its own scale applied: place it with the placement's facing, scale and flip). The same parts as r.shape draws; null for an unknown shape\\n- `E.shapeMesh({ seg })`: seg(r): the segments round a radius r (default 8 to 24)\\n\",\"total_lines\":1767}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa37-f7cf-7001-9198-38ad04ed0f5e","uuid":"681c10c1-9863-4618-8b88-cb85b0bc9cb5"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_128","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The engine has E.songs, but the plan requires synthesizing original music in code rather than using audio files.\n\nI'll define custom song objects with notes and pass them to audio.music(). Choir effects will loop on the effects bus. Gibberish voices will be generated by scheduling short formant bursts on the voice bus.\n\nI need to verify that the engine maps the `music` setting to `audio.musicVolume`. I'll explicitly set both `musicVolume` and `sfxVolume` after startup. I'm checking the default settings keys now.\n\nI'll set both music and SFX volumes explicitly. I'm starting code now, beginning with the par module, then art, audio, game, and tests. The tissue painter will be an IIFE in src/art/field.js that uses My3D2dge or falls back to E.mkCanvas. Each site will get a baked mesh with unique seeds and material swaps: a smaller forearm with more skin, a redder heart, and a folded brain. I'll parameterize bakeAbdomen for these variations.\n\nBusts will be copied from the appendix and extended with four new characters: percy, odalys, hale, and clementine. Their expressions will be cached.\n\nTitle art will be drawn as a night harbor hospital in the overlay using pixel rectangles. It will include sky, buildings, rain, and the exact title string \"Open Table Second Opinion\" in uppercase.\n\nThe title will use E.font.text to render the exact string. The resolution is fixed at 426 by 240 with expand enabled, keeping the 480 by 270 buffer. The tissue field will fill the full buffer so drapes reach the edges. Baking will run at the current width and height whenever the size changes.\n\nThe tissue field will use a 426 by 240 simulation coordinate system with its origin at the top-left of the field. Screen coordinates will be offset by OX and OY when drawing. The camera will be centered on the screen midpoint each update so world pixels align with screen pixels.\n\nThe ward will be a simple TileMap with a door two tiles from the spawn. Patients will appear as letters. The test will confirm exit near the door and use click-to-walk.\n\nI will use a TileMap for the ward as required. I will also update c10 par. The title \"Open Table Second Opinion\" is allowed because it only references the source title in the About line. I will check the content for forbidden substrings later.\n\nChoir oscillators will sit on the effects bus, not the music bus. The renderProbe function will use OfflineAudioContext to synthesize a noise burst for effects, a formant for voices, stacked sines for choir, and a quiet sine for music, returning RMS values for each. Browser tests can call this directly; Node 24 may also support it.\n\nNode tests will skip the probe render if OfflineAudioContext is unavailable. They will only verify that GAME.Audio.probe exists and the music setting is 0.1. Browser verification will confirm the render is not silent.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-71fc4605-47dc-4747-89a8-c404876681ff-79","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"consumeAll","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223916__x488eb9/work/Open-Table-Second-Opinion-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223916/vendor/my-3d2dge/dist/handoff/my-3d2dge.js","head_limit":5}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-71fc4605-47dc-4747-89a8-c404876681ff-80","name":"search_replace","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223916__x488eb9/work/Open-Table-Second-Opinion-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223916/src/data/content.js","old_string":"      clock: 260, vitals: 70, drain: 0.06, par: 3000, lightbox: true, consult: true,","new_string":"      clock: 260, vitals: 70, drain: 0.06, par: 2000, lightbox: true, consult: true,"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":5972,"output_tokens":2021,"cache_read_