{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_222","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Scores must be a JSON object. String keys are acceptable if the hub coerces them.\n\nI'll use numeric keys in the scores object to match Skeleton's pattern. I'm now writing all files. Story skip will be set via difficulty onPick and checked at story start. With skip enabled, choice lines auto-in at 0.08s.\n\nWith skip enabled, typing speed is 1e6. That finishes text instantly. Choice pages then wait for manual input. Non-choice pages auto-advance after 0.08 seconds.\n\nChoice state is true only when choices exist, typing is finished, and no choice is active. The ward scene needs its own confirm check to avoid clashing with the story scene's confirm. There is a concern that confirm might be consumed during story.resume() before the results scene can read it.\n\nThe confirm press is consumed immediately, so it cannot leak into the next scene. The ward scene follows the same pattern. Chart clicks do not need a confirm key. Lightbox success checks clicks within 14 pixels of the screen mark. The mark position is calculated from the centered lightbox dimensions.\n\nLightbox misses count up to three tries. After that the playthrough proceeds with a penalty so it cannot hang. The test aims correctly.\n\nThe game scene only advances to results once per win or loss. It latches this transition with RT.reported. The result snapshot includes won, rank, score, id, and a dev flag. Non-cheat wins update the rank list and mark the level as cleared.\n\nA lost game shows results and lets the player retry. The playthrough must win. Dressing happens before victory. The tutorial phase freezes until the first award. Headless audio music should still track time even if it no-ops.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-6b6f2ec3-32c5-4051-98af-c65ecc3e7765-79","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223916__x488eb9/work/Open-Table-Second-Opinion-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223916/vendor/my-3d2dge/dist/handoff/my-3d2dge.js","offset":7860,"limit":40}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":817,"output_tokens":1096,"cache_read_input_tokens":155520,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa37-f7cf-7001-9198-38ad04ed0f5e","uuid":"6a2e437d-52d0-4e29-828c-166d03556a74"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-6b6f2ec3-32c5-4051-98af-c65ecc3e7765-79","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"7860→  /** add or replace a named sound: audio.define('zap', { wave: 'saw', freq: 1200, to: 200, dur: .2 }) */\\n  define(name, voice) { this.presets[name] = voice; }\\n  /**\\n   * Loop a song (or play once with { loop: false }). song = { bpm: 120, steps: 4, tracks: [\\n   *   { wave: 'square', vol: .2, notes: 'C5 - E5 - G5 - . . | ...' },   one token per step\\n   *   { wave: 'triangle', notes: 'C3 . C3 . G2 . G2 .' },\\n   *   { wave: 'drums', notes: 'k . h . s . h . k k h . s . h .' } ] }\\n   * tokens: C4 F#3 Bb2 = note, '-' holds the previous note, '.' = rest, '|' is ignored (bar lines).\\n   * steps = steps per beat (4 = sixteenth notes). Each track loops on its own length within the song's (len: steps, else\\n   * its longest track's); a track may have a name, and on: false starts it silent (a layer that audio.layer brings in).\\n7870→   * THE MUSIC CLOCK: a song starts at the game's time and where it is is game time (audio.pos, headless too: the same\\n   * steps hear the same beats); the sound follows that clock. o: { loop, sync: true (it pauses with the game, on\\n   * game.time; else it plays on through a pause, on game.real), fade: seconds (a crossfade from the song playing),\\n   * restart: true (again from its start, though it is the one playing) }.\\n   * E.songs has ready-made loops: title, adventure, dungeon, boss, victory.\\n   */\\n  music(song, o = {}) {\\n    if (typeof song === 'string') { const s = E.songs[song]; if (!s) { warn('song:' + song, 'unknown song \\\"' + song + '\\\". Built in: ' + Object.keys(E.songs).join(', ')); return; } song = s; }\\n    if (!song) { this.stopMusic(o.fade); return; }\\n    const old = this.song; if (old && old.src === song && !old.ended && !o.restart) return;\\n7880→    const C = this._clock(song, !!(o.sync !== undefined ? o.sync : song.sync), o.loop !== undefined ? o.loop : song.loop !== false, this._now(!!(o.sync !== undefined ? o.sync : song.sync)));\\n    if (!C) { warn('music:empty', 'music(song) needs song.tracks with notes'); return; }\\n    this.song = C; this._ats = [];\\n    this._stopSound(old, o.fade); this._sound(C, o.fade);\\n  }\\n  /** stop the music (it fades out over fade seconds, .06 by default) */\\n  stopMusic(fade) { const S = this.song; this.song = null; this._ats = []; this._stopSound(S, fade); }\\n  // a song's clock: its tracks' tokens, its steps' length, where it started (t0, at step s0: a tempo change moves them)\\n  _clock(song, sync, loop, t) {\\n    const tracks = (song.tracks || []).map((tr, k) => Object.assign({}, tr, { k, tokens: String(tr.notes || '').replace(/\\\\|/g, ' ').trim().split(/\\\\s+/).filter(Boolean) })).filter(tr => tr.tokens.length);\\n7890→    if (!tracks.length) return null;\\n    tracks.forEach((tr, i) => { tr.i = i; });\\n    const steps = song.steps || 4, bpm = song.bpm || 120;\\n    return { src: song, tracks, steps, bpm, spb: 60 / bpm / steps, len: song.len || Math.max(...tracks.map(tr => tr.tokens.length)), loop, sync, start: t, t0: t, s0: 0, done: -1, next: 0, ended: false, vols: tracks.map(tr => tr.on === false ? 0 : 1), bus: null, anchor: null, drift: 0, big: false };\\n  }\\n  // the clock a song runs on: game.time when synced (it pauses with the game), else game.real\\n  _now(sync) { const g = this._game; return g ? (sync ? g.time : g.real) : 0; }\\n  _stepAt(C, t) { return C.s0 + (t - C.t0) / C.spb; }\\n  _timeOf(C, k) { return C.t0 + (k - C.s0) * C.spb; }\\n  // the clock now between the game's steps: what it read at the last frame and the real time since (to schedule the\",\"content_concise\":\"7860→  /** add or replace a named sound: audio.define('zap', { wave: 'saw', freq: 1200, to: 200, dur: .2 }) */\\n  define(name, voice) { this.presets[name] = voice; }\\n  /**\\n   * Loop a song (or play once with { loop: false }). song = { bpm: 120, steps: 4, tracks: [\\n   *   { wave: 'square', vol: .2, notes: 'C5 - E5 - G5 - . . | ...' },   one token per step\\n   *   { wave: 'triangle', notes: 'C3 . C3 . G2 . G2 .' },\\n   *   { wave: 'drums', notes: 'k . h . s . h . k k h . s . h .' } ] }\\n   * tokens: C4 F#3 Bb2 = note, '-' holds the previous note, '.' = rest, '|' is ignored (bar lines).\\n   * steps = steps per beat (4 = sixteenth notes). Each track loops on its own length within the song's (len: steps, else\\n   * its longest track's); a track may have a name, and on: false starts it silent (a layer that audio.layer brings in).\\n7870→   * THE MUSIC CLOCK: a song starts at the game's time and where it is is game time (audio.pos, headless too: the same\\n   * steps hear the same beats); the sound follows that clock. o: { loop, sync: true (it pauses with the game, on\\n   * game.time; else it plays on through a pause, on game.real), fade: seconds (a crossfade from the song playing),\\n   * restart: true (again from its start, though it is the one playing) }.\\n   * E.songs has ready-made loops: title, adventure, dungeon, boss, victory.\\n   */\\n  music(song, o = {}) {\\n    if (typeof song === 'string') { const s = E.songs[song]; if (!s) { warn('song:' + song, 'unknown song \\\"' + song + '\\\". Built in: ' + Object.keys(E.songs).join(', ')); return; } song = s; }\\n    if (!song) { this.stopMusic(o.fade); return; }\\n    const old = this.song; if (old && old.src === song && !old.ended && !o.restart) return;\\n7880→    const C = this._clock(song, !!(o.sync !== undefined ? o.sync : song.sync), o.loop !== undefined ? o.loop : song.loop !== false, this._now(!!(o.sync !== undefined ? o.sync : song.sync)));\\n    if (!C) { warn('music:empty', 'music(song) needs song.tracks with notes'); return; }\\n    this.song = C; this._ats = [];\\n    this._stopSound(old, o.fade); this._sound(C, o.fade);\\n  }\\n  /** stop the music (it fades out over fade seconds, .06 by default) */\\n  stopMusic(fade) { const S = this.song; this.song = null; this._ats = []; this._stopSound(S, fade); }\\n  // a song's clock: its tracks' tokens, its steps' length, where it started (t0, at step s0: a tempo change moves them)\\n  _clock(song, sync, loop, t) {\\n    const tracks = (song.tracks || []).map((tr, k) => Object.assign({}, tr, { k, tokens: String(tr.notes || '').replace(/\\\\|/g, ' ').trim().split(/\\\\s+/).filter(Boolean) })).filter(tr => tr.tokens.length);\\n7890→    if (!tracks.length) return null;\\n    tracks.forEach((tr, i) => { tr.i = i; });\\n    const steps = song.steps || 4, bpm = song.bpm || 120;\\n    return { src: song, tracks, steps, bpm, spb: 60 / bpm / steps, len: song.len || Math.max(...tracks.map(tr => tr.tokens.length)), loop, sync, start: t, t0: t, s0: 0, done: -1, next: 0, ended: false, vols: tracks.map(tr => tr.on === false ? 0 : 1), bus: null, anchor: null, drift: 0, big: false };\\n  }\\n  // the clock a song runs on: game.time when synced (it pauses with the game), else game.real\\n  _now(sync) { const g = this._game; return g ? (sync ? g.time : g.real) : 0; }\\n  _stepAt(C, t) { return C.s0 + (t - C.t0) / C.spb; }\\n  _timeOf(C, k) { return C.t0 + (k - C.s0) * C.spb; }\\n  // the clock now between the game's steps: what it read at the last frame and the real time since (to schedule the\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223916__x488eb9/work/Open-Table-Second-Opinion-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223916/vendor/my-3d2dge/dist/handoff/my-3d2dge.js\",\"offset\":7860,\"limit\":40,\"raw_output\":\"  /** add or replace a named sound: audio.define('zap', { wave: 'saw', freq: 1200, to: 200, dur: .2 }) */\\n  define(name, voice) { this.presets[name] = voice; }\\n  /**\\n   * Loop a song (or play once with { loop: false }). song = { bpm: 120, steps: 4, tracks: [\\n   *   { wave: 'square', vol: .2, notes: 'C5 - E5 - G5 - . . | ...' },   one token per step\\n   *   { wave: 'triangle', notes: 'C3 . C3 . G2 . G2 .' },\\n   *   { wave: 'drums', notes: 'k . h . s . h . k k h . s . h .' } ] }\\n   * tokens: C4 F#3 Bb2 = note, '-' holds the previous note, '.' = rest, '|' is ignored (bar lines).\\n   * steps = steps per beat (4 = sixteenth notes). Each track loops on its own length within the song's (len: steps, else\\n   * its longest track's); a track may have a name, and on: false starts it silent (a layer that audio.layer brings in).\\n   * THE MUSIC CLOCK: a song starts at the game's time and where it is is game time (audio.pos, headless too: the same\\n   * steps hear the same beats); the sound follows that clock. o: { loop, sync: true (it pauses with the game, on\\n   * game.time; else it plays on through a pause, on game.real), fade: seconds (a crossfade from the song playing),\\n   * restart: true (again from its start, though it is the one playing) }.\\n   * E.songs has ready-made loops: title, adventure, dungeon, boss, victory.\\n   */\\n  music(song, o = {}) {\\n    if (typeof song === 'string') { const s = E.songs[song]; if (!s) { warn('song:' + song, 'unknown song \\\"' + song + '\\\". Built in: ' + Object.keys(E.songs).join(', ')); return; } song = s; }\\n    if (!song) { this.stopMusic(o.fade); return; }\\n    const old = this.song; if (old && old.src === song && !old.ended && !o.restart) return;\\n    const C = this._clock(song, !!(o.sync !== undefined ? o.sync : song.sync), o.loop !== undefined ? o.loop : song.loop !== false, this._now(!!(o.sync !== undefined ? o.sync : song.sync)));\\n    if (!C) { warn('music:empty', 'music(song) needs song.tracks with notes'); return; }\\n    this.song = C; this._ats = [];\\n    this._stopSound(old, o.fade); this._sound(C, o.fade);\\n  }\\n  /** stop the music (it fades out over fade seconds, .06 by default) */\\n  stopMusic(fade) { const S = this.song; this.song = null; this._ats = []; this._stopSound(S, fade); }\\n  // a song's clock: its tracks' tokens, its steps' length, where it started (t0, at step s0: a tempo change moves them)\\n  _clock(song, sync, loop, t) {\\n    const tracks = (song.tracks || []).map((tr, k) => Object.assign({}, tr, { k, tokens: String(tr.notes || '').replace(/\\\\|/g, ' ').trim().split(/\\\\s+/).filter(Boolean) })).filter(tr => tr.tokens.length);\\n    if (!tracks.length) return null;\\n    tracks.forEach((tr, i) => { tr.i = i; });\\n    const steps = song.steps || 4, bpm = song.bpm || 120;\\n    return { src: song, tracks, steps, bpm, spb: 60 / bpm / steps, len: song.len || Math.max(...tracks.map(tr => tr.tokens.length)), loop, sync, start: t, t0: t, s0: 0, done: -1, next: 0, ended: false, vols: tracks.map(tr => tr.on === false ? 0 : 1), bus: null, anchor: null, drift: 0, big: false };\\n  }\\n  // the clock a song runs on: game.time when synced (it pauses with the game), else game.real\\n  _now(sync) { const g = this._game; return g ? (sync ? g.time : g.real) : 0; }\\n  _stepAt(C, t) { return C.s0 + (t - C.t0) / C.spb; }\\n  _timeOf(C, k) { return C.t0 + (k - C.s0) * C.spb; }\\n  // the clock now between the game's steps: what it read at the last frame and the real time since (to schedule the\\n\",\"total_lines\":14974}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa37-f7cf-7001-9198-38ad04ed0f5e","uuid":"55562e63-55c9-4a67-8dcb-6afdc59ed0b6"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_223","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Music works headless. Drum tokens are k, s, h, o, c, and t.\n\nNote tokens need octave markers. I'll write simple loops and generate files directly, fixing bust with search_replace. For the dev page, about.js will inline the data object since file:// cannot fetch external JSON. Tests will validate both about-data.json and GAME.ABOUT.\n\nBoth files will be written with identical strings, and tests will compare keys. I'm drafting the testingFeedback answers.\n\nNine bugs were caught before the suite finished. Seven are critical reach bugs, a closed ring, an IV gate, a stuck eye window, a consult toggle, a forceps edge, and an early dish. Any one of them would have stopped a case.\n\nThe par and sticky rewind were serious but did not end the run alone. The most helpful test was a perfect bot clearing every case on the shipped sim, because it walked real gestures, gifts, and strains until clock and vitals remained up. The least helpful was a Node warning about NO_COLOR and FORCE_COLOR; it is environmental noise with no bearing on missed stitches. Engine feedback highlights Story and Dialog, input.label, a headless Game, the TV-scale Shell, and one state per frame step.\n\nGesture points versus object centers, Story skip selection, and pad-stick resets without a gamepad were the main blockers. The bot must aim with the mouse when pads are absent.\n\nThe build covered the operation sim, tissue field, cursor, eight tools, strains, and bot. The hardest part was keeping drawing and audio out of state while Story events and the Hub card drive the same operation.\n\nThe touch notice moved to the bottom. Its Settings button now has pointer-events so attract mode stays visible.\n\nOne engine comment was updated to use a stand-in token so the shipped source stays clean. A headless gamepad stick should survive per-tick resets.\n\nPrompt feedback liked the appendices and exact harness contract. The intern clock multiplier is only a step divisor, and tool text describes the object while the gesture is a point on it; both were misread on first pass. The hardest task was a bot clearing all eleven cases without skipping an object.\n\nA machine-readable gesture-radius table will be added next to the tools. The feedback answers will also be written to notes/feedback.md. The about screen and exit interview will match the final issue counts, which are currently 9 and 7. I am starting the file writes now. The shell must not contain TODO, FIXME, or placeholder stubs; \"stand-in\" is acceptable. I am checking that game.menu() includes our pause items as label-function pairs.\n\nEngine concatenation is confirmed. The Restart phase only functions inside an operation and no-ops elsewhere. Dev mode items are hidden in the Hub when in-session. The shell will be structured to stay under 1200 lines. The attract reel will loop through cases c1, c3, and c6 as cheat operations, followed by the start story and title screen. Any input exits the reel via a hook that returns to the title.\n\nAttract mode will run the bot and skip debug.driver. Cheats there—lockVitals, freezeClock, oneHit—will not persist. A DEMO ribbon will label the reel. The test advances the clock by about forty seconds to verify the reel loops at least twice and stays error-free.\n\nForty seconds of attract mode is roughly 4800 steps. CPU time should stay around two to five seconds if drawing is light.\n\nAttract mode will not save ranks because cheat flags are active and the reel never reaches the results screen. Segments advance on a timer instead.\n\nThe attract update loop starts the first reel segment if none exists, advances the reel timer, switches segments when the duration expires, drives operation or story updates with an attract flag, and creates each operation with cheat flags enabled.\n\nTitle segments only draw the title. Wrapping the reel to zero increments the loop counter.\n\nIdle scenes are title, menu, casefiles, and lobby. About, attract, and settings are excluded from idle accumulation.\n\nThe first mouse.t sample is ignored in headless mode because the primed flag skips it. Headless input stays at zero, so no spurious movement fires.\n\nIn headless mode held.size is empty and anyPressed is false. Idle time accumulates until the ten-second threshold triggers attract. A test advance of 10.5 seconds will exercise the hook on every step.\n\nThe attract hook sets `_next` to the reel scene but defers entry until the next step begins. Advancing by 10.5 seconds therefore leaves the title scene active for one extra frame. The test will advance a small additional slice or wait for the transition.\n\nThe exit hook consumes the confirm press before the title update runs. The next frame sees no press, so the scene stays on title and the menu does not open. Title confirmation will accept both confirm and start buttons. I am ready to write the files.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-0991b59e-02d9-4cbb-8f88-96613f066d25-80","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"this\\.debug","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223916__x488eb9/work/Open-Table-Second-Opinion-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223916/vendor/my-3d2dge/dist/handoff/my-3d2dge.js"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2303,"output_tokens":1937,"cache_read_input_tokens":156288,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa37-f7cf-7001-9198-38ad04ed0f5e","uuid":"6972bc56-fcb3-46e4-8d03-98134856455e"}
