{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_251","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The title font uses the same glyphs as text, which includes lowercase letters.\n\nI'll write shell.js with a 14×10 tile grid. The door sits at (12, 5) and the spawn at (11, 5). The hero starts at a door-adjacent cell. Movement uses WASD or arrow keys via input.move().\n\nWASD will be kept on both the cursor and movement actions. The base input spread runs after the override, so both sets of keys remain available.\n\nThe input map binds Arrow keys and D-pad buttons to movement, while WASD and ArrowUp are reserved for the cursor. Ward walking will combine movement controls with cursor keys. The hero spawns within 36 pixels of the door, so the nearDoor check passes immediately without any movement.\n\nAutosave triggers on entering the ward. Rank saves are restricted to wins without tuning or cheat usage, stored in RT.ranks and persisted via saves.write. Save and load appear only during the ward, with canSave disabled outside it. A DEV tag suppresses rank saving when tuning or cheats are active.\n\nAttract mode will idle for ten seconds on the title, menu, casefiles, and lobby screens. It ignores input while paused or on about, settings, and controls. Any input during attract advances to the title screen and suppresses that frame.\n\nA reel state machine will cycle through Op/Bot segments with cheat flags enabled.\n\nThe hub scene starts only if active; otherwise it loads the title screen without connecting. Headless tests default to no game loop.\n\nShell initializes before start, and draw is enabled so errors are collected. The game object exposes a debug snapshot function at window.__ot. Standalone tests get a hub probe and an inject helper via G.__hubProbe and G.__hubInject. For the two-frame stroke test, the client sends a reliable `i` message that the host applies to the correct seat.\n\nThe two-frame stroke test will use __hubInject to send a reliable `i` message from the client iframe to the host. The host handler applies that position immediately and queues it as pending input for the next step. The hub probe will also expose player positions.\n\nThe hub probe returns each player’s id, position, tool, and trigger state. It also reports the hub time when active.\n\nSeat colors come from CFG.SEAT rather than the hub’s q.color. Player ids match their seat index in info.players.\n\nThe host seeds roundsCase(n) using HUB.rng. A seedInt helper converts the generator output to an integer.\n\nThe server runs roundsCase with the human player count and resident difficulty. It draws nothing.\n\nPhase transitions wait for confirm, answer ready, more, or a click. Play begins when HUB.time() reaches zero.\n\nHost snapshots run at twelve frames per second. Clients send inputs at fifteen frames per second.\n\nEach snapshot carries a compact payload with a sequence number, clock, vitals, phase, and boolean flags for win, loss, and breath.\n\nThe snapshot payload also includes a code-blue flag, a slice of living objects with their positions and types, and player states with tool, trigger, actions, and gone flags.\n\nClients apply remote snapshots while keeping their own cursor local. The host simulates all other hands and authority. Clients still send inputs and mirror the host's remote state for other players and objects.\n\nThe host applies remote stroke inputs immediately to the matching player object and stores the position in H.remote. During the next step, remote seats receive those coordinates directly via Op.step. Local seats keep their own cursor updates. The server has no local player, so every seat is treated as remote.\n\nThe server sends only snapshot messages and returns immediately from draw.\n\nThe round finishes on time-up for the host alone, once. Victory requires the round to be closed or won. Standings list every player exactly once, ordered by actions from highest to lowest. Awards include one Steadiest Hand title with a 24-character limit and a 40-character detail line.\n\nIf the host leaves, clients stop finishing because they are no longer the host. The round is voided if the host exits before time-up.\n\nProgress messages are rate-capped. Card events store the card for drawing. The countdown waits until time() reaches zero before play begins.\n\nThe connect function catches connection errors and returns null so the game falls back to the title screen. Music initializes after start and plays the night track. The settings music value of 0.1 applies correctly.\n\nCustom settings apply functions must not touch TUNE.dirty. Preferences will be stored on GAME.PREFS so tests can read them. The assist slider maps to a 0.6 offset that matches the default surgical tune. playTune copies TUNE and overrides hands.assist and hands.cursor from these prefs. Difficulty is a next-case preference, not a mid-operation setting.\n\nThe dev panel draws on the canvas when RT.devOpen is true. Left and right arrows change values, up and down move between items, and confirm applies changes. Tabs are tune, cheat, and jump.\n\nThe dev panel offers cheat toggles for reveal, infSteady, lockVitals, freezeClock, oneHit, slowmo, autosurgeon, force win, force lose, and code blue. The jump tab lists cases and phases to begin directly. When the dev panel is open, the operation scene skips cursor keys so the panel can handle left and right input; WASD and arrow keys remain bound to the cursor.\n\nSlow-mo cycles game.timeScale between 0.25, 0.5, 1, and 2 independently of the breath effect. Autosurgeon enables RT.auto so the operation uses Bot.device for seat 0. Force-lose triggers GAME.Sim.lose(S, 'dev'). Force-win marks all objects done and releases the hold flag to clear the phase.\n\nA \"Clear phase\" cheat marks every object done and releases the hold flag so the phase advances. The attract reel skips rank recording while RT.attract is true. A fast debug driver runs sixteen steps at 1/45 when debug mode is active and attract is off. beginCase loads a ferry case with a player count or a standard case definition, sets the runtime definition and mode, then creates the operation state.\n\nbeginCase accepts seed, difficulty, player count, and phase options. It applies clock bonuses or penalties and returns the operation state. The chart scene calls beginCase with bonus values after the lightbox.\n\nThe chart scene loads the lightbox for the selected case and resets miss count. A click within fourteen pixels of the screen mark counts as a right hit; otherwise it counts as a miss. The mark position is calculated from the field origin plus the lightbox offset. Three misses trigger a proceed action that applies clock and steady bonuses before starting the operation. The plan also considers allowing the confirm key to count as a try.\n\nThe story scene now routes to the chart using RT.fromStory and RT.redirect. It sets RT.chartFor to the case id only when that case is not already in progress, avoiding a sticky flag that would skip later lightboxes. Because each case has a distinct id, this check naturally prevents repeated charting for the same case.\n\nThe chart scene sets `RT.chartFor` to the current case id only after the lightbox finishes. On entry it checks that the id is not already stored, so a retry of the same case skips the lightbox. A global `chartDone` flag is avoided.\n\nThe results scene handles confirm after a loss by calling Op.retryPhase to restart the operation without returning to the chart. The summary also specifies that a held story resumes on confirm, while other outcomes route to the lab, lobby, or ward.\n\nThe results scene will show a canvas menu with Retry and Give up after a loss, and Continue after a win. A lost round may retry via Op.retryPhase rather than the confirm key itself. Ranks are recorded only on a clean win that is not dirty, used a cheat, in attract mode, lab, or rounds.\n\nThe cleared flag is set when entering results, not on confirm. Saved patients are tracked by mapping each case to a person name; winning a case marks that person saved. Ward visitors are the intersection of the current chapter list and the saved-patient map. The ward scene places the hero near the door and treats them as already inside if they start within thirty-six pixels.\n\nVisitors are placed along the wall. Near the door, pressing confirm resumes a held story and returns to the story scene if no redirect is active; otherwise it goes to the menu. Clicking a visitor shows their line. The attract reel input hook suppresses inputs for a brief period, then checks for any pressed key, click, held button, or stick movement.\n\nThe attract reel treats a primed mouse press as a hit. That triggers a title screen transition with no fade, consumes all input, and resets the idle counter. The hook then checks whether the current scene is one of the watchable screens while the shell is closed and the game is not paused or in a session. If so, it counts any pressed key, click, or held button as active.\n\nThe attract reel also counts active input when the pad aim or move exceeds 0.25. If no activity occurs for ten seconds, the reel starts and the scene switches to attract with no fade. Tests that idle for ten and a half seconds must advance one extra frame so the transition completes.\n\nThe input hook receives a delta-time argument. It remains active while paused and ignores the settings shell, so idle time is not counted during settings. The About scene is excluded from the attract reel watch list.\n\nThe reel will run ten segments: a story beat, case one, case three pools, case six tremolo, case seven second look, grand rounds with four bots, cantor, results, an eight-second title, and a looping wrap-around.\n\nThe attract reel will be a standalone scene that switches segments internally and counts loops. To avoid corrupting the campaign story state, it will use its own Story instance with a minimal node set for the portrait beat.\n\nThe reel will use a tiny E.Story instance for the portrait beat rather than drawing it manually. Each operation segment runs beginCase with cheats and bot control for a fixed duration, then advances without entering the real results scene. The results segment will instead snapshot a finished state by running the bot briefly and drawing the HUD directly. The title segment draws the title for eight seconds before wrapping to the next loop. Music will be triggered from the enter hook based on the current scene.\n\nThe enter hook maps each scene to a specific audio track, and music only plays during attract segments. Quill barks are selected by the current case index rather than random chance. The sustain toggle setting latches the trigger state on every press.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-38f57922-085b-47aa-bc87-98f0a57050de-39","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"lastDevice","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223916__x488eb9/work/Open-Table-Second-Opinion-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223916/vendor/my-3d2dge/dist/handoff/my-3d2dge.js","head_limit":15}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-38f57922-085b-47aa-bc87-98f0a57050de-40","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223916__x488eb9/work/Open-Table-Second-Opinion-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223916/src/about.js","limit":70}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2917,"output_tokens":5876,"cache_read_input_tokens":116992,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa37-f7cf-7001-9198-38ad04ed0f5e","uuid":"667d216b-e0c6-4157-98e9-b35f79ad158a"}
