{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-b2cee12f-5c86-449e-87e8-f4e240f99739-49","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"7971→  _sound(C, fade) {\\n    if (!C || !this._init()) return;\\n    const c = this.ctx, bus = c.createGain(); bus.connect(this._musBus); C.bus = bus;\\n    if (fade > 0) { bus.gain.setValueAtTime(0, c.currentTime); bus.gain.linearRampToValueAtTime(1, c.currentTime + fade); }\\n    for (const t of C.tracks) { t.gain = c.createGain(); t.gain.gain.value = C.vols[t.i]; t.gain.connect(bus); }\\n    if (!this._timer) this._timer = setInterval(() => { try { this._schedule(); } catch (e) { warn('music-fail', 'music stopped: ' + e.message); this.stopMusic(); } }, 25);\\n    this._schedule();\\n  }\\n  _stopSound(S, fade) {\\n7980→    if (!S || !S.bus || !this.ctx) return;\\n    const c = this.ctx, n = c.currentTime, f = Math.max(.02, fade || .06), bus = S.bus, g = bus.gain;\\n    g.cancelScheduledValues(n); g.setValueAtTime(g.value, n); g.linearRampToValueAtTime(0, n + f);\\n    if (fade > .06) this._fading.push(S);   // (a crossfade: it plays on as it fades)\\n    setTimeout(() => { try { bus.disconnect(); } catch (e) { /* gone */ } this._fading = this._fading.filter(x => x !== S); }, f * 1000 + 300);\\n  }\\n  _schedule() {\\n    const c = this.ctx; if (!c || c.state !== 'running') return;\\n    for (const C of [this.song, ...this._fading, ...this._stings]) if (C && C.bus) this._ahead(C);\\n    if (this._stings.length) this._stings = this._stings.filter(S => { const live = this._clockNow(S) < S.end + .5; if (!live) try { S.bus.disconnect(); } catch (e) { /* gone */ } return live; });\\n7990→    if (!this.song && !this._fading.length && !this._stings.length) { clearInterval(this._timer); this._timer = null; }\\n  }\\n  // a song's notes for the next 150 ms of its clock and the offset's more (each is sounded the offset early, so a 250 ms offset\\n  // needs its notes 400 ms ahead, or they would fall in the past and be skipped), each at the sound's time its anchor maps it\\n  // to (less the offset)\\n  _ahead(C) {\",\"content_concise\":\"7971→  _sound(C, fade) {\\n    if (!C || !this._init()) return;\\n    const c = this.ctx, bus = c.createGain(); bus.connect(this._musBus); C.bus = bus;\\n    if (fade > 0) { bus.gain.setValueAtTime(0, c.currentTime); bus.gain.linearRampToValueAtTime(1, c.currentTime + fade); }\\n    for (const t of C.tracks) { t.gain = c.createGain(); t.gain.gain.value = C.vols[t.i]; t.gain.connect(bus); }\\n    if (!this._timer) this._timer = setInterval(() => { try { this._schedule(); } catch (e) { warn('music-fail', 'music stopped: ' + e.message); this.stopMusic(); } }, 25);\\n    this._schedule();\\n  }\\n  _stopSound(S, fade) {\\n7980→    if (!S || !S.bus || !this.ctx) return;\\n    const c = this.ctx, n = c.currentTime, f = Math.max(.02, fade || .06), bus = S.bus, g = bus.gain;\\n    g.cancelScheduledValues(n); g.setValueAtTime(g.value, n); g.linearRampToValueAtTime(0, n + f);\\n    if (fade > .06) this._fading.push(S);   // (a crossfade: it plays on as it fades)\\n    setTimeout(() => { try { bus.disconnect(); } catch (e) { /* gone */ } this._fading = this._fading.filter(x => x !== S); }, f * 1000 + 300);\\n  }\\n  _schedule() {\\n    const c = this.ctx; if (!c || c.state !== 'running') return;\\n    for (const C of [this.song, ...this._fading, ...this._stings]) if (C && C.bus) this._ahead(C);\\n    if (this._stings.length) this._stings = this._stings.filter(S => { const live = this._clockNow(S) < S.end + .5; if (!live) try { S.bus.disconnect(); } catch (e) { /* gone */ } return live; });\\n7990→    if (!this.song && !this._fading.length && !this._stings.length) { clearInterval(this._timer); this._timer = null; }\\n  }\\n  // a song's notes for the next 150 ms of its clock and the offset's more (each is sounded the offset early, so a 250 ms offset\\n  // needs its notes 400 ms ahead, or they would fall in the past and be skipped), each at the sound's time its anchor maps it\\n  // to (less the offset)\\n  _ahead(C) {\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223916__x488eb9/work/Open-Table-Second-Opinion-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223916/vendor/my-3d2dge/dist/handoff/my-3d2dge.js\",\"offset\":7971,\"limit\":25,\"raw_output\":\"  _sound(C, fade) {\\n    if (!C || !this._init()) return;\\n    const c = this.ctx, bus = c.createGain(); bus.connect(this._musBus); C.bus = bus;\\n    if (fade > 0) { bus.gain.setValueAtTime(0, c.currentTime); bus.gain.linearRampToValueAtTime(1, c.currentTime + fade); }\\n    for (const t of C.tracks) { t.gain = c.createGain(); t.gain.gain.value = C.vols[t.i]; t.gain.connect(bus); }\\n    if (!this._timer) this._timer = setInterval(() => { try { this._schedule(); } catch (e) { warn('music-fail', 'music stopped: ' + e.message); this.stopMusic(); } }, 25);\\n    this._schedule();\\n  }\\n  _stopSound(S, fade) {\\n    if (!S || !S.bus || !this.ctx) return;\\n    const c = this.ctx, n = c.currentTime, f = Math.max(.02, fade || .06), bus = S.bus, g = bus.gain;\\n    g.cancelScheduledValues(n); g.setValueAtTime(g.value, n); g.linearRampToValueAtTime(0, n + f);\\n    if (fade > .06) this._fading.push(S);   // (a crossfade: it plays on as it fades)\\n    setTimeout(() => { try { bus.disconnect(); } catch (e) { /* gone */ } this._fading = this._fading.filter(x => x !== S); }, f * 1000 + 300);\\n  }\\n  _schedule() {\\n    const c = this.ctx; if (!c || c.state !== 'running') return;\\n    for (const C of [this.song, ...this._fading, ...this._stings]) if (C && C.bus) this._ahead(C);\\n    if (this._stings.length) this._stings = this._stings.filter(S => { const live = this._clockNow(S) < S.end + .5; if (!live) try { S.bus.disconnect(); } catch (e) { /* gone */ } return live; });\\n    if (!this.song && !this._fading.length && !this._stings.length) { clearInterval(this._timer); this._timer = null; }\\n  }\\n  // a song's notes for the next 150 ms of its clock and the offset's more (each is sounded the offset early, so a 250 ms offset\\n  // needs its notes 400 ms ahead, or they would fall in the past and be skipped), each at the sound's time its anchor maps it\\n  // to (less the offset)\\n  _ahead(C) {\\n\",\"total_lines\":14974}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa37-f7cf-7001-9198-38ad04ed0f5e","uuid":"447043ac-b400-4c2f-bdab-eb95ff6b3297"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_259","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The _init function returns false on failure. Headless mode sets audio.\n\nmusic() sets this.song via _clock before _sound. musicVolume is readable. sfx() may no-op if _init fails. I'll add try/catch onEv. shell.js will be a single file. Dialog voice blips will be set once. CAST members are the people. Ward patients will use small busts. A simple pixel room is enough for the ward instead of E.TileMap.\n\nHero speed is 70 pixels per second. Movement combines input.move() for arrows, dpad, and stick with WASD keys, avoiding double-counting shared inputs like ArrowLeft.\n\nHero movement now uses I.move() for stick and arrow input, plus WASD keys. Clicking targets the hero. The room is a 16-pixel wall rectangle with a door at the bottom center. The hero starts just above that door. A near-door threshold of 36 pixels is used.\n\nInteract prompts will use input.label('interact'). Visitors will stand at x=40+i*36, y=50. A portrait talk prompt appears when the hero is near a visitor and not near the door. The door takes priority if both are true. Each line shows in a box for three seconds. A snapshot function is being added for tests to capture screen state.\n\nThe snapshot function returns the current scene, menu state, story node, held status, choice availability, game state, lightbox mark, and ward visitor data.\n\nThe snapshot function now also captures attract mode, dev mode, ranks, cleared flags, and reel loops. Choice waiting is confirmed through the Dialog choices property. A debug snapshot will be exposed on window.__ot. Tests load scripts via shell, which calls game.start once.\n\nA single Game instance will serve the entire test suite. Sim unit tests can load without shell. The official runner loads all scripts once. A reset function will return to the title and clear story state between scene tests.\n\nThe reset function avoids forcing fadeTime to zero so tests can keep their own value. It returns to the title with no fade.\n\nAttract mode idle time is being calculated in steps. Ten seconds equals roughly 1200 steps plus one extra frame. The input hook may report zero delta on the first frame, but idle accumulation should still work.\n\nMouse priming is being handled so the initial mouse state change does not count as input.\n\nThe mouse priming logic now uses a `mouseInit` flag to ignore the first tick's sample, preventing an undefined-to-zero transition from counting as input. Stuck keys would block attract mode, so tests must not hold any keys. The tap input only registers for one step during advance. During the title scene, `shell.screen` is null.\n\nInput hooks run during pause and fade. The attract idle check must verify active input before starting. When returning to title from attract, the press is consumed this step, so the next step enters title without seeing it.\n\nThe title transition from attract mode will be handled only in the input hook, not the scene update. This avoids a double go call. The attract reel will run for one extra frame after the hook sets the next scene.\n\nDev mode tests can verify the pause menu includes a Dev Mode entry by checking game.o.menu.items. They can also confirm that calling GAME.Dev.set and GAME.openDev toggles devOpen and marks TUNE.dirty.\n\nChanging the assist setting via game.settings.set should update PREFS without touching TUNE.dirty.\n\nThe assist slider is being defined with a default of 0.6 and a range of 0 to 1.4, applying its value directly to GAME.PREFS. A cursor speed slider is also being added with a default of 330 and a range of 80 to 500. The voices slider is being configured with a default of 0.9.\n\nThe voices slider now applies its value to `g.audio.voiceVolume` and calls `applyVolume()`. A swap sticks toggle and a sustain tools selector are being added. Hints, blood, text speed, difficulty, glyphs, and music settings are also being configured. The menu.settings array will be passed to the Game constructor. The game id is set to `GAME_TITLE` and touch input is disabled.\n\nThe menu configuration is being split by session state. During a session, live, autoPause, and corner are enabled while menu.quit is disabled. Outside a session, menu.quit returns to the title screen and the menu items array is populated with a Dev Mode entry that calls GAME.openDev. That function is referenced at call time via closure.\n\nThe menu items array is defined before the Game constructor so the Dev Mode callback can reference `GAME.openDev` after it is assigned. Save access starts disabled and is enabled only when entering the ward, then disabled again on exit. The `game.saves` property is lazy and creates the Saves instance on first access.\n\nAutosave will be wrapped in try/catch to avoid headless errors. Rank data persists in RT.ranks and is saved with the game state. A PERSON map links character IDs to names. On winning a chapter, the player saves that character and advances the chapter counter. Ward visitors are filtered from GAME.WARD by chapter and only include characters already marked as saved.\n\nHub snapshots will use short objects and a type index map. The host is the source of truth; clients mirror state for drawing. A `__hubInject` path lets the host immediately apply injected coordinates to its own state, so client probes see live updates.\n\nThe host handler for injected messages applies the coordinates directly to the local player state. A client-side inject sends the data to the host, which updates its own state so probes see the change immediately. The sender is mapped to a seat index by matching their ID against the players array.\n\nThe host stores remote input in `H.hands[idx]` for each seat. The step loop uses that stored data for remote players instead of bots, passing the saved coordinates directly to the simulation so remote position sticks stay accurate. The local player index is found by matching `info.me` against the players array; if the server has no local player, every seat falls back to bot logic.\n\nBots will play when no input arrives, but they cannot finish the case. The round only ends on time-up, and won is set to S.closed if the world closed; otherwise it stays false. Early finishing is forbidden. A host-leave event does not trigger a finish.\n\nA server copy without a local player still runs the world and finishes rounds. It is identified by `info.server`, `HUB.server`, or a missing `info.me`. The draw loop returns early on the server. Player count comes from `info.players.length`. The simulation seed is generated with `HUB.rng('sim')` and clamped to a 31-bit positive integer. The host initializes at connection before the ready event.\n\nThe world is created during the connect event before the ready call. The ready function is invoked with no key after all answer handlers are registered. The host initializes its local state and waits for the start event. It counts until the phase time reaches zero before beginning play. During the wait phase the host does not simulate. During play it steps the simulation and sends snapshots. Clients apply those snapshots to draw. If the host steps before clients send input, bots fill the empty hands.\n\nA single accumulator tracks both snapshot and input timing. The host sends snapshots every ten engine steps. Clients send input every fifteen steps. Message size is small enough for unreliable delivery. Snapshot objects carry a flags bitfield for done, hidden, marked, and alive states. Clients replace local objects with snapshot data and restore their own cursor position afterward.\n\nClients will mirror the full snapshot into `H.view` for drawing. The host draws from `RT.S`. Probes read positions from the same mirrored state on both sides to stay consistent.\n\nThe probe reads player positions from RT.S when available, so the host can be tested without a client-side state object. A player who leaves will be marked as gone and included in the standings list.\n\nThe standings list includes every player ID exactly once, sorted by action count descending. Tied scores are grouped into sub-arrays.\n\nThe round ends by sending a final snapshot and then calling finish. The over message carries standings, scores, won status, awards, and optional stats. Awards include a \"Steadiest Hand\" badge for the player with the most clean actions. The hub scene shows a whiteboard during the wait phase with the title, players, ticks, controls, and remaining time. Alert, confirm, and prompt dialogs are avoided.\n\nTouch input on the card area below the lower seventy percent triggers an answer; the rest of the card triggers ready. The game debug driver is set to null by default. A shade latch resets each frame and applies field shading using beat, slow, and dim values from the simulation state. Art.force is exposed on GAME for screenshots.\n\nThe tissue canvas fills the full buffer, so the 426×240 simulation space centers the cavity at the buffer midpoint. The shade pass is expensive at 480×270 but acceptable once per frame; headless tests skip it unless forced, and one test may enable force for a single frame. The pixel loop covers roughly 100,000 pixels, which is manageable. Shading runs only once per frame, not every substep. Vector drawing always runs.\n\nDrape is a rectangle around the cavity. Objects are colored by type: shard, laceration, wound, bleeder, pool, polyp, mass, suture, bead, and nest. Guides are drawn as lines. The hand drawer adds its own offset, so callers pass raw coordinates. The HUD shows vitals and clock at the top left in a scaled white font with a dark outline.\n\nThe HUD will show the phase name, a tool icon at the bottom, and a quill bark at the bottom. A cold field overlay uses a teal outline with the word LONG BREATH when breathing is active; the simulation still runs this test even if it is not visual. Code blue mode draws large SHOCK text with peaks. Menu theme colors are scaled to 2x.\n\nIntern, Resident, and Attending are the three difficulty levels. Case files list the names from GAME.ORDER. Selecting a file sets RT.fileCase and RT.pending to 'file'. The difficulty menu then begins that case with the chosen level and switches to the operation scene. A new game resets story progress and saved characters.\n\nA new game resets story progress, saved characters, ranks, and cleared flags while preserving the music tune. It sets the chapter to one and enters the story scene. Returning from an operation preserves the running story state so it does not restart. After the epilogue ends, the next entry goes to the title screen instead of restarting the story.\n\nA `startStory` helper stops the current story, clears the chart cache, and enters the story scene. The story update checks `!story.running && !story._held` to redirect to the title screen when the story ends.\n\nStory enter sets `story.skip` from `game.debug.skipStory` and `story.talk.speed` from the text speed setting before calling `story.run('start')`. The lightbox chart scene does not use the story system. Tests must set `skipStory` before confirming difficulty so the story skip flag is active when the scene enters. Talk voice is initialized once at startup.\n\nDialog portraits use canvas save and restore. Expressions come from the current step. CAST holds character names. A hudText helper will draw HUD text. Credits pages are an array of title and body objects. The about scene is similar and returns to the menu on cancel. Incoming page text says a chart waits on the night desk after shift end; confirming resumes. The shell file gets an index manifest comment. Banned words are scanned after writing. The OPEN TABLE title uses uppercase font.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-1b0a6547-21ca-4190-9c90-fe6810243d01-50","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"VIEWS = \\{|side:|overhead:|threequarter:|front:","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223916__x488eb9/work/Open-Table-Second-Opinion-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223916/vendor/my-3d2dge/dist/handoff/my-3d2dge.js","head_limit":20}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":879,"output_tokens":6306,"cache_read_input_tokens":140288,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa37-f7cf-7001-9198-38ad04ed0f5e","uuid":"642cff9d-488c-424f-b4dc-984f1d01d0d3"}
