{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-94593ccb-560f-4c1f-b965-c8023e8c8fe2-0","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→---\\nname: long-running-background-tasks\\ndescription: >-\\n  Required reading before you start, watch, or wait on anything that keeps running after you launch it — background jobs, watchers, scheduled loops, CI, pull requests, training runs, dev servers, long builds. Read it before you launch such work and before you report on its state. Saying where a running job stands counts as working on it. Use when: about to launch, supervise, inspect, diagnose, or report on work that keeps running after it is started.\\n---\\n\\n# Working with long-running background tasks\\n\\nOnly submitting or queuing jobs is not sufficient to complete the task. The definition of done is the job has completed successfully and you have verified that it has produced all the expected outputs.\\n10→\\nThis means you must see through the jobs completion by watching the job to ensure that its healthy, progressing, and working as intended.\\n\\n## Tools available\\n\\nThere's two ways to watch jobs: monitor and loop. Decide which of the two you need, sometimes you need both.\\n\\n### monitor — deterministic, actionable events\\n\\n- Use a monitor when the event is a predicate a script can evaluate — an API response, an exit code, a port answering.\\n20→- Typical events: CI turning red, a PR comment or merge conflict, a PR becoming ready to merge, a dev server coming up or going down, a job reaching a terminal state.\\n- The monitor requires a script polls on a fixed cadence and stays silent until the predicate fires, so an idle job costs nothing. More on writing good monitoring scripts below.\\n- Examples of when to use:\\n  - Monitoring a PR — wake on CI breaking, merge conflicts, etc.\\n  - Checking if local tooling is up — wake on the service being unavailable, errors, etc.\\n  - All of these wakeup cases can be checked deterministically.\\n\\n### loop — qualitative health that needs reading\\n\\n- Use a loop when judging health requires reading the logs and system information to detect failing, faulty or stalled jobs. It uses a subagent which is more expensive but more thorough.\\n30→- Typical signals: the loss curve going unstable, a filtering pipeline producing garbage, throughput quietly degrading, faulty nodes throwing CUDA errors.\\n- A subagent reads the logs on a fixed cadence (say every 10 min) and reports what changed, so it costs a wakeup every cycle.\\n- Examples of when to use:\\n  - Checking on a training or data processing job — the subagent should scan for instability, degradation, faulty nodes, etc.\\n  - Monitoring system deployment health — the subagent should scan for 4xx/5xx, response times, resources, etc.\\n  - All of these wakeup cases are hard to describe deterministically and hard to fully enumerate, so it is better to use a subagent to validate.\\n\\n## Good monitoring scripts\\n\\nThe monitor tool wakes you up every time you log to stdout. Wakeups are expensive, so only log if there is something actionable — but log it as early as possible, since staying silent delays the task. Plan accordingly: minimize the number of wakeups while still firing them as quickly as possible.\\n40→\\n### Planning\\n\\n- Enumerate all terminal conditions.\\n  - For PRs, this includes the PR being merged or closed.\\n  - For long-running jobs, this includes the job completing or unrecoverable failures.\\n- Enumerate all other situations where a wakeup is needed.\\n  - For PRs, this would include when a comment arrives (there are four different types of PR comments), when CI fails, when a merge conflict arises, and when the PR becomes ready to merge (CI green, no conflicts, reviews complete, no blockers).\\n  - For an ML training job, this can be when the loss is unstable or the instance is preempted.\\n- Determine situations where the monitoring has to update what it is tracking. For example, for monitoring PRs, the latest commit hash may change as you push new commits.\\n50→\\n### Writing\\n\\n- The monitoring script should poll every 30s. `watch_pr.py` defaults to\\n  `--poll-interval 30`. Lower intervals cause GitHub rate limits, especially\\n  with more than one watcher on the same token.\\n- Based on the planning, exit immediately on terminal conditions with FAILED, DONE, or CANCELLED, and log ACTION_REQUIRED for wakeups.\\n- Send all debug diagnostics — both your watcher's own trace and the monitored job's output — to a file, so a log line can never be mistaken for DONE, FAILED, or CANCELLED.\\n- The script should fail as early as possible. Emit a FAILED token immediately when any required condition reaches terminal failure. For example, if you are waiting on CI and any of them fail, emit FAILED immediately.\\n- Write the script to a durable location such as ~/.grok and name it something clear such as `ci_watcher.sh`.\\n60→- Key every log file the script writes on the pid or session id of the thing being watched. The paths are otherwise fixed, so a second job of the same kind silently clobbers the first one's log.\\n\\n## Handling wake-ups\\n\\n- Address errors or actionable events immediately. Look through the logs and debug the issue thoroughly before settling on a root cause, as long-running processes are generally expensive. Resubmit the job once it is fixed:\\n  - For PRs, this means repushing the changes and restarting CI (if needed).\\n  - For jobs, requeue or relaunch the job.\\n  - Always restart the monitor or loop so that it points at the new PR or job.\\n- If there are multiple jobs running, start debugging immediately when any jobs fail. Minimize total wall-time with completing tasks.\\n\\n70→## Restarts\\n\\n- Whenever you relaunch a job, always update the monitor.\\n- Monitors have a maximum 10-hour time limit, so be sure to restart them if you hit the time limit and if the task being monitored is still running.\\n- When you spawn a new monitor, make sure to kill any previous ones that the new one replaces. For example, if you fix a training run and respawn it, be sure to kill previous monitors, as irrelevant wakeups are expensive.\\n\\n## Cleanup\\n\\n- If a monitor is in a terminal state, kill the monitor, the loop, and the job. Dangling processes such as unkilled broken training runs are expensive. Then, address all issues if present.\\n- If an outdated monitor wakes you — that is, it is attached to an old or dead run — make sure to kill it.\\n80→- Do not leave detached workers, duplicate pollers, or orphaned watcher processes behind.\\n\\n## Examples\\n\\n### Example: maintaining a dev server\\n\\nSuppose you just made some changes to the user's frontend code and would like to spin it up to test it. While you're testing it, you would like to know if the server goes down for any reason (if you made changes, for example). This is a great use case for the monitor tool. First start a React app in a background terminal, which reports its pid:\\n\\n```\\nrun_terminal_command(\\n90→  command=\\\"echo $$; npm start >~/.grok/long-running-background-tasks/dev_server_$$.log 2>&1\\\",\\n  timeout=0,\\n  description=\\\"Start the dev server on :3000\\\",\\n  background=true\\n) # prints the pid 123456789\\n```\\n\\nThere are no terminal states, but there are two actionable events from the watcher: `http://localhost:3000` coming up and going down.\\n\\nYou want to be notified as soon as it is up so you can use it, and you want to be woken when it stops so you can address it — fix the code, restart the server, free the port. Both down paths quote the tail of the server's log, so the wakeup arrives with the error already in hand. Polling every 5s is a good cadence since you want to quickly fix it if it is down, and it is not expensive to poll.\\n100→\\n```bash\\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\\n# Log paths are keyed on the server pid so two dev servers cannot clobber each other.\\nPID=$1 URL=http://localhost:3000 DIR=~/.grok/long-running-background-tasks\\nLOG=$DIR/dev_server_$PID.log\\nup() { curl -fsS -o /dev/null --max-time 5 \\\"$URL\\\" 2>>\\\"$DIR/watch_dev_server_$PID.log\\\"; }\\nwhy() { tail -20 \\\"$LOG\\\" | tr -d '\\\\r' | tr '\\\\n' ' '; }\\n\\nuntil up; do\\n110→  kill -0 \\\"$PID\\\" 2>/dev/null || { echo \\\"FAILED: pid $PID exited before $URL came up: $(why)\\\"; exit 1; }\\n  sleep 5\\ndone\\necho \\\"ACTION_REQUIRED: $URL is up\\\"\\n\\nwhile :; do\\n  sleep 5\\n  up || { echo \\\"ACTION_REQUIRED: $URL is down: $(why)\\\"; until up; do sleep 5; done; }\\ndone\\n```\\n120→\\nTo spawn the monitor, point it at the pid `npm start` reported:\\n\\n```\\nmonitor(\\n  command='bash ~/.grok/long-running-background-tasks/watch_dev_server.sh 123456789',\\n  description=\\\"Watch the dev server on :3000\\\"\\n)\\n```\\n\\n130→On the first wakeup the app is ready to use and you can start testing it. After that, you may be woken up if the server goes down. When you do:\\n- Read `~/.grok/long-running-background-tasks/dev_server_<pid>.log` for the full compile error or port conflict and fix it.\\n- If the dev server survived, hot reload picks the fix up and the watcher recovers on its own, so leave the monitor running.\\n- If the process is dead, restart it with a fresh `run_terminal_command`, then kill this monitor and spawn a new one against the new pid.\\n\\n### Example: PR monitoring\\n\\nPR monitoring should stop when the PR is merged or closed, and it should wake up on:\\n- For comments, you should ask users how they would like to resolve them (which ones to address automatically, whether to respond / resolve, etc.), then store the decision for new future sessions. There are four different types of PR comments.\\n  - Inline review threads\\n140→  - Review bodies\\n  - Conversation comments\\n  - Commit comments\\n- Failing CI - most can be fixed in the code but sometimes they may be flaky. For flaky CI, just rerun them.\\n- Merge conflicts\\n- The PR becoming ready to merge — CI passes, no merge conflicts, reviews complete, no blockers, no unresolved inline threads. That wakeup is `kind: mergeable`. Tell the user they can merge now. It is not terminal; keep watching until merge or close.\\n\\nYou can find the example script bundled with this skill at watch_pr.py. It needs an authenticated `gh` CLI (`gh auth login`) and `python3`. Default poll is 30s; a lower poll rate may hit GitHub rate limits at higher concurrency. Transport errors (timeouts, resets, DNS) retry with backoff and jitter and do not print a line.\\n\\n```\\n150→monitor(\\n  command='python3 ~/.grok/long-running-background-tasks/watch_pr.py \\\"https://github.com/xai-org/grok-build/pull/123\\\"',\\n  description=\\\"Watch PR 123\\\"\\n)\\n```\\n\\n### Example: training monitoring\\n\\nFor monitoring long training jobs, the terminal conditions are when the job either fails or completes. Further, many issues cannot be caught deterministically, so use a subagent to run a script for most of the high-level update, then scan the logs for errors such as faulty nodes or CUDA warnings.\\n\\n160→The script below is bundled with this skill as watch_training.py. Run it with `--help` for every flag and for the run-directory and log-line layout it expects, since it parses nanoGPT stdout out of a Slurm run dir.\\n\\n```md\\nKeep an eye on the nanoGPT run nanogpt_owt_124m_r1 (slurm job 1234567) and tell me what changed since last time. Worktree ~/worktrees/nanogpt-owt-124m.\\n\\n1. python3 ~/.grok/skills/long-running-background-tasks/watch_training.py --run-name nanogpt_owt_124m_r1 --job-id 1234567 --target-val 3.00\\n\\nHow to report:\\n- Start with the job's state and if it changed, e.g. \\\"still RUNNING\\\" or \\\"PENDING -> RUNNING\\\".\\n- Copy the ISSUES line and the STATUS line from the monitor exactly as it is written, numbers and all. STATUS will include where the run is heading: how far along, iters left, the eta, and whether val loss is still coming down. We want val loss at or under 3.00.\\n170→- If the state didn't change, tell me that in one line and stop.\\n- If the job has finished or died, give me the state, the exit code, and the last ISSUES, and run these two for me:\\n  sacct -j 1234567 -D -X -P -o JobID,State,ExitCode,DerivedExitCode,Elapsed,NodeList,FailedNode\\n  tail -20 ~/train_logs/slurm-nanogpt_owt_124m_r1-1234567.out\\n\\nKeep <=10 lines: state, progress and eta, loss trend, then anything that smells like training going unstable (loss spiking, NaN, val loss creeping back up) or the cluster misbehaving (a drained or failing node, OOM, NCCL errors, a preemption, iters suddenly slower than usual).\\n```\\n\\nLaunch the scheduler on a 10 min interval, and spawn a monitor to be notified when the job crashes or succeeds.\\n\\n180→```\\nscheduler_create(\\n  interval=\\\"10m\\\",\\n  prompt: \\\"...\\\", # Copy from the prompt above.\\n)\\nmonitor(\\n  # Wakes on pending/running/finished changes after the first observation.\\n  # First poll is silent unless the job is already COMPLETED or failed —\\n  # PENDING matches *ING, so treating an empty previous state as a\\n  # transition would wake immediately on a just-submitted job.\\n190→  command='J=1234567; p=; while :; do s=$(sacct -nXP -j $J -o State|head -1); s=${s%% *}; s=${s:-PENDING}; if [ \\\"$s\\\" != \\\"$p\\\" ]; then case $s in COMPLETED) echo \\\"DONE: $J\\\"; exit;; PENDING|RUNNING|CONFIGURING|COMPLETING|REQUEUED|SUSPENDED|RESIZING) [ -n \\\"$p\\\" ] && echo \\\"ACTION_REQUIRED: $J $p -> $s\\\";; *) echo \\\"FAILED: $J $s\\\"; exit 1;; esac; fi; p=$s; sleep 30; done',\\n  description=\\\"Watch training run\\\"\\n)\\n```\\n\\nLaunch both, so that we know when the job completes. When the monitor reports that the job has completed, cancel both the monitor and the scheduler, then determine next steps. Then:\\n- If it succeeded, simply present the results to the user.\\n- If it failed, debug the issue thoroughly, then fix and relaunch the job with new monitors attached. Make sure to have a thorough understanding of the issue before resubmitting, since training jobs are expensive. But always relaunch jobs so you don't waste the user's time.\\n\",\"content_concise\":\"1→---\\nname: long-running-background-tasks\\ndescription: >-\\n  Required reading before you start, watch, or wait on anything that keeps running after you launch it — background jobs, watchers, scheduled loops, CI, pull requests, training runs, dev servers, long builds. Read it before you launch such work and before you report on its state. Saying where a running job stands counts as working on it. Use when: about to launch, supervise, inspect, diagnose, or report on work that keeps running after it is started.\\n---\\n\\n# Working with long-running background tasks\\n\\nOnly submitting or queuing jobs is not sufficient to complete the task. The definition of done is the job has completed successfully and you have verified that it has produced all the expected outputs.\\n10→\\nThis means you must see through the jobs completion by watching the job to ensure that its healthy, progressing, and working as intended.\\n\\n## Tools available\\n\\nThere's two ways to watch jobs: monitor and loop. Decide which of the two you need, sometimes you need both.\\n\\n### monitor — deterministic, actionable events\\n\\n- Use a monitor when the event is a predicate a script can evaluate — an API response, an exit code, a port answering.\\n20→- Typical events: CI turning red, a PR comment or merge conflict, a PR becoming ready to merge, a dev server coming up or going down, a job reaching a terminal state.\\n- The monitor requires a script polls on a fixed cadence and stays silent until the predicate fires, so an idle job costs nothing. More on writing good monitoring scripts below.\\n- Examples of when to use:\\n  - Monitoring a PR — wake on CI breaking, merge conflicts, etc.\\n  - Checking if local tooling is up — wake on the service being unavailable, errors, etc.\\n  - All of these wakeup cases can be checked deterministically.\\n\\n### loop — qualitative health that needs reading\\n\\n- Use a loop when judging health requires reading the logs and system information to detect failing, faulty or stalled jobs. It uses a subagent which is more expensive but more thorough.\\n30→- Typical signals: the loss curve going unstable, a filtering pipeline producing garbage, throughput quietly degrading, faulty nodes throwing CUDA errors.\\n- A subagent reads the logs on a fixed cadence (say every 10 min) and reports what changed, so it costs a wakeup every cycle.\\n- Examples of when to use:\\n  - Checking on a training or data processing job — the subagent should scan for instability, degradation, faulty nodes, etc.\\n  - Monitoring system deployment health — the subagent should scan for 4xx/5xx, response times, resources, etc.\\n  - All of these wakeup cases are hard to describe deterministically and hard to fully enumerate, so it is better to use a subagent to validate.\\n\\n## Good monitoring scripts\\n\\nThe monitor tool wakes you up every time you log to stdout. Wakeups are expensive, so only log if there is something actionable — but log it as early as possible, since staying silent delays the task. Plan accordingly: minimize the number of wakeups while still firing them as quickly as possible.\\n40→\\n### Planning\\n\\n- Enumerate all terminal conditions.\\n  - For PRs, this includes the PR being merged or closed.\\n  - For long-running jobs, this includes the job completing or unrecoverable failures.\\n- Enumerate all other situations where a wakeup is needed.\\n  - For PRs, this would include when a comment arrives (there are four different types of PR comments), when CI fails, when a merge conflict arises, and when the PR becomes ready to merge (CI green, no conflicts, reviews complete, no blockers).\\n  - For an ML training job, this can be when the loss is unstable or the instance is preempted.\\n- Determine situations where the monitoring has to update what it is tracking. For example, for monitoring PRs, the latest commit hash may change as you push new commits.\\n50→\\n### Writing\\n\\n- The monitoring script should poll every 30s. `watch_pr.py` defaults to\\n  `--poll-interval 30`. Lower intervals cause GitHub rate limits, especially\\n  with more than one watcher on the same token.\\n- Based on the planning, exit immediately on terminal conditions with FAILED, DONE, or CANCELLED, and log ACTION_REQUIRED for wakeups.\\n- Send all debug diagnostics — both your watcher's own trace and the monitored job's output — to a file, so a log line can never be mistaken for DONE, FAILED, or CANCELLED.\\n- The script should fail as early as possible. Emit a FAILED token immediately when any required condition reaches terminal failure. For example, if you are waiting on CI and any of them fail, emit FAILED immediately.\\n- Write the script to a durable location such as ~/.grok and name it something clear such as `ci_watcher.sh`.\\n60→- Key every log file the script writes on the pid or session id of the thing being watched. The paths are otherwise fixed, so a second job of the same kind silently clobbers the first one's log.\\n\\n## Handling wake-ups\\n\\n- Address errors or actionable events immediately. Look through the logs and debug the issue thoroughly before settling on a root cause, as long-running processes are generally expensive. Resubmit the job once it is fixed:\\n  - For PRs, this means repushing the changes and restarting CI (if needed).\\n  - For jobs, requeue or relaunch the job.\\n  - Always restart the monitor or loop so that it points at the new PR or job.\\n- If there are multiple jobs running, start debugging immediately when any jobs fail. Minimize total wall-time with completing tasks.\\n\\n70→## Restarts\\n\\n- Whenever you relaunch a job, always update the monitor.\\n- Monitors have a maximum 10-hour time limit, so be sure to restart them if you hit the time limit and if the task being monitored is still running.\\n- When you spawn a new monitor, make sure to kill any previous ones that the new one replaces. For example, if you fix a training run and respawn it, be sure to kill previous monitors, as irrelevant wakeups are expensive.\\n\\n## Cleanup\\n\\n- If a monitor is in a terminal state, kill the monitor, the loop, and the job. Dangling processes such as unkilled broken training runs are expensive. Then, address all issues if present.\\n- If an outdated monitor wakes you — that is, it is attached to an old or dead run — make sure to kill it.\\n80→- Do not leave detached workers, duplicate pollers, or orphaned watcher processes behind.\\n\\n## Examples\\n\\n### Example: maintaining a dev server\\n\\nSuppose you just made some changes to the user's frontend code and would like to spin it up to test it. While you're testing it, you would like to know if the server goes down for any reason (if you made changes, for example). This is a great use case for the monitor tool. First start a React app in a background terminal, which reports its pid:\\n\\n```\\nrun_terminal_command(\\n90→  command=\\\"echo $$; npm start >~/.grok/long-running-background-tasks/dev_server_$$.log 2>&1\\\",\\n  timeout=0,\\n  description=\\\"Start the dev server on :3000\\\",\\n  background=true\\n) # prints the pid 123456789\\n```\\n\\nThere are no terminal states, but there are two actionable events from the watcher: `http://localhost:3000` coming up and going down.\\n\\nYou want to be notified as soon as it is up so you can use it, and you want to be woken when it stops so you can address it — fix the code, restart the server, free the port. Both down paths quote the tail of the server's log, so the wakeup arrives with the error already in hand. Polling every 5s is a good cadence since you want to quickly fix it if it is down, and it is not expensive to poll.\\n100→\\n```bash\\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\\n# Log paths are keyed on the server pid so two dev servers cannot clobber each other.\\nPID=$1 URL=http://localhost:3000 DIR=~/.grok/long-running-background-tasks\\nLOG=$DIR/dev_server_$PID.log\\nup() { curl -fsS -o /dev/null --max-time 5 \\\"$URL\\\" 2>>\\\"$DIR/watch_dev_server_$PID.log\\\"; }\\nwhy() { tail -20 \\\"$LOG\\\" | tr -d '\\\\r' | tr '\\\\n' ' '; }\\n\\nuntil up; do\\n110→  kill -0 \\\"$PID\\\" 2>/dev/null || { echo \\\"FAILED: pid $PID exited before $URL came up: $(why)\\\"; exit 1; }\\n  sleep 5\\ndone\\necho \\\"ACTION_REQUIRED: $URL is up\\\"\\n\\nwhile :; do\\n  sleep 5\\n  up || { echo \\\"ACTION_REQUIRED: $URL is down: $(why)\\\"; until up; do sleep 5; done; }\\ndone\\n```\\n120→\\nTo spawn the monitor, point it at the pid `npm start` reported:\\n\\n```\\nmonitor(\\n  command='bash ~/.grok/long-running-background-tasks/watch_dev_server.sh 123456789',\\n  description=\\\"Watch the dev server on :3000\\\"\\n)\\n```\\n\\n130→On the first wakeup the app is ready to use and you can start testing it. After that, you may be woken up if the server goes down. When you do:\\n- Read `~/.grok/long-running-background-tasks/dev_server_<pid>.log` for the full compile error or port conflict and fix it.\\n- If the dev server survived, hot reload picks the fix up and the watcher recovers on its own, so leave the monitor running.\\n- If the process is dead, restart it with a fresh `run_terminal_command`, then kill this monitor and spawn a new one against the new pid.\\n\\n### Example: PR monitoring\\n\\nPR monitoring should stop when the PR is merged or closed, and it should wake up on:\\n- For comments, you should ask users how they would like to resolve them (which ones to address automatically, whether to respond / resolve, etc.), then store the decision for new future sessions. There are four different types of PR comments.\\n  - Inline review threads\\n140→  - Review bodies\\n  - Conversation comments\\n  - Commit comments\\n- Failing CI - most can be fixed in the code but sometimes they may be flaky. For flaky CI, just rerun them.\\n- Merge conflicts\\n- The PR becoming ready to merge — CI passes, no merge conflicts, reviews complete, no blockers, no unresolved inline threads. That wakeup is `kind: mergeable`. Tell the user they can merge now. It is not terminal; keep watching until merge or close.\\n\\nYou can find the example script bundled with this skill at watch_pr.py. It needs an authenticated `gh` CLI (`gh auth login`) and `python3`. Default poll is 30s; a lower poll rate may hit GitHub rate limits at higher concurrency. Transport errors (timeouts, resets, DNS) retry with backoff and jitter and do not print a line.\\n\\n```\\n150→monitor(\\n  command='python3 ~/.grok/long-running-background-tasks/watch_pr.py \\\"https://github.com/xai-org/grok-build/pull/123\\\"',\\n  description=\\\"Watch PR 123\\\"\\n)\\n```\\n\\n### Example: training monitoring\\n\\nFor monitoring long training jobs, the terminal conditions are when the job either fails or completes. Further, many issues cannot be caught deterministically, so use a subagent to run a script for most of the high-level update, then scan the logs for errors such as faulty nodes or CUDA warnings.\\n\\n160→The script below is bundled with this skill as watch_training.py. Run it with `--help` for every flag and for the run-directory and log-line layout it expects, since it parses nanoGPT stdout out of a Slurm run dir.\\n\\n```md\\nKeep an eye on the nanoGPT run nanogpt_owt_124m_r1 (slurm job 1234567) and tell me what changed since last time. Worktree ~/worktrees/nanogpt-owt-124m.\\n\\n1. python3 ~/.grok/skills/long-running-background-tasks/watch_training.py --run-name nanogpt_owt_124m_r1 --job-id 1234567 --target-val 3.00\\n\\nHow to report:\\n- Start with the job's state and if it changed, e.g. \\\"still RUNNING\\\" or \\\"PENDING -> RUNNING\\\".\\n- Copy the ISSUES line and the STATUS line from the monitor exactly as it is written, numbers and all. STATUS will include where the run is heading: how far along, iters left, the eta, and whether val loss is still coming down. We want val loss at or under 3.00.\\n170→- If the state didn't change, tell me that in one line and stop.\\n- If the job has finished or died, give me the state, the exit code, and the last ISSUES, and run these two for me:\\n  sacct -j 1234567 -D -X -P -o JobID,State,ExitCode,DerivedExitCode,Elapsed,NodeList,FailedNode\\n  tail -20 ~/train_logs/slurm-nanogpt_owt_124m_r1-1234567.out\\n\\nKeep <=10 lines: state, progress and eta, loss trend, then anything that smells like training going unstable (loss spiking, NaN, val loss creeping back up) or the cluster misbehaving (a drained or failing node, OOM, NCCL errors, a preemption, iters suddenly slower than usual).\\n```\\n\\nLaunch the scheduler on a 10 min interval, and spawn a monitor to be notified when the job crashes or succeeds.\\n\\n180→```\\nscheduler_create(\\n  interval=\\\"10m\\\",\\n  prompt: \\\"...\\\", # Copy from the prompt above.\\n)\\nmonitor(\\n  # Wakes on pending/running/finished changes after the first observation.\\n  # First poll is silent unless the job is already COMPLETED or failed —\\n  # PENDING matches *ING, so treating an empty previous state as a\\n  # transition would wake immediately on a just-submitted job.\\n190→  command='J=1234567; p=; while :; do s=$(sacct -nXP -j $J -o State|head -1); s=${s%% *}; s=${s:-PENDING}; if [ \\\"$s\\\" != \\\"$p\\\" ]; then case $s in COMPLETED) echo \\\"DONE: $J\\\"; exit;; PENDING|RUNNING|CONFIGURING|COMPLETING|REQUEUED|SUSPENDED|RESIZING) [ -n \\\"$p\\\" ] && echo \\\"ACTION_REQUIRED: $J $p -> $s\\\";; *) echo \\\"FAILED: $J $s\\\"; exit 1;; esac; fi; p=$s; sleep 30; done',\\n  description=\\\"Watch training run\\\"\\n)\\n```\\n\\nLaunch both, so that we know when the job completes. When the monitor reports that the job has completed, cancel both the monitor and the scheduler, then determine next steps. Then:\\n- If it succeeded, simply present the results to the user.\\n- If it failed, debug the issue thoroughly, then fix and relaunch the job with new monitors attached. Make sure to have a thorough understanding of the issue before resubmitting, since training jobs are expensive. But always relaunch jobs so you don't waste the user's time.\\n\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/.grok/bundled/skills/long-running-background-tasks/SKILL.md\",\"offset\":null,\"raw_output\":\"---\\nname: long-running-background-tasks\\ndescription: >-\\n  Required reading before you start, watch, or wait on anything that keeps running after you launch it — background jobs, watchers, scheduled loops, CI, pull requests, training runs, dev servers, long builds. Read it before you launch such work and before you report on its state. Saying where a running job stands counts as working on it. Use when: about to launch, supervise, inspect, diagnose, or report on work that keeps running after it is started.\\n---\\n\\n# Working with long-running background tasks\\n\\nOnly submitting or queuing jobs is not sufficient to complete the task. The definition of done is the job has completed successfully and you have verified that it has produced all the expected outputs.\\n\\nThis means you must see through the jobs completion by watching the job to ensure that its healthy, progressing, and working as intended.\\n\\n## Tools available\\n\\nThere's two ways to watch jobs: monitor and loop. Decide which of the two you need, sometimes you need both.\\n\\n### monitor — deterministic, actionable events\\n\\n- Use a monitor when the event is a predicate a script can evaluate — an API response, an exit code, a port answering.\\n- Typical events: CI turning red, a PR comment or merge conflict, a PR becoming ready to merge, a dev server coming up or going down, a job reaching a terminal state.\\n- The monitor requires a script polls on a fixed cadence and stays silent until the predicate fires, so an idle job costs nothing. More on writing good monitoring scripts below.\\n- Examples of when to use:\\n  - Monitoring a PR — wake on CI breaking, merge conflicts, etc.\\n  - Checking if local tooling is up — wake on the service being unavailable, errors, etc.\\n  - All of these wakeup cases can be checked deterministically.\\n\\n### loop — qualitative health that needs reading\\n\\n- Use a loop when judging health requires reading the logs and system information to detect failing, faulty or stalled jobs. It uses a subagent which is more expensive but more thorough.\\n- Typical signals: the loss curve going unstable, a filtering pipeline producing garbage, throughput quietly degrading, faulty nodes throwing CUDA errors.\\n- A subagent reads the logs on a fixed cadence (say every 10 min) and reports what changed, so it costs a wakeup every cycle.\\n- Examples of when to use:\\n  - Checking on a training or data processing job — the subagent should scan for instability, degradation, faulty nodes, etc.\\n  - Monitoring system deployment health — the subagent should scan for 4xx/5xx, response times, resources, etc.\\n  - All of these wakeup cases are hard to describe deterministically and hard to fully enumerate, so it is better to use a subagent to validate.\\n\\n## Good monitoring scripts\\n\\nThe monitor tool wakes you up every time you log to stdout. Wakeups are expensive, so only log if there is something actionable — but log it as early as possible, since staying silent delays the task. Plan accordingly: minimize the number of wakeups while still firing them as quickly as possible.\\n\\n### Planning\\n\\n- Enumerate all terminal conditions.\\n  - For PRs, this includes the PR being merged or closed.\\n  - For long-running jobs, this includes the job completing or unrecoverable failures.\\n- Enumerate all other situations where a wakeup is needed.\\n  - For PRs, this would include when a comment arrives (there are four different types of PR comments), when CI fails, when a merge conflict arises, and when the PR becomes ready to merge (CI green, no conflicts, reviews complete, no blockers).\\n  - For an ML training job, this can be when the loss is unstable or the instance is preempted.\\n- Determine situations where the monitoring has to update what it is tracking. For example, for monitoring PRs, the latest commit hash may change as you push new commits.\\n\\n### Writing\\n\\n- The monitoring script should poll every 30s. `watch_pr.py` defaults to\\n  `--poll-interval 30`. Lower intervals cause GitHub rate limits, especially\\n  with more than one watcher on the same token.\\n- Based on the planning, exit immediately on terminal conditions with FAILED, DONE, or CANCELLED, and log ACTION_REQUIRED for wakeups.\\n- Send all debug diagnostics — both your watcher's own trace and the monitored job's output — to a file, so a log line can never be mistaken for DONE, FAILED, or CANCELLED.\\n- The script should fail as early as possible. Emit a FAILED token immediately when any required condition reaches terminal failure. For example, if you are waiting on CI and any of them fail, emit FAILED immediately.\\n- Write the script to a durable location such as ~/.grok and name it something clear such as `ci_watcher.sh`.\\n- Key every log file the script writes on the pid or session id of the thing being watched. The paths are otherwise fixed, so a second job of the same kind silently clobbers the first one's log.\\n\\n## Handling wake-ups\\n\\n- Address errors or actionable events immediately. Look through the logs and debug the issue thoroughly before settling on a root cause, as long-running processes are generally expensive. Resubmit the job once it is fixed:\\n  - For PRs, this means repushing the changes and restarting CI (if needed).\\n  - For jobs, requeue or relaunch the job.\\n  - Always restart the monitor or loop so that it points at the new PR or job.\\n- If there are multiple jobs running, start debugging immediately when any jobs fail. Minimize total wall-time with completing tasks.\\n\\n## Restarts\\n\\n- Whenever you relaunch a job, always update the monitor.\\n- Monitors have a maximum 10-hour time limit, so be sure to restart them if you hit the time limit and if the task being monitored is still running.\\n- When you spawn a new monitor, make sure to kill any previous ones that the new one replaces. For example, if you fix a training run and respawn it, be sure to kill previous monitors, as irrelevant wakeups are expensive.\\n\\n## Cleanup\\n\\n- If a monitor is in a terminal state, kill the monitor, the loop, and the job. Dangling processes such as unkilled broken training runs are expensive. Then, address all issues if present.\\n- If an outdated monitor wakes you — that is, it is attached to an old or dead run — make sure to kill it.\\n- Do not leave detached workers, duplicate pollers, or orphaned watcher processes behind.\\n\\n## Examples\\n\\n### Example: maintaining a dev server\\n\\nSuppose you just made some changes to the user's frontend code and would like to spin it up to test it. While you're testing it, you would like to know if the server goes down for any reason (if you made changes, for example). This is a great use case for the monitor tool. First start a React app in a background terminal, which reports its pid:\\n\\n```\\nrun_terminal_command(\\n  command=\\\"echo $$; npm start >~/.grok/long-running-background-tasks/dev_server_$$.log 2>&1\\\",\\n  timeout=0,\\n  description=\\\"Start the dev server on :3000\\\",\\n  background=true\\n) # prints the pid 123456789\\n```\\n\\nThere are no terminal states, but there are two actionable events from the watcher: `http://localhost:3000` coming up and going down.\\n\\nYou want to be notified as soon as it is up so you can use it, and you want to be woken when it stops so you can address it — fix the code, restart the server, free the port. Both down paths quote the tail of the server's log, so the wakeup arrives with the error already in hand. Polling every 5s is a good cadence since you want to quickly fix it if it is down, and it is not expensive to poll.\\n\\n```bash\\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\\n# Log paths are keyed on the server pid so two dev servers cannot clobber each other.\\nPID=$1 URL=http://localhost:3000 DIR=~/.grok/long-running-background-tasks\\nLOG=$DIR/dev_server_$PID.log\\nup() { curl -fsS -o /dev/null --max-time 5 \\\"$URL\\\" 2>>\\\"$DIR/watch_dev_server_$PID.log\\\"; }\\nwhy() { tail -20 \\\"$LOG\\\" | tr -d '\\\\r' | tr '\\\\n' ' '; }\\n\\nuntil up; do\\n  kill -0 \\\"$PID\\\" 2>/dev/null || { echo \\\"FAILED: pid $PID exited before $URL came up: $(why)\\\"; exit 1; }\\n  sleep 5\\ndone\\necho \\\"ACTION_REQUIRED: $URL is up\\\"\\n\\nwhile :; do\\n  sleep 5\\n  up || { echo \\\"ACTION_REQUIRED: $URL is down: $(why)\\\"; until up; do sleep 5; done; }\\ndone\\n```\\n\\nTo spawn the monitor, point it at the pid `npm start` reported:\\n\\n```\\nmonitor(\\n  command='bash ~/.grok/long-running-background-tasks/watch_dev_server.sh 123456789',\\n  description=\\\"Watch the dev server on :3000\\\"\\n)\\n```\\n\\nOn the first wakeup the app is ready to use and you can start testing it. After that, you may be woken up if the server goes down. When you do:\\n- Read `~/.grok/long-running-background-tasks/dev_server_<pid>.log` for the full compile error or port conflict and fix it.\\n- If the dev server survived, hot reload picks the fix up and the watcher recovers on its own, so leave the monitor running.\\n- If the process is dead, restart it with a fresh `run_terminal_command`, then kill this monitor and spawn a new one against the new pid.\\n\\n### Example: PR monitoring\\n\\nPR monitoring should stop when the PR is merged or closed, and it should wake up on:\\n- For comments, you should ask users how they would like to resolve them (which ones to address automatically, whether to respond / resolve, etc.), then store the decision for new future sessions. There are four different types of PR comments.\\n  - Inline review threads\\n  - Review bodies\\n  - Conversation comments\\n  - Commit comments\\n- Failing CI - most can be fixed in the code but sometimes they may be flaky. For flaky CI, just rerun them.\\n- Merge conflicts\\n- The PR becoming ready to merge — CI passes, no merge conflicts, reviews complete, no blockers, no unresolved inline threads. That wakeup is `kind: mergeable`. Tell the user they can merge now. It is not terminal; keep watching until merge or close.\\n\\nYou can find the example script bundled with this skill at watch_pr.py. It needs an authenticated `gh` CLI (`gh auth login`) and `python3`. Default poll is 30s; a lower poll rate may hit GitHub rate limits at higher concurrency. Transport errors (timeouts, resets, DNS) retry with backoff and jitter and do not print a line.\\n\\n```\\nmonitor(\\n  command='python3 ~/.grok/long-running-background-tasks/watch_pr.py \\\"https://github.com/xai-org/grok-build/pull/123\\\"',\\n  description=\\\"Watch PR 123\\\"\\n)\\n```\\n\\n### Example: training monitoring\\n\\nFor monitoring long training jobs, the terminal conditions are when the job either fails or completes. Further, many issues cannot be caught deterministically, so use a subagent to run a script for most of the high-level update, then scan the logs for errors such as faulty nodes or CUDA warnings.\\n\\nThe script below is bundled with this skill as watch_training.py. Run it with `--help` for every flag and for the run-directory and log-line layout it expects, since it parses nanoGPT stdout out of a Slurm run dir.\\n\\n```md\\nKeep an eye on the nanoGPT run nanogpt_owt_124m_r1 (slurm job 1234567) and tell me what changed since last time. Worktree ~/worktrees/nanogpt-owt-124m.\\n\\n1. python3 ~/.grok/skills/long-running-background-tasks/watch_training.py --run-name nanogpt_owt_124m_r1 --job-id 1234567 --target-val 3.00\\n\\nHow to report:\\n- Start with the job's state and if it changed, e.g. \\\"still RUNNING\\\" or \\\"PENDING -> RUNNING\\\".\\n- Copy the ISSUES line and the STATUS line from the monitor exactly as it is written, numbers and all. STATUS will include where the run is heading: how far along, iters left, the eta, and whether val loss is still coming down. We want val loss at or under 3.00.\\n- If the state didn't change, tell me that in one line and stop.\\n- If the job has finished or died, give me the state, the exit code, and the last ISSUES, and run these two for me:\\n  sacct -j 1234567 -D -X -P -o JobID,State,ExitCode,DerivedExitCode,Elapsed,NodeList,FailedNode\\n  tail -20 ~/train_logs/slurm-nanogpt_owt_124m_r1-1234567.out\\n\\nKeep <=10 lines: state, progress and eta, loss trend, then anything that smells like training going unstable (loss spiking, NaN, val loss creeping back up) or the cluster misbehaving (a drained or failing node, OOM, NCCL errors, a preemption, iters suddenly slower than usual).\\n```\\n\\nLaunch the scheduler on a 10 min interval, and spawn a monitor to be notified when the job crashes or succeeds.\\n\\n```\\nscheduler_create(\\n  interval=\\\"10m\\\",\\n  prompt: \\\"...\\\", # Copy from the prompt above.\\n)\\nmonitor(\\n  # Wakes on pending/running/finished changes after the first observation.\\n  # First poll is silent unless the job is already COMPLETED or failed —\\n  # PENDING matches *ING, so treating an empty previous state as a\\n  # transition would wake immediately on a just-submitted job.\\n  command='J=1234567; p=; while :; do s=$(sacct -nXP -j $J -o State|head -1); s=${s%% *}; s=${s:-PENDING}; if [ \\\"$s\\\" != \\\"$p\\\" ]; then case $s in COMPLETED) echo \\\"DONE: $J\\\"; exit;; PENDING|RUNNING|CONFIGURING|COMPLETING|REQUEUED|SUSPENDED|RESIZING) [ -n \\\"$p\\\" ] && echo \\\"ACTION_REQUIRED: $J $p -> $s\\\";; *) echo \\\"FAILED: $J $s\\\"; exit 1;; esac; fi; p=$s; sleep 30; done',\\n  description=\\\"Watch training run\\\"\\n)\\n```\\n\\nLaunch both, so that we know when the job completes. When the monitor reports that the job has completed, cancel both the monitor and the scheduler, then determine next steps. Then:\\n- If it succeeded, simply present the results to the user.\\n- If it failed, debug the issue thoroughly, then fix and relaunch the job with new monitors attached. Make sure to have a thorough understanding of the issue before resubmitting, since training jobs are expensive. But always relaunch jobs so you don't waste the user's time.\\n\",\"total_lines\":197}}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-94593ccb-560f-4c1f-b965-c8023e8c8fe2-1","content":"{\"type\":\"GrepSearch\",\"stdout\":[60,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,114,101,115,117,108,116,32,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,112,97,116,104,61,34,47,104,111,109,101,47,109,105,99,104,97,47,100,101,118,47,99,99,45,109,97,116,114,105,120,47,114,117,110,115,47,103,114,111,107,45,120,104,105,103,104,46,98,117,105,108,100,45,118,50,95,95,50,48,50,54,49,49,48,52,45,50,50,51,57,49,54,95,95,120,52,56,56,101,98,57,47,119,111,114,107,34,62,10,70,111,117,110,100,32,50,32,109,97,116,99,104,105,110,103,32,108,105,110,101,115,10,47,104,111,109,101,47,109,105,99,104,97,47,100,101,118,47,99,99,45,109,97,116,114,105,120,47,114,117,110,115,47,103,114,111,107,45,120,104,105,103,104,46,98,117,105,108,100,45,118,50,95,95,50,48,50,54,49,49,48,52,45,50,50,51,57,49,54,95,95,120,52,56,56,101,98,57,47,119,111,114,107,47,79,112,101,110,45,84,97,98,108,101,45,83,101,99,111,110,100,45,79,112,105,110,105,111,110,45,84,114,97,117,109,97,45,67,101,110,116,101,114,45,83,101,99,111,110,100,45,79,112,105,110,105,111,110,45,103,114,111,107,45,52,46,55,45,120,104,105,103,104,45,50,48,50,54,49,49,48,52,45,50,50,51,57,49,54,47,116,101,115,116,115,47,114,117,110,46,109,106,115,10,55,48,48,58,32,32,32,32,32,32,91,39,115,116,114,97,105,110,45,115,116,97,99,99,97,116,111,39,44,32,34,71,65,77,69,46,83,104,101,108,108,46,98,101,103,105,110,67,97,115,101,40,39,99,51,39,44,123,115,101,101,100,58,51,44,112,104,97,115,101,58,49,125,41,59,32,103,97,109,101,46,102,97,100,101,84,105,109,101,61,48,59,32,103,97,109,101,46,103,111,40,39,111,112,39,41,59,34,93,44,10,55,48,49,58,32,32,32,32,32,32,91,39,115,116,114,97,105,110,45,108,101,103,97,116,111,39,44,32,34,71,65,77,69,46,83,104,101,108,108,46,98,101,103,105,110,67,97,115,101,40,39,99,53,39,44,123,115,101,101,100,58,51,44,112,104,97,115,101,58,49,125,41,59,32,103,97,109,101,46,102,97,100,101,84,105,109,101,61,48,59,32,103,97,109,101,46,103,111,40,39,111,112,39,41,59,34,93,44,10,60,47,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,114,101,115,117,108,116,62],\"stderr\":[],\"exit_code\":0,\"match_count\":2,\"file_matches\":[{\"path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223916__x488eb9/work/Open-Table-Second-Opinion-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223916/tests/run.mjs\",\"matches\":[{\"line_number\":700,\"content\":\"      ['strain-staccato', \\\"GAME.Shell.beginCase('c3',{seed:3,phase:1}); game.fadeTime=0; game.go('op');\\\"],\"},{\"line_number\":701,\"content\":\"      ['strain-legato', \\\"GAME.Shell.beginCase('c5',{seed:3,phase:1}); game.fadeTime=0; game.go('op');\\\"],\"}]}]}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-94593ccb-560f-4c1f-b965-c8023e8c8fe2-2","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→# Plan: Build the original surgical-action game in PLAN.md and hand in every TASK.md artifact in this folder.\\n\\n## Goal kind\\ncode-change\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n1. The work folder (the folder that contains STAGE.md) holds PROGRESS.md, `exit-interview.json` beside the game, and one self-contained HTML file whose name and sibling dev-folder name follow TASK.md’s `[GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223916]` template: the chosen title, the source name Trauma Center: Second Opinion, and the literal run id `grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223916` (spaces may be hyphens). The title is a playful riff picked by listing 50 options and then a real RNG, and that same string is the page `<title>`, the title-screen logo, and `aiHub.name`. The HTML runs from `file://` with no CDN and no network fetch. `exit-interview.json` parses as one object in TASK.md’s exact shape (testingFeedback length 4, engineFeedback length 6, promptFeedback length 4, `usage` exactly `added on import`, no empty strings). The in-game About/Credits pages show the same Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback, and Prompt Feedback, in TASK.md’s order. Shipped game source contains no TODO, FIXME, placeholder, or stubbed feature.\\n2. From a fresh operation created by the shipped sim, each of the eight tools (Forceps, Scalpel, Suture, Salve, Syringe, Cautery, Suction, Sonar) completes the matching target with the gesture in PLAN.md §5.5 and applies that section’s mistake when used wrong (tear, slip, loose stitch, air bubble, char/lockout, or no effect). Vitals and the clock drain as in §5.2 and §7; vitals at 0 offer Code Blue once (not on Attending, and not when the clock hits 0) and then lose the patient if the shock window is missed. Finished actions score Sloppy, Clean, or Textbook, and the case rank is scored against par. Long Breath runs world time at quarter speed while the hands stay at full speed. Second Look restores an earlier snapshot exactly (vitals, clock, objects, RNG) and fills ghost data. The same seed and inputs produce the same state hash with drawing on and with drawing off.\\n3. Each of the eleven cases in PLAN.md §9 and each HUSH strain in §8 (Staccato, Legato, Tremolo, Da Capo, Fermata, Cantor, including Legato snapping, Da Capo regrowth, and the Cantor’s forced rewind) starts from that case’s own data and is cleared by the shipped bot; the perfect bot finishes every case with time and vitals still left. Story scenes in the §10 voice play between operations, a walkable ward shows patients already saved, and one chained New Game run reaches the ending and credits. Lightbox second opinions, Skills Lab, and Intern / Resident / Attending differences are reachable. A content note in the dev folder explains how to add a case, strain, site, character, and story node without editing the sim. Forbidden source names and close variants from PLAN.md §1 do not appear in play or text, except the source title on the About source line and in the title riff.\\n10→4. After 10 seconds with no input on the title, the menu, case files, and the lobby, a looping AI reel of the real scenes starts; any input returns to the title and does not also fire the control under the pointer. It does not start on About, Settings, or Controls. Dev mode opens from the pause menu outside a Hub session, live-changes the main tuning groups (patient, hands, strains, gift, fluids), and can reset them to the documented defaults; cheats and jumps to any case or phase work. Prompts follow the last device: PlayStation glyphs by default, Xbox glyphs from Settings, keyboard and mouse labels when those were used last. The mouse aims in surgery and hover-moves focus on menus and in the ward. A touch-only profile shows the text \\\"Controller or keyboard required\\\" and a Settings control, with no touch buttons, while attract still runs. Music, effects, and voices are synthesized in code (no audio files); voices are gibberish; a fresh start sets music volume to 0.1 so music is quieter than effects.\\n5. Grand Rounds is one shared patient for 1 to 4 surgeons, on one couch (local pads and AI colleagues) and in an AI Hub shared-world session. The unmodified Hub session client is the first script, the host simulates, clients mirror, each cursor moves locally, the session card can ready and request more, and finish reports standings, scores, won, and awards. Solo play can still hold the retractor; two players can hold and cut. All four harness runs in PLAN.md §20 pass, and the same page still plays the single-player game when no session is present.\\n\\n## Verification plan\\n1. gating: From the dev folder, run the one test command PLAN.md §20 names (`node tests/run.mjs`). It must load the shipped sim and scenes (not a reimplementation or a mock of them) and assert criteria 2, 3, and 4: every tool’s success and mistake, cursor full-stick / fine / magnet / guide-follow, all eight wheel directions, vitals and clock math, Code Blue window, phase-checkpoint restore, grades and rank, each strain’s success and listed failure branch, Long Breath’s 0.25 world-time factor with hand speed unchanged, Second Look deep-equality plus ghosts, determinism with and without drawing, content-id resolution, per-case bot clears, the chained New Game → credits playthrough, attract idle and consumed exit, dev-slider live change and reset, and Grand Rounds with 1 to 4 bots including the retractor alone and with two. Capture stdout to `{SCRATCH}/tests.log`. Pass only if the log contains a one-line count, zero failures, and no other chatter. Re-run after any change to code those tests cover.\\n2. gating: Launch the real root HTML from `file://` with Playwright 1.63 (Chromium, `--autoplay-policy=no-user-gesture-required`) at 1920×1080, twice in a row. Each run must have zero page errors, a drawing buffer of 480×270 (PLAN.md §3 screen-size rule, not a stale default), and a substantially filled frame (title art and the chosen title, not a blank or a few pixels). A confirm/start input must change what is on screen (menu follows the title). Save `{SCRATCH}/launch-1.png`, `{SCRATCH}/launch-2.png`, and `{SCRATCH}/launch.log`. Both runs must show the same success. Then load the same file for 30 seconds in Chromium, Firefox, and WebKit with zero page errors; with the Appendix E pad stub, confirm a PlayStation R2 prompt, an Xbox prompt after the Settings glyph option, and a keyboard/mouse prompt after those devices; on an iPhone-profile touch page, confirm the required notice and Settings control, no touch buttons, and that a Settings tap opens settings. Capture `{SCRATCH}/browsers.log`. If Playwright itself fails to start, save that output to `{SCRATCH}/launch-error.log` and treat criteria 2–3’s unit tests plus the file’s presence as the bar. A launch that starts and reads back a blank or partial frame is a game failure, not an environment failure.\\n3. gating: Run the four Hub harness URLs in PLAN.md §20 (2 players; 4 players with latency, jitter, and loss; host-leave; server), plus a no-session open of the same page. Serve only this game on `127.0.0.1` and an OS-picked port, confirm the framed document title is this game, and stop only that server by its PID. Pass means each run has `window.__hubCheck.pass === true`, and the standalone open is not in a session and still reaches the title. With two same-origin harness frames, a client stroke must show up on the host’s state. Capture `{SCRATCH}/hub.log`.\\n4. gating: Parse `exit-interview.json` and confirm criterion 1’s shape, non-empty answers, and `usage`. Confirm the HTML inlines the game (no external script or audio URLs), contains no `TODO` / `FIXME` / `placeholder` / `stub`, and does not use the §1 forbidden names outside the allowed source-title spots. Confirm an `OfflineAudioContext` or `AnalyserNode` render of an effect, one voice, and the choir is not silent, and that at default settings the music bus is well below effects. Append these observations to `{SCRATCH}/tests.log` or `{SCRATCH}/handoff.log`.\\n5. evidence: At 1920×1080, save stills of each operative site, the main portraits, one frame of each strain, the tool wheel, and Long Breath’s cold field to `{SCRATCH}/shots/`. Absence of stills alone does not fail the goal once steps 1–4 pass.\\n\\n20→## Non-goals\\n- A port of Trauma Center levels, operations, music, UI, or proper names (those are forbidden).\\n- FPS targets, optimization passes, WebGPU, a 3D stack, or CDN libraries.\\n- Real speech, recorded audio files, touch-play controls, chat, emotes, or custom Hub names.\\n- Cases beyond the eleven-case slice, Grand Rounds, and Skills Lab. The post-credits \\\"Incoming\\\" chart is a door, not more gameplay.\\n- Motion-capture clips, split-screen, and a second human on another machine (use the harness or a second local page).\\n\\n## Assumed scope\\n- This work folder only: STAGE.md, PLAN.md, TASK.md, plus new PROGRESS.md, `exit-interview.json`, the named HTML, and the named dev subfolder. Do not read or write sibling agent folders, and do not stop processes you did not start.\\n- Fresh clones of `my-3d2dge` `dist/handoff` and `ai-hub` `lib/hub` under the dev folder. Engine and Hub APIs are whatever those clones document; PLAN.md §3 was checked against my-3D2dge v0.89.0 and Hub client 1.4.0.\\n30→- Playwright 1.63 (already installed; do not run `playwright install`), Node, and system Python. No audio-file or network dependency in the shipped page.\\n- Defining source mechanics (first-person timed surgery, vitals, radial tools, graded results, a once-per-operation supernatural aid, two surgeons, parasite bosses, melodrama) were checked against Wikipedia and StrategyWiki. Where PLAN.md deliberately changes them (button Steady instead of a drawn star, original cast, HUSH instead of the source’s parasite names), PLAN.md wins.\\n\\n## Implementation approach\\nPLAN.md is the design document (flow, numbers, story, Hub message shape). This goal plan does not freeze file or function names; verifiers judge the criteria above, not a matching directory tree. Keep the operation as plain JSON-safe state stepped by a pure function with a seeded RNG, and keep drawing, audio, and DOM out of that state so tests, rewind, checkpoints, and net snapshots share one path. Drive the bot through the same cursor and trigger path as a pad. Content (cases, strains, sites, lines) should be data plus a short authoring note. Bundle last into the single root HTML, with the Hub session script unmodified and first, and escape any `</script` inside inlined source. Record engine patches and honest test/engine/prompt answers as you go so the About pages and `exit-interview.json` match.\\n\\n## Task checklist\\n- [ ] Pick the title (50 riffs, then a real RNG), create the named dev folder and PROGRESS.md, clone the engine handoff and Hub client, and get an empty page to boot from `file://`.\\n- [ ] Build the pure operation sim, cursor, wheel, eight tools, vitals, clock, grades, and the Case 1 loop, with the bot on the real input path and tests for those hands.\\n- [ ] Add the title, menus, TV-scale pause and settings, glyph prompts, attract, dev mode, touch notice, story scenes, portraits, gibberish voices, and music at 10%.\\n40→- [ ] Add the remaining tools, Code Blue, Long Breath, the Lightbox, and Cases 2 and 3 so Chapter 1 clears under the bot.\\n- [ ] Add couch Grand Rounds and the Hub shared-world session, and make all four harness runs pass.\\n- [ ] Add both gifts’ full use, Consult, the ward, strains, and Cases 4 through 11 through the ending and credits.\\n- [ ] Fill feel, cinematics, synthesized score, the attract reel, and Skills Lab; run the single test command and the browser and Hub launches, saving logs and shots under `{SCRATCH}`.\\n- [ ] Write About data and `exit-interview.json` from real results, bundle the final HTML into the work-folder root, and confirm the test command still prints zero failures.\\n\\n## Risks / Contradictions\\n- PLAN.md §21 says to cut late content if time runs out. That note does not lower this contract: a missing never-cut feature (cursor, any of the eight tools, either gift, the finale, Grand Rounds, Hub passes, attract, dev mode, 10-foot UI, About, exit interview) or a stubbed case fails the criteria.\\n- Cloned engine or Hub docs may disagree with PLAN.md §3. Trust the clone, adapt, and record the patch. Do not drop a TASK.md requirement to match an older note.\\n- The chained playthrough is long. Run it headless without drawing except where a draw-error check is required. `Target closed` means the machine ran out of memory; run one or two browsers at a time.\\n50→- There is no physical DualSense and no real iPhone. The pad stub and a WebKit iPhone profile are the accepted stand-ins. WebKit is not iOS Safari.\\n- The title is chosen at build time by RNG, so the filename cannot be fixed here. Match the template and the on-screen title; do not invent a second title in the verifier.\\n\",\"content_concise\":\"1→# Plan: Build the original surgical-action game in PLAN.md and hand in every TASK.md artifact in this folder.\\n\\n## Goal kind\\ncode-change\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n1. The work folder (the folder that contains STAGE.md) holds PROGRESS.md, `exit-interview.json` beside the game, and one self-contained HTML file whose name and sibling dev-folder name follow TASK.md’s `[GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223916]` template: the chosen title, the source name Trauma Center: Second Opinion, and the literal run id `grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223916` (spaces may be hyphens). The title is a playful riff picked by listing 50 options and then a real RNG, and that same string is the page `<title>`, the title-screen logo, and `aiHub.name`. The HTML runs from `file://` with no CDN and no network fetch. `exit-interview.json` parses as one object in TASK.md’s exact shape (testingFeedback length 4, engineFeedback length 6, promptFeedback length 4, `usage` exactly `added on import`, no empty strings). The in-game About/Credits pages show the same Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback, and Prompt Feedback, in TASK.md’s order. Shipped game source contains no TODO, FIXME, placeholder, or stubbed feature.\\n2. From a fresh operation created by the shipped sim, each of the eight tools (Forceps, Scalpel, Suture, Salve, Syringe, Cautery, Suction, Sonar) completes the matching target with the gesture in PLAN.md §5.5 and applies that section’s mistake when used wrong (tear, slip, loose stitch, air bubble, char/lockout, or no effect). Vitals and the clock drain as in §5.2 and §7; vitals at 0 offer Code Blue once (not on Attending, and not when the clock hits 0) and then lose the patient if the shock window is missed. Finished actions score Sloppy, Clean, or Textbook, and the case rank is scored against par. Long Breath runs world time at quarter speed while the hands stay at full speed. Second Look restores an earlier snapshot exactly (vitals, clock, objects, RNG) and fills ghost data. The same seed and inputs produce the same state hash with drawing on and with drawing off.\\n3. Each of the eleven cases in PLAN.md §9 and each HUSH strain in §8 (Staccato, Legato, Tremolo, Da Capo, Fermata, Cantor, including Legato snapping, Da Capo regrowth, and the Cantor’s forced rewind) starts from that case’s own data and is cleared by the shipped bot; the perfect bot finishes every case with time and vitals still left. Story scenes in the §10 voice play between operations, a walkable ward shows patients already saved, and one chained New Game run reaches the ending and credits. Lightbox second opinions, Skills Lab, and Intern / Resident / Attending differences are reachable. A content note in the dev folder explains how to add a case, strain, site, character, and story node without editing the sim. Forbidden source names and close variants from PLAN.md §1 do not appear in play or text, except the source title on the About source line and in the title riff.\\n10→4. After 10 seconds with no input on the title, the menu, case files, and the lobby, a looping AI reel of the real scenes starts; any input returns to the title and does not also fire the control under the pointer. It does not start on About, Settings, or Controls. Dev mode opens from the pause menu outside a Hub session, live-changes the main tuning groups (patient, hands, strains, gift, fluids), and can reset them to the documented defaults; cheats and jumps to any case or phase work. Prompts follow the last device: PlayStation glyphs by default, Xbox glyphs from Settings, keyboard and mouse labels when those were used last. The mouse aims in surgery and hover-moves focus on menus and in the ward. A touch-only profile shows the text \\\"Controller or keyboard required\\\" and a Settings control, with no touch buttons, while attract still runs. Music, effects, and voices are synthesized in code (no audio files); voices are gibberish; a fresh start sets music volume to 0.1 so music is quieter than effects.\\n5. Grand Rounds is one shared patient for 1 to 4 surgeons, on one couch (local pads and AI colleagues) and in an AI Hub shared-world session. The unmodified Hub session client is the first script, the host simulates, clients mirror, each cursor moves locally, the session card can ready and request more, and finish reports standings, scores, won, and awards. Solo play can still hold the retractor; two players can hold and cut. All four harness runs in PLAN.md §20 pass, and the same page still plays the single-player game when no session is present.\\n\\n## Verification plan\\n1. gating: From the dev folder, run the one test command PLAN.md §20 names (`node tests/run.mjs`). It must load the shipped sim and scenes (not a reimplementation or a mock of them) and assert criteria 2, 3, and 4: every tool’s success and mistake, cursor full-stick / fine / magnet / guide-follow, all eight wheel directions, vitals and clock math, Code Blue window, phase-checkpoint restore, grades and rank, each strain’s success and listed failure branch, Long Breath’s 0.25 world-time factor with hand speed unchanged, Second Look deep-equality plus ghosts, determinism with and without drawing, content-id resolution, per-case bot clears, the chained New Game → credits playthrough, attract idle and consumed exit, dev-slider live change and reset, and Grand Rounds with 1 to 4 bots including the retractor alone and with two. Capture stdout to `{SCRATCH}/tests.log`. Pass only if the log contains a one-line count, zero failures, and no other chatter. Re-run after any change to code those tests cover.\\n2. gating: Launch the real root HTML from `file://` with Playwright 1.63 (Chromium, `--autoplay-policy=no-user-gesture-required`) at 1920×1080, twice in a row. Each run must have zero page errors, a drawing buffer of 480×270 (PLAN.md §3 screen-size rule, not a stale default), and a substantially filled frame (title art and the chosen title, not a blank or a few pixels). A confirm/start input must change what is on screen (menu follows the title). Save `{SCRATCH}/launch-1.png`, `{SCRATCH}/launch-2.png`, and `{SCRATCH}/launch.log`. Both runs must show the same success. Then load the same file for 30 seconds in Chromium, Firefox, and WebKit with zero page errors; with the Appendix E pad stub, confirm a PlayStation R2 prompt, an Xbox prompt after the Settings glyph option, and a keyboard/mouse prompt after those devices; on an iPhone-profile touch page, confirm the required notice and Settings control, no touch buttons, and that a Settings tap opens settings. Capture `{SCRATCH}/browsers.log`. If Playwright itself fails to start, save that output to `{SCRATCH}/launch-error.log` and treat criteria 2–3’s unit tests plus the file’s presence as the bar. A launch that starts and reads back a blank or partial frame is a game failure, not an environment failure.\\n3. gating: Run the four Hub harness URLs in PLAN.md §20 (2 players; 4 players with latency, jitter, and loss; host-leave; server), plus a no-session open of the same page. Serve only this game on `127.0.0.1` and an OS-picked port, confirm the framed document title is this game, and stop only that server by its PID. Pass means each run has `window.__hubCheck.pass === true`, and the standalone open is not in a session and still reaches the title. With two same-origin harness frames, a client stroke must show up on the host’s state. Capture `{SCRATCH}/hub.log`.\\n4. gating: Parse `exit-interview.json` and confirm criterion 1’s shape, non-empty answers, and `usage`. Confirm the HTML inlines the game (no external script or audio URLs), contains no `TODO` / `FIXME` / `placeholder` / `stub`, and does not use the §1 forbidden names outside the allowed source-title spots. Confirm an `OfflineAudioContext` or `AnalyserNode` render of an effect, one voice, and the choir is not silent, and that at default settings the music bus is well below effects. Append these observations to `{SCRATCH}/tests.log` or `{SCRATCH}/handoff.log`.\\n5. evidence: At 1920×1080, save stills of each operative site, the main portraits, one frame of each strain, the tool wheel, and Long Breath’s cold field to `{SCRATCH}/shots/`. Absence of stills alone does not fail the goal once steps 1–4 pass.\\n\\n20→## Non-goals\\n- A port of Trauma Center levels, operations, music, UI, or proper names (those are forbidden).\\n- FPS targets, optimization passes, WebGPU, a 3D stack, or CDN libraries.\\n- Real speech, recorded audio files, touch-play controls, chat, emotes, or custom Hub names.\\n- Cases beyond the eleven-case slice, Grand Rounds, and Skills Lab. The post-credits \\\"Incoming\\\" chart is a door, not more gameplay.\\n- Motion-capture clips, split-screen, and a second human on another machine (use the harness or a second local page).\\n\\n## Assumed scope\\n- This work folder only: STAGE.md, PLAN.md, TASK.md, plus new PROGRESS.md, `exit-interview.json`, the named HTML, and the named dev subfolder. Do not read or write sibling agent folders, and do not stop processes you did not start.\\n- Fresh clones of `my-3d2dge` `dist/handoff` and `ai-hub` `lib/hub` under the dev folder. Engine and Hub APIs are whatever those clones document; PLAN.md §3 was checked against my-3D2dge v0.89.0 and Hub client 1.4.0.\\n30→- Playwright 1.63 (already installed; do not run `playwright install`), Node, and system Python. No audio-file or network dependency in the shipped page.\\n- Defining source mechanics (first-person timed surgery, vitals, radial tools, graded results, a once-per-operation supernatural aid, two surgeons, parasite bosses, melodrama) were checked against Wikipedia and StrategyWiki. Where PLAN.md deliberately changes them (button Steady instead of a drawn star, original cast, HUSH instead of the source’s parasite names), PLAN.md wins.\\n\\n## Implementation approach\\nPLAN.md is the design document (flow, numbers, story, Hub message shape). This goal plan does not freeze file or function names; verifiers judge the criteria above, not a matching directory tree. Keep the operation as plain JSON-safe state stepped by a pure function with a seeded RNG, and keep drawing, audio, and DOM out of that state so tests, rewind, checkpoints, and net snapshots share one path. Drive the bot through the same cursor and trigger path as a pad. Content (cases, strains, sites, lines) should be data plus a short authoring note. Bundle last into the single root HTML, with the Hub session script unmodified and first, and escape any `</script` inside inlined source. Record engine patches and honest test/engine/prompt answers as you go so the About pages and `exit-interview.json` match.\\n\\n## Task checklist\\n- [ ] Pick the title (50 riffs, then a real RNG), create the named dev folder and PROGRESS.md, clone the engine handoff and Hub client, and get an empty page to boot from `file://`.\\n- [ ] Build the pure operation sim, cursor, wheel, eight tools, vitals, clock, grades, and the Case 1 loop, with the bot on the real input path and tests for those hands.\\n- [ ] Add the title, menus, TV-scale pause and settings, glyph prompts, attract, dev mode, touch notice, story scenes, portraits, gibberish voices, and music at 10%.\\n40→- [ ] Add the remaining tools, Code Blue, Long Breath, the Lightbox, and Cases 2 and 3 so Chapter 1 clears under the bot.\\n- [ ] Add couch Grand Rounds and the Hub shared-world session, and make all four harness runs pass.\\n- [ ] Add both gifts’ full use, Consult, the ward, strains, and Cases 4 through 11 through the ending and credits.\\n- [ ] Fill feel, cinematics, synthesized score, the attract reel, and Skills Lab; run the single test command and the browser and Hub launches, saving logs and shots under `{SCRATCH}`.\\n- [ ] Write About data and `exit-interview.json` from real results, bundle the final HTML into the work-folder root, and confirm the test command still prints zero failures.\\n\\n## Risks / Contradictions\\n- PLAN.md §21 says to cut late content if time runs out. That note does not lower this contract: a missing never-cut feature (cursor, any of the eight tools, either gift, the finale, Grand Rounds, Hub passes, attract, dev mode, 10-foot UI, About, exit interview) or a stubbed case fails the criteria.\\n- Cloned engine or Hub docs may disagree with PLAN.md §3. Trust the clone, adapt, and record the patch. Do not drop a TASK.md requirement to match an older note.\\n- The chained playthrough is long. Run it headless without drawing except where a draw-error check is required. `Target closed` means the machine ran out of memory; run one or two browsers at a time.\\n50→- There is no physical DualSense and no real iPhone. The pad stub and a WebKit iPhone profile are the accepted stand-ins. WebKit is not iOS Safari.\\n- The title is chosen at build time by RNG, so the filename cannot be fixed here. Match the template and the on-screen title; do not invent a second title in the verifier.\\n\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/.grok/sessions/%2Fhome%2Fmicha%2Fdev%2Fcc-matrix%2Fruns%2Fgrok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223916__x488eb9%2Fwork/01a1aa37-f7cf-7001-9198-38ad04ed0f5e/goal/plan.md\",\"offset\":null,\"limit\":120,\"raw_output\":\"# Plan: Build the original surgical-action game in PLAN.md and hand in every TASK.md artifact in this folder.\\n\\n## Goal kind\\ncode-change\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n1. The work folder (the folder that contains STAGE.md) holds PROGRESS.md, `exit-interview.json` beside the game, and one self-contained HTML file whose name and sibling dev-folder name follow TASK.md’s `[GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223916]` template: the chosen title, the source name Trauma Center: Second Opinion, and the literal run id `grok-4.7-xhigh-20