{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_505","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":" the spawn-rate test is dishonest. It sets strains.spawn via Dev.set, but spawnStrain ignores that value and calls strainSpawn(tuneOf(null)) instead.\n\nThe spawn-rate test passed because `tuneOf(null)` reads the live `GAME.TUNE` state, so `Dev.set` on `strains.spawn` is respected. The reedWait test sets stage 2 and a 0.02 wake, then steps once with `T.strains.spawn`.Drafting a JavaScript reed wait test.\n\nThe reedWait test expects wake to reset to 6/spawn after one step. The step subtracts 0.05 from the 0.02 wake, making it negative, then restores it via strainSpawn. The key question is whether hold:true freezes world stepping or merely prevents phase advance. If hold freezes the world, the test would not reach that reset.\n\nThe reedWait test passed, confirming the strain step logic works.\n\nThe reed wait test A new concern is that spawnStrain ignores passed. The wake timer the tune argument passed to step and instead drops below zero and reads the global GAME.TUNE resets to 6 or 3 depending via tuneOf(null). Since playTune clones the on spawn rate. A subtle tune object, the operation receives a separate bug exists in the stepTremolo clone while the global logic. state is mutated by Dev.set. This means spawnStrain may not be using the cloned tune at all.\n\nThe spawnStrain global-read concern is resolved. playTune clones GAME.TUNE and only overwrites cursor and assist, leaving strains.spawn untouched. Live spawn rates therefore follow the dev slider correctly.\n\nSpawn slider and beat changes now correctly update reed wait, bead counts, landing, and reed window. The clear phase test passed for c4 with 166 checks. A potential bug remains: phaseClear may still return false if live objects are not marked done, since the cheat\n\nThe beat fix is confirmed: only sets them done playTune clones the without advancing the simulation enough to global tune, so live trigger phaseClear. beat changes flow through to stepStaccato and stepTremolo. Now examining the clear-phase test, which begins a case with phase 1 and then steps through the dev menu to confirm the strain remains active.\n\nThe confirm press advances the frame, so phaseClear returned true and the test passed. The bead logic is being examined: beads are skipped in the object loop, but a later staccato check may affect phase clearing.\n\nVerifying the clear-phase test drives the real dev menu. The CHEATS array places \"Clear phase\" at index 7. After pressing toolNext to switch tabs, seven\n\nThe bead logic is  downs from index 0 confirmed safe becauselands on that entry. phaseClear returns  The press function true once the strainperforms a tap plus  is marked done. Thetwo advances. focus has shifted to whether the cheat marks lobe objects as done, since lobes are counted in the object loop.\n\nThe clear-phase test passed, confirming the prior bug is fixed.\n\nThe cheat marks all The cheat now sets strain.done so phaseClear objects done before succeeds and the staccato the strain is marked phase advances to Repair. done. phaseClear should A potential remaining defect remains: Da  pass on the next stepCapo regrowth or other unless something in strains might regenerate the same frame undoes objects on the next it. step before phaseClear runs.\n\nThe clear-phase fix is confirmed safe for all strain kinds. phaseClear only checks needIv, so no other flags block advancement. The next step is to verify whether\n\nThe prior clear phase S.hold affects phaseClear bug is confirmed fixed. The cheat returns without or advancePhase. stepping, so the next frame's stepStrain exits early when the strain is done and cannot revive objects. The cautery lock test is now being drafted to verify that burning applies a lock effect and a slip outcome.\n\nTests for magnet, guide, wheel, loose stitch, ghosts, and cautery lock all appear to drive real simulation functions. The cautery lock test now requires both a lock event and an active lock timer. Previously the check passed whenever any slips occurred. The test passed, confirming the implementation emits the 'lock' event only during genuine lockout.\n\nThe cautery burn test requires 44 steps to accumulate enough heat for the lock effect. A loop bug was noted\n\nI should verify the where event arrays  cautery implementationmight duplicate entries, is not cheating by  but it is harmless.always emitting a lock The test setup uses event. I will read stepCautery to confirm a bare burn at coordinates the test would fail (40, 40) with no target. without a real lockout. I am now hunting for real bugs in the shipped fixes. I began examining the ghost painting code.\n\nThe test runner loads source JavaScript files via vm.runInContext.\n\nThe ghost painting The HTML bundle is  code draws every recordedseparate and was rebuilt. hand position as a  Launch logs show bothstatic cyan dot at once, launches reported zero rather than animating errors. However the them over time. This text field is empty, matches the Second Look behavior and is which is unexpected not a bug relative  for a filled frame.to the original gap. I will inspect the  The next step is toPNG screenshots to verify verify whether ghostT the actual output. gets cleared.\n\nThe tests.log file contains exactly 166 checks with zero failures.\n\nghostT decrements  The tests-fast.log and stops painting whenshows 134 checks, likely it reaches zero. Ghosts an older or subset  are excluded from therun. I will inspect  simulation hash, sothe launch screenshots toggling drawing on to confirm they are or off will not break substantially filled. determinism. I am now checking whether playTune's cursor default logic matches the documented TUNING.hands.cursor value.\n\nThe cursor default logic is correct for normal usage. A floating-point\n\nI will not re-run  edge case remains: the full test suite the slider step sizeunless the captured  might not land exactlylog looks suspicious. on the default value, I am counting check() so the strict equality calls to verify the check could fail to 166 result is plausible. reset the cursor properly. A cheap ghost drawing bug was spotted: ghost marks appear at both cursor positions and strain positions.\n\nThe ghost drawing feature is intentional.\n\nThe slider step size It renders every recorded is 21, so the default cursor from ring samples cursor of 330 can be after the rewind point. returned to exactly\n\nState hashing is deterministic by reversing direction. because drawing only This rules out the  reads ghosts and neverfloating-point edge  modifies them.\n\nA minor playTune bug existscase.\n\nspawnBeadsN passes where integer comparisons the live tune object may fail if Dev.set directly into strainSpawn, stores a float value so spawnStrain uses like 330.001 instead the cloned tune rather of 330. than global state.\n\nspawnBeadsN passes the live tune object into strainSpawn, so it reads the cloned tune. spawnStrain still\n\nCursor speed settings calls tuneOf(null), apply correctly when which reads global  the dev slider is atstate. Initial beads its default of 330. spawn from global state, while later bead spawns The tuned speed of  use the cloned tune.480 and the preference speed of 200 produce Mid-phase slider changes therefore only affect a ratio near 2.4, which the test confirmed. future beads.\n\nA potential real bug was spotted: spawnStrain calls tuneOf(null) instead of using the tune argument passed to Op.step.\n\nThe spawnStrain tune bug is not a real issue. The dev slider writes to GAME.TUNE, so spawnStrain reads the correct value\n\nTremolo reed wait  via tuneOf(null). Staccatouses the cloned tune passed to stepTremolo, initial spawns also use GAME.TUNE. stepTremolo so live slider changes mid-strain are respected. receives the tune object The clear-phase fix from Op.step. is confirmed safe for all strains, including Da Capo, because stepDacapo returns early when strain.done is set. I am now re-examining the clear-phase code itself.\n\nThe beat slider timing test passed. The phase name is confirmed as 'Repair'. One successful clear-phase test validates\n\nThe clear-phase fix the mechanism, so exhaustive is confirmed safe for all strain kinds. phaseClear strain testing is unnecessary. returns true once the strain is marked done and all objects are finished, allowing advancePhase to proceed. The prior gap is resolved.\n\nA new concern is whether the dev menu navigation actually lands on the Clear phase. The test presses down seven times from tab zero, but toolNext switches to tab one first, so the index arithmetic needs verification.\n\nThe clear-phase mechanism is sound for all strains.\n\nThe test reached phase The cheat marks every object done without 2, confirming the Clear-phase advancing the simulation, cheat was triggered. The bot driver was  so the next frame's stepStrain exits immediatelynull, so automatic phase and phaseClear succeeds. advancement is unlikely; New objects are unlikely the cheat itself caused to spawn or revive  the transition.in that same frame, and vitals remain stable.\n\nThe confirm press test passed, confirming the phase advances correctly. The prior gaps are genuinely fixed. I will inspect the launch PNGs to verify they are substantially filled, since the log text field is empty. Browsers and hub logs also pass.\n\nThe clear-phase test passed, but I am now inspecting the dev menu input handler to rule out accidental phase advancement from other rows. A \"lose\" selection would kill the patient rather than advance the phase, and an \"auto\" toggle would not clear a staccato phase in two frames.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-f4c048ae-677f-430f-ab19-8345d2a9c8cc-24","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223916__x488eb9/work/Open-Table-Second-Opinion-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-2